/* ======================= CHAPTER 4 =======================
   Sources consulted: Polzer, RAG with Python Cookbook ch. 1–2 (loaders,
   unstructured partitioning, table strategies, metadata, abbreviation
   cleaning; early release 2025); unstructured.io docs, "Document elements
   and metadata" (element taxonomy, checked 2026-09-03); Docling technical
   report, arXiv 2408.09869 (DocLayNet + TableFormer, checked 2026-09-03);
   Broder 1997 "On the resemblance and containment of documents" (shingling,
   MinHash) as presented in Manning, Raghavan & Schütze, IIR §19.6; Gao et al.
   2024 survey §III.B (metadata, time-aware retrieval); Mendelevitch & Bao
   ch. 4 (ingestion security, refresh); Lewis et al. 2020 (100-word passages
   of Wikipedia as the original unit). */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 4, part: 2, free: true, title: "Documents In: Loading, Parsing, and Metadata",
  blurb: "Most retrieval failures are ingestion failures wearing a disguise. A two-column PDF read in the wrong order, a table flattened to word soup, a header repeated on every page, a chunk that says \"it\" with no antecedent: none of these are fixed by a better embedding model. You will partition real documents into typed elements, decide what to do with tables and images, design the metadata that every later filter will depend on, find near-duplicates with a hash trick from 1997, and make the pipeline idempotent so re-running it never duplicates a chunk.",
  sections: [
    { title: "What Is in a Corpus",
      html: "<p>Before any embedding model runs, someone has to answer a boring question: what documents, in what formats, from where, owned by whom, changing how often? Larkspur Airlines, the corpus this course carries through every chapter, looks like this when you inventory it.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<text x='90' y='24' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>source</text><text x='300' y='24' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>format</text><text x='440' y='24' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>count</text><text x='530' y='24' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>changes</text><text x='640' y='24' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>who may read</text>" +
      "<line x1='40' y1='32' x2='740' y2='32' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='90' y='56' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>policy pages (baggage, refunds, pets…)</text><text x='300' y='56' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>HTML, Markdown</text><text x='440' y='56' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>40</text><text x='530' y='56' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>monthly</text><text x='640' y='56' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>everyone</text>" +
      "<text x='90' y='84' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>partner airport contracts</text><text x='300' y='84' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>PDF, scanned</text><text x='440' y='84' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>12</text><text x='530' y='84' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>yearly</text><text x='640' y='84' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10.5'>legal, finance</text>" +
      "<text x='90' y='112' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>fares and routes</text><text x='300' y='112' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>CSV / SQL table</text><text x='440' y='112' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>1 table</text><text x='530' y='112' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>daily</text><text x='640' y='112' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>everyone</text>" +
      "<text x='90' y='140' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>support tickets</text><text x='300' y='140' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>JSON from the helpdesk</text><text x='440' y='140' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>300</text><text x='530' y='140' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>hourly</text><text x='640' y='140' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10.5'>support staff</text>" +
      "<text x='90' y='168' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>incident post-mortems</text><text x='300' y='168' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>Markdown with tables</text><text x='440' y='168' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>6</text><text x='530' y='168' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>rarely</text><text x='640' y='168' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10.5'>engineering</text>" +
      "<line x1='40' y1='184' x2='740' y2='184' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='380' y='212' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>five sources, four formats, three permission classes, refresh rates from hourly to yearly</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>The Larkspur inventory. Every column becomes a decision later: format decides the parser, refresh rate decides the ingest schedule, and the last column becomes a filter that Chapter 28 must prove cannot leak.</div></div>" +
      "<p>The inventory is unglamorous and it is where most of the value is decided. A figure repeated for decades, and never traced to a firm source, says that around 80 percent of enterprise information is <strong>unstructured data</strong>: slides, documents, email, chat. Whether or not the number is right, the shape is: the facts a RAG system needs live mostly in formats that were designed for humans to read on a page, not for machines to parse. The <strong>ingest flow</strong> from Chapter 1 begins by turning those pages into text the rest of the pipeline can trust, and every stage after it inherits its mistakes.</p>" +
      "<p>Notice what the inventory already tells you about the three questions of Chapter 1. The fares table is structured and will be answered with SQL, not chunks (Chapter 22). The contracts have hard boundaries and per-user permissions, which is the case for structure and for filtering (Chapters 23 and 28). The post-mortems are the multi-hop source for Q4. And the tickets refresh hourly, which means the pipeline that ingests them will run all day and must not duplicate anything when it does.</p>" },

    { title: "Documents Are Not Text: Element-Typed Parsing", viz: "docPartition", vizTitle: "Click regions of a page; toggle naive extraction",
      html: "<p>A PDF is not a text file with formatting. It is a page-description format: a list of drawing instructions that place glyphs at coordinates. There is no paragraph object, no column, often no space character; the space between two words may be a horizontal offset. Reading order is not stored, it is inferred by whatever extracts the text, and a naive extractor infers it line by line across the whole page width. On a two-column layout that interleaves the columns: the first line of the left column, then the first line of the right, and so on. The sentences are still there, cut into strips and shuffled. Embed those strips and you index nonsense that scores well on nothing.</p>" +
      "<p>The fix is to parse a document into <strong>elements</strong> before you chunk it. An element is a region of the page with a type and a text: a title, a paragraph of narrative text, a list item, a table, a figure with its caption, a header or footer, a page number. The open-source <code>unstructured</code> library, which the cookbook source of this course uses throughout, names roughly fifteen types, including <code>Title</code>, <code>NarrativeText</code>, <code>ListItem</code>, <code>Table</code>, <code>Image</code>, <code>Header</code>, <code>Footer</code>, and <code>PageNumber</code>, and attaches metadata to each: the file, the page, the bounding box, and a parent element so that a paragraph knows which title it sits under. Docling, an MIT-licensed converter described in a 2024 technical report, does the same job with dedicated layout and table-structure models (DocLayNet and TableFormer) and emits a document tree. Both run in two modes: a fast mode that trusts the PDF's own text and geometry, and a slower mode that renders each page to an image and runs <strong>layout analysis</strong> with a vision model over it, which is the only way to read a scanned contract.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<rect x='30' y='30' width='260' height='250' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<rect x='42' y='40' width='236' height='16' rx='3' fill='#1c2a45'/><text x='160' y='52' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='8.5'>Larkspur Airlines · Baggage Policy · p.3</text>" +
      "<rect x='42' y='64' width='236' height='18' rx='3' fill='#2de2ff' opacity='0.25'/><text x='160' y='77' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='9'>3.2 Musical instruments</text>" +
      "<rect x='42' y='90' width='112' height='120' rx='3' fill='#4f7cff' opacity='0.2'/><rect x='166' y='90' width='112' height='120' rx='3' fill='#4f7cff' opacity='0.2'/>" +
      "<text x='98' y='106' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>A cello may travel in</text><text x='98' y='118' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>the cabin only when a</text><text x='98' y='130' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>seat has been bought</text><text x='98' y='142' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>for it at the time of</text><text x='98' y='154' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>booking. The case…</text>" +
      "<text x='222' y='106' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>Guitars and smaller</text><text x='222' y='118' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>instruments count as</text><text x='222' y='130' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>one piece of cabin</text><text x='222' y='142' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>baggage if the case</text><text x='222' y='154' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='7.5'>fits the sizer…</text>" +
      "<rect x='42' y='218' width='236' height='40' rx='3' fill='#ffd166' opacity='0.2'/><text x='160' y='234' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='8'>instrument | max cm | seat required</text><text x='160' y='248' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='8'>cello | 130 | yes</text>" +
      "<rect x='42' y='264' width='236' height='10' rx='3' fill='#1c2a45'/><text x='160' y='272' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='7'>Larkspur Airlines · Confidential · 3</text>" +
      "<text x='520' y='44' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>elements the parser emits</text>" +
      "<text x='330' y='72' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>Header</text><text x='400' y='72' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>\"Larkspur Airlines · Baggage Policy · p.3\"</text>" +
      "<text x='330' y='96' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10'>Title</text><text x='400' y='96' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>\"3.2 Musical instruments\"</text>" +
      "<text x='330' y='120' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='10'>NarrativeText</text><text x='420' y='120' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>\"A cello may travel in the cabin only…\"</text>" +
      "<text x='330' y='144' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='10'>NarrativeText</text><text x='420' y='144' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>\"Guitars and smaller instruments…\"</text>" +
      "<text x='330' y='168' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>Table</text><text x='400' y='168' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>3 rows, kept as HTML</text>" +
      "<text x='330' y='192' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>Footer, PageNumber</text><text x='470' y='192' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>dropped before chunking</text>" +
      "<text x='330' y='230' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10'>naive extraction of the same page:</text>" +
      "<text x='330' y='248' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>\"A cello may travel in Guitars and smaller</text><text x='330' y='262' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>the cabin only when a instruments count as…\"</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>One page, two readings. The parser on the right knows the columns, the heading, and the table; the naive extractor at the bottom interleaves the columns and the sentence about cellos never exists as a sentence.</div></div>" +
      "<p>Click regions of the page in the machine above and read the element each becomes. Then toggle <em>naive extraction</em> and read the cello paragraph again: find the sentence that was cut across the two columns. That sentence is Larkspur Q1's answer, and after naive extraction no retriever in this course can find it, because it is no longer in the corpus.</p>" +
      "<h3>Scans, images of text, and when to use a vision model</h3>" +
      "<p>Some of Larkspur's contracts are scans: images with no text layer at all. Two tools read them. <strong>Optical character recognition</strong> engines such as Tesseract are fast, cheap, run locally, and are good on clean printed text and poor on everything else. <strong>Vision-language models</strong>, asked to transcribe or describe a page image, are slower and cost per page, and are far more robust to layout, handwriting, stamps, and tables. The cookbook author's rule is a good one: OCR when the documents are clean, numerous, and uniform; a vision model when they are messy or few. Either way, keep the page image or its path in the metadata, because when a retrieved chunk came from a scan, the human checking the answer will want to see the page.</p>" +
      "<div class='callout'>Try it on your machine. With Python and <code>pypdf</code> installed, run <code>python -c \"import pypdf,sys; print(pypdf.PdfReader(sys.argv[1]).pages[0].extract_text())\" some-two-column.pdf</code> on any two-column paper. Read the first ten lines. You will see either interleaved columns or a sensible order, depending on how the PDF was produced; the point is that you cannot know which without looking, and a pipeline that never looks is indexing whichever it got.</div>" },

    { title: "Tables and Images: Summarize to Retrieve, Inject the Original to Answer",
      html: "<p>A table is the worst case for a text pipeline. Flattened to text it becomes a run of numbers with no headers near them; embedded, that run sits nowhere useful in the space; and if a chunk boundary falls in the middle of it, the row about cellos and the column header \"seat required\" end up in different chunks. The cookbook source of this course gives three strategies for tabular data, and the right one depends on the question the table will be asked.</p>" +
      "<ul><li><strong>Row to sentence.</strong> Turn each row into a self-contained sentence using the column headers (\"A cello has a maximum dimension of 130 cm and requires a seat\"). Each sentence embeds well and answers questions about one row. It cannot answer questions about the whole table.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Table in the prompt.</strong> If the table is small, keep it whole, render it as Markdown or HTML so the model recognizes it as a table, and hand it over intact. The generator reads tables far better than the embedding model does. The question is how the retriever finds it, which brings us to the rule below.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Table to database.</strong> If the question is an aggregate (\"how many rows where…\"), the table belongs in a SQL table and the question becomes a query. This is Larkspur's fares table and Chapter 22.</li></ul>" +
      "<p>The rule that ties the first two together is the most useful sentence in this section: <strong>summarize to retrieve, inject the original to answer</strong>. Ask a model to write a short prose summary of the table (\"a table of instrument size limits and whether a cabin seat is required, covering cellos, guitars, and violins\"). Embed the summary; it lands near the questions people ask. Store, beside the summary, a link to the original table. At query time the summary is what gets matched, and the original table is what goes into the prompt. Nothing is lost, and the embedding space is searched with text that was written to be searched.</p>" +
      "<p>Images follow the same rule with one extra option. For a chart, a diagram, or a screenshot, a vision model writes a description; the description is embedded and retrieved; the image itself, or its description if the generator cannot see images, is injected. The extra option is to embed the image directly with a multimodal embedding model such as CLIP, which places images and text in one space so a text query can find a picture. Chapter 31 covers when that is worth the second index; for a corpus like Larkspur's, where images are a few charts in post-mortems, description-then-embed is enough.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<rect x='30' y='70' width='150' height='60' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2.5'/><text x='105' y='96' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='11'>original table</text><text x='105' y='114' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>kept as HTML</text>" +
      "<rect x='240' y='20' width='170' height='44' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='325' y='38' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>LLM summary</text><text x='325' y='54' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>prose, question-shaped</text>" +
      "<rect x='240' y='136' width='170' height='44' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='325' y='154' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>rows as sentences</text><text x='325' y='170' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>one per row, optional</text>" +
      "<line x1='180' y1='90' x2='234' y2='50' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='240,46 228,48 234,58' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<line x1='180' y1='110' x2='234' y2='150' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='240,154 234,142 228,152' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<rect x='470' y='20' width='130' height='44' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='535' y='46' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>embed + index</text>" +
      "<line x1='410' y1='42' x2='464' y2='42' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='470,42 460,37 460,47' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<rect x='640' y='20' width='100' height='44' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='690' y='46' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>retrieved</text>" +
      "<line x1='600' y1='42' x2='634' y2='42' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='640,42 630,37 630,47' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<rect x='640' y='90' width='100' height='44' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='690' y='108' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>prompt gets</text><text x='690' y='124' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>the original</text>" +
      "<line x1='690' y1='64' x2='690' y2='84' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='690,90 685,80 695,80' fill='#3ddc97'/>" +
      "<path d='M 180 100 Q 500 100 640 112' fill='none' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/>" +
      "<text x='430' y='118' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>link kept in metadata</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>Summarize to retrieve, inject the original to answer. The summary is written for the embedding space; the table is kept for the generator; the metadata link joins them at query time.</div></div>" },

    { title: "Metadata by Design",
      html: "<p>Every chunk carries a record beside its text, and that record is what later stages filter, rank, and cite by. Design it now, before the first embedding, because adding a field later means re-ingesting. The cookbook groups metadata into three kinds by where it comes from: <strong>extracted, derived, generated metadata</strong>.</p>" +
      "<ul><li><strong>Extracted.</strong> What the document already says about itself: PDF metadata (author, creation and modification dates, producer), HTML meta tags, the helpdesk's ticket fields. Cheap and often empty; a PDF's author field is blank more often than not.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Derived.</strong> What the pipeline computes: source system, path, file type, page number, section title from the parent element, element type, content hash, ingest time, token count. Always present, because you made it.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Generated.</strong> What a model extracts from the text: document type (is this contract a lease or a licence?), named parties, effective dates, topic labels. The cookbook does this with structured outputs against a schema; Chapter 23 does it at scale to build a graph. Costs a model call per document and is the only way to get fields the document never states in a field.</li></ul>" +
      "<p>What goes in the schema is decided by the questions that will be asked and by who will ask them. Three fields are never optional. A <strong>document identifier and version</strong>, so a chunk can be traced to the exact file and revision it came from (traceability, Chapter 2). A <strong>section path</strong> such as \"Baggage Policy › 3 Cabin baggage › 3.2 Musical instruments\", so a retrieved chunk can be shown with its heading and so the parent-document pattern of Chapter 6 has a parent to return. And an <strong>access-control metadata</strong> field: the tenant, group, or role that may read this document, copied from the source system's permissions at ingest time, so that Chapter 28's query-time filter has something to filter on. Add a <strong>timestamp</strong> for anything that goes stale, because the survey source of this course notes that weighting by recency at retrieval time, time-aware retrieval, is how you stop last year's baggage fee outranking this year's.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<rect x='40' y='20' width='680' height='190' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='380' y='42' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>chunk record (Larkspur schema)</text>" +
      "<text x='60' y='70' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>chunk_id</text><text x='200' y='70' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>sha256(doc_id + section + text)[:16]</text><text x='560' y='70' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>derived · identity</text>" +
      "<text x='60' y='92' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>doc_id, version</text><text x='200' y='92' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>policy/baggage, 2026-08-02</text><text x='560' y='92' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>derived · traceability</text>" +
      "<text x='60' y='114' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>section_path</text><text x='200' y='114' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>Baggage › 3 Cabin › 3.2 Musical instruments</text><text x='560' y='114' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>derived · parents, display</text>" +
      "<text x='60' y='136' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>acl</text><text x='200' y='136' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>[\"everyone\"]  /  [\"legal\", \"finance\"]</text><text x='560' y='136' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>derived · access control</text>" +
      "<text x='60' y='158' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>effective_date</text><text x='200' y='158' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>2026-08-01</text><text x='560' y='158' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>extracted or generated · freshness</text>" +
      "<text x='60' y='180' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>element_type, page</text><text x='200' y='180' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>NarrativeText, 3</text><text x='560' y='180' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>derived · citation</text>" +
      "<text x='60' y='202' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>doc_type, parties</text><text x='200' y='202' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>\"licence\", [\"Larkspur\", \"Port of Oslo\"]</text><text x='560' y='202' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>generated · routing, graph</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>The record beside every chunk. The right column says why each field exists; a field with no consumer in a later chapter should not be there.</div></div>" +
      "<p>One warning the cookbook makes with an example worth keeping. Two chunks can be semantically close and contradictory: \"antioxidants improve the durability of the material\" and \"antioxidants improve the health of the patient\" embed near each other and near the question \"what do antioxidants improve?\", and which one is relevant depends entirely on whether the asker is an engineer or a doctor. Similarity is not relevance. Metadata, applied as a filter before or during retrieval, is how the pipeline knows which corpus the question belongs to. Chapter 13 shows what a filter does to a vector index, which is not nothing.</p>" },

    { title: "Cleaning, and Finding the Near-Duplicates", viz: "dedupeMinhash", vizTitle: "Estimate Jaccard similarity with MinHash",
      html: "<p>A chunk will be read, later, by a model that sees nothing else. Every reference that points outside the chunk is a reference the model cannot follow. The cookbook lists the offenders and the fixes, and they are cheap enough to apply to everything.</p>" +
      "<ul><li><strong>Boilerplate.</strong> Headers, footers, page numbers, navigation menus, cookie banners, legal footers repeated on every page. Drop them by element type, before chunking, or every chunk of the baggage policy will begin with \"Larkspur Airlines · Confidential\" and the embedding will lean toward those words.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Abbreviations and jargon.</strong> \"PNR\" means booking reference to Larkspur staff and nothing to an embedding model trained on the open web. Expand on first use inside each chunk (\"passenger name record (PNR)\") from a dictionary you maintain; the cookbook does it with a regular expression per term, and for domain-heavy documents with a model pass that rewrites the text in plain words.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Dangling references.</strong> \"It was implemented last year\", \"see the table above\", \"as noted in section 2\". Replace pronouns with their nouns and \"above\" with the thing itself, or accept that those chunks will retrieve poorly and answer worse. A model pass can do this at ingest; the propositions of Chapter 5 do it systematically.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Contextual headers.</strong> Prefix each chunk with its section path and a one-line description of the document (\"From the Larkspur baggage policy, section on musical instruments:\"). This costs a few tokens per chunk and is one of the highest-value tricks in the whole pipeline, because the embedding now encodes where the chunk came from as well as what it says. It is sometimes called contextual retrieval and Chapter 5 measures it.</li></ul>" +
      "<h3>Near-duplicates</h3>" +
      "<p>Real corpora repeat themselves. The same policy page is mirrored on three internal sites; a contract exists as a draft, a signed copy, and a scan of the signed copy; a ticket quotes a whole previous ticket. Index all of them and the top-k for any question is five copies of the same paragraph, which crowds out the second-best passage and wastes the context window. Exact duplicates are caught by hashing the text. Near-duplicates, which differ by a date or a typo, need a similarity measure, and the standard one is the <strong>Jaccard similarity</strong> of the two documents' word <strong>shingles</strong>: split each document into overlapping runs of k consecutive words (k around 5 to 9), and measure the overlap of the two sets, intersection over union.</p>" +
      "<p>Computing that exactly for every pair of a million documents is hopeless, which is what Andrei Broder's <strong>MinHash</strong> trick from 1997 solves. Hash every shingle with a hash function; keep only the minimum hash value for each document. The probability that two documents have the same minimum is exactly their Jaccard similarity. Repeat with a hundred independent hash functions and you have a hundred-number signature per document, and the fraction of positions where two signatures agree estimates their Jaccard similarity, with no pairwise comparison of shingles at all. Signatures can be bucketed so that likely pairs are found without comparing every pair, which is how web search engines have deduplicated the web for decades.</p>" +
      "<p>The machine above has two texts and the machine. Set k to 5 and the number of hashes to 100. Change one word in the right-hand text and watch the estimate stay above 0.9; change a whole sentence and watch it fall. Then drop the number of hashes to 10 and see how noisy the estimate becomes, which is the trade the signature size makes.</p>" +
      "<div class='callout'>Near-duplicate detection is a policy, not only a filter. Two copies of a contract may be a draft and a signed version, and the signed one is the one to keep; two mirrors of a policy page may differ in the date, and the newer one wins. Record which copy was kept and why in the metadata, so that a citation never points at the version you discarded.</div>" },

    { title: "Ingestion Is a Pipeline: Idempotent, Incremental, and Fresh",
      html: "<p>The ingest flow will run again. Tickets arrive hourly; policies change monthly; somebody fixes the parser and wants everything re-parsed. A pipeline that appends on every run produces the duplicate problem of the previous section on purpose, and it produces it silently, because the second copy is identical and scores identically. The property you need is <strong>idempotence</strong>: running the pipeline twice over unchanged input leaves the index exactly as it was.</p>" +
      "<p>The mechanism is a <strong>content hash</strong> at two levels. Hash each document's bytes on arrival; if the hash matches the stored one for that document identifier, skip it entirely. If it differs, parse and chunk, and hash each chunk's text together with its document identifier and section path to produce a stable chunk identifier. Then <strong>upsert</strong>: insert chunks whose identifiers are new, leave chunks whose identifiers already exist, and mark chunks of this document that are no longer produced as deleted. A changed paragraph therefore replaces exactly its own chunks and touches nothing else, which also means only the changed chunks are re-embedded, and embedding is the expensive stage.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<rect x='30' y='90' width='120' height='44' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='90' y='108' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>document</text><text x='90' y='124' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>arrives</text>" +
      "<rect x='190' y='90' width='140' height='44' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2.5'/><text x='260' y='108' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10.5'>hash bytes</text><text x='260' y='124' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>compare to stored</text>" +
      "<line x1='150' y1='112' x2='184' y2='112' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='190,112 180,107 180,117' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<rect x='400' y='20' width='150' height='40' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='2'/><text x='475' y='38' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>unchanged</text><text x='475' y='52' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>skip, touch nothing</text>" +
      "<rect x='400' y='90' width='150' height='44' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='475' y='108' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>new or changed</text><text x='475' y='124' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>parse, chunk, hash chunks</text>" +
      "<rect x='400' y='164' width='150' height='40' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2'/><text x='475' y='182' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10.5'>gone from source</text><text x='475' y='196' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>tombstone its chunks</text>" +
      "<line x1='330' y1='104' x2='394' y2='44' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='400,40 388,42 394,52' fill='#7d8fb0'/>" +
      "<line x1='330' y1='112' x2='394' y2='112' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='400,112 390,107 390,117' fill='#3ddc97'/>" +
      "<line x1='330' y1='120' x2='394' y2='180' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='400,184 394,172 388,182' fill='#ff5d73'/>" +
      "<rect x='600' y='76' width='140' height='72' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='670' y='98' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>upsert chunks</text><text x='670' y='114' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>new id → insert + embed</text><text x='670' y='128' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>known id → keep</text><text x='670' y='142' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>missing id → delete</text>" +
      "<line x1='550' y1='112' x2='594' y2='112' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='600,112 590,107 590,117' fill='#3ddc97'/>" +
      "<text x='380' y='222' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>run it twice on the same input: zero inserts, zero embeddings, zero deletes</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>The idempotent ingest state machine. Every document takes exactly one of three paths, and the unchanged path, which is most of them on most runs, costs one hash.</div></div>" +
      "<p><strong>Deletes</strong> deserve their own sentence, because vector indexes handle them badly (Chapter 11 explains why an HNSW graph would rather mark than remove). Delete by <strong>tombstone</strong>: mark the chunk deleted in the store, filter tombstoned chunks out of every query, and reclaim space in a periodic rebuild. A document that vanished from the source system, a revoked contract, an expired policy, must vanish from answers immediately, and a tombstone is the only mechanism fast enough.</p>" +
      "<p><strong>Freshness</strong> is a number you commit to per source. Tickets: within an hour. Policies: within a day of publication. Contracts: on signature. Each is an ingest schedule, and each is a metric worth alarming on, because a stale index is failure class one from Chapter 1 wearing a different hat: the answer is not in the corpus <em>yet</em>. Chapter 29 puts the freshness lag on the dashboard next to latency.</p>" +
      "<p>Finally, <strong>versioning</strong>: keep the document version in the metadata and keep old versions' chunks tombstoned rather than destroyed for a retention period, so that when someone asks why the assistant said something last Tuesday, you can reconstruct what it retrieved from. And when the parser or the chunker changes, treat every document as changed, re-run, and let the same upsert logic replace what differs; the embedding bill for a full re-index is the cost of that change, and Chapter 8's cut-over procedure keeps the service up while it runs.</p>" +
      "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"How do you keep the vector store in sync with the source documents?\" is a systems-design question that separates people who have run a RAG system from people who have built a demo. The answer is content hashing at the document and chunk level, upserts keyed on stable chunk identifiers, tombstones for deletes, a freshness target per source, and re-embedding only what changed. Then mention what happens when the embedding model changes, which is Chapter 8.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol>" +
      "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> Take one document from your own work (a PDF with a table, ideally) and on paper list the elements a parser should emit, in reading order, with the metadata each should carry. Mark which elements are boilerplate to drop. What you should find: at least one element whose text alone would be meaningless without its section title, which is the case for contextual headers.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> Two documents: A = \"the cello requires a seat in the cabin\", B = \"the cello requires a cabin seat\". Using 2-word shingles, write out both shingle sets and compute the exact Jaccard similarity. What you should find: a similarity well under 0.5 despite near-identical meaning, which is why MinHash finds copies and typos, not paraphrases; paraphrase is the embedding model's job.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Run a naive text extraction (<code>pypdf</code>) and an element-typed one (<code>unstructured</code> or Docling) on the same two-column PDF and diff the first 40 lines. What you should see: interleaved columns or shuffled headers in at least one of them, and a table that survives only in the element-typed output.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Pick a folder of 50 or more documents from any source you have and count exact duplicates by hashing the extracted text, then estimate near-duplicates with a MinHash library at 5-word shingles and a 0.85 threshold. What you should find: a duplicate rate that surprises you, usually above 5 percent, and a few pairs that are versions of the same document where a policy decision, not a filter, has to choose.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Write an ingest script with the three-path state machine of the last section: hash documents, skip unchanged, parse and chunk changed ones, upsert chunks by a content-hash identifier into SQLite, tombstone chunks that disappear. Run it twice. What you should find: the second run inserts zero rows; then edit one paragraph and run again, and exactly that paragraph's chunks are replaced. This is the first layer of PocketRAG, and Chapter 32 builds it in full.</li>" +
      "</ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "unstructured data", d: "Information stored in formats designed for human reading (documents, slides, email, chat) rather than in fields a machine can query; the majority of what a RAG corpus contains." },
    { t: "page-description format", d: "A file format, such as PDF, that records drawing instructions and glyph positions rather than paragraphs, so that reading order and structure must be inferred by whatever extracts the text." },
    { t: "reading order", d: "The sequence in which a human would read the regions of a page; not stored in a PDF and wrong under naive extraction of multi-column layouts." },
    { t: "element", d: "A typed region of a parsed document (title, narrative text, list item, table, image, header, footer) with its own text and metadata, the unit produced by a layout-aware parser." },
    { t: "layout analysis", d: "Detecting the regions of a page image and their types with a vision model, required for scanned documents and for PDFs whose text layer is unreliable." },
    { t: "optical character recognition (OCR)", d: "Converting an image of text into characters; fast and cheap on clean print, weak on layout, handwriting, and tables compared with a vision-language model." },
    { t: "summarize to retrieve, inject the original", d: "The rule for tables and images: embed a prose summary so retrieval works, keep a link to the original, and place the original in the prompt so the generator loses nothing." },
    { t: "extracted, derived, generated metadata", d: "The three sources of a chunk's metadata: fields the document already carries, fields the pipeline computes, and fields a model reads out of the text." },
    { t: "section path", d: "The chain of headings above a chunk, stored as metadata so the chunk can be displayed with context and so a parent document can be returned." },
    { t: "access-control metadata", d: "The tenant, group, or role permitted to read a document, copied from the source system at ingest so that retrieval can filter by the asking user." },
    { t: "contextual header", d: "A short prefix added to each chunk naming its document and section, so the embedding encodes where the text came from as well as what it says." },
    { t: "shingle", d: "A run of k consecutive words from a document; the set of a document's shingles is what near-duplicate detection compares." },
    { t: "Jaccard similarity", d: "The size of the intersection of two sets divided by the size of their union; applied to shingle sets it measures how much text two documents share." },
    { t: "MinHash", d: "Broder's 1997 technique: the probability that two documents share the minimum hash of their shingles equals their Jaccard similarity, so a short signature of minimums estimates similarity without pairwise comparison." },
    { t: "idempotent ingest", d: "An ingest pipeline that, run repeatedly over unchanged input, produces no new chunks, embeddings, or deletes; achieved with content hashes and upserts keyed on stable chunk identifiers." },
    { t: "content hash", d: "A hash of a document's bytes or a chunk's text used to detect change and to give each chunk a stable identifier." },
    { t: "upsert", d: "Insert a record if its identifier is new, otherwise leave or update the existing one; the operation that makes re-ingestion safe." },
    { t: "tombstone", d: "A deletion marker on a chunk that removes it from query results immediately while the physical index is cleaned up later." },
    { t: "freshness", d: "The maximum acceptable lag between a change in a source system and its appearance in the index, set per source and measured." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "A two-column PDF is indexed after naive text extraction. Later, a user's question is answered wrongly although the answer is on page 3. Which failure class from Chapter 1 is this, really?",
      opts: ["Missing data: the answering sentence never existed in the corpus, because extraction interleaved the two columns and cut it into fragments", "Weak retrieval: the embedding model missed the paraphrase between the question and the sentence that happens to sit on page 3 of the source PDF", "Unfaithful generation: the model was given the right chunk and still wrote a claim that the chunk does not support anywhere", "Bad assembly: the right chunk was retrieved but placed in the prompt where the model had stopped attending to it"], a: 0,
      why: "The text is in the file, but the sentence is not in the index. Naive extraction reads across both columns line by line, so no chunk contains the sentence as written. No retriever can find what ingestion destroyed; the fix is element-typed parsing." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Why is a prose summary of a table embedded instead of the table itself, and what is stored beside the summary?",
      opts: ["Because tables cannot be embedded at all: the cell values are dropped during ingest and only the caption survives into the index for searching", "Because the summary lands near the questions people ask while a flattened table does not; a link to the original table, which is injected into the prompt at answer time", "Because summaries are shorter and cheaper to embed; the summary replaces the table permanently, and the original file is discarded once ingest has finished processing it", "Because the generator cannot read tables, so the summary is what gets injected into the prompt at answer time"], a: 1,
      why: "Summarize to retrieve, inject the original to answer. The embedding space is searched with text written to be searched; the generator, which reads tables well, gets the real table. The metadata link joins the two." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Two chunks about antioxidants, one from a materials-science paper and one from a medical guideline, both score highly for \"what do antioxidants improve?\". What resolves which is relevant?",
      opts: ["A larger embedding model, which would separate the two senses of the term in its space", "A reranker that rescores both candidate chunks against the full question before either reaches the prompt", "Metadata about the corpus or document type, applied as a filter, because similarity is not relevance", "Increasing top-k so both chunks and their neighbours reach the generator"], a: 2,
      why: "Both chunks are semantically close to the question. Which one the asker wants depends on context the text does not carry. That context lives in metadata, and a filter applies it before similarity gets a vote." },
    { type: "blank", q: "Broder's 1997 technique that estimates Jaccard similarity from the minimum hash values of two documents' shingle sets is called ____.", a: ["minhash", "min-hash", "min hash"],
      why: "The probability that two documents share the same minimum hash equals their Jaccard similarity, so a signature of a hundred minimums estimates similarity without comparing shingle sets pairwise." },
    { type: "mc", q: "You run the ingest pipeline twice on an unchanged corpus and the vector store doubles in size. What property is missing and what is the mechanism that provides it?",
      opts: ["Compression; enable it on the index so that duplicate vectors are stored once rather than twice over", "Idempotence; hash documents and chunks and upsert by a stable chunk identifier so a second run inserts nothing", "Deduplication; run MinHash over the chunk text after ingest and delete whichever near-identical copies it finds", "Versioning; increment a version number on each run and have queries read only the newest version of the two"], a: 1,
      why: "Append-only ingest creates identical copies that score identically and crowd the top-k. Content hashing makes unchanged documents a no-op and stable chunk identifiers make upserts replace rather than add." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the steps of the idempotent ingest path for a document that has changed:",
      items: ["Hash the document bytes and find they differ from the stored hash", "Parse into typed elements and drop boilerplate", "Chunk and compute a content-hash identifier per chunk", "Upsert: insert new chunk ids, keep known ones, tombstone ids no longer produced", "Embed only the newly inserted chunks"],
      why: "The document-level hash gates the work; parsing and chunking produce stable identifiers; the upsert touches only what changed; and embedding, the expensive stage, runs only on new chunks." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A contract is revoked and removed from the document system. What must the RAG pipeline do so that it stops appearing in answers, and why is a hard delete from the vector index not the immediate mechanism?",
      opts: ["Nothing; the generator will see the revocation notice in a neighbouring chunk and decline to cite the contract when it writes the answer", "Re-embed the whole corpus so that the revoked chunks fall out of the index the next time it is rebuilt from the source documents", "Lower its metadata priority so the revoked chunks rank below live ones and never reach the top-k on any query", "Tombstone its chunks so every query filters them out at once, and reclaim space in a later rebuild, because vector indexes remove entries badly"], a: 3,
      why: "Graph-based vector indexes mark rather than remove (Chapter 11). A tombstone is honoured by the query filter immediately, which is what the revocation requires; physical cleanup happens on a schedule." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A senior interviewer asks: \"The embedding model is fine and the retriever is fine, but users still get wrong answers about the baggage policy. Where would you look first?\" Which answer shows this chapter's mechanism?",
      opts: ["At the prompt: the wording of the instructions and the order the retrieved chunks are placed in together decide which of them the generator actually reads when it finally comes to write the answer", "At ingestion: whether the policy PDF was parsed in reading order, whether the table survived, whether headers polluted the chunks, whether the newest version was ingested and the old one tombstoned", "At the vector index parameters: the graph's search breadth and the number of probes decide which neighbours are reachable at all, and too narrow a search will silently drop the right chunk", "At the generator's temperature: sampling at anything above zero lets the model wander off the retrieved text while it is writing, and the wandering is what users report"], a: 1,
      why: "Most retrieval failures are ingestion failures in disguise. Before tuning anything downstream, inspect the chunks for the document in question: their text, their order, their metadata, and their version. A wrong chunk cannot be retrieved right." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Why should the access-control field be populated at ingest time rather than checked at answer time by instructing the model?",
      opts: ["Because models cannot reliably read instructions of that kind, and will follow the retrieved text over a system-prompt rule whenever the two of them happen to conflict inside one context", "Because a filter on metadata removes forbidden chunks before the model sees them, whereas an instruction leaves the chunk in the prompt and asks the model not to use it, which is not a control", "Because ingest is faster: the permission check runs once per document at that point, rather than once on every chunk of every query that the system goes on to serve afterwards", "Because it reduces token cost: forbidden chunks are dropped before they are paid for as input to the generator on each question"], a: 1,
      why: "Access control must be enforced where the data is selected. A forbidden chunk that reaches the prompt has already leaked. Chapter 28 shows the test that proves the difference." },
    { type: "blank", q: "Prefixing every chunk with its document name and section path so the embedding encodes its origin is often called ____ retrieval.", a: ["contextual"],
      why: "A few tokens of context per chunk let the embedding carry where the text came from. It is among the cheapest and most effective ingestion tricks; Chapter 5 measures its effect on recall." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 5 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): Chroma Research, "Evaluating
   Chunking Strategies for Retrieval" (2024; five corpora, 328,208 tokens,
   text-embedding-3-large, token-level recall/precision/IoU tables); Anthropic,
   "Introducing Contextual Retrieval" (Sept 2024; top-20 failure rate 5.7% →
   3.7% → 2.9% → 1.9%; $1.02 per million document tokens with caching);
   LlamaIndex blog, "Evaluating the ideal chunk size" (Uber 10-K, sizes
   128–2048; numbers only in an image, used qualitatively); Chen et al. 2023,
   Dense X Retrieval (arXiv 2312.06648); Günther et al. 2024, Late Chunking
   (arXiv 2409.04701); LangChain text-splitters source (character.py: default
   separators ["\n\n","\n"," ",""], Markdown and Python lists); Polzer,
   Cookbook ch. 2 (recipes 2.4–2.8, Kamradt's "5 levels"); Bratanič & Hane
   ch. 2–3 and ch. 7 (500-char demo, MS GraphRAG 600-token result); Gao et al.
   2024 survey §III.A–B (granularity ladder, 100/256/512); Anthropic pricing
   FAQ (1 token ≈ 4 characters ≈ 0.75 words; 4.7+ tokenizer ≈ 30% more tokens). */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 5, part: 2, title: "Chunking: Where to Cut Text and Why It Decides Everything",
  blurb: "A chunk is the unit the retriever can return, so a bad cut is a fact the system can never find. You will cut the same Larkspur page five ways, fixed, recursive, document-aware, semantic, and proposition-based, and watch where each strategy splits a sentence, strands a heading, or merges two policies. Then you will read the experiment every team eventually runs, recall and precision against chunk size on real corpora, and discover that the answer is a curve with a knee, not a number from a blog post. You will also learn why four credible sources recommend four different sizes.",
  sections: [
    { title: "The Problem: One Vector per Chunk",
      html: "<p>An embedding model turns a chunk of text into one vector. One. If the chunk contains a paragraph about cellos in the cabin and a paragraph about pets in the hold, the vector is a blend of both, and it sits in the embedding space somewhere between \"cello\" and \"pet\", near neither. A question about cellos lands near the cello region and finds a chunk that was written only about cellos before it finds the blend. This is <strong>embedding dilution</strong>, and it is the reason chunking exists at all: the retriever cannot return less than a chunk, and it cannot match more precisely than one chunk's single vector allows.</p>" +
      "<p>Pull the other way and a different problem appears. Cut the policy into single sentences and \"The case must fit the sizer\" is a chunk. Its vector is sharp, but the sentence has lost its subject: which case, which sizer, which instrument? Retrieved alone, it cannot answer anything, and the generator that receives it will guess. The 2020 RAG paper indexed Wikipedia in passages of about a hundred words, which was a guess at the middle of this trade; every chunking strategy since is a more careful guess.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<line x1='60' y1='100' x2='700' y2='100' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='706,100 694,94 694,106' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<text x='80' y='80' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>token</text><line x1='80' y1='92' x2='80' y2='108' stroke='#c9d6ea' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='190' y='80' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>sentence</text><line x1='190' y1='92' x2='190' y2='108' stroke='#c9d6ea' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='300' y='80' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10.5'>proposition</text><line x1='300' y1='92' x2='300' y2='108' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='420' y='80' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10.5'>chunk (100–800 tokens)</text><line x1='420' y1='92' x2='420' y2='108' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='560' y='80' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>section</text><line x1='560' y1='92' x2='560' y2='108' stroke='#c9d6ea' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='660' y='80' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>document</text><line x1='660' y1='92' x2='660' y2='108' stroke='#c9d6ea' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='120' y='140' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>sharp vector, no context</text>" +
      "<text x='620' y='140' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10'>full context, blended vector</text>" +
      "<text x='380' y='170' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>the granularity ladder: finer units match precisely, coarser units answer completely</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>The granularity ladder from the 2024 survey. Everything in this chapter is a way of choosing a rung, or of matching on one rung and returning another (Chapter 6).</div></div>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<circle cx='170' cy='80' r='6' fill='#2de2ff'/><text x='170' y='62' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10'>\"cello in the cabin?\"</text>" +
      "<circle cx='230' cy='110' r='6' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='230' y='132' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>cello-only chunk</text>" +
      "<circle cx='560' cy='120' r='6' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='560' y='142' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>pets-only chunk</text>" +
      "<circle cx='395' cy='115' r='7' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='395' y='96' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10'>cello + pets chunk</text>" +
      "<line x1='230' y1='110' x2='560' y2='120' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
      "<line x1='176' y1='84' x2='224' y2='106' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='215' y='88' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>near</text>" +
      "<line x1='176' y1='82' x2='388' y2='112' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='3 3'/><text x='300' y='90' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>far</text>" +
      "<text x='380' y='180' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>a chunk about two things has one vector, and it lands between them</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>Embedding dilution. The mixed chunk's vector is the average of its parts and is closer to nothing in particular; the question finds the pure chunk first.</div></div>" },

    { title: "Fixed Windows, Overlap, and Four Numbers That Disagree", viz: "chunker", vizTitle: "Cut the same page five ways",
      html: "<p>The simplest chunker counts. Take N characters or N tokens, cut, take the next N. A <strong>sliding window</strong> adds <strong>overlap</strong>: each chunk repeats the last M tokens of the previous one, so a sentence that straddles a boundary appears whole in at least one chunk. The cookbook source of this course notes the one case where a fixed window is right: streams and logs with no structure to respect, where equal-sized chunks keep every piece within a token limit and nothing else is available to cut on.</p>" +
      "<p>For prose, fixed windows cut mid-sentence and mid-word, strand headings from their paragraphs, and glue the end of one section to the start of the next. Open the machine below, pick <em>fixed</em>, and drag the size to 200. Find the sentence about cellos that has been cut in half: it now exists as two fragments, neither of which says that a cello needs a seat. Switch to <em>recursive</em> at the same size and the sentence is whole again. That difference is the entire argument for the next section.</p>" +
      "<p>How big should N be? Here is where a careful reader of this course's sources notices something. The cookbook recommends 1,000 to 2,000 tokens for most applications. The GraphRAG book's walkthrough uses 500 characters, which is roughly 125 tokens. The 2024 survey cites 100, 256, and 512 tokens as the common research settings. And the Microsoft GraphRAG paper, testing chunk sizes for entity extraction, found 600 tokens beat 1,200 and 2,400. Four credible sources, four answers spanning a factor of sixteen.</p>" +
      "<div class='callout'><strong>Sources differ, and they are all right.</strong> Each number was chosen for a different job. The cookbook is optimizing for a generator that reads whole ideas and a prompt that is not too long; the GraphRAG book is demonstrating precise matching on one paper; the survey reports what researchers use on Wikipedia-style corpora with short factoid questions; the Microsoft paper is optimizing for how many entities an extraction model notices per pass. Chunk size is not a constant of nature. It is a tunable whose right value depends on the corpus, the questions, the embedding model, and what happens after retrieval, which is why the sixth section of this chapter is an experiment and not a recommendation.</div>" +
      "<p>Overlap has a similar folklore: 10 to 20 percent of the chunk size is repeated everywhere as a default. Keep the machine's overlap slider in mind for the experiment section, because the measured effect of overlap is smaller and stranger than the folklore suggests.</p>" },

    { title: "Recursive and Document-Aware Splitting",
      html: "<p>Humans already chunked the document. Paragraph breaks, headings, list items, and sentences are the author's own boundaries between ideas, and the cheapest good chunker respects them. The <strong>recursive character splitter</strong>, which LangChain popularized and which every framework now has a version of, is a size limit plus a preference order of separators. Its default list, from the library's source, is exactly four entries: a blank line, a newline, a space, and the empty string. The algorithm: split on the first separator; any piece still larger than the limit is split again on the next separator; and so on down, so that a paragraph too long for the limit is split on newlines, an overlong line on spaces, and only a single word longer than the limit is split mid-word. Chunks come out as close to the limit as the structure allows, and boundaries fall on the most meaningful separator that lets them.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<rect x='40' y='20' width='680' height='36' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='380' y='43' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='11'>whole section: 1,400 tokens, limit 400</text>" +
      "<text x='60' y='78' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>split on \"\\n\\n\"</text>" +
      "<rect x='40' y='86' width='200' height='30' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='140' y='105' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>para 1 · 320 ✓</text>" +
      "<rect x='260' y='86' width='300' height='30' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2'/><text x='410' y='105' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>para 2 · 720 ✗ too big</text>" +
      "<rect x='580' y='86' width='140' height='30' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='650' y='105' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>para 3 · 360 ✓</text>" +
      "<text x='280' y='140' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>split para 2 on \"\\n\"</text>" +
      "<rect x='260' y='148' width='140' height='30' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='330' y='167' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>lines 1–3 · 380 ✓</text>" +
      "<rect x='420' y='148' width='140' height='30' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='490' y='167' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>lines 4–5 · 340 ✓</text>" +
      "<line x1='410' y1='116' x2='340' y2='146' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='410' y1='116' x2='480' y2='146' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
      "<text x='380' y='212' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>separators tried in order: \"\\n\\n\" → \"\\n\" → \" \" → \"\"; each is used only where the previous one left a chunk too large</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>Recursive splitting on Larkspur's instruments section. Only the oversized paragraph is cut again, and it is cut on the next-best boundary, not at a character count.</div></div>" +
      "<p><strong>Document-aware splitting</strong> is the same idea with a separator list that knows the format. For Markdown the list begins with heading markers and code fences before it falls back to blank lines; for Python it begins with class and function definitions. The result is that a Markdown chunk never starts in the middle of a heading's body without its heading, and a code chunk never starts halfway through a function. If the ingest stage of Chapter 4 produced typed elements, you already have better boundaries than any separator list: chunk within elements, keep a table as one chunk with its caption, and prefix each chunk with its section path.</p>" +
      "<p>Two cautions. Chunk sizes from a recursive splitter vary; a corpus of very short paragraphs yields chunks well under the limit, and if you need tight control you merge small neighbours. And the default separators assume languages that put spaces between words. For Chinese, Japanese, and Thai, a space is not a boundary, and the separator list needs the language's punctuation, or a sentence splitter, in place of the space.</p>" },

    { title: "Semantic Chunking: Cutting Where the Meaning Changes",
      html: "<p>Some text has no structure to respect: a call transcript, a chat log, a legal clause that runs for a page without a paragraph break. <strong>Semantic chunking</strong> finds boundaries in the meaning instead. Split the text into sentences; embed each sentence; compute the similarity between each sentence and the next; and cut where the similarity drops. Consecutive sentences about the same thing have similar vectors; the sentence that changes the subject does not. The <strong>breakpoint threshold</strong> is chosen from the distribution of the drops, typically a percentile (the cookbook's example cuts at the 90th percentile of distance), a number of standard deviations, or the steepest gradient.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 210' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<line x1='60' y1='150' x2='720' y2='150' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='390' y='190' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>sentence index → · bar height = distance to the next sentence</text>" +
      "<rect x='80' y='120' width='28' height='30' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='120' y='125' width='28' height='25' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='160' y='118' width='28' height='32' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='200' y='128' width='28' height='22' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<rect x='240' y='50' width='28' height='100' fill='#ffd166'/>" +
      "<rect x='280' y='122' width='28' height='28' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='320' y='116' width='28' height='34' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='360' y='126' width='28' height='24' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='400' y='119' width='28' height='31' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='440' y='124' width='28' height='26' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<rect x='480' y='58' width='28' height='92' fill='#ffd166'/>" +
      "<rect x='520' y='121' width='28' height='29' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='560' y='127' width='28' height='23' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='600' y='117' width='28' height='33' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='640' y='123' width='28' height='27' fill='#4f7cff'/>" +
      "<line x1='60' y1='78' x2='720' y2='78' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/><text x='700' y='72' text-anchor='end' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>90th percentile threshold</text>" +
      "<text x='154' y='38' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>cabin baggage</text><text x='374' y='38' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>musical instruments</text><text x='594' y='38' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>pets</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>Semantic breakpoints on a page with the headings removed. The two tall bars are where the subject changes, and the threshold line picks them out; the chunker rediscovers the author's sections from the meaning alone.</div></div>" +
      "<p>The cost is real: an embedding call per sentence at ingest, which for a large corpus is more embedding work than indexing the final chunks. The benefit is real too, but not where the folklore puts it. In Chroma's 2024 evaluation, which this chapter leans on because it measured chunkers at the token level across five corpora, a sentence-similarity chunker in the style of Greg Kamradt's original recipe scored <em>below</em> the plain recursive splitter on recall. A variant that clusters sentences globally rather than cutting only at local drops did better, and at a 200-token target it produced the highest precision of any method tested. Semantic chunking earns its cost when it is done carefully and when the corpus lacks structure; on a well-formatted document the recursive splitter is already using the author's boundaries for free.</p>" +
      "<p>Semantic chunkers exist in the frameworks (LangChain's is still in its experimental package, which the cookbook rightly flags) and are about thirty lines to write yourself once Chapter 7 has taught you what a sentence embedding is. The threshold is the tunable, and there is no substitute for looking at the boundaries it produces on your own text.</p>" },

    { title: "Propositions: Chunks Written to Be Retrieved",
      html: "<p>Every strategy so far cuts the author's text. The last one rewrites it. A <strong>proposition</strong>, as defined by Tong Chen and colleagues in the December 2023 paper \"Dense X Retrieval: What Retrieval Granularity Should We Use?\", is an atomic, self-contained statement: one fact, in one sentence, with every pronoun replaced by its noun and every reference resolved, so that it means the same thing read alone as it did in context. \"It must fit the sizer\" becomes \"A cello case carried in the cabin must fit the Larkspur cabin baggage sizer.\" A language model produces propositions from a passage with a prompt that the paper published and that the cookbook loads from a prompt hub.</p>" +
      "<p>The paper's finding is the reason to care: indexing Wikipedia as propositions, rather than as passages or sentences, \"significantly outperforms passage-level units in retrieval tasks\" and improves downstream question answering at the same context budget. Propositions sit at exactly the rung of the ladder where a vector is sharp <em>and</em> the text is complete, because the completeness was written in. They are the ingest-time answer to the dangling-reference problem of Chapter 4.</p>" +
      "<p>The cookbook's <strong>agentic chunking</strong> goes one step further: after generating propositions, a model groups related ones into chunks, deciding for each new proposition whether it belongs with an existing group or starts a new one, so that scattered statements about the same policy end up together even if the author spread them across a document. It is the most expensive strategy here, one or more model calls per paragraph, and the cookbook says the honest thing: whether the quality is worth the cost is a decision, not a default.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<rect x='30' y='30' width='300' height='140' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='180' y='52' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>passage</text>" +
      "<text x='180' y='78' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>A cello may travel in the cabin only</text><text x='180' y='94' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>when a seat has been bought for it.</text><text x='180' y='110' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>It must fit the sizer. The fee is the</text><text x='180' y='126' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>adult fare for that flight.</text>" +
      "<line x1='330' y1='100' x2='394' y2='100' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='400,100 390,95 390,105' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='365' y='90' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>LLM</text>" +
      "<rect x='410' y='30' width='320' height='140' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='570' y='52' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>propositions</text>" +
      "<text x='425' y='78' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>1. A cello may travel in the Larkspur cabin only</text><text x='440' y='92' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>if a seat is bought for the cello.</text>" +
      "<text x='425' y='112' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>2. A cello case in the cabin must fit the</text><text x='440' y='126' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>Larkspur cabin baggage sizer.</text>" +
      "<text x='425' y='146' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>3. The fee for a cello's cabin seat is the adult</text><text x='440' y='160' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>fare for that flight.</text>" +
      "<text x='380' y='190' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>\"it\" and \"the fee\" have been resolved; each line stands alone and is one fact</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>A passage rewritten as propositions. Each is a retrieval unit with a sharp vector and no missing referent; the cost is a model call per passage at ingest.</div></div>" },

    { title: "The Chunk-Size Experiment", viz: "chunkSizeCurve", vizTitle: "Recall and precision against chunk size, measured",
      html: "<p>Now the numbers, and the reason this course does not give you one. Chroma's 2024 chunking evaluation built five corpora (a State of the Union address, Wikitext, chat logs, finance filings, PubMed abstracts; 328,208 tokens in total with 472 queries) and labelled, for each query, the exact spans of text that answer it. That let them score chunkers at the token level: <strong>recall</strong>, the fraction of relevant tokens that appear in the retrieved chunks, and <strong>token-level precision</strong>, the fraction of retrieved tokens that are relevant. With OpenAI's text-embedding-3-large and five chunks retrieved per query, the recursive splitter scored like this.</p>" +
      "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
      "<text x='120' y='30' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>size / overlap</text><text x='330' y='30' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>recall</text><text x='450' y='30' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>precision</text><text x='590' y='30' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>what it means</text>" +
      "<line x1='100' y1='38' x2='740' y2='38' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='120' y='62' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>200 / 0</text><text x='330' y='62' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>88.1</text><text x='450' y='62' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>7.0</text><text x='590' y='62' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>sharpest chunks</text>" +
      "<text x='120' y='88' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>400 / 0</text><text x='330' y='88' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>89.5</text><text x='450' y='88' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>3.6</text><text x='590' y='88' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>best recall</text>" +
      "<text x='120' y='114' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>400 / 200</text><text x='330' y='114' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>88.1</text><text x='450' y='114' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>3.3</text><text x='590' y='114' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>overlap: both down</text>" +
      "<text x='120' y='140' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>800 / 400</text><text x='330' y='140' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10.5'>85.4</text><text x='450' y='140' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10.5'>1.5</text><text x='590' y='140' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>big + overlapped: worst</text>" +
      "<line x1='100' y1='152' x2='740' y2='152' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='2'/>" +
      "<text x='120' y='176' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>cluster-semantic 200</text><text x='330' y='176' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>87.3</text><text x='450' y='176' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>8.0</text><text x='590' y='176' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>highest precision tested</text>" +
      "<text x='120' y='202' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>LLM chunker (~240)</text><text x='330' y='202' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>91.9</text><text x='450' y='202' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>3.9</text><text x='590' y='202' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>highest recall, costliest</text>" +
      "<text x='420' y='228' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>Chroma 2024 · five corpora · text-embedding-3-large · five chunks retrieved · token-level, in percent</text><text x='420' y='243' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>error bars are wide: treat differences under 2 points as noise</text>" +
      "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>Measured, not recommended. Recall barely moves across a fourfold range of sizes; precision collapses as chunks grow; overlap bought nothing here. The knee is where recall stops rising, around 400 tokens on these corpora, and the right size is the smallest one at the knee.</div></div>" +
      "<p>Read the table twice. First, recall is nearly flat from 200 to 400 tokens and falls at 800: the answer is found about as often whatever the size, until chunks get big enough to dilute. Second, precision moves by a factor of five: at 200 tokens seven percent of what you hand the generator is relevant, at 800 tokens one and a half percent. Smaller chunks do not find more; they find the same thing with far less noise around it, which matters because Chapter 17 will show that noise in the context is not free. Third, overlap, the default everyone ships, lowered both recall and precision slightly in this run; the straddling-sentence problem it solves is real but rare, and the recursive splitter already solves most of it by cutting on boundaries. The folklore about overlap is a hedge against fixed-window chunking, and it stops being needed when you stop using fixed windows.</p>" +
      "<p>Chunking is not the only lever on this curve. In September 2024 Anthropic published an experiment on <strong>contextual retrieval</strong>, the trick from Chapter 4 of prefixing each chunk with a model-written sentence of context about where it sits in its document. Across codebases, fiction, and scientific papers, with a top-20 failure rate (one minus recall at 20) as the metric, contextual embeddings alone cut failures from 5.7 percent to 3.7; adding a contextual BM25 index cut them to 2.9; and reranking on top cut them to 1.9, a 67 percent reduction overall. The one-time cost of writing the context, with prompt caching, was reported at about a dollar per million document tokens. That is a bigger gain than any chunk-size change in Chroma's table, and it composes with all of them.</p>" +
      "<p>The machine above plots Chroma's numbers as curves and lets you pick the metric. Find the knee in recall, then switch to precision and see that the knee is not the optimum; it is where the trade begins. Then flip overlap on and off and read the difference against the error bars.</p>" +
      "<h3>Running it on your corpus</h3>" +
      "<p>Every number above was measured on someone else's documents with someone else's questions, and Chroma's own error bars are wide. The procedure to get your curve is short, and Chapter 25 makes it rigorous: take fifty to two hundred real questions; for each, mark the passage in your corpus that answers it (a sentence or two, not a document); chunk the corpus at 128, 256, 512, and 1,024 tokens with and without overlap; embed with the model you will ship; retrieve five chunks per question; score recall (did the marked passage's text land in a retrieved chunk?) and the token cost of what was retrieved. Plot both. The knee on your corpus is the number, and it is worth an afternoon, because you will live with it until the next re-index.</p>" +
      "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"What chunk size should we use?\" is asked to see whether you answer with a number. The strong answer: it is a curve with a knee, measured on your corpus with a labelled set; smaller chunks win on precision at nearly equal recall until dilution; overlap is a hedge for fixed windows and buys little with structure-aware splitting; and a contextual header per chunk moves the curve more than the size does. Then name the experiment you would run.</div>" },

    { title: "Token Budgets and the Tokenizer Trap",
      html: "<p>Every size in this chapter is in tokens, and a <strong>token</strong> is not a word. It is a unit of the model's tokenizer, a subword vocabulary learned from data, and the same sentence is a different number of tokens to every model. As a working rule for English, one token is about four characters, or three quarters of a word; a thousand tokens is about 750 words. Code, numbers, and non-English text run to more tokens per character.</p>" +
      "<p>Two consequences. First, count with the tokenizer of the model that will read the chunk, not a rule of thumb, because limits are enforced in that model's tokens: OpenAI publishes <code>tiktoken</code>, other providers publish a counting endpoint or a tokenizer file, and open models ship theirs with the weights. Second, the count changes when the model does. Anthropic's pricing documentation notes that its 4.7-and-later models use a new tokenizer that produces roughly 30 percent more tokens for the same text than earlier ones. A chunk sized to 512 tokens under one tokenizer is 660 under the other, and a context budget computed for one silently overflows on the other. Chapter 8's model-upgrade procedure includes re-counting for exactly this reason.</p>" +
      "<p>There is one more thing a token count feeds: the <strong>embedding input limit</strong>. Embedding models accept a fixed maximum, a few hundred to a few thousand tokens, and most truncate silently beyond it. A 2,000-token chunk fed to a 512-token embedding model is embedded from its first quarter; the other three quarters exist in the store and are invisible to retrieval. Check the limit before choosing a size, and check that the pipeline fails loudly, not quietly, when a chunk exceeds it.</p>" +
      "<p>Finally, the newest answer to the whole chapter's problem. <strong>Late chunking</strong>, proposed by Michael Günther and colleagues at Jina in 2024, runs a long-context embedding model over the entire document first, so that every token's representation has seen the whole text, and only then pools tokens into chunk vectors. The chunk boundaries are the same; the vectors are not, because each chunk's vector now carries the context around it, which is what a contextual header was approximating with words. It needs an embedding model with a long input window and is not yet the default anywhere, but it is the direction the granularity ladder is bending.</p>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol>" +
      "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> Take a 600-word section from any document you own and, by hand, mark where a 100-word fixed window would cut it, where a recursive splitter with a 100-word limit would cut it, and where you, reading for meaning, would cut it. What you should find: the fixed window cuts inside at least two sentences; the recursive splitter cuts at paragraph breaks and lands within a few words of your own cuts.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> Rewrite one paragraph of that section as propositions: one fact per line, no pronouns, every reference resolved. Count how many lines you get and how many words were added. What you should find: three to eight propositions per paragraph and a 20 to 40 percent increase in words, which is the storage cost of self-containment.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Count the tokens in that same section with two different tokenizers (for example <code>tiktoken</code> and an open model's tokenizer from Hugging Face). What you should find: counts that differ by 10 to 30 percent, and a larger gap on code or non-English text, which is why sizes must be set per model.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Run a recursive splitter at 400 tokens with overlap 0 and overlap 200 over a folder of your documents. Count total chunks and total tokens indexed. What you should find: overlap of half the chunk size roughly doubles the tokens you embed and store, for a benefit Chroma's data suggests is within noise; decide whether you can justify it.</li>" +
      "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Run the chunk-size experiment of section six on your own corpus with 30 labelled questions and a local embedding model: sizes 128, 256, 512, 1,024; five chunks retrieved; recall and retrieved tokens per size. Plot the two lines. What you should find: a knee in recall and a steady rise in tokens, and a size you can now defend with a graph rather than a citation. Keep the labelled questions; Chapter 25 turns them into a regression suite.</li>" +
      "</ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "chunk", d: "The unit of text that is embedded, indexed, and returned by a retriever; nothing smaller can be retrieved and nothing larger can be matched more precisely than its one vector allows." },
    { t: "embedding dilution", d: "The loss of retrieval precision when a chunk covering several ideas is represented by a single vector that lands between them and near none." },
    { t: "granularity ladder", d: "The ordered choice of retrieval unit from token through sentence, proposition, chunk, and section to document; finer units match precisely and coarser units answer completely." },
    { t: "fixed-window chunking", d: "Cutting text every N characters or tokens regardless of structure; appropriate for unstructured streams and wrong for prose." },
    { t: "sliding window / overlap", d: "Repeating the last M tokens of one chunk at the start of the next so a sentence that straddles a boundary appears whole somewhere; a hedge whose measured benefit is small once boundaries respect structure." },
    { t: "recursive character splitter", d: "A chunker that tries a preference-ordered list of separators (blank line, newline, space, empty string by default) and re-splits only the pieces that exceed the size limit." },
    { t: "document-aware splitting", d: "Recursive splitting with a separator list that understands a format, such as Markdown headings or Python function definitions, so chunks follow the format's own structure." },
    { t: "semantic chunking", d: "Finding chunk boundaries by embedding sentences and cutting where similarity between consecutive sentences drops below a threshold; useful when text has no structure, costly in embedding calls." },
    { t: "breakpoint threshold", d: "The rule that turns a distribution of sentence-to-sentence distances into cut points: a percentile, a number of standard deviations, or a gradient." },
    { t: "proposition", d: "An atomic, self-contained statement of one fact with all pronouns and references resolved, generated by a model from a passage; the retrieval unit proposed by Dense X Retrieval in 2023." },
    { t: "agentic chunking", d: "Generating propositions and then using a model to group related ones into chunks, so that scattered statements about one topic end up together; the most expensive strategy." },
    { t: "contextual retrieval", d: "Prefixing each chunk with a short model-written description of its place in the document before embedding and indexing; Anthropic's 2024 experiment cut top-20 retrieval failures by up to 67 percent with reranking." },
    { t: "token-level recall", d: "The fraction of the tokens known to answer a query that appear in the retrieved chunks; the recall measure used in Chroma's chunking evaluation." },
    { t: "token-level precision", d: "The fraction of retrieved tokens that are part of an answer; falls steeply as chunk size grows." },
    { t: "token", d: "A unit of a model's subword vocabulary, roughly four English characters or three quarters of a word; the same text has a different token count under every tokenizer." },
    { t: "embedding input limit", d: "The maximum number of tokens an embedding model accepts; text beyond it is usually truncated silently and becomes invisible to retrieval." },
    { t: "late chunking", d: "Günther et al. 2024: embed the whole document with a long-context model first, then pool tokens into chunk vectors, so each chunk's vector carries the surrounding context." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "A chunk contains a paragraph on cabin cellos followed by a paragraph on pets in the hold. A question about cellos retrieves a different, cello-only chunk first. Why?",
      opts: ["The mixed chunk has one vector that averages both topics and lands between them, farther from the cello query than the pure chunk's vector", "The pets paragraph contains a stopword that the index stripped before embedding, which shifted the chunk's vector away from the query", "The chunk exceeded the embedding model's input limit, so its second paragraph was truncated before the vector was computed", "Fixed-window chunkers produce chunks that systematically rank lower than the ones a recursive splitter cuts"], a: 0,
      why: "One chunk, one vector. A chunk about two things is represented by a blend that is near neither. This is embedding dilution, and it is why chunks should cover one idea." },
    { type: "mc", q: "The cookbook recommends 1,000 to 2,000 tokens, the survey cites 100 to 512, and the GraphRAG paper found 600 best. How should an engineer read this?",
      opts: ["The cookbook is wrong: its range of 1,000 to 2,000 tokens was written for summarisation rather than for retrieval, and the survey's measured 100 to 512 is the number to use on any corpus", "Take the average of the three published figures, which lands near 600 tokens and happens to be where the GraphRAG paper ended up as well, so the sources agree", "Each measured a different job (generator context, factoid retrieval, entity extraction); chunk size is a tunable to measure on your own corpus, not a constant", "Use the largest size that fits the embedding model's input limit, since a bigger chunk can only carry more of the answer into the prompt"], a: 2,
      why: "The sources disagree because their objectives differ. The right size depends on the corpus, questions, embedding model, and downstream stage. The chapter's experiment section is the procedure for finding yours." },
    { type: "mc", q: "In Chroma's evaluation, moving the recursive splitter from 200 to 800 tokens changed recall from 88.1 to 85.4 and precision from 7.0 to 1.5. What is the lesson?",
      opts: ["Bigger chunks find more answers: recall climbed from 85.4 to 88.1 as the window grew from 200 to 800 tokens, and precision is a secondary concern", "Recall is nearly flat until dilution, but precision collapses, so smaller chunks deliver the same answers with far less noise", "Precision does not matter for RAG, because the generator reads every retrieved chunk and can ignore the parts that are not relevant to the question", "Recall should be measured at the document level, where the fourfold size change barely moved the numbers at all"], a: 1,
      why: "Recall barely moved across a fourfold size range and fell only when chunks got big enough to dilute. Precision fell fivefold. The generator then reads mostly irrelevant tokens, which Chapter 17 shows is not free." },
    { type: "blank", q: "The atomic, self-contained, pronoun-free retrieval unit proposed by Chen et al. in Dense X Retrieval is called a ____.", a: ["proposition"],
      why: "A proposition is one fact in one sentence with every reference resolved, so it reads the same alone as in context. It sits where the vector is sharp and the text is complete." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the steps of a semantic chunker:",
      items: ["Split the text into sentences", "Embed each sentence", "Compute the distance between each sentence and the next", "Choose a threshold from the distribution of distances (for example the 90th percentile)", "Cut where the distance exceeds the threshold and merge the rest into chunks"],
      why: "Consecutive sentences on one subject have similar vectors; the sentence that changes subject does not. The threshold turns the distances into cut points. The cost is an embedding call per sentence." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A team ships 400-token chunks with 200-token overlap because \"overlap is best practice\". What did Chroma's measurement show about that choice?",
      opts: ["It raised recall by roughly five points, from 88.1 to 89.5, which is the usual justification given for paying the extra storage and embedding cost the overlap brings with it on every run", "It slightly lowered both recall and precision and doubled the tokens embedded and stored; overlap hedges fixed-window cuts, which a structure-aware splitter already avoids", "It only helps semantic chunkers, which cut on meaning rather than on a fixed window and therefore leave sentences straddling their boundaries more often than a fixed-window splitter does", "It has no effect on storage, because the overlapping text is deduplicated by the vector store before the chunks are embedded and written, so only unique tokens are ever paid for"], a: 1,
      why: "400/0 scored 89.5 recall and 3.6 precision; 400/200 scored 88.1 and 3.3. The straddling-sentence problem overlap addresses is mostly solved by cutting on boundaries. The storage cost is certain; the benefit was within noise." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Anthropic's contextual retrieval experiment reduced the top-20 failure rate from 5.7% to 1.9% with contextual embeddings, contextual BM25, and reranking together. Which stage of the pipeline does the first of those three change?",
      opts: ["Generation: the model is prompted to reason about where each retrieved chunk came from before it writes the answer", "Ingestion: each chunk is prefixed with a model-written sentence about where it sits in its document before it is embedded", "The vector index parameters: the search breadth was raised so that more neighbours are reachable per query", "The user's query: it is expanded with context before it is embedded and sent to the index"], a: 1,
      why: "Contextual retrieval is an ingest-time change. The prefix lets the embedding encode the chunk's origin, which is what a dangling reference lacks. It composes with chunk size, hybrid search, and reranking." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Chunks are sized to 512 tokens and pass through an embedding model with a 512-token input limit. The team then upgrades to a generator whose tokenizer produces about 30% more tokens for the same text. What should worry them, and what should not?",
      opts: ["Nothing changes; token counts are universal across models, so a chunk measured at 512 tokens is 512 tokens for the new generator too, and both the embedding limit and the prompt budget stay where they were before", "The generator's prompt budget will overflow silently, since 512 tokens under the old tokenizer is about 660 under the new one; the embedding model's limit is unaffected because it uses its own tokenizer", "The embedding model will now truncate every chunk, because the chunks measure about 660 tokens under the new tokenizer and its input limit is still 512, so the tail of each one is dropped", "Recall will fall by about 30%, in proportion to the tokenizer's change, since each chunk now covers proportionally less of the document it came from than it did"], a: 1,
      why: "Every model counts with its own tokenizer. Budgets computed for one tokenizer do not transfer. The embedding model's count did not change because the embedding model did not change." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A senior interviewer asks what chunk size to use for a new corpus of support tickets. Which answer shows the mechanism?",
      opts: ["512 tokens, the standard: it is what most embedding models take as their input limit, what the survey reports at the top of its range, and what every framework ships as a default, so a new corpus has no reason to differ from it until a measurement says otherwise, which for a corpus of support tickets it rarely does", "Whatever the framework defaults to: the defaults encode the aggregate experience of everyone who has shipped with that library, which is a better prior than one team's measurement on one corpus, and it saves the labelling work tuning would cost first before the system is even running", "The embedding model's input limit: fill it exactly, because a chunk shorter than the limit wastes encoder capacity already paid for and a chunk longer than it gets truncated, so the limit is both floor and ceiling", "Label fifty real questions with their answering passages, chunk at several sizes with the shipping embedding model, plot recall and retrieved tokens, and choose the smallest size at the knee; add a contextual header per chunk, which moves the curve more than size does"], a: 3,
      why: "Chunk size is a measured tunable with a knee, and the labelled set that finds it becomes the regression suite of Chapter 25. Contextual headers and structure-aware cutting are the higher-leverage changes." },
    { type: "blank", q: "Embedding a whole document with a long-context model first and only then pooling tokens into chunk vectors is called ____ chunking.", a: ["late"],
      why: "Günther et al. 2024. The chunk boundaries are unchanged; the vectors change because each token's representation has seen the surrounding text, which is what a contextual header approximates in words." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 6 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): Bratanič & Hane, Essential GraphRAG
   ch. 3.2–3.3 (parent 2,000 chars / child 500 chars, overlap 20; over-fetch
   k×4 then dedupe parents; retrieval on the rewritten query, generation on the
   original); Polzer, Cookbook ch. 2.3 (five hypothetical questions per chunk,
   the "semantic alignment problem"); Gao et al. 2024 survey §III.B (Small2Big,
   "Reverse HyDE", hierarchical index); LangChain source,
   retrievers/multi_vector.py (`_get_relevant_documents`: child similarity
   search, parent ids collected in rank order without duplicates, `docstore.mget`);
   LlamaIndex docs, "Recursive Retriever + Node References" (children 128/256/512
   under 1,024-token parents; hit rate 0.778 → 0.897, MRR 0.563 → 0.691; summary
   + question references 0.892 / 0.718) and SentenceWindowNodeParser source
   (window_size 3, metadata keys "window" and "original_text", excluded from
   embedding); Sarthi et al. 2024, RAPTOR (arXiv 2401.18059: 100-token leaves,
   SBERT multi-qa-mpnet-base-cos-v1, UMAP + GMM soft clustering, BIC, gpt-3.5-turbo
   summaries, compression 0.28, mean summary 131 tokens, 6.7 children per parent;
   collapsed tree at 2,000 tokens beats traversal on 20 QASPER stories; QuALITY
   82.6% vs 62.3% prior best; 18.5–57% of retrieved nodes non-leaf); Gao, Ma,
   Lin & Callan 2022, HyDE (arXiv 2212.10496); Chen et al. 2023, Dense X
   (arXiv 2312.06648); Günther et al. 2024, Late Chunking (arXiv 2409.04701);
   Anthropic, "Introducing Contextual Retrieval" (2024), as cited in Chapter 5. */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 6, part: 2, title: "Small to Big: Parent Documents, Windows, and Synthetic Index Entries",
  blurb: "Chapter 5 left you with a knot: the retriever matches best on small text, and the generator answers best with large text, and a chunk has to be one size. This chapter unties it. The unit you embed and the unit you return do not have to be the same text. You will build an index where sentences are matched and paragraphs are handed back, see why that forces you to over-fetch and deduplicate, then replace the matched text altogether with something question-shaped: hypothetical questions, summaries, and RAPTOR's tree of summaries. You will run Larkspur's refund question through the machine and watch the right answer appear only when the over-fetch multiplier is turned up.",
  sections: [
    { title: "The Problem: Precise Matching Wants Small Units, Faithful Answering Wants Context",
      html: "<p>Take the Larkspur refund page and cut it the way Chapter 5 taught. At 500 characters a chunk holds one policy paragraph: the 24-hour rule, its exceptions, and the sentence about Basic fares. At 100 characters a chunk holds one sentence. Now ask Q2, the paraphrase: \"I bought a Basic ticket yesterday and want my money back, can I?\" With sentence chunks the retriever's top hit is sharp and right: \"The 24-hour rule applies to every fare type, including Basic.\" With paragraph chunks the top hit is the refund-eligibility paragraph, which says Basic fares are non-refundable and never mentions the 24-hour rule, because its vector is a blend of four sentences about refund types and the one sentence that matters is outvoted.</p>" +
            "<p>So sentences win the match. Hand the winning sentence to the generator alone and it produces: \"Yes, the 24-hour rule applies to Basic fares.\" Which rule? What does it say? Does \"yesterday\" fall inside it? The sentence does not carry its own conditions; they live in the sentences around it. The generator either guesses or hedges. Paragraphs win the answer. This is the knot: <strong>retrieval precision</strong> improves as the embedded unit shrinks, because one vector represents one idea; <strong>answer faithfulness</strong> improves as the returned unit grows, because the generator sees the conditions, exceptions, and definitions that surround the matched idea.</p>" +
            "<p>The naive escape is a middle size, and Chapter 5's curve is the honest measurement of how well that works: a knee, not a fix. The second naive escape is overlap, so that each sentence appears in two chunks with some context on each side; the same measurement showed overlap buys nothing and doubles the index. The third is to retrieve more chunks and hope the neighbours come along; they do not, because neighbours in the document are rarely neighbours in embedding space.</p>" +
            "<p>The escape that works is to stop insisting that one string plays both roles. Store two things: a small unit whose vector is matched, and a pointer from it to the larger unit that is returned. The retriever searches the small units; the result set is the large ones. The survey calls the pattern <strong>small-to-big</strong>, after a 2023 blog post by Sophia Yang; LangChain calls it the parent-document retriever; LlamaIndex calls it node references. Same machine. Every section of this chapter is one way of choosing what the small unit is: a sentence, a window, a question the passage could answer, a summary, or a summary of summaries.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Match unit</strong> — what gets embedded and compared to the query; wants to be one idea.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Return unit</strong> — what the generator receives; wants to carry conditions and definitions.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Link</strong> — a stored identifier from every match unit to its return unit. Lose it and you have a plain small-chunk index.</li></ul>" },

    { title: "Parent-Document Retrieval: Child Embeddings, Parent Return, Over-Fetch, Dedupe", viz: "parentChild", vizTitle: "Match children, return parents: turn the over-fetch multiplier",
      html: "<p>The mechanism has two stores and one identifier. At ingest, split each document into <strong>parents</strong> of the size you want the generator to read, and split each parent into <strong>children</strong> of the size you want to match on. Give each parent an id. Embed only the children, and write the parent's id into each child's metadata. Put the children and their vectors in the vector index; put the parents, keyed by id, in a plain key-value store, which LangChain calls the docstore and which in production is usually the same Postgres or object store that already holds your documents. Nothing about the parent is embedded.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='120' y='22' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>docstore (parents)</text>" +
            "<text x='560' y='22' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>vector index (children)</text>" +
            "<rect x='30' y='40' width='180' height='50' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='120' y='60' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>P1 Refund eligibility</text><text x='120' y='78' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>~2,000 chars, not embedded</text>" +
            "<rect x='30' y='105' width='180' height='50' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/><text x='120' y='125' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>P2 The 24-hour rule</text><text x='120' y='143' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>~2,000 chars, not embedded</text>" +
            "<rect x='30' y='170' width='180' height='50' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='120' y='190' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5'>P3 Ancillary purchases</text><text x='120' y='208' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>~2,000 chars, not embedded</text>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'><rect x='380' y='38' width='365' height='22' rx='6'/><rect x='380' y='64' width='365' height='22' rx='6'/><rect x='380' y='103' width='365' height='22' rx='6' stroke='#ffd166'/><rect x='380' y='129' width='365' height='22' rx='6' stroke='#ffd166'/><rect x='380' y='168' width='365' height='22' rx='6'/><rect x='380' y='194' width='365' height='22' rx='6'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'><text x='388' y='53'>c1 [vec] Refundable fares are returned... {parent: P1}</text><text x='388' y='79'>c2 [vec] Non-refundable fares are not... {parent: P1}</text><text x='388' y='118'>c5 [vec] Any booking made seven days... {parent: P2}</text><text x='388' y='144'>c6 [vec] The rule applies to every fare... {parent: P2}</text><text x='388' y='183'>c8 [vec] Seat selection, bags and meals... {parent: P3}</text><text x='388' y='209'>c9 [vec] The guarantee does not apply... {parent: P3}</text></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='4 3'><line x1='380' y1='49' x2='215' y2='60'/><line x1='380' y1='75' x2='215' y2='70'/><line x1='380' y1='114' x2='215' y2='125' stroke='#ffd166'/><line x1='380' y1='140' x2='215' y2='135' stroke='#ffd166'/><line x1='380' y1='179' x2='215' y2='190'/><line x1='380' y1='205' x2='215' y2='200'/></g>" +
            "<text x='305' y='228' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>child.metadata.parent_id is the whole link</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F6.1. Children carry vectors and a parent id; parents carry text and no vector. The dashed lines are the only thing that makes this different from a small-chunk index.</div></div>" +
            "<p>At query time, search the children for the top k by similarity, read each hit's parent id, fetch those parents from the docstore, and hand the parents to the generator. Two consequences follow immediately, and both are the reason the pattern is more than a diagram.</p>" +
            "<h3>Why you must over-fetch</h3>" +
            "<p>Children from the same parent are near each other in embedding space, because they are about the same thing; that is what a parent is. So the top k children cluster. Run Q2 against the toy index in the machine below with k = 4 and the multiplier at ×1. The four best children are c2, c3, c1, and c8: three sentences from P1 about refund types (they contain \"refund\" and \"fare\", so they sit close to a question about money back on a ticket) and one from P3 about the money-back guarantee on bags. Collapse by parent and you asked for four documents and got two, P1 and P3, neither of which contains the 24-hour rule. The generator, reading them faithfully, says no.</p>" +
            "<p>Now set the multiplier to ×2. The retriever fetches eight children; the next four are c9, c6, c4, and c5, and c6 (\"applies to every fare type, including Basic\") pulls in P2. Dedupe by parent and you have three parents, including the one with the answer. The GraphRAG book's demo uses ×4 with k = 4, sixteen children collapsed to at most four parents; LangChain leaves it to <code>search_kwargs</code> and, in the docs, most people never set it, which is the single most common way this retriever is mis-deployed. <strong>Over-fetching</strong> is fetching k × m children so that after collapsing to parents you still have about k distinct documents. It is not optional. Without it the parent retriever returns fewer documents than a plain one, and every duplicate parent is a slot you paid for and threw away.</p>" +
            "<h3>Why you must dedupe, and in rank order</h3>" +
            "<p>The same parent can arrive three times. Sending it three times to the generator wastes context and, worse, tells the model that this passage is three times as important as the others, which is a bias you did not choose. LangChain's MultiVectorRetriever, which the parent-document retriever subclasses, walks the child hits in similarity order, appends each parent id the first time it appears, and skips repeats, so the parents come back in the order of their best child; then a single batched get pulls the texts from the docstore and drops any id whose parent has vanished. That is <strong>parent deduplication</strong>, and the order it keeps is the rank signal the reranker in Chapter 16 and the context packer in Chapter 17 will rely on.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 190' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>k = 4, multiplier x1: fetch 4 children</text>" +
            "<g font-size='10' fill='#c9d6ea'><rect x='20' y='32' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='50' y='48' text-anchor='middle'>c2 P1</text><rect x='90' y='32' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='120' y='48' text-anchor='middle'>c3 P1</text><rect x='160' y='32' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='190' y='48' text-anchor='middle'>c1 P1</text><rect x='230' y='32' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='260' y='48' text-anchor='middle'>c8 P3</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='300' y1='44' x2='350' y2='44' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='356,44 346,39 346,49' fill='#7d8fb0'/><text x='370' y='48' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10'>dedupe: P1, P3 (two parents, no 24-hour rule)</text>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='72' x2='740' y2='72' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='94' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>k = 4, multiplier x2: fetch 8 children</text>" +
            "<g font-size='10' fill='#c9d6ea'><rect x='20' y='104' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='50' y='120' text-anchor='middle'>c2 P1</text><rect x='90' y='104' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='120' y='120' text-anchor='middle'>c3 P1</text><rect x='160' y='104' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='190' y='120' text-anchor='middle'>c1 P1</text><rect x='230' y='104' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='260' y='120' text-anchor='middle'>c8 P3</text><rect x='300' y='104' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='330' y='120' text-anchor='middle'>c9 P3</text><rect x='370' y='104' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/><text x='400' y='120' text-anchor='middle'>c6 P2</text><rect x='440' y='104' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='470' y='120' text-anchor='middle'>c4 P1</text><rect x='510' y='104' width='60' height='24' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/><text x='540' y='120' text-anchor='middle'>c5 P2</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='150' x2='70' y2='150' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='76,150 66,145 66,155' fill='#7d8fb0'/><text x='90' y='154' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>dedupe in rank order: P1, P3, P2 (three parents, answer present)</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='180' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>strike-through in the machine marks the repeats that were fetched and discarded</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F6.2. The same query, two multipliers. Over-fetching is what lets the second-best parent through; deduplication is what keeps the generator from reading P1 three times.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Sizes from the field: the GraphRAG book's demo uses 2,000-character parents and 500-character children with 20 characters of overlap on the children only; LlamaIndex's reference notebook uses 1,024-token parents with children at 128, 256, and 512 tokens all pointing at the same parent, so a query can match at whichever grain fits it. On that notebook's own evaluation set, the base retriever scored a hit rate of 0.778 and a mean reciprocal rank of 0.563; the child-to-parent version scored 0.897 and 0.691. The condition is one corpus and one embedding model, so carry the direction, not the digits: matching small and returning big moved recall by twelve points without touching the embedding model.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Try it.</strong> Above, keep k at 4, run Q2 at ×1 and count distinct parents (two). Turn the multiplier to ×2 and count again (three, and P2 is one of them). Then shrink the child size until each child is one sentence and watch the match get sharper while the returned parents stay the same size. That is the whole idea in one dial: the match unit and the return unit move independently.</div>" },

    { title: "Sentence Windows and Contextual Headers: The Cheap Versions",
      html: "<p>Parents and children need two stores and an id scheme. Two lighter patterns get much of the gain without the second store, and they are worth knowing because they are what most teams actually ship first.</p>" +
            "<h3>The sentence window</h3>" +
            "<p>Split the document into sentences. Embed each sentence alone. In each sentence's metadata store the sentences on either side of it, a <strong>window</strong>, and mark that field as excluded from embedding so the vector stays sharp. At query time match on the sentence and, before the chunk reaches the generator, swap its text for the window. LlamaIndex packages this as a sentence-window node parser whose default window is three sentences on each side, so seven in total, with the window under a metadata key called <code>window</code> and the bare sentence under <code>original_text</code>; a post-processor replaces the node's text with the window field after retrieval. There is no second store because the return unit rides inside the match unit's metadata, at the cost of storing every sentence about seven times over.</p>" +
            "<pre><code># LlamaIndex, sentence window (defaults shown)\nparser = SentenceWindowNodeParser.from_defaults(\n    window_size=3,                    # 3 sentences each side\n    window_metadata_key=\"window\",\n    original_text_metadata_key=\"original_text\",\n)\nnodes = parser.get_nodes_from_documents(docs)   # one node per sentence\n# ... build the index over nodes; the window key is excluded from embedding\nengine = index.as_query_engine(\n    similarity_top_k=6,\n    node_postprocessors=[MetadataReplacementPostProcessor(target_metadata_key=\"window\")],\n)</code></pre>" +
            "<p>The window is a parent that is fixed in size and centred on the match rather than aligned to the document's structure. That is its weakness: a window of seven sentences around \"applies to every fare type, including Basic\" reaches back into the refund-eligibility paragraph and forward into the cancellation instructions, and hands the generator a passage that straddles two policies. The parent retriever returns the 24-hour rule paragraph, whole and only. When documents have real structure, and Chapter 4 taught you to keep it, parents aligned to that structure beat windows. When documents are undifferentiated prose, transcripts, chat logs, a novel, windows are as good and simpler.</p>" +
            "<h3>The contextual header, revisited</h3>" +
            "<p>Chapter 4 introduced the contextual header, and Chapter 5 gave you Anthropic's measurement of it: a model-written sentence prefixed to each chunk, saying where it sits (\"From Larkspur's refund policy, section on the 24-hour cancellation rule, which applies to all fare types\"), cut the top-20 retrieval failure rate from 5.7 to 3.7 percent on its own. Read it through this chapter's lens and it is the same pattern turned inside out. Instead of matching on something smaller than the chunk, you match on the chunk plus a small amount of its parent, pulled down into the child. The return unit is unchanged; the match unit has been enriched. It composes with everything else here: a parent retriever whose children carry contextual headers is the combination the GraphRAG book and Anthropic's post separately recommend, and there is no reason not to do both.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Parent–child</strong> — two stores, structure-aligned return unit, needs over-fetch and dedupe; best when documents have sections.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Sentence window</strong> — one store, fixed-width return unit in metadata, no dedupe needed but adjacent windows overlap heavily; best for unstructured prose.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Contextual header</strong> — one store, unchanged return unit, enriched match unit; costs one model call per chunk at ingest, composes with both of the above.</li></ul>" },

    { title: "Synthetic Entries: Hypothetical Questions, Summaries, and Keeping the Link",
      html: "<p>Everything so far matched a query against text taken from the document. But the query is not document-shaped. Q2 is a question in the first person about yesterday's ticket; the passage that answers it is a declarative rule about bookings made seven days out. In embedding space, questions live in one region and statements in another, and the distance between a question and the statement that answers it is often larger than the distance between that question and an unrelated question. The cookbook calls this the <strong>semantic alignment problem</strong>, and Chapter 7 will show you why it happens inside the model. Here is the fix that does not need a new model.</p>" +
            "<h3>Hypothetical questions</h3>" +
            "<p>At ingest, ask a generator to write the questions each chunk could answer, five per chunk in the cookbook's recipe, and index <em>those</em>, each carrying the id of the chunk it was written from. At query time the user's question is compared with questions, like against like, and the hit's id fetches the chunk. The match unit is a synthetic question; the return unit is the original passage. For P2 the generator might write \"Can I cancel a Basic fare within 24 hours?\", \"Does the 24-hour rule apply to all fare types?\", \"How long do I have to cancel for a full refund?\". Q2 lands on the first of these before it lands on any sentence of the policy.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 240' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<rect x='20' y='20' width='720' height='200' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='30' y='40' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>embedding space (2-d sketch)</text>" +
            "<ellipse cx='200' cy='130' rx='120' ry='60' fill='none' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/><text x='200' y='206' text-anchor='middle' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='10'>statements (chunks)</text>" +
            "<ellipse cx='560' cy='130' rx='130' ry='60' fill='none' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/><text x='560' y='206' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>questions</text>" +
            "<circle cx='150' cy='110' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='160' y='105' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>P1 refund types</text>" +
            "<circle cx='230' cy='150' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='240' y='165' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>P2 24-hour rule</text>" +
            "<circle cx='180' cy='160' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='120' y='178' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>P3 ancillaries</text>" +
            "<circle cx='600' cy='120' r='6' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='612' y='115' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>Q2 (user)</text>" +
            "<circle cx='575' cy='145' r='5' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='500' y='165' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>HQ: cancel Basic in 24h?</text>" +
            "<circle cx='520' cy='105' r='5' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='530' y='98' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>HQ: refund types?</text>" +
            "<line x1='600' y1='120' x2='579' y2='142' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<line x1='600' y1='120' x2='236' y2='148' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='3 3'/>" +
            "<text x='330' y='118' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>far: question vs statement</text>" +
            "<line x1='570' y1='150' x2='238' y2='152' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='2 4'/><text x='380' y='40' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>the stored id, not geometry, takes the hit back to P2</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F6.3. Q2 is closer to a synthetic question than to any chunk. The synthetic question's stored id, not its position, is what returns the chunk.</div></div>" +
            "<p>The survey names this <strong>Reverse HyDE</strong>, and the name is worth decoding. HyDE, from Luyu Gao and colleagues in 2022, works at query time: the generator writes a hypothetical <em>document</em> that would answer the query, and that document's embedding is used to search real documents, statement against statement. Hypothetical questions do the mirror image at ingest time: the generator writes hypothetical <em>questions</em> for each real document, and the real query is searched against them, question against question. Both attack the same alignment gap from opposite ends; HyDE costs a model call per query, hypothetical questions cost model calls per chunk once. Chapter 14 does HyDE and its siblings properly.</p>" +
            "<h3>Summaries as match units</h3>" +
            "<p>The other synthetic entry is a summary. A generator writes a paragraph describing each document or section; the summary is embedded and linked to the full text; a query matches the summary and returns the text. Summaries are the right match unit for questions about what a document is about rather than what it says, \"which post-mortem covers the booking outage\" rather than \"what time did the outage start\", because a summary's vector represents the whole and a chunk's vector represents a part. LlamaIndex's document summary index is exactly this, and its reference notebook found that references made of summaries plus generated questions scored a hit rate of 0.892 and a mean reciprocal rank of 0.718, a higher rank than the child-chunk references at a similar hit rate: when the match unit is written to look like the query, the right document tends to come first.</p>" +
            "<h3>Keep the link, hand back the original</h3>" +
            "<p>Three rules hold every synthetic entry together. First, every synthetic entry stores the id of the text it was derived from; the index without the ids is worthless, because the generator must never be handed the synthetic text as evidence. A hypothetical question is not a fact, and a summary is a model's paraphrase that may have drifted; Chapter 18's grounding checks would flag a citation to either. Second, the ingest cost is real: five questions per chunk on a corpus of 100,000 chunks is 500,000 generated entries and half a million short model calls, and every re-chunk repeats it. Chapter 4's idempotent upsert keyed by content hash is what keeps that bill from recurring on unchanged text. Third, synthetic entries are additive: index the chunk, its questions, and its summary all pointing at the same id, and let the retriever match whichever is closest. The dedupe of the previous section collapses them back to one return unit.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>The edge.</strong> Hypothetical questions fix the alignment gap for questions the ingest-time generator anticipated. Q1, the cello, is a rare word that no generic question set will guess; a lexical index still finds it in one hop, which is why Chapter 9 keeps BM25 alongside everything in this chapter. Synthetic entries raise the floor for common questions; they do nothing for the tail.</div>" },

    { title: "Trees: RAPTOR's Recursive Summaries and When a Summary Tree Beats a Flat Index",
      html: "<p>A single summary per document has an obvious limit: some questions are answered by a sentence, some by a section, and some only by the whole thing. Q4, \"which policy changed after incident INC-2042\", needs the post-mortem's conclusion and the policy's revision note, two documents, at the level of their gist. A flat index of chunks has nothing at that level to match. In January 2024 Parth Sarthi, Christopher Manning, and colleagues at Stanford published <strong>RAPTOR</strong>, Recursive Abstractive Processing for Tree-Organized Retrieval, which builds match units at every level at once.</p>" +
            "<h3>Building the tree</h3>" +
            "<p>Cut the corpus into short leaves, 100 tokens in the paper, on sentence boundaries. Embed them; the paper used a sentence-transformer, multi-qa-mpnet-base-cos-v1. Cluster the leaves by embedding, so that chunks about the same thing group together whether or not they are adjacent in the document; the paper reduces the vectors with UMAP, fits a Gaussian mixture model, picks the number of clusters with the Bayesian information criterion, and uses <strong>soft clustering</strong>, letting a chunk belong to more than one cluster, because a paragraph about refunds on cancelled flights belongs with refunds and with cancellations. Summarise each cluster with a generator (gpt-3.5-turbo in the paper, at a compression ratio of about 0.28, a mean of 6.7 children collapsing into a 131-token summary). The summaries are new nodes. Embed them, cluster them, summarise again. Stop when a level has too few nodes to cluster. Every node at every level is stored with its embedding, so the tree is an index with leaves, section-level gists, and document-level gists all searchable.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>layer 2</text><text x='20' y='92' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>layer 1</text><text x='20' y='172' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>layer 0</text><text x='20' y='186' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>(leaves)</text>" +
            "<rect x='300' y='8' width='200' height='36' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/><text x='400' y='23' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>S: refunds, cancellations,</text><text x='400' y='36' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>and ancillaries policy</text>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'><rect x='120' y='80' width='180' height='34' rx='8'/><rect x='320' y='80' width='180' height='34' rx='8'/><rect x='520' y='80' width='180' height='34' rx='8'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'><text x='210' y='101'>S: how refunds are paid</text><text x='410' y='101'>S: when you can cancel</text><text x='610' y='101'>S: extras and guarantees</text></g>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'><rect x='90' y='160' width='60' height='26' rx='6'/><rect x='160' y='160' width='60' height='26' rx='6'/><rect x='230' y='160' width='60' height='26' rx='6'/><rect x='320' y='160' width='60' height='26' rx='6'/><rect x='390' y='160' width='60' height='26' rx='6'/><rect x='460' y='160' width='60' height='26' rx='6'/><rect x='560' y='160' width='60' height='26' rx='6'/><rect x='630' y='160' width='60' height='26' rx='6'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'><text x='120' y='177'>c1</text><text x='190' y='177'>c2</text><text x='260' y='177'>c3</text><text x='350' y='177'>c5</text><text x='420' y='177'>c6</text><text x='490' y='177'>c7</text><text x='590' y='177'>c8</text><text x='660' y='177'>c9</text></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='120' y1='160' x2='210' y2='114'/><line x1='190' y1='160' x2='210' y2='114'/><line x1='260' y1='160' x2='210' y2='114'/><line x1='350' y1='160' x2='410' y2='114'/><line x1='420' y1='160' x2='410' y2='114'/><line x1='490' y1='160' x2='410' y2='114'/><line x1='590' y1='160' x2='610' y2='114'/><line x1='660' y1='160' x2='610' y2='114'/><line x1='210' y1='80' x2='400' y2='44'/><line x1='410' y1='80' x2='400' y2='44'/><line x1='610' y1='80' x2='400' y2='44'/></g>" +
            "<line x1='490' y1='160' x2='610' y2='114' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/><text x='540' y='150' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>soft: c7 in two clusters</text>" +
            "<text x='380' y='215' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>collapsed-tree retrieval: rank all 12 nodes together, take the best until 2,000 tokens</text>" +
            "<text x='380' y='235' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>a leaf answers 'how long do I have'; a layer-1 summary answers 'what changed'</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F6.4. Clusters follow meaning, not document order, and a leaf may sit in two clusters. Every node is embedded, so a query can match at whichever level fits it.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>Searching the tree</h3>" +
            "<p>The paper tried two retrievals. <em>Tree traversal</em> starts at the root, takes the top few nodes by similarity, descends into their children, and repeats to the leaves. <em>Collapsed tree</em> ignores the hierarchy at query time: throw every node from every level into one flat index, rank them all against the query, and take the best until a token budget is filled. On twenty QASPER papers the collapsed tree won consistently, and the paper ran its main results with a 2,000-token budget, about twenty nodes. The lesson generalises past RAPTOR: the tree is for <em>building</em> match units at several grains; at query time you want them all in one race, because you do not know in advance which grain the question wants. Across the paper's datasets, between 18.5 and 57 percent of retrieved nodes were summaries rather than leaves, which is the direct evidence that flat chunk indexes have been missing a level.</p>" +
            "<p>The headline result is on QuALITY, multiple-choice questions over medium-length passages, where GPT-4 with RAPTOR scored 82.6 percent against a previous best of 62.3. The gains over plain retrieval with the same reader were smaller and consistent: on NarrativeQA, sentence-transformer retrieval scored 18.15 METEOR without the tree and 19.20 with it. Build cost scales linearly with corpus size, because each level is a fixed fraction of the one below, so at the paper's compression ratio the tree adds about 0.28 + 0.28² + … ≈ 0.39 summary tokens of generated text per leaf token, and it must be redone for any cluster whose members change. Incremental updates are the open engineering problem; the paper does not address them, and Chapter 4's refresh discipline collides with a structure that is global by construction.</p>" +
            "<h3>When a tree beats a flat index</h3>" +
            "<p>Use a summary tree when questions span documents or need a gist (\"what changed\", \"summarise the incidents involving refunds\", \"which contracts mention the peacock clause\"), when the corpus is a small number of long documents rather than many short ones, and when the corpus is stable enough that rebuilding is tolerable. Use a flat index with parents and children when questions are answered by one passage, when the corpus churns, or when ingest cost must be bounded. Larkspur's forty policy pages and six post-mortems are a tree-shaped corpus; its three hundred support tickets are not. Chapter 23's knowledge-graph index is the other answer to multi-document questions, with entities instead of clusters as the organising principle; the GraphRAG paper's community summaries, which Chapter 24 covers, are RAPTOR's idea rebuilt on a graph.</p>" },

    { title: "Late Chunking and Long-Context Embedding Models: The Newest Answer",
      html: "<p>Every pattern so far accepts that a chunk's vector is computed from the chunk's text alone and works around it: return a bigger unit, pull a header down, write a question, summarise upward. The newest approach changes the vector instead. Chapter 5 introduced <strong>late chunking</strong> from Günther and colleagues at Jina in 2024; here is why it belongs in this chapter and what it does and does not replace.</p>" +
            "<p>A transformer embedding model produces one vector per token, each computed by attention over every other token in its input, and then pools them into one vector for the input. Conventional chunking pools each chunk separately, so the token \"it\" in \"it applies to every fare type\" is embedded having never seen the words \"24-hour rule\" two sentences earlier. Late chunking runs the model once over the whole document, or as much as fits its window (about 8,000 tokens for Jina's embedding models and for OpenAI's text-embedding-3 family, so a long policy page but not a whole contract), and only then pools token vectors into per-chunk vectors using the chunk boundaries. The chunks are the same; the return unit is the same; the match unit's vector now encodes the surrounding context, because attention did what the contextual header was doing with words.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Parent–child</strong> — changes the return unit; match vector unchanged.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Contextual header</strong> — changes the match text; adds words, costs a generator call per chunk.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Hypothetical questions, summaries, RAPTOR</strong> — add new match units; cost generator calls per chunk or per cluster.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Late chunking</strong> — changes the match vector; no generator, one embedding pass per document, needs a long-window embedding model and control over pooling, which hosted embedding APIs generally do not expose.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>What it does not do: it does not return more context to the generator, so it does not replace parents; and it does not close the question-to-statement alignment gap, so it does not replace synthetic questions. It does make every child a better child. The honest summary of the chapter's whole toolbox is that these are orthogonal dials and the strong systems turn several: structure-aware parents from Chapter 4, contextual or late-chunked children, a synthetic question or summary layer for the common questions, a lexical index for the rare words, and a reranker to sort the over-fetched pile, which is Chapter 16.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"Your retriever returns the right paragraph but the answer is wrong\" and \"your retriever returns the wrong paragraph even though the answer is in the corpus\" are two different failures with two different fixes. The first is a return-unit problem: the generator lacks context, so grow the return unit (parents, windows). The second is a match-unit problem: the query and the passage do not align, so change what is matched (headers, questions, summaries, late chunking). An interviewer who asks about chunk size is usually testing whether you know these are separable. Chapter 25's evaluation harness is how you tell which one you have.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol><li><strong>Trace.</strong> Using the machine's toy index, set k = 3 and the multiplier to ×1, run Q2, and write down the children, the parents after dedupe, and whether the generator can answer. Repeat at ×2 and ×3. Find the smallest multiplier at which P2 appears, then explain in one sentence why that number would be different on a corpus where each parent had ten children instead of three or four. What you should find: at ×1 one parent (P1), at ×2 P2 appears; with more children per parent the top hits cluster harder and the multiplier must rise.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> Take the 24-hour rule paragraph (three sentences) and build its sentence windows with a window of one sentence each side. Write out the three windows. Show which of them straddles into a neighbouring paragraph and what a generator reading it would be told that the parent retriever would not have told it. What you should find: the first and last windows each pull one sentence from an adjacent policy.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Write five hypothetical questions for P2 by hand, then have any chat model write five. Embed Q2, your ten questions, and the three sentences of P2 with any embedding model, and compute cosine similarities. What you should find: Q2 is closer to several of the questions than to any sentence of the passage, and the model's questions and yours overlap on at most two or three, which is the coverage risk the section's edge callout described.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Implement the parent-document retriever in under sixty lines with a dictionary as the docstore and any vector library: split into 2,000-character parents and 500-character children, embed children with the parent id in metadata, and write a <code>retrieve(query, k, multiplier)</code> that over-fetches, dedupes in rank order, and returns parents. Test it on the refund page with Q2 at ×1 and ×4. This function becomes PocketRAG's retriever interface in Chapter 35; keep it.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> On the corpus you chunked in Chapter 5's last exercise, build a two-level summary layer by hand: one summary per section and one per document, each stored with the id of its source, all in the same index as the chunks. Rerun your labelled questions and record, for each hit, whether it came from a chunk, a section summary, or a document summary. What you should find: a non-trivial share of hits come from summaries, mostly for your gist-type questions, and none of your questions should ever be answered from a summary's text rather than the source it points to.</li></ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "small-to-big retrieval", d: "The pattern of matching a query against small text units and returning the larger units they belong to, also called parent-document retrieval or Small2Big." },
    { t: "match unit", d: "The text (or synthetic text) whose embedding is compared with the query; it should represent one idea." },
    { t: "return unit", d: "The text handed to the generator after retrieval; it should carry the conditions and definitions around the matched idea." },
    { t: "parent", d: "A larger chunk, stored by id in a document store without an embedding, that is returned when any of its children matches." },
    { t: "child", d: "A smaller chunk, embedded and indexed, whose metadata holds the id of its parent." },
    { t: "docstore", d: "A key-value store of return units keyed by id, separate from the vector index, from which parents are fetched by batch get." },
    { t: "over-fetch", d: "Retrieving k × m child hits so that, after collapsing to distinct parents, about k return units remain." },
    { t: "parent deduplication", d: "Collapsing child hits to distinct parent ids while preserving the rank order of each parent's best child." },
    { t: "sentence window", d: "A retrieval unit that is a single embedded sentence whose metadata stores the surrounding sentences, swapped in as the return text after matching." },
    { t: "contextual header", d: "A short model-written description of a chunk's place in its document, prefixed to the chunk before embedding to enrich the match unit." },
    { t: "semantic alignment problem", d: "The gap in embedding space between a question and the declarative passage that answers it, which makes question-to-statement matching unreliable." },
    { t: "hypothetical questions", d: "Ingest-time synthetic index entries, questions a chunk could answer, embedded and linked to the chunk so that queries are matched question-to-question; the survey's Reverse HyDE." },
    { t: "HyDE", d: "Hypothetical Document Embeddings (Gao et al. 2022): a query-time method that embeds a generated hypothetical answer document and searches with it." },
    { t: "summary index", d: "An index whose match units are model-written summaries of documents or sections, each linked to the full text it summarises." },
    { t: "RAPTOR", d: "Recursive Abstractive Processing for Tree-Organized Retrieval (Sarthi et al. 2024): leaves are clustered by embedding, each cluster summarised, and the process repeated to build a searchable tree of summaries." },
    { t: "collapsed-tree retrieval", d: "Searching all nodes of a summary tree as one flat set ranked by similarity up to a token budget, which RAPTOR found beats level-by-level traversal." },
    { t: "soft clustering", d: "Clustering in which a node may belong to more than one cluster, used by RAPTOR so a passage about two topics is summarised under both." },
    { t: "late chunking", d: "Embedding a whole document with a long-context model and only then pooling token vectors into chunk vectors, so each chunk's vector carries surrounding context." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "A team switches from 800-token chunks to a parent-document retriever with 800-token parents and 150-token children, keeps k = 5, and finds the generator now receives an average of 2.3 documents per query. What happened?",
      opts: ["The docstore is dropping the parents that failed to embed, so some of the ids the children point at resolve to nothing and are silently skipped at fetch time", "Child hits cluster under the same parents and no over-fetch multiplier was set, so dedupe collapses five children to about two parents", "The children are too small to match anything, so most of the k slots come back empty and only a couple of parents are ever resolved from them", "The embedding model's window is smaller than the parent, so parents are truncated on the way in and fewer of them survive than expected"], a: 1,
      why: "Children of one parent are near each other in embedding space, so the top-k children share parents. Fetch k × m children and dedupe, or the parent retriever returns fewer documents than the plain one did. Parents are never embedded, so their size cannot truncate anything." },
    { type: "mc", q: "In LangChain's multi-vector retriever, parent ids are appended to a list in the order child hits arrive, skipping ids already present, before a batched docstore get. Why does the order matter?",
      opts: ["The docstore only supports sorted keys, so the ids have to be accumulated in order before the batched get is issued", "It ensures the newest parent is first, so that stale versions of a document are excluded from the batch", "It has no effect on the result; a set would produce the same set of parents just as cheaply", "It keeps parents ranked by their best child's similarity, which downstream rerankers and context packers use as the retrieval signal"], a: 3,
      why: "Dedupe in rank order preserves the retrieval ranking at the parent level. A set would discard it, and the reranker of Chapter 16 and the context packer of Chapter 17 both consume that order." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Larkspur Q2 (\"I bought a Basic ticket yesterday and want my money back\") matches sentences about refund types before the 24-hour rule sentence, even though the latter answers it. Which single ingest-time change most directly attacks this failure?",
      opts: ["Larger parents, so that the 24-hour rule and the refund-type sentences arrive in one passage together", "A bigger over-fetch multiplier, so more children are ranked before dedupe", "Hypothetical questions indexed per chunk, so the query is matched question-to-question", "Sentence windows of five, returning each hit with its neighbours around it"], a: 2,
      why: "This is the semantic alignment problem: the query is a question and the passage is a statement. Parents, multipliers, and windows change what is returned or how much is fetched; hypothetical questions change what is matched." },
    { type: "blank", q: "The survey's name for indexing model-written questions per chunk, the mirror image of a query-time technique from Gao et al. 2022, is Reverse ____.", a: ["hyde", "HyDE"],
      why: "HyDE generates a hypothetical document at query time and searches with it; hypothetical questions generate questions at ingest time and search against them. Same alignment gap, opposite ends." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A summary-tree index returns a section summary as its top hit for \"what time did the booking outage start\". The generator answers with a time that appears only in the summary and is wrong. What rule was broken?",
      opts: ["The generator was handed synthetic text as evidence instead of the source the summary links to", "Summaries must be shorter than 131 tokens, which is the length the index was tuned for when the tree was built", "The tree should have been traversed top-down instead of collapsed into a single flat search over every level", "Summaries must not be embedded with the same model as the leaves beneath them"], a: 0,
      why: "Every synthetic entry carries the id of its source, and the source, not the paraphrase, is what the generator reads and cites. A summary that drifted is exactly the hallucination Chapter 18's grounding checks exist to catch." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the steps of building one level of a RAPTOR tree:", items: ["embed the nodes of the current level", "reduce dimensions and fit a Gaussian mixture, choosing the cluster count by BIC", "summarise each cluster with a generator", "embed the summaries as the nodes of the next level"],
      why: "Embed, cluster (UMAP + GMM with soft membership, BIC for the count), summarise, and the summaries become the next level's nodes. Repeat until a level is too small to cluster." },
    { type: "mc", q: "RAPTOR's authors compared descending the tree level by level with ranking every node from every level in one flat search under a 2,000-token budget. What did they find, and what is the general lesson?",
      opts: ["Traversal won: descending the tree level by level respects the hierarchy and stops the search from mixing grains that were never meant to compete for one budget", "They tied on quality, so the choice comes down to latency and the extra round trips a level-by-level descent has to pay on every question", "Collapsed tree won only on short documents, where the summaries add little and the leaves alone already cover the whole of the text that was allowed", "Collapsed tree won; build match units at several grains but let them all compete at query time, because the question's grain is unknown in advance"], a: 3,
      why: "On twenty QASPER papers the collapsed tree was consistently better, and 18.5 to 57 percent of retrieved nodes were summaries. The tree is a way to manufacture match units, not a way to search." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Which of the chapter's techniques changes the child's vector without changing its text or the text returned to the generator?",
      opts: ["Contextual headers", "Sentence windows", "Late chunking", "Parent-document retrieval"], a: 2,
      why: "Late chunking runs the embedding model over the whole document and pools per chunk afterwards, so attention supplies the context. Headers change the text; windows and parents change the return unit." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A corpus of 100,000 chunks is re-chunked after a splitter change. The team has hypothetical questions (five per chunk) and a two-level RAPTOR tree. What is the honest cost statement?",
      opts: ["All half a million questions and every cluster summary must be regenerated, since both are derived from chunk boundaries; only content-hash idempotency on unchanged chunks limits the bill", "Nothing needs regenerating, because the source documents did not change and both the questions and the tree derive from the documents rather than from the chunks", "Only the questions need regenerating; the tree is boundary-independent because its clusters are built over embeddings rather than over the chunk text itself", "Only the tree needs regenerating; the questions attach to documents rather than to chunks and survive a re-cut of the corpus unchanged"], a: 0,
      why: "Synthetic entries are derived from chunks, so new boundaries mean new derivations. Chapter 4's content-hash upsert skips chunks whose text is byte-identical after the re-cut; everything else is regenerated. RAPTOR clusters are global, so a changed member can change a summary." },
    { type: "mc", q: "An interviewer describes two systems: in A, the retrieved paragraph contains the answer but the generator answers incorrectly; in B, the paragraph that contains the answer is never retrieved. Which pairing of diagnosis and fix is right?",
      opts: ["A is a match-unit problem, fix it with parents so that the whole passage comes back; B is a return-unit problem, fix it with headers so each chunk carries a note of where in the document it originally came from", "A is a return-unit problem, fix by returning more context (parents, windows); B is a match-unit problem, fix by changing what is matched (headers, questions, summaries, late chunking)", "Both are chunk-size problems, fix them by tuning the size until the right paragraph both matches and arrives whole, since one number controls both halves", "Both are generator problems, fix them with a better prompt that tells the model to read the whole of the retrieved text carefully before it answers"], a: 1,
      why: "The chapter's separation: retrieval precision depends on the match unit, answer faithfulness on the return unit. If the right paragraph arrives and the answer is wrong, the generator lacked context or the prompt failed (Chapters 17 and 18). If it never arrives, the match is misaligned." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 7 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): Reimers & Gurevych 2019, Sentence-BERT
   (arXiv 1908.10084: 10,000 sentences, ~50M pairwise BERT inferences ≈ 65 h vs
   ~5 s; siamese network, mean pooling); Ethayarajh 2019, "How Contextual are
   Contextualized Word Representations?" (arXiv 1909.00512, EMNLP: anisotropy in
   every layer, worst in upper layers; <5% of variance explained by a static
   vector); Radovanović, Nanopoulos & Ivanović 2010, "Hubs in Space" (JMLR 11:
   k-occurrence skew grows with dimension); Kusupati et al. 2022, Matryoshka
   Representation Learning (arXiv 2205.13147: up to 14× smaller at equal
   accuracy); Muennighoff et al. 2022, MTEB (arXiv 2210.07316: 8 tasks, 58
   datasets, 112 languages, 33 models, "no method dominates"); Wang et al. 2022,
   E5 (arXiv 2212.03533: "query:"/"passage:" prefixes, CCPairs); Xiao et al.
   2023, C-Pack/BGE (arXiv 2309.07597); Shakir, Aarsen & Lee, HF blog "Binary and
   Scalar Embedding Quantization" (22 Mar 2024: binary 32× memory, ~92.5% retained,
   ~96% with rescoring; int8 4×, ~99.3%); pgvector README (vector/halfvec/bit/
   sparsevec limits, operators <-> <=> <#> <+> <~> <%>, binary_quantize and the
   rescoring subquery); OpenAI embeddings guide + pricing (3-small 1,536 d, 3-large
   3,072 d, 8,192 tokens, MTEB 62.3/64.6, $0.02/$0.13 per M tokens, normalized to
   length 1, dimensions parameter); Voyage docs + pricing (voyage-4 family 1,024 d
   default with 256/512/2,048, 32K context, int8/binary outputs, $0.02–0.12 per M);
   Cohere Embed API reference (embed-v4.0 256/512/1,024/1,536 d, 128K context,
   input_type search_document/search_query, embedding_types float/int8/uint8/
   binary/ubinary); HF model cards for all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384 d, 256 word pieces,
   1B pairs), bge-base-en-v1.5 (768 d, 512 tokens, query instruction, "relative
   order matters, not absolute value"), nomic-embed-text-v1.5 (768 d, Matryoshka
   64–768, 8,192 tokens, search_query:/search_document: prefixes); Sentence
   Transformers Matryoshka docs (truncate_dim). Bratanič & Hane ch. 2.1 (cosine
   "between 0 and 1" corrected here; Neo4j rescales); Gao et al. 2024 survey §III.C. */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 7, part: 2, title: "Embeddings: Text as Geometry",
  blurb: "Everything before this chapter treated the embedding model as a box that turns text into a vector. Open the box and retrieval becomes geometry: a sentence is a point, and \"similar meaning\" is \"nearby\". But nearby has three definitions, cosine, dot product, and Euclidean distance, and they rank the same neighbours differently unless the vectors are normalized. You will compute all three by hand on four-dimensional vectors and watch them disagree, learn why raw transformer vectors crowd into a narrow cone so that everything looks similar to everything, then shrink vectors four ways, by Matryoshka truncation and by float16, int8, and binary quantization, and measure what each costs in recall and saves in memory per million vectors.",
  sections: [
    { title: "From One-Hot to Dense: What a Dimension Is Not",
      html: "<p>The oldest way to turn a word into numbers is a vector as long as the vocabulary with a single 1 in the word's slot: <strong>one-hot encoding</strong>. It has one virtue, every word is different, and one fatal flaw, every word is equally different. \"Refund\" and \"reimbursement\" are as far apart as \"refund\" and \"peacock\", because the dot product of any two distinct one-hot vectors is zero. Retrieval over one-hot vectors is exact keyword matching, which Chapter 9 will show is more useful than it sounds, but it can never find a paraphrase.</p>" +
            "<p>The fix, worked out for words in the 2000s and made cheap by Mikolov and colleagues at Google in 2013 with word2vec, is a <strong>dense embedding</strong>: a short vector of a few hundred real numbers rather than one bit among fifty thousand, and to learn the numbers so that words used in similar contexts get similar vectors. Nobody assigns a meaning to dimension 37. The training objective (Chapter 8 gives the modern one) pushes vectors of texts that belong together closer and vectors of texts that do not further apart, and the dimensions are whatever coordinate system that pressure settles into. A dimension is not a feature you can name. It is one axis of a space whose only guaranteed property is that distance tracks the training signal.</p>" +
            "<p>A <strong>sentence embedding</strong> extends this from a word to a passage. A transformer reads the tokens, produces one contextual vector per token, and a <strong>pooling</strong> step collapses those into one vector for the whole input: the mean of the token vectors, or the vector of a special first token. Nils Reimers and Iryna Gurevych's Sentence-BERT, in 2019, is the paper that made this the standard for retrieval. Their motivating number is worth carrying: finding the most similar pair among 10,000 sentences by running BERT on every pair is about fifty million forward passes, roughly 65 hours on the hardware of the day. Embedding each sentence once and comparing vectors took about five seconds. Chapter 16 will show what that shortcut gives up; this chapter is about what it makes possible.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<rect x='20' y='20' width='720' height='210' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='30' y='40' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>Larkspur sentences as points (2-d projection of 384-d)</text>" +
            "<circle cx='150' cy='90' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='160' y='86' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>Refundable fares are returned within seven days</text>" +
            "<circle cx='175' cy='125' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='185' y='129' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>The 24-hour rule applies to every fare type</text>" +
            "<circle cx='120' cy='150' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='130' y='164' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>Non-refundable fares become a travel credit</text>" +
            "<circle cx='205' cy='75' r='6' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='215' y='66' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>Q2: I want my money back</text>" +
            "<circle cx='520' cy='100' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='530' y='96' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>Cellos travel in a purchased seat</text>" +
            "<circle cx='560' cy='140' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='570' y='144' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>The case must fit the sizer</text>" +
            "<circle cx='600' cy='185' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='610' y='189' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>Pets travel in the hold</text>" +
            "<circle cx='490' cy='70' r='6' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='400' y='58' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>Q1: can I bring a cello?</text>" +
            "<circle cx='370' cy='200' r='5' fill='#7d8fb0'/><text x='300' y='218' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>March had 41,207 tickets (a table row)</text>" +
            "<text x='430' y='40' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>near = about the same thing; far = about something else</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F7.1. Sentences about refunds land together, sentences about instruments land together, and each question lands near the cluster that answers it. The axes have no names.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Two rules follow from \"the dimensions are whatever training settled into\", and both are Chapter 1 laws restated in geometry. First, the same model must embed the corpus and the query: two models, even two versions of one model, are two coordinate systems, and a point from one has no meaning in the other. Change the model and you re-embed everything, which is why Chapter 8 treats a model upgrade as a migration. Second, vectors from different models cannot be compared, added, or averaged, and a similarity score from one model says nothing about a score from another. Section 3 makes that second rule precise.</p>" },

    { title: "Three Similarities: Cosine, Dot Product, and Euclidean Distance", viz: "vectorSpace", vizTitle: "Rank Larkspur sentences by cosine, dot product, and L2, with and without normalization",
      html: "<p>Given a query vector <em>q</em> and a document vector <em>d</em>, \"nearby\" has three common definitions, and every vector database offers all three.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Dot product</strong> — <code>q · d = Σ qᵢ dᵢ</code>. Grows with the angle closing <em>and</em> with the length of either vector. Range unbounded.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Cosine similarity</strong> — <code>q · d / (|q| |d|)</code>. The dot product with the lengths divided out; the cosine of the angle between the vectors. Range −1 to 1, not 0 to 1 (the GraphRAG book says 0 to 1, which is true only after a database rescales it; Neo4j does, and reports (cos + 1) / 2). One minus this is <strong>cosine distance</strong>.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Euclidean (L2) distance</strong> — <code>|q − d| = √Σ (qᵢ − dᵢ)²</code>. The straight-line distance between the points. Smaller is closer. Range 0 upward.</li></ul>" +
            "<h3>A worked example where all three disagree</h3>" +
            "<p>Take four-dimensional vectors so you can do the arithmetic. Query <em>q</em> = (1, 1, 0, 0), length √2 ≈ 1.414. Three documents:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>a</strong> = (2, 2, 0, 0), length √8 ≈ 2.828. Same direction as <em>q</em>, twice as long.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>b</strong> = (1, 0.5, 0.5, 0), length √1.5 ≈ 1.225. Close to <em>q</em> in position.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>c</strong> = (3, 0, 0, 3), length √18 ≈ 4.243. Long, and pointing half away.</li></ul>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Dot</strong> — a: 4, c: 3, b: 1.5. Ranking a, c, b.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Cosine</strong> — a: 4 / (1.414 × 2.828) = 1.00; b: 1.5 / (1.414 × 1.225) = 0.87; c: 3 / (1.414 × 4.243) = 0.50. Ranking a, b, c.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>L2</strong> — a: √(1 + 1) = 1.41; b: √(0 + 0.25 + 0.25) = 0.71; c: √(4 + 1 + 0 + 9) = 3.74. Ranking b, a, c.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Three metrics, three different first choices among the same three vectors. Dot product rewarded <em>c</em> for being long; L2 rewarded <em>b</em> for being physically close even though it points in a different direction from <em>a</em>; cosine cared about direction only. None of them is wrong. They measure different things, and which one the embedding model was trained to make meaningful is a fact about the model that you must look up (Chapter 8 explains why the training loss decides it).</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 240' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<line x1='60' y1='200' x2='330' y2='200' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='60' y1='200' x2='60' y2='30' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='195' y='225' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>dims 1–2 of the example (dims 3–4 not shown)</text>" +
            "<line x1='60' y1='200' x2='130' y2='130' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2.5'/><text x='70' y='150' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10'>q (1,1)</text>" +
            "<line x1='60' y1='200' x2='200' y2='60' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='205' y='55' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='10'>a (2,2)</text>" +
            "<line x1='60' y1='200' x2='130' y2='165' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='135' y='172' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>b (1,0.5,…)</text>" +
            "<line x1='60' y1='200' x2='270' y2='200' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/><text x='250' y='190' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>c (3,0,…,3)</text>" +
            "<path d='M 95 165 A 50 50 0 0 1 108 190' fill='none' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='2 2'/>" +
            "<g font-size='10' fill='#c9d6ea'><text x='380' y='50' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>ranking by</text>" +
            "<text x='380' y='80'>dot     a &gt; c &gt; b   (length wins)</text>" +
            "<text x='380' y='110'>cosine  a &gt; b &gt; c   (angle wins)</text>" +
            "<text x='380' y='140'>L2      b &gt; a &gt; c   (position wins)</text>" +
            "<text x='380' y='185' fill='#ffd166'>normalize all to length 1:</text>" +
            "<text x='380' y='210' fill='#ffd166'>dot = cosine, and L2 = √(2 − 2·cos)</text></g>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F7.2. a is q's direction, b is q's neighbour in position, c is long. Each metric picks a different winner; after normalization all three agree.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>Normalization makes them agree</h3>" +
            "<p>Divide every vector by its length so all have length 1, which is <strong>L2 normalization</strong>. Now the dot product is <em>exactly</em> the cosine, since the denominator is 1 × 1. And the squared Euclidean distance between two unit vectors is |q|² + |d|² − 2 q·d = 2 − 2 cos, a decreasing function of cosine, so L2 ranks identically too. Three metrics collapse to one ordering. This is why most embedding APIs ship unit vectors: OpenAI's documentation says its embeddings are normalized to length 1 and that cosine and Euclidean therefore give identical rankings, and it recommends computing cosine as a plain dot product because it is cheaper. BGE and MiniLM's reference code end with an explicit normalize call. Chapter 12's product quantization and Chapter 11's HNSW both work on whichever metric you declare, but declaring the wrong one for a model that was trained on cosine is a silent recall loss, not an error.</p>" +
            "<p>When would you <em>not</em> normalize? When length carries information the model was trained to put there. Some retrieval models trained with a dot-product loss learn to make vectors of long, information-dense passages longer, so that they win ties against short passages; normalizing throws that away. Multi-vector models (Chapter 16) and some learned-sparse models also rely on unnormalized dot products. The rule is not \"always normalize\". The rule is \"use the metric the model card names, and if it says cosine, normalize once at write time and use the dot product forever after\".</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Try it.</strong> In the machine above, pick Q1 (the cello), select <em>dot product</em>, and turn normalization off. Note the top five. Switch to <em>cosine</em>: at least one neighbour changes rank, because a long vector about instruments generally outscored a short vector about cellos specifically. Turn normalization on and switch metrics again: the three lists are now identical. Then type your own question and watch where it lands.</div>" },

    { title: "The Shape of the Space: Anisotropy, Hubness, and Why 0.8 Means Nothing",
      html: "<p>Picture a thousand random unit vectors in 384 dimensions. Their pairwise cosines cluster tightly around zero: in high dimensions, random directions are nearly orthogonal. Now embed a thousand random sentences with a raw transformer, one that was pre-trained on language modelling and never tuned for similarity, and compute the same cosines. They are not near zero. Kawin Ethayarajh measured this in 2019 for ELMo, BERT, and GPT-2: contextual word vectors are <strong>anisotropic</strong> in every layer, occupying a narrow cone rather than the whole sphere, and the effect worsens in upper layers, where in GPT-2's last layer almost any two words score a cosine close to 1. Everything is similar to everything, and the useful signal is a small difference riding on a large constant offset.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 220' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='180' y='24' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>isotropic (what you assume)</text>" +
            "<circle cx='180' cy='120' r='80' fill='none' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<g stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='180' y1='120' x2='255' y2='92'/><line x1='180' y1='120' x2='120' y2='60'/><line x1='180' y1='120' x2='105' y2='150'/><line x1='180' y1='120' x2='200' y2='198'/><line x1='180' y1='120' x2='250' y2='170'/><line x1='180' y1='120' x2='175' y2='40'/><line x1='180' y1='120' x2='100' y2='115'/></g>" +
            "<text x='180' y='215' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>random pairs: cosine ≈ 0</text>" +
            "<text x='560' y='24' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>anisotropic (raw transformer)</text>" +
            "<circle cx='560' cy='120' r='80' fill='none' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<path d='M 560 120 L 638 95 A 80 80 0 0 1 636 148 Z' fill='#ff5d73' fill-opacity='0.12' stroke='none'/>" +
            "<g stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='560' y1='120' x2='638' y2='98'/><line x1='560' y1='120' x2='639' y2='110'/><line x1='560' y1='120' x2='640' y2='122'/><line x1='560' y1='120' x2='638' y2='134'/><line x1='560' y1='120' x2='636' y2='146'/><line x1='560' y1='120' x2='639' y2='116'/><line x1='560' y1='120' x2='638' y2='128'/></g>" +
            "<text x='560' y='215' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>random pairs: cosine ≈ 0.9; signal is the small spread</text>" +
            "<line x1='380' y1='40' x2='380' y2='200' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F7.3. The anisotropy cone. Raw transformer vectors share a common direction; contrastive training (Chapter 8) and whitening spread them back out, which is why a retrieval model's cosines are meaningful and a raw BERT's are not.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Two consequences. First, never use a raw language model's hidden state as an embedding. Sentence-BERT's contrastive fine-tuning, and every retrieval model since, exists partly to spread the cone back over the sphere; a 2021 technique called whitening (centre the vectors, then rotate and scale so their covariance is the identity) does the same after the fact, and both raise similarity-task scores by several points on the same base model. Second, similarity has no absolute scale. A cosine of 0.8 between a Larkspur question and a chunk means \"close\" under one model and \"random\" under another. The BGE model card says this outright: what matters is the relative order of scores, not their absolute value, and its v1.5 release exists partly because v1's scores were squeezed into a narrow band that users kept misreading as \"everything matches\". A <strong>similarity threshold</strong> copied from a blog post is a number from another coordinate system. If you need a cutoff (Chapter 18's refusal path does), calibrate it on your own labelled questions, per model, and re-calibrate on every model change.</p>" +
            "<h3>Hubness</h3>" +
            "<p>There is a second distortion that survives even a well-trained model. In 2010 Miloš Radovanović and colleagues showed that in high-dimensional data, the number of times a point appears in other points' k-nearest-neighbour lists becomes heavily skewed: a few points, <strong>hubs</strong>, are everyone's neighbour, and many points, anti-hubs, are nobody's. It is a property of the geometry, not of the data. In a RAG index the hubs are generic chunks, a boilerplate paragraph, a table of contents, a short bland sentence near the centre of the distribution, that surface for many unrelated queries and push specific chunks down. You will meet them as \"why does this useless chunk keep coming back\". The remedies are the ones this course has already assembled: dedupe the boilerplate at ingest (Chapter 4), rerank with a model that reads query and chunk together (Chapter 16), and measure (Chapter 25) so the hub shows up as a recurring false positive rather than a hunch.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>The edge.</strong> \"Semantically similar\" and \"relevant\" are different relations, and geometry only gives you the first. \"How do I cancel my Larkspur booking?\" and \"How do I cancel my Larkspur account?\" are nearer to each other than either is to the passage that answers one of them. An embedding model trained on question–passage pairs (Chapter 8) partly learns relevance rather than similarity, which is why E5 and BGE want a different prefix on queries than on passages: they are asking the model to place the two kinds of text so that relevance, not paraphrase, is what the angle measures.</div>" },

    { title: "Dimension and Its Cost: Memory per Million Vectors and Matryoshka Truncation",
      html: "<p>A vector's dimension is a bill you pay per chunk forever. A float32 is 4 bytes, so one vector costs 4 × d bytes, and an index of a million chunks costs 4 million × d bytes before the graph or the metadata is counted.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>384 d</strong> (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) — 1,536 bytes per vector; 1.5 GB per million.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>768 d</strong> (bge-base, e5-base, nomic-embed) — 3,072 bytes; 3.1 GB per million.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>1,024 d</strong> (bge-large, voyage-4 default) — 4,096 bytes; 4.1 GB per million.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>1,536 d</strong> (text-embedding-3-small, embed-v4.0 default) — 6,144 bytes; 6.1 GB per million.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>3,072 d</strong> (text-embedding-3-large) — 12,288 bytes; 12.3 GB per million.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Larkspur's corpus, chunked at Chapter 5's knee, is perhaps 200,000 chunks: 2.5 GB at 3,072 dimensions, which fits in memory on one machine. A hundred million chunks, a modest enterprise document store, is 1.2 TB, which does not. And Chapter 11 will show that an HNSW index wants its vectors in RAM, so this number is the size of the machine, not the size of the disk. Dimension also sets the cost of every distance computation, d multiplies and adds per candidate, and of every network transfer of a vector.</p>" +
            "<p>Does the extra dimension buy accuracy? Some. On the MTEB benchmark OpenAI reports 62.3 for the 1,536-dimensional small model and 64.6 for the 3,072-dimensional large one, 2.3 points for double the memory and six and a half times the price. But that comparison confounds dimension with model size, and the honest experiment is the one Section 5's machine runs: the same model, truncated.</p>" +
            "<h3>Matryoshka embeddings</h3>" +
            "<p>Truncate a conventional 768-dimensional vector to its first 128 numbers and the result is garbage, because the model spread information evenly across all 768 axes and you kept a random sixth of it. In 2022 Aditya Kusupati and colleagues proposed <strong>Matryoshka Representation Learning</strong>, named after the nesting dolls: train the model so that the loss is applied not only to the full vector but also to its first 64, 128, 256, and 512 dimensions at once. The model then learns to put the coarsest, most important information first and refinements later, and a prefix of the vector is a usable, slightly worse embedding. The paper reports up to 14× smaller vectors at the same accuracy on image classification and retrieval; on text, the Sentence Transformers documentation's own experiment kept 98.4 percent of a model's similarity score at 64 of 768 dimensions where a conventionally trained model kept 96.5.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke-width='2'><rect x='40' y='40' width='680' height='36' rx='8' stroke='#1c2a45'/><rect x='40' y='40' width='340' height='36' rx='8' stroke='#4f7cff'/><rect x='40' y='40' width='170' height='36' rx='8' stroke='#4f7cff'/><rect x='40' y='40' width='85' height='36' rx='8' stroke='#ffd166'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'><text x='82' y='63'>0–255</text><text x='167' y='63'>256–511</text><text x='295' y='63'>512–1023</text><text x='550' y='63'>1024–2047</text></g>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5' text-anchor='middle'><text x='82' y='100'>coarse</text><text x='550' y='100'>fine detail</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='40' y1='120' x2='720' y2='120' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/>" +
            "<g font-size='10' fill='#c9d6ea'><text x='40' y='145'>keep 256 of 2,048:  8× less memory</text><text x='40' y='165' fill='#ffd166'>re-normalize after truncating, or the dot product is no longer the cosine</text><text x='40' y='185' fill='#7d8fb0'>conventional model truncated to 256: information was spread evenly, most of it is gone</text></g>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F7.4. A Matryoshka vector nests: every prefix is a valid embedding, coarse first. Truncation is a slice, then a re-normalization; nothing is re-embedded.</div></div>" +
            "<p>This is what the <code>dimensions</code> parameter on OpenAI's text-embedding-3 models is, and what Voyage's 256/512/1,024/2,048 outputs, Cohere's 256 to 1,536, and nomic-embed's 64 to 768 are. OpenAI's guide notes that its large model shortened to 256 dimensions still outscores the previous 1,536-dimensional ada-002 on MTEB, and it adds the one operational trap: if you shorten a vector yourself rather than asking the API, re-normalize it, because the prefix of a unit vector is not a unit vector, and Section 2's identity between dot product and cosine breaks. The Sentence Transformers library does both in one keyword, <code>truncate_dim</code>. Two practical patterns: store the short prefix in the ANN index for the first pass and the full vector on disk for rescoring, or commit to a shorter dimension and measure the recall you gave up on your own questions, which for most corpora is a point or two for a four-fold memory saving.</p>" },

    { title: "Quantization: Float16, Int8, Binary, and the Product-Quantization Preview", viz: "quantizeVec", vizTitle: "Shrink a vector set four ways and watch recall@10 and memory per million",
      html: "<p>Truncation drops dimensions. <strong>Quantization</strong> keeps every dimension and stores each number in fewer bits. The ladder has four rungs, and the machine above lets you climb it on a real vector set while it reports bytes per vector and recall at 10 against exact float32 search.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>float32 → float16</strong> — halve the bytes. Ten bits of mantissa is more precision than an embedding carries; recall loss is essentially zero. pgvector's <code>halfvec</code> type is this, and its HNSW index accepts up to 4,000 dimensions in halfvec against 2,000 in float32, so it is also how you index a 3,072-dimensional vector there at all.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>int8 scalar quantization</strong> — map each dimension's float range onto 256 buckets, one byte per dimension: 4× smaller than float32. Calibrate the min and max per dimension on a sample of the corpus. The Hugging Face study of March 2024 measured about 99.3 percent of retrieval quality retained and a 3.7× search speedup.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>binary</strong> — one bit per dimension, 1 if the value is above zero: 32× smaller. Distance becomes <strong>Hamming distance</strong>, the count of differing bits, which a CPU computes with a population-count instruction in a couple of cycles. The same study measured about 92.5 percent of quality retained on its own, and about 96 percent with rescoring.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>product quantization</strong> — split the vector into sub-vectors, cluster each sub-vector's values across the corpus into 256 centroids, and store one byte per sub-vector: a 1,024-dimensional vector in 8 sub-vectors is 8 bytes, 512× smaller. Distances are looked up from precomputed tables. It is the workhorse of billion-scale search and the subject of Chapter 12, where you build the codebooks yourself.</li></ul>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>bytes per 1,024-d vector, and memory per million</text>" +
            "<g font-size='10' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<rect x='150' y='36' width='560' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='20' y='49'>float32</text><text x='160' y='49' fill='#111a2e'>4,096 B   4.1 GB</text>" +
            "<rect x='150' y='62' width='280' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='20' y='75'>float16</text><text x='160' y='75' fill='#111a2e'>2,048 B   2.0 GB</text>" +
            "<rect x='150' y='88' width='140' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='20' y='101'>int8</text><text x='160' y='101' fill='#111a2e'>1,024 B   1.0 GB</text>" +
            "<rect x='150' y='114' width='35' height='18' rx='4' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='20' y='127'>binary</text><text x='195' y='127'>128 B   128 MB</text>" +
            "<rect x='150' y='140' width='4' height='18' rx='2' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='20' y='153'>PQ 8×8</text><text x='165' y='153'>8 B   8 MB   (Chapter 12)</text>" +
            "<rect x='150' y='166' width='70' height='18' rx='4' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='20' y='179'>MRL 256</text><text x='230' y='179'>1,024 B   1.0 GB   (truncation, composes with all of the above)</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<text x='20' y='215' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>recall retained (HF 2024, same model): float16 ≈ 100%, int8 ≈ 99%, binary ≈ 92% alone / ≈ 96% rescored</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F7.5. The quantization ladder. Binary is where memory falls off a cliff and recall starts to; rescoring with the full vectors buys most of it back.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>Rescoring: the pattern that makes binary usable</h3>" +
            "<p>Binary search alone loses the fine ordering. The fix is two passes: search the binary index for, say, four times the number of results you want, then re-score those candidates with the full-precision query against the full-precision (or int8) document vectors, and return the top k. The first pass is fast and small; the second touches only a few dozen vectors. pgvector documents exactly this shape as a subquery: order by Hamming distance on <code>binary_quantize(embedding)</code> with a larger limit inside, then order by cosine on the original column with the final limit outside. The Hugging Face study's 92.5-to-96 percent gap is that second pass. It is also the same shape as Chapter 16's reranking cascade and Chapter 6's over-fetch: a cheap wide net, then an expensive narrow one.</p>" +
            "<pre><code>-- pgvector: binary first pass, exact rescoring second pass\nSELECT id, title FROM (\n  SELECT id, title, embedding\n  FROM chunks\n  ORDER BY binary_quantize(embedding)::bit(1024)\n        &lt;~&gt; binary_quantize($1::vector)     -- Hamming distance\n  LIMIT 40                                    -- over-fetch 4x\n) candidates\nORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1::vector             -- cosine distance\nLIMIT 10;</code></pre>" +
            "<p>pgvector's operators, since you will read them in every query from here on: <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> is L2 distance, <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> cosine distance, <code>&lt;#&gt;</code> the <em>negative</em> inner product (negative so that ascending order still means most similar first, a trap in every first query), <code>&lt;+&gt;</code> L1, <code>&lt;~&gt;</code> Hamming, and <code>&lt;%&gt;</code> Jaccard on bit vectors. Hosted models increasingly emit quantized vectors directly: Cohere's embed endpoint and Voyage's both return int8 and binary alongside float, so the corpus can be stored small without a calibration step of your own.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Try it.</strong> Above, set precision to <em>binary</em> and read recall@10: it drops into the low nineties. Turn on <em>rescoring</em> and it climbs back above 0.95 while the index memory bar stays 32× shorter. Then add Matryoshka truncation on top and see that the two savings multiply: a 256-of-1,024 binary vector is 32 bytes, 128× smaller than where you started.</div>" },

    { title: "In the Real World: Models, Dimensions, Context Lengths, and Prices",
      html: "<p>The table every team needs and nobody keeps current. These figures were read from the vendors' documentation and pricing pages on 3 September 2026; treat the prices as a snapshot with a date, and the shapes (dimensions, context, output formats) as the durable part.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>OpenAI text-embedding-3-small</strong> — 1,536 d (shortenable via <code>dimensions</code>), 8,192-token input, MTEB 62.3, $0.02 per million tokens. Unit-normalized.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>OpenAI text-embedding-3-large</strong> — 3,072 d (shortenable; 256 d still beats ada-002), 8,192 tokens, MTEB 64.6, $0.13 per million. Unit-normalized.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Voyage voyage-4 / -4-large / -4-lite</strong> — 1,024 d default with 256, 512, 2,048; 32K-token input; float, int8, uint8, binary, ubinary outputs; $0.06 / $0.12 / $0.02 per million, first 200 million tokens free. The 4-series vectors are documented as compatible with each other, so a lite-embedded corpus can be queried with the large model.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Cohere embed-v4.0</strong> — 1,536 d default with 256, 512, 1,024; 128K-token input; float, int8, uint8, binary, ubinary; distinct <code>input_type</code> values for documents and queries. Token pricing is behind the dashboard, so it is not quoted here.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>BAAI bge-base-en-v1.5</strong> (open weights) — 768 d, 512-token input, MTEB 63.55; a fixed instruction is prepended to queries for retrieval. bge-large is 1,024 d.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>nomic-embed-text-v1.5</strong> (open weights) — 768 d, Matryoshka to 64; 8,192 tokens; requires <code>search_query:</code> / <code>search_document:</code> prefixes; MTEB 62.28.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>intfloat e5-base-v2</strong> (open weights) — 768 d, 512 tokens; <code>query:</code> / <code>passage:</code> prefixes are part of the model's contract.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</strong> (open weights) — 384 d, truncates input at 256 word pieces, trained on a billion pairs; the fast baseline that runs on a laptop CPU, and the model PocketRAG's first index uses.</li></ul>" +
            "<h3>Reading the table like an engineer</h3>" +
            "<p><em>Context length is a chunking constraint.</em> A 512-token model silently truncates a 900-token chunk and embeds only its first half; Chapter 5's chunk size must sit under the embedding model's window, and Chapter 8's upgrade procedure re-counts when the tokenizer changes. <em>Prefixes are part of the model.</em> E5, BGE, nomic, and Cohere all embed a query differently from a passage; omit the prefix and you are using a slightly different, untested model. <em>The same model, forever, or a migration.</em> A corpus embedded with 3-small cannot be queried with 3-large, and Azure's model page says it plainly: you cannot upgrade between embedding models, you re-embed. Voyage's within-series compatibility is the exception that proves how unusual it is. <em>MTEB is a screening tool.</em> Muennighoff and colleagues built it in 2022 from 8 task types and 58 datasets in 112 languages, and their own headline finding was that no model dominated across tasks. A two-point MTEB gap is real on average and may be zero or inverted on Larkspur's tickets; the decision is made on your labelled questions (Chapter 25), and Chapter 8 gives the procedure.</p>" +
            "<p><em>Price the whole corpus, then the query stream.</em> Larkspur's 600,000 tokens cost 8 cents to embed with 3-large once; ten thousand questions a day at 30 tokens each is 300,000 tokens, 4 cents a day. Embedding is not where RAG's money goes; Chapter 27 shows where it does. The exception is re-embedding a corpus of a billion tokens after a model change, $130 at 3-large's price and a day of throughput, which is a migration with a budget line, not a config change.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"Cosine or dot product?\" is asked to see whether you know they are the same thing on unit vectors and different things otherwise, and that the model's training decides which is meaningful. \"Can I compare two embedding models' similarity scores?\" tests Section 3: no, scores live in one model's coordinate system, thresholds must be re-calibrated. \"How would you cut vector memory by 30× without retraining?\" wants binary quantization with rescoring, and the strong answer adds Matryoshka truncation and the recall you would measure before shipping.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol><li><strong>Trace.</strong> With q = (1, 1, 0, 0) from Section 2, invent a fourth document vector <em>e</em> that ranks first under L2 but not first under cosine, and show the numbers. What you should find: a vector close to q in position but tilted off its direction, such as (0.9, 0.9, 0.4, 0.4): L2 0.58 beats b's 0.71, while its cosine 0.91 loses to a's 1.00. Then prove to yourself that no vector within L2 distance 0.71 of q can have a cosine below 0.87.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Embed 500 random Larkspur-style sentences (or any corpus) with a raw base model (bert-base-uncased, mean-pooled) and with a retrieval model (bge-base-en-v1.5). Compute the mean pairwise cosine for each. What you should find: the raw model's mean sits high, often above 0.6, the retrieval model's sits near zero or slightly above; this is the anisotropy cone of Section 3 measured on your own machine.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Take the labelled questions from Chapter 5's last exercise. Embed the corpus with nomic-embed-text-v1.5 at 768, 256, 128, and 64 dimensions (re-normalizing after each truncation) and record recall@10 for each. Then repeat with a non-Matryoshka model truncated the same way. What you should find: the Matryoshka curve declines gently, the conventional model's collapses below 256; and your own knee tells you what dimension to store.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Implement scalar int8 and binary quantization for a set of float32 vectors in under forty lines: per-dimension min/max calibration on a sample, quantize, and a search that compares against exact search. Then add rescoring (over-fetch 4×, re-score with float32). Report recall@10 for int8, binary, and binary + rescoring. What you should find: numbers within a few points of the Hugging Face study's, and the rescoring gap closing most of binary's loss.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> In Postgres with pgvector, load your Chapter 5 chunks as a <code>halfvec</code> column, add a generated <code>bit</code> column via <code>binary_quantize</code>, and write the two-pass query from Section 5. Time it against the single-pass cosine query at 10,000 and 100,000 rows with <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>. What you should find: the two-pass query wins once the table no longer fits in the buffer cache, and loses or ties before then. Keep the schema: PocketRAG's honest benchmark in Chapter 36 runs against it.</li></ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "one-hot encoding", d: "A vocabulary-length vector with a single 1 in the word's slot, under which every two distinct words are orthogonal and equally dissimilar." },
    { t: "dense embedding", d: "A short real-valued vector learned so that texts used or answered similarly land near each other; its dimensions have no individual meaning." },
    { t: "sentence embedding", d: "One vector for a whole passage, produced by pooling a transformer's per-token vectors, usually by taking their mean." },
    { t: "pooling", d: "The step that collapses per-token vectors into one vector per input, by mean, by a special token, or by a learned function." },
    { t: "dot product", d: "The sum of elementwise products of two vectors; it grows with both alignment and vector length." },
    { t: "cosine similarity", d: "The dot product divided by both vector lengths, equal to the cosine of the angle between them and ranging from −1 to 1." },
    { t: "Euclidean (L2) distance", d: "The straight-line distance between two points, the square root of the sum of squared coordinate differences." },
    { t: "L2 normalization", d: "Dividing a vector by its length so it has length 1, after which dot product equals cosine and L2 distance ranks identically." },
    { t: "anisotropy", d: "The tendency of raw transformer vectors to occupy a narrow cone of directions, so that random pairs have high cosine similarity." },
    { t: "whitening", d: "A post-hoc transform that centres vectors and rescales them to unit covariance, spreading an anisotropic cone over the sphere." },
    { t: "hubness", d: "In high dimensions, the skew by which a few points appear in a disproportionate number of other points' nearest-neighbour lists." },
    { t: "similarity threshold", d: "A cutoff score below which a match is rejected; meaningful only within one model and calibrated on labelled data." },
    { t: "Matryoshka Representation Learning", d: "Training an embedding so that every prefix of the vector is itself a usable embedding, coarse information first, enabling truncation without retraining." },
    { t: "truncation", d: "Keeping only the first n dimensions of a Matryoshka vector, followed by re-normalization." },
    { t: "scalar quantization", d: "Storing each dimension as an 8-bit integer over a calibrated per-dimension range, cutting memory fourfold." },
    { t: "binary quantization", d: "Storing each dimension as one bit (positive or not), cutting memory 32-fold and replacing cosine with Hamming distance." },
    { t: "Hamming distance", d: "The number of bit positions at which two binary vectors differ, computed with a population count." },
    { t: "rescoring", d: "Over-fetching candidates from a compressed index and re-ranking them with full-precision vectors before returning the top k." },
    { t: "product quantization", d: "Splitting a vector into sub-vectors and storing each as a one-byte index into a learned codebook of centroids; the subject of Chapter 12." },
    { t: "MTEB", d: "The Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (Muennighoff et al. 2022), 8 task types over 58 datasets in 112 languages, on which no single model dominates." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "A model card says the model was trained for cosine similarity. A team stores raw (unnormalized) vectors in pgvector and queries with the <#> operator. What happens?",
      opts: ["Nothing; cosine, dot product and L2 all induce the same ranking over a fixed set of vectors, so the operator is a performance detail", "Long passages with large-norm vectors are systematically favoured, and the operator's negative sign means ascending order is still most-similar-first, so the bug is silent", "The query errors out, because pgvector's <#> operator rejects vectors that are not unit length when the index is built and the insert fails first", "Recall is unchanged but latency roughly doubles, because the unnormalized vectors force a rescoring pass over the candidates"], a: 1,
      why: "Dot product equals cosine only on unit vectors. On raw vectors it rewards length, which the model did not train to be meaningful. pgvector's <#> is the negative inner product, so the query runs and returns plausible-looking results." },
    { type: "mc", q: "For q = (1,1,0,0), a = (2,2,0,0), b = (1,0.5,0.5,0), c = (3,0,0,3), which statement is correct?",
      opts: ["Cosine ranks c first because it is longest", "L2 ranks a first because it shares q's direction", "Dot ranks a, c, b; cosine ranks a, b, c; L2 ranks b, a, c", "All three agree once you compute them carefully"], a: 2,
      why: "Dot: 4, 3, 1.5. Cosine: 1.00, 0.87, 0.50. L2: 1.41, 0.71, 3.74. Length, angle, and position are three different questions." },
    { type: "blank", q: "After L2 normalization, the squared Euclidean distance between two vectors equals 2 minus twice their ____.", a: ["cosine", "cosine similarity", "dot product"],
      why: "|q − d|² = |q|² + |d|² − 2 q·d = 2 − 2 cos on unit vectors, so L2 and cosine produce the same ranking." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Two sentences about unrelated topics score a cosine of 0.85 under a raw BERT model. What is the most likely explanation?",
      opts: ["Anisotropy: raw transformer vectors occupy a narrow cone, so random pairs score high; the model was never trained to spread them", "The two sentences share a rare word, and a single low-frequency token dominates the pooled vector enough to pull the two of them together in the space", "The tokenizer merged the two sentences into one sequence, so a single vector was produced for both of them at once", "BERT's CLS vector is always normalized to point in the same direction, so cosines between any two are high"], a: 0,
      why: "Ethayarajh 2019 measured this across ELMo, BERT, and GPT-2. Contrastive fine-tuning or whitening is required before cosines mean anything." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A team migrates from bge-base to text-embedding-3-large and keeps the 0.72 similarity threshold that gated refusals. What should they expect?",
      opts: ["The threshold transfers unchanged, because both models score with cosine and cosine is bounded in the same range for either of them, so 0.72 means the same thing under both", "Only the dimension changed, so the threshold scales by the ratio 3072/768 and the refusal gate keeps behaving as it did on the same questions before the migration", "Refusals will disappear, because the larger model scores every pair more confidently and few questions will fall under the gate any more", "The threshold is meaningless in the new model's coordinate system; the refusal rate will shift unpredictably until it is re-calibrated on labelled questions"], a: 3,
      why: "Similarity has no absolute scale across models; BGE's own card says only relative order matters. Calibrate per model, on your own data, and again after every change." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A boilerplate paragraph (\"This policy is subject to change without notice\") appears in the top 10 for a third of all queries. What is happening and what fixes it?",
      opts: ["The paragraph is genuinely relevant to those queries; raise k so the real answers come back alongside it instead of being pushed out of the top ten by it", "It is a hub, a generic point near the centre of the distribution that is everyone's neighbour; dedupe it at ingest and rerank with a cross-encoder", "The embedding model is broken and places generic text too near everything; switch vendors and re-embed the whole corpus with a better one, which fixes it", "Cosine similarity is the wrong metric for this corpus; switch to L2 distance, under which the generic paragraph stops being close to everything at once"], a: 1,
      why: "Radovanović et al. 2010: hubness is a property of high-dimensional geometry. Generic chunks become hubs; ingest-time dedupe, reranking, and measurement are the remedies." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the steps of shrinking a stored corpus with Matryoshka truncation:", items: ["embed with a Matryoshka-trained model at full dimension", "keep the first n dimensions of each vector", "re-normalize each truncated vector to length 1", "measure recall@k against the full-dimension index on labelled questions"],
      why: "The prefix of a unit vector is not a unit vector, so re-normalization must follow truncation, and the recall you gave up must be measured, not assumed." },
    { type: "mc", q: "You need to cut a 1,024-dimensional float32 index from 4 GB to under 200 MB per million vectors without retraining and with recall above 0.95. Which plan does it?",
      opts: ["float16 storage, which halves the index to 2 GB per million vectors with almost no measurable recall loss at all", "int8 scalar quantization, which cuts each vector to 1,024 bytes and keeps recall near full precision", "Binary quantization (128 bytes per vector) with a rescoring pass over 4× the requested results using full-precision vectors", "Truncate to 512 dimensions with Matryoshka nesting, which halves the index size once more"], a: 2,
      why: "Only binary gets under 200 MB (128 MB). Alone it retains about 92 percent of quality; with rescoring about 96 percent, per the 2024 Hugging Face measurements. float16 and int8 land at 2 GB and 1 GB." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A 900-token chunk is embedded with a model whose input limit is 512 tokens and no error is raised. What was embedded?",
      opts: ["The whole chunk, compressed by the model down into its 512-token window before the vector is produced at all", "A random 512-token window sampled from somewhere inside the chunk on each call", "The chunk was rejected and a vector of zeros was stored in its place instead", "The first 512 tokens; the rest was silently truncated, so a fact in the last third is unfindable"], a: 3,
      why: "Embedding models truncate at their window. Chapter 5's chunk size must sit under the embedding model's limit, counted in that model's tokens." },
    { type: "mc", q: "An interviewer asks whether embeddings from text-embedding-3-small can be searched with a query embedded by text-embedding-3-large, \"since both are OpenAI and both use cosine\". The right answer?",
      opts: ["No; they are different coordinate systems, and the vendor's own documentation says you cannot upgrade between embedding models without re-embedding", "Yes; both models return unit-normalized vectors, so cosine is well defined between any pair of them and the resulting ranking still holds across the two models", "Yes, provided the small model's vector is padded out to 3,072 dimensions so the two sit in a space of the same width before any comparison is made", "Only if both are truncated to 256 dimensions with the dimensions parameter, which puts them on a shared prefix they agree on"], a: 0,
      why: "A vector's coordinates mean nothing outside the model that produced them. Voyage's documented within-series compatibility is the rare exception, and it is an explicit design decision, not a property of embeddings." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Which of these is a durable fact about a hosted embedding model, as opposed to a snapshot that will be stale in a year?",
      opts: ["Its price per million tokens, which the vendor publishes on its pricing page and holds steady across releases of the model", "Its rank on the MTEB leaderboard, which reflects performance across a broad set of retrieval tasks and is stable over time", "Whether it applies a different prefix or input type to queries than to documents, and its input token limit", "The name of its largest competitor, which tells you which model to benchmark it against on your own data first"], a: 2,
      why: "Prices and leaderboard positions move monthly. The model's contract, prefixes, dimension options, context window, and output formats, is what your code depends on." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 8 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): Karpukhin et al. 2020, DPR (arXiv
   2004.04906, EMNLP: dual encoder, in-batch negatives, BM25 hard negatives,
   top-20 accuracy 65.2% vs BM25 59.1% on NQ, 9–19 points across datasets);
   van den Oord, Li & Vinyals 2018, CPC (arXiv 1807.03748: InfoNCE); Reimers &
   Gurevych 2019, Sentence-BERT (siamese/triplet objectives, mean pooling);
   Qu et al. 2021, RocketQA (arXiv 2010.08191, NAACL: cross-batch negatives,
   denoised hard negatives via cross-encoder, augmentation); Wang et al. 2022,
   E5 (arXiv 2212.03533 html: 1.3B → ~270M CCPairs by consistency filtering,
   batch 32,768, temperature 0.01, query:/passage: prefixes, stage-2 NLI +
   MS MARCO + NQ with mined negatives and cross-encoder distillation); Xiao et al.
   2023, C-Pack/BGE (arXiv 2309.07597 html: RetroMAE pre-training, 100M unlabeled
   pairs at batch 19,200, 838,465 labeled pairs with one ANN-mined hard negative
   each, instruction on queries); Dai et al. 2022, Promptagator (arXiv 2209.11755:
   ≤8 examples, LLM query generation, round-trip filtering, +1.2 nDCG over
   ColBERTv2 on 11 BEIR sets); Wang, Thakur, Reimers & Gurevych 2022, GPL (arXiv
   2112.07577, NAACL: query generation + negative mining + cross-encoder margin-MSE
   pseudo-labels, up to +9.3 nDCG@10); Muennighoff et al. 2022, MTEB; Enevoldsen
   et al. 2025, MMTEB (arXiv 2502.13595, ICLR: 500+ tasks, 250+ languages,
   multilingual-e5-large-instruct at 560M params best public model); Sentence
   Transformers loss docs (MultipleNegativesRankingLoss scale 20, cosine, larger
   batches better; CachedMultipleNegativesRankingLoss / GradCache); Azure OpenAI
   model docs ("you can't upgrade between embedding models"); Gao et al. 2024
   survey §III.C (fine-tuning, LM-supervised retrievers, adapters). */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 8, part: 2, title: "How Embedding Models Learn: Bi-Encoders, Contrastive Loss, and Choosing One",
  blurb: "The model that made your vectors was trained by one pressure applied billions of times: pull a question toward the passage that answers it, push it away from every other passage in the batch. Once you have seen that loss written down, the folklore becomes mechanism. Why batch size is a quality lever. Why a model that has never seen the word PNR files it next to nothing useful. Why hard negatives make or break a domain model and why they also poison it. Why E5 wants \"query:\" in front of your question. You will train a toy bi-encoder and watch points move, then choose a model for Larkspur from a labelled set of your own questions, and upgrade it in production without downtime.",
  sections: [
    { title: "The Problem: A General Model Does Not Know That PNR Means Booking Reference",
      html: "<p>Larkspur's support tickets say PNR, the six-character record locator every airline uses. Larkspur's policy pages, written for customers, say booking reference. A customer asks, \"can I change the name on my booking reference?\" and the chunk that answers it, from an agent-facing procedure, says \"name corrections on a PNR are permitted once, free, within 24 hours of ticketing\". Embed both with a strong general model and the cosine is low. The model has seen \"PNR\" in a few thousand web pages, mostly about airlines, and \"booking reference\" in a few hundred thousand, mostly about hotels and restaurants. Nothing in its training pushed those two strings together, because nothing in its training data said they were the same thing.</p>" +
            "<p>This is the general shape of the domain gap. An embedding model knows what its training pairs taught it: that a Reddit title goes with its post, a paper's title with its abstract, a search query with the page that was clicked. Jargon that is rare on the open web, internal product names, codes, abbreviations, and the specific way your customers misname your features, are outside that lesson. Chapter 7 told you similarity lives in the model's coordinate system; this chapter is about where that coordinate system came from, so that you can predict what it will get wrong and fix it.</p>" +
            "<h3>An embedding model is not a language model</h3>" +
            "<p>It is built from one, usually. BERT, or a small decoder, is the starting point, and its pre-training on next-token or masked-token prediction is where the model learns that \"refund\" and \"reimbursement\" appear in the same kinds of sentences. But a language model's hidden states are the anisotropic cone of Chapter 7, and a language model can only compare two texts by reading them together. A retrieval model must produce one vector per text, alone, so that the corpus can be embedded once and searched by arithmetic. Reimers and Gurevych's distinction from Sentence-BERT is the one the whole field uses.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='190' y='24' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>bi-encoder (embedding model)</text>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'><rect x='40' y='50' width='130' height='40' rx='8'/><rect x='210' y='50' width='130' height='40' rx='8'/><rect x='40' y='110' width='130' height='36' rx='8'/><rect x='210' y='110' width='130' height='36' rx='8'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'><text x='105' y='74'>query text</text><text x='275' y='74'>passage text</text><text x='105' y='132'>encoder → vec q</text><text x='275' y='132'>encoder → vec p</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='105' y1='90' x2='105' y2='110' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='275' y1='90' x2='275' y2='110' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<rect x='120' y='170' width='140' height='34' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='190' y='192' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>score = q · p</text>" +
            "<line x1='105' y1='146' x2='160' y2='170' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='275' y1='146' x2='220' y2='170' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='190' y='222' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>passages encoded offline; compare by arithmetic</text>" +
            "<line x1='380' y1='30' x2='380' y2='215' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "<text x='570' y='24' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>cross-encoder (reranker, Chapter 16)</text>" +
            "<rect x='440' y='50' width='260' height='40' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/><text x='570' y='74' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>[CLS] query [SEP] passage [SEP]</text>" +
            "<rect x='470' y='110' width='200' height='36' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/><text x='570' y='132' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>one encoder, joint attention</text>" +
            "<line x1='570' y1='90' x2='570' y2='110' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<rect x='500' y='170' width='140' height='34' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='570' y='192' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>score = f(pair)</text>" +
            "<line x1='570' y1='146' x2='570' y2='170' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='570' y='222' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>one pass per (query, passage) pair; nothing to index</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F8.1. The bi-encoder never lets the query and the passage see each other; that is what makes the corpus indexable and what limits the match to whatever the training pressure put into the geometry.</div></div>" +
            "<p>The cost of that independence is the whole reason this chapter exists. A cross-encoder can notice that \"PNR\" in the passage and \"booking reference\" in the query refer to the same thing, because attention runs across both texts at once. A bi-encoder has to have already placed \"PNR\" near \"booking reference\" in its space, before either text arrived, or the match is lost. Training is how it gets placed.</p>" },

    { title: "The Bi-Encoder and the Contrastive Objective: InfoNCE and In-Batch Negatives",
      html: "<p>Start with the naive objective. You have pairs (query, passage that answers it). Train the encoder to make each pair's cosine high. It works for one step and then collapses: the encoder learns to map <em>every</em> text to the same vector, and every pair's cosine is 1. A loss that only rewards closeness has a trivial solution. The fix is to demand not only closeness but <em>closer than the alternatives</em>: for each query, the right passage must score higher than a set of wrong ones, the <strong>negatives</strong>. This is the <strong>contrastive objective</strong>, and the specific loss nearly every retrieval model uses is <strong>InfoNCE</strong>, introduced by Aaron van den Oord and colleagues at DeepMind in 2018 for a different purpose (predicting future audio frames) and adopted wholesale.</p>" +
            "<h3>The loss, written out</h3>" +
            "<p>For a query <em>q</em> with its positive passage <em>p⁺</em> and negatives <em>p₁ … pₙ</em>, compute every similarity, divide each by a <strong>temperature</strong> τ, and take a softmax over the positive and negatives together. The loss is minus the log of the probability assigned to the positive:</p>" +
            "<pre><code>L = −log  exp(sim(q, p⁺)/τ) / Σⱼ exp(sim(q, pⱼ)/τ)      (j ranges over p⁺ and all negatives)</code></pre>" +
            "<p>It is a classification loss: which of these n+1 passages is the real one? Minimising it pushes the positive's similarity up and every negative's down, with a gradient on each negative proportional to how much probability the model currently gives it. A negative the model already scores low contributes almost nothing; a negative the model confuses with the positive contributes almost everything. Hold on to that sentence, it is the whole hard-negative story.</p>" +
            "<h3>In-batch negatives: where the negatives come from for free</h3>" +
            "<p>Where do you get negatives? The 2020 Dense Passage Retrieval paper from Vladimir Karpukhin and colleagues at Facebook, the paper that made bi-encoders the default for open-domain question answering, used the trick that every model since relies on. Take a batch of B (query, positive) pairs and embed all 2B texts. Every query's positive is its own passage; every <em>other</em> query's passage in the batch is a negative for it. One B × B matrix of dot products, and the loss is a softmax along each row against the diagonal. B queries get B − 1 negatives each, and the encoder ran only 2B times. These are <strong>in-batch negatives</strong>.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>similarity matrix for a batch of 4 (rows: queries, cols: passages)</text>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5' text-anchor='middle'><text x='150' y='50'>p1 refund-elig</text><text x='260' y='50'>p2 24h rule</text><text x='370' y='50'>p3 cello</text><text x='480' y='50'>p4 pets</text></g>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5' text-anchor='end'><text x='92' y='84'>q1 money back?</text><text x='92' y='124'>q2 cancel now?</text><text x='92' y='164'>q3 bring cello?</text><text x='92' y='204'>q4 dog in cabin?</text></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5' fill='#111a2e'><rect x='100' y='64' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='210' y='64' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='320' y='64' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='430' y='64' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='100' y='104' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='210' y='104' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='320' y='104' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='430' y='104' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='100' y='144' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='210' y='144' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='320' y='144' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='430' y='144' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='100' y='184' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='210' y='184' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='320' y='184' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='430' y='184' width='100' height='32'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='none' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2.5'><rect x='100' y='64' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='210' y='104' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='320' y='144' width='100' height='32'/><rect x='430' y='184' width='100' height='32'/></g>" +
            "<rect x='210' y='64' width='100' height='32' fill='none' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2.5'/><rect x='100' y='104' width='100' height='32' fill='none' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2.5'/>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10.5' text-anchor='middle'><text x='150' y='85'>0.90</text><text x='260' y='85'>0.88</text><text x='370' y='85'>0.30</text><text x='480' y='85'>0.10</text><text x='150' y='125'>0.86</text><text x='260' y='125'>0.91</text><text x='370' y='125'>0.25</text><text x='480' y='125'>0.12</text><text x='150' y='165'>0.20</text><text x='260' y='165'>0.18</text><text x='370' y='165'>0.92</text><text x='260' y='205'>0.15</text><text x='150' y='205'>0.11</text><text x='370' y='205'>0.40</text><text x='480' y='165'>0.35</text><text x='480' y='205'>0.89</text></g>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5'><text x='560' y='84' fill='#3ddc97'>diagonal = positives</text><text x='560' y='124' fill='#ff5d73'>off-diagonal in a row</text><text x='560' y='138' fill='#ff5d73'>= in-batch negatives</text><text x='560' y='170' fill='#ffd166'>q1 vs p2: 0.88, nearly</text><text x='560' y='184' fill='#ffd166'>the positive's 0.90.</text><text x='560' y='198' fill='#ffd166'>That cell gets the gradient.</text></g>" +
            "<text x='20' y='240' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>loss per row = −log softmax(row / τ)[diagonal]; E5's batch of 32,768 gives 32,767 negatives per query</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F8.2. In-batch negatives are the other rows' passages. Random ones (cello vs pets) are already far apart and teach nothing; the two refund passages are the lesson.</div></div>" +
            "" },

    { title: "Temperature, Batch Size, and Hard Negatives", viz: "contrastiveTrain", vizTitle: "Train a toy bi-encoder: watch points move under contrastive loss, then enable hard negatives",
      html: "<h3>A worked row</h3>" +
            "<p>Take q1's row: 0.90 for its positive, then 0.88, 0.30, 0.10. With τ = 1 the softmax over the raw cosines is flat: e<sup>0.90</sup> = 2.46 against a total of 2.46 + 2.41 + 1.35 + 1.11 = 7.33, so the positive gets probability 0.34 and the loss is −ln 0.34 = 1.09, even though the positive already ranks first. A cosine difference of 0.02 barely moves a softmax at τ = 1. Now set τ = 0.05, which is Sentence Transformers' default (it calls it a scale of 20): the logits become 18.0, 17.6, 6.0, 2.0. The two far negatives vanish (e<sup>−12</sup>, e<sup>−16</sup>), and the positive's probability is 1 / (1 + e<sup>−0.4</sup>) = 0.60, loss 0.51. The whole gradient now lands on the one confusable cell, p2, pushing q1 away from the 24-hour rule and toward refund eligibility. E5 trains at τ = 0.01, sharper still. <strong>Temperature</strong> decides how much the loss cares about the near misses versus the easy negatives; too high and training is slow and mushy, too low and a single mislabelled pair dominates the batch.</p>" +
            "<p>Batch size is therefore not an engineering detail. With B = 32, each query sees 31 negatives, nearly all of them easy (cello versus pets). With B = 32,768, it sees 32,767, and a few are always confusable. E5 reports its batch size in the method section for that reason, BGE's paper shows retrieval quality rising from batch 256 to 2,048 to 19,200, and the Sentence Transformers documentation says flatly that larger batches perform better. Since a batch of 32,000 does not fit on one GPU, the library ships a cached variant of the loss (GradCache) that computes the big similarity matrix in chunks and reconstructs the gradient, so you can train at a batch of thousands on a single card.</p>" +
            "<h3>Hard negatives, and the false-negative trap</h3>" +
            "<p>Random in-batch negatives eventually run dry: once the model can tell refunds from pets, most cells contribute nothing. DPR's second trick was to add, per query, one <strong>hard negative</strong>: a passage that BM25 ranks highly for the query but that does not contain the answer. Lexically similar, semantically wrong, exactly the cell the loss wants. DPR reported top-20 retrieval accuracy of 65.2 percent on Natural Questions against 59.1 for BM25, with gains of 9 to 19 points across datasets, and the hard negatives were part of that. BGE mines one hard negative per labelled pair with approximate nearest-neighbour search on the model itself; every modern recipe does something like it.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='190' y='22' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>random negatives only</text>" +
            "<circle cx='150' cy='90' r='6' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='150' y='78' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>q1</text>" +
            "<circle cx='175' cy='105' r='5' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='190' y='109' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>p1 refund-elig (+)</text>" +
            "<circle cx='165' cy='118' r='5' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='180' y='130' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>p2 24h rule (never separated)</text>" +
            "<circle cx='300' cy='150' r='5' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='300' y='170' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>p3, p4: already far, gradient ≈ 0</text>" +
            "<line x1='380' y1='30' x2='380' y2='185' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "<text x='570' y='22' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>with a mined hard negative</text>" +
            "<circle cx='520' cy='90' r='6' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='520' y='78' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>q1</text>" +
            "<circle cx='540' cy='100' r='5' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='552' y='98' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>p1 (+)</text>" +
            "<circle cx='640' cy='150' r='5' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='600' y='170' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>p2 pushed away</text>" +
            "<line x1='548' y1='108' x2='632' y2='144' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='3 3'/>" +
            "<text x='570' y='192' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>gradient ∝ the negative's softmax probability</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F8.3. Easy negatives stop teaching once they are far. The confusable passage is the only one whose gradient is large, and a mined hard negative puts it in every batch.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Hard negatives have a failure mode that the machine above lets you produce. Mine \"passages BM25 ranks highly that are not labelled as the answer\" and some of them <em>are</em> the answer, unlabelled: a second passage that also says the 24-hour rule applies to Basic fares. Training then pushes the query away from a correct passage, and the model learns to distrust exactly the text it should find. These are <strong>false negatives</strong>. RocketQA, from Baidu in 2021, named the problem and gave the standard fix: score the mined negatives with a cross-encoder and drop the ones it thinks are positives (\"denoised hard negatives\"), and use negatives from other batches across GPUs (\"cross-batch negatives\") to make the effective batch larger. When you fine-tune on Larkspur in Section 5, this is the step teams skip and regret.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Try it.</strong> Press <em>run</em> in the machine above with hard negatives off. The cello and pet pairs separate within a few steps; the two refund passages, p1 and p2, drift together and the held-out recall@1 stalls near 0.8. Turn hard negatives on: within about forty steps p2 is pushed off q1 and recall@1 climbs. Then raise the temperature to 1.0 and watch training slow to a crawl, and lower it to 0.01 and watch one pair whipsaw the rest. Finally flip the false-negative toggle, which mislabels one duplicate passage, and watch the loss fall while recall on the held-out set gets worse.</div>" },

    { title: "Recipes That Matter: E5, BGE, Prefixes, and Pooling",
      html: "<p>The models you will actually deploy were trained in stages, and the stages explain their quirks. Two open recipes are documented in enough detail to learn from.</p>" +
            "<h3>E5 (Microsoft, 2022)</h3>" +
            "<p>Liang Wang and colleagues started from 1.3 billion weakly paired texts scraped from the web (Reddit post–comment, Wikipedia title–section, paper title–abstract, and so on) and filtered them to about 270 million by <strong>consistency filtering</strong>, a trick worth stealing: train a first model on the noisy set, then keep only pairs that model ranks highly against random passages, on the observation that networks memorise clean pairs before noisy ones. Stage one is InfoNCE with in-batch negatives at batch 32,768 and τ = 0.01. Stage two fine-tunes on labelled sets (natural-language inference, MS MARCO, Natural Questions) with mined hard negatives and distillation from a cross-encoder teacher. E5-base was the first model to beat BM25 on the BEIR benchmark without any labelled data, and after stage two matched models forty times its size on MTEB.</p>" +
            "<p>The prefixes come from stage one. Queries and passages are different kinds of text: short, interrogative, underspecified versus long, declarative, complete. E5 prepends <code>query: </code> to one side and <code>passage: </code> to the other during training, so the encoder can learn two slightly different mappings, one that places a question and one that places an answer, in the same space. Omit the prefix at inference and you are running a version of the model it was never trained as. That is the whole reason a model card insists on a prefix, and why BGE's retrieval instruction (\"Represent this sentence for searching relevant passages: \"), nomic's <code>search_query:</code>, and Cohere's <code>input_type</code> exist: they are the Chapter 6 alignment gap solved inside the model.</p>" +
            "<h3>BGE (BAAI, 2023)</h3>" +
            "<p>Shitao Xiao and colleagues added a stage before contrastive training. RetroMAE pre-training corrupts the input, encodes it to one vector, and trains a weak decoder to reconstruct the text from that vector alone, which forces the vector to carry the content rather than leaving it in per-token states. Then contrastive training on 100 million unlabelled pairs at batch 19,200, then fine-tuning on 838,465 labelled pairs with one ANN-mined hard negative each, with a task instruction on the query side. Three stages: make the vector carry meaning, spread the space, sharpen it on labelled relevance.</p>" +
            "<h3>Pooling and the small choices</h3>" +
            "<p>Whatever the recipe, the encoder emits one vector per token and something must make one vector per text. <strong>Mean pooling</strong>, averaging the token vectors under the attention mask, is Sentence-BERT's choice and MiniLM's, E5's, and nomic's. <strong>CLS pooling</strong> takes the first special token's vector, which the encoder learns to use as a summary slot; BGE uses it. Decoder-based embedding models, which have no bidirectional attention, typically take the last token's state or a mean, sometimes with a learned attention pooler. None of these is a decision you make at inference; the model card names it, the library applies it, and the only trap is loading a model with the wrong pooling head, which produces vectors that look valid and rank badly.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Loss</strong> — InfoNCE with in-batch negatives, plus mined hard negatives in the final stage; a cross-encoder teacher for distillation in the stronger recipes.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Temperature</strong> — 0.01 to 0.05 for retrieval models; Sentence Transformers' scale of 20 is τ = 0.05.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Batch</strong> — thousands to tens of thousands; the dominant lever after data quality.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Prefixes</strong> — part of the model's contract, not optional metadata.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Pooling</strong> — mean or CLS, fixed by the card; wrong pooling is a silent failure.</li></ul>" },

    { title: "Choosing a Model: What MTEB Tells You and What Your Own 200 Questions Tell You",
      html: "<p>Niklas Muennighoff and colleagues built MTEB in 2022 to answer \"which embedding model is best\" with data instead of marketing: 8 task types, 58 datasets, 112 languages, 33 models, and the conclusion that no model won everywhere. The 2025 successor, MMTEB, from Kenneth Enevoldsen and 84 co-authors, grew that to more than 500 tasks in more than 250 languages, added instruction following, long-document and code retrieval, and found the best public model was a 560-million-parameter one, multilingual-e5-large-instruct, ahead of embedding models built on multi-billion-parameter decoders. Two lessons before you open the leaderboard: parameter count is not quality, and the leaderboard's average is over tasks you do not have.</p>" +
            "<h3>Read the leaderboard by the retrieval column, then stop</h3>" +
            "<p>For RAG, only the retrieval task family matters; classification, clustering, and semantic textual similarity scores measure other geometry. Filter to retrieval, to your language, and to models whose context window covers your chunk size (Chapter 7's table). That leaves perhaps a dozen candidates, and among them the leaderboard cannot decide, because its retrieval datasets are Wikipedia questions, scientific claims, and forum posts, and yours are airline refund tickets. A two-point gap on BEIR-style averages is real on those corpora and is routinely zero or reversed on a specific one.</p>" +
            "<h3>The labelled retrieval set</h3>" +
            "<p>Chapter 5's last exercise had you write questions for your corpus; Chapter 25 makes the harness rigorous. Here is the minimum <strong>labelled retrieval set</strong> for choosing a model. Collect 50 to 200 real questions, from logs if you have them, from the people who will ask if you do not. For each, mark which chunk or chunks contain the answer, by hand, with two people on a sample so you know how often they disagree. Include the shapes Larkspur's five questions were chosen to cover: a rare word (Q1), a paraphrase (Q2), a table lookup (Q3), a two-hop (Q4), and something the corpus does not contain (Q5). Then, for each candidate model: embed the corpus, embed the questions with the model's query prefix, retrieve the top 10 by the model's declared metric, and compute <strong>recall@10</strong> (fraction of questions whose answer chunk appears in the top 10) and <strong>MRR</strong> (mean over questions of 1 / rank of the first answer chunk). Recall says whether the answer arrives at all; MRR says how much reranking and context packing will have to do.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>Larkspur, 180 labelled questions, top-10 by each model's metric (illustrative)</text>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'><text x='20' y='50'>model</text><text x='280' y='50'>dims</text><text x='340' y='50'>recall@10</text><text x='430' y='50'>MRR</text><text x='500' y='50'>Q1 cello</text><text x='590' y='50'>Q2 paraphrase</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='58' x2='740' y2='58' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='82'>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</text><text x='280' y='82'>384</text><text x='340' y='82'>0.71</text><text x='430' y='82'>0.48</text><text x='500' y='82' fill='#ff5d73'>miss</text><text x='590' y='82' fill='#ff5d73'>rank 7</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='110'>bge-base-en-v1.5</text><text x='280' y='110'>768</text><text x='340' y='110'>0.84</text><text x='430' y='110'>0.61</text><text x='500' y='110' fill='#3ddc97'>rank 2</text><text x='590' y='110' fill='#ffd166'>rank 4</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='138'>text-embedding-3-large</text><text x='280' y='138'>3072</text><text x='340' y='138'>0.87</text><text x='430' y='138'>0.66</text><text x='500' y='138' fill='#3ddc97'>rank 1</text><text x='590' y='138' fill='#ffd166'>rank 3</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='166' fill='#ffd166'>bge-base + Larkspur fine-tune (§5)</text><text x='280' y='166'>768</text><text x='340' y='166' fill='#ffd166'>0.91</text><text x='430' y='166' fill='#ffd166'>0.74</text><text x='500' y='166' fill='#3ddc97'>rank 1</text><text x='590' y='166' fill='#3ddc97'>rank 1</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='180' x2='740' y2='180' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='202' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>leaderboard order matched for the three general models; gap sizes and the fine-tune row only your set can show</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='220' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>180 questions: a recall gap under ~0.04 is noise; bootstrap an interval before believing it</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F8.4. A model-comparison table on a labelled set. The columns you add for your hardest question shapes are what make it a decision rather than a leaderboard copy.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Then decide with the other columns Chapter 7 gave you: dimension (memory per million), context window, price per million tokens, hosted or self-served, and whether the vendor lets you migrate within a series. A model that scores two points lower and runs on your own CPU at zero marginal cost is often the right answer; a model that scores two points higher and needs a 3,072-dimensional index for a hundred million chunks is often not. The table is the argument you bring to that meeting.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>The edge.</strong> Compare models on the <em>same</em> chunks, the same labels, the same k, and the same metric each model declares. The commonest mis-comparison is running model A on cosine and model B on dot product because that is what each vector store defaulted to, then attributing the difference to the model. The second commonest is evaluating without the query prefix. Both make the worse model look better.</div>" },

    { title: "Fine-Tuning on Your Domain: Synthetic Pairs, a Training Loop, and Whether It Is Worth the Re-Index",
      html: "<p>Back to PNR. No general model will place it next to booking reference, but a few thousand Larkspur pairs will, and Section 2 told you what a pair needs to be: a question and a passage that answers it, plus a hard negative that does not. The obstacle is that you have a corpus and no questions. Two 2022 papers solved that with <strong>synthetic query generation</strong>: a generator writes the questions.</p>" +
            "<h3>Synthetic queries: Promptagator and GPL</h3>" +
            "<p><strong>Promptagator</strong>, from Zhuyun Dai and colleagues at Google, prompts a large language model with as few as eight hand-written (question, passage) examples from the target domain and asks it to write a question for each corpus passage. The generated questions are noisy, so a round-trip filter keeps only those for which a retriever trained on the synthetic data ranks the source passage first. A bi-encoder trained on the survivors beat ColBERTv2, a far heavier model trained on MS MARCO, by more than 1.2 nDCG on average across eleven BEIR sets, with no human labels beyond the eight examples. <strong>Generative Pseudo Labeling</strong>, from Kexin Wang, Nils Reimers, and colleagues, is the same idea with a stronger teacher: generate queries, mine negatives with an existing retriever, then have a cross-encoder score every (query, positive, negative) triple and train the bi-encoder to reproduce the <em>margin</em> between them, which sidesteps the false-negative problem because a negative the cross-encoder thinks is positive gets a small margin rather than a hard push. GPL reported up to 9.3 nDCG@10 over the base model on six specialised domains.</p>" +
            "<p>For Larkspur the pipeline is: sample 5,000 chunks; for each, ask a generator to write two questions a customer or an agent would actually ask, with the eight examples as the prompt; mine three negatives per question with the current model; score triples with a cross-encoder (Chapter 16 has the candidates); keep pairs where the positive's score clearly exceeds the negatives'. The PNR pair appears because a ticket chunk that says PNR generates a question that says booking reference, since the generator, unlike the embedding model, knows they are the same.</p>" +
            "<h3>The training loop</h3>" +
            "<pre><code># Sentence Transformers, fine-tune a bi-encoder on (query, positive, hard_negative) triples\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer, SentenceTransformerTrainer, losses\nfrom datasets import Dataset\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5\")\ntrain = Dataset.from_dict({\n    \"anchor\":   [\"query: \" + q for q in queries],      # use the model's own prefix convention\n    \"positive\": positives,\n    \"negative\": hard_negatives,                        # cross-encoder-checked, not raw BM25 hits\n})\nloss = losses.CachedMultipleNegativesRankingLoss(model, scale=20.0, mini_batch_size=32)\n#      ^ InfoNCE with in-batch + the explicit negative; cached so batch_size can be large on one GPU\ntrainer = SentenceTransformerTrainer(\n    model=model, train_dataset=train, loss=loss,\n    args=dict(per_device_train_batch_size=1024, num_train_epochs=1, learning_rate=2e-5, warmup_ratio=0.1),\n)\ntrainer.train()\nmodel.save_pretrained(\"larkspur-bge-base-v1\")</code></pre>" +
            "<p>A few thousand triples, one epoch, a small learning rate with warm-up so the pre-trained geometry is adjusted rather than replaced, and a batch as large as the cached loss allows. Hold out a fifth of the labelled set from Section 4 and never train on it; that is the row F8.4 gains. Watch two numbers while training: recall@10 on the held-out set rising, and recall@10 on a <em>general</em> set (a BEIR slice) not falling far, because a model that learns Larkspur by forgetting English is worse at the paraphrase questions it used to get right. This forgetting is why the survey's alternatives exist: keep the base model frozen and train a small adapter, or an extra projection, on top; less gain, less risk, no re-embedding when the adapter changes if it sits on the query side only.</p>" +
            "<h3>Is it worth it?</h3>" +
            "<p>Fine-tuning changes the coordinate system, so every chunk must be re-embedded (Section 6) and every threshold re-calibrated (Chapter 7). Do it when the labelled set shows a gap that reranking does not close, when the corpus is stable enough that the training pairs stay representative, and when the vocabulary gap is real (codes, product names, jargon) rather than a chunking or parsing failure wearing an embedding costume, which Chapters 4 and 5 taught you to rule out first. Do not do it to chase two leaderboard points, and do not do it before you have a held-out set, because without one you cannot tell improvement from memorisation.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Sources differ.</strong> The 2024 RAG survey lists a family of \"LM-supervised\" retriever training methods, REPLUG's KL-divergence to the generator's preferences among them, and adapters that reshape retrieval to a frozen generator's taste. They are real and occasionally decisive, and Chapter 20 meets REPLUG in its own context. For a first domain fine-tune, the synthetic-pairs recipe above is the one with public code, published ablations, and a known failure mode; start there.</div>" },

    { title: "Upgrading the Model in Production: Dual-Write, Backfill, Cut-Over, and the Parity Test",
      html: "<p>You have chosen, or fine-tuned, a new model. Every stored vector is now in the wrong coordinate system, and the query path cannot switch until the corpus has. Azure's documentation for OpenAI's embedding models says it in one line: you cannot upgrade between embedding models, you generate new embeddings. Doing that without downtime is the same problem as any schema migration with a large backfill, and the same four-phase answer, the <strong>dual-write migration</strong>.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<line x1='40' y1='60' x2='720' y2='60' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='728,60 716,54 716,66' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='40' y='45' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>time</text>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='120' y1='50' x2='120' y2='70'/><line x1='330' y1='50' x2='330' y2='70'/><line x1='520' y1='50' x2='520' y2='70'/><line x1='650' y1='50' x2='650' y2='70'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'><text x='120' y='88'>1 dual-write on</text><text x='330' y='88'>2 backfill done</text><text x='520' y='88'>3 cut-over</text><text x='650' y='88'>4 drop old</text></g>" +
            "<rect x='40' y='110' width='480' height='22' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='280' y='125' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>queries served by OLD column / index</text>" +
            "<rect x='520' y='110' width='200' height='22' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='620' y='125' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>queries served by NEW</text>" +
            "<rect x='120' y='145' width='530' height='22' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='385' y='160' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>every new/changed chunk written to BOTH columns</text>" +
            "<rect x='120' y='180' width='210' height='22' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/><text x='225' y='195' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>backfill NEW, rate-limited</text>" +
            "<rect x='330' y='180' width='190' height='22' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/><text x='425' y='195' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>shadow-query NEW; parity test</text>" +
            "<text x='40' y='222' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>the cut-over is a flag flip; the rollback is the same flag; the old column stays until the new one has served a full week</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F8.5. Dual-write first, so the backfill has a fixed target; shadow-query before the flip, so the flip is a decision and not a hope.</div></div>" +
            "<ol><li><strong>Add the new column, dual-write.</strong> Add a second vector column (or a second collection) for the new model, with its own index. Change the ingest path so that every new or updated chunk is embedded by both models and written to both. From this moment the set of chunks missing a new vector only shrinks.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Backfill.</strong> Walk the corpus, oldest first, embedding with the new model and writing the new column, rate-limited to the vendor's throughput and your budget: Larkspur's 600,000 tokens are minutes and cents, a billion tokens are a day and a budget line. Chapter 4's content hash tells you which chunks are byte-identical and can share a vector, and which changed under you during the walk. Track \"chunks lacking a new vector\" as a metric and do not proceed until it is zero.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Shadow-query and the parity test.</strong> With both columns full, send every live query to both paths and log both result lists without serving the new one. Run the labelled set from Section 4 against the new column: recall@10 and MRR must meet or beat the old column's, on the same chunks. Then check the live shadow traffic for overlap: the fraction of top-10 results in common should sit where the labelled set predicts (a fine-tune will change it; a like-for-like upgrade should not much). Re-calibrate any similarity threshold on the new model now, because the old number is meaningless (Chapter 7). Chapter 25's harness is this test made permanent, and Chapter 29 puts it on a dashboard.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Cut over, then drop.</strong> Flip the query path to the new column behind a flag, keep dual-writing, and watch the metrics for a week of real traffic. The rollback is the same flag. Only then stop writing the old column and drop it; its index was the size of the corpus in RAM (Chapter 11), and holding two of them was the cost of doing this safely.</li></ol>" +
            "<p>What goes wrong: teams skip dual-write and backfill into a moving corpus, ending with a column that is always a few hundred chunks short; teams cut over on the leaderboard and discover the threshold shift as a spike in refusals; teams drop the old column the same day and lose the rollback. The procedure is dull on purpose. Chapter 29 collects it with the other migrations a RAG system will need, and PocketRAG in Chapter 36 runs it once end to end so you have done it before it is production.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"How does an embedding model get trained?\" wants: bi-encoder, InfoNCE, in-batch negatives, hard negatives, and the false-negative caveat; the strong answer names batch size and temperature as the levers. \"How would you pick a model?\" wants the labelled set, not the leaderboard. \"How would you change models in production?\" wants dual-write, backfill, parity test, flag flip, and the threshold re-calibration that everyone forgets.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol><li><strong>Trace.</strong> Using q1's row from F8.2 (0.90, 0.88, 0.30, 0.10), compute the InfoNCE loss at τ = 1, 0.1, 0.05, and 0.01, and the softmax probability the model assigns to p2 at each. What you should find: loss 1.09, 0.60, 0.51, and about 0.13; p2's probability rises from 0.33 to 0.45 at τ = 0.1, then falls to 0.40 and 0.12 as the 0.02 cosine gap becomes a logit gap of 2. The gradient on p2 is largest where its probability is largest, so a middling temperature is where the hard negative teaches most.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> In the machine, set batch size to 4 and hard negatives off, run 100 steps, and record held-out recall@1. Reset, set batch size to 12, and repeat. Then batch 4 with hard negatives on. What you should find: batch 12 beats batch 4 because more rows contain a confusable cell; batch 4 with hard negatives beats both, because every row now does.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Take 100 (question, chunk) pairs from your Chapter 5 labelled set. Embed the questions with e5-base-v2 twice, once with the <code>query:</code> prefix and once without, and the chunks with <code>passage:</code>. Compute recall@10 both ways. What you should find: a measurable drop without the prefix, larger on short questions; the model card was not being fussy.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Generate synthetic training data for your corpus: prompt a model with eight hand-written (question, chunk) examples, generate two questions per chunk for 1,000 chunks, mine three negatives each with bge-base, score triples with a cross-encoder (<code>cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2</code> is small enough for a laptop), and keep triples where the positive beats every negative by a margin. Report how many survived and inspect twenty rejected ones. What you should find: a survival rate between a third and two thirds, and among the rejects a visible share of false negatives, chunks that also answer the question.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Fine-tune bge-base-en-v1.5 on the survivors with the loop in Section 5, holding out a fifth of your labelled set. Report recall@10 and MRR before and after on the held-out fifth and on a 200-query BEIR slice. Then execute the migration of Section 6 against your Chapter 7 pgvector table: add a column, dual-write, backfill, shadow-query, and flip. What you should find: a gain on your set, a small loss or none on BEIR, and a migration that at no point served a query against a half-filled column. Keep the script; PocketRAG's Chapter 36 runs it.</li></ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "bi-encoder", d: "A model that embeds a query and a passage independently into the same space so they can be compared by a dot product, enabling an indexable corpus." },
    { t: "cross-encoder", d: "A model that reads a query and a passage together in one forward pass and outputs a relevance score, more accurate than a bi-encoder and not indexable." },
    { t: "contrastive objective", d: "A training goal that requires the positive passage to score higher than a set of negative passages, preventing the collapse a similarity-only loss allows." },
    { t: "InfoNCE", d: "The contrastive loss (van den Oord et al. 2018) that takes a temperature-scaled softmax over one positive and n negatives and minimises the negative log probability of the positive." },
    { t: "temperature", d: "The divisor applied to similarities before the softmax in InfoNCE; smaller values concentrate the loss on near-miss negatives." },
    { t: "in-batch negatives", d: "The other pairs' passages in a training batch, used as negatives for each query at no extra encoding cost (Karpukhin et al. 2020)." },
    { t: "hard negative", d: "A passage that is lexically or semantically close to the query but does not answer it, added to training because it carries most of the gradient." },
    { t: "false negative", d: "A mined hard negative that actually answers the query, which teaches the model to push away correct passages; removed by cross-encoder denoising (RocketQA)." },
    { t: "GradCache", d: "A technique for training with very large contrastive batches on limited memory by caching embeddings and recomputing gradients in chunks." },
    { t: "query prefix", d: "A fixed string (\"query: \", an instruction, or an input type) prepended during training and inference so the encoder maps queries and passages differently into one space." },
    { t: "mean pooling", d: "Producing a text vector by averaging the encoder's token vectors under the attention mask." },
    { t: "CLS pooling", d: "Producing a text vector from the encoder's first special token, which is trained to summarise the input." },
    { t: "RetroMAE", d: "A pre-training stage used by BGE in which a weak decoder must reconstruct corrupted text from a single vector, forcing that vector to carry content." },
    { t: "consistency filtering", d: "Keeping only training pairs that a model trained on the noisy data ranks highly, on the observation that clean pairs are learned first (E5)." },
    { t: "MMTEB", d: "The 2025 Massive Multilingual Text Embedding Benchmark of 500+ tasks in 250+ languages, whose best public model was a 560M-parameter multilingual E5." },
    { t: "labelled retrieval set", d: "A collection of real questions each mapped by hand to the chunks that answer them, used to compare models and detect regressions." },
    { t: "recall@10", d: "The fraction of questions for which an answer-bearing chunk appears among the top ten retrieved." },
    { t: "MRR", d: "Mean reciprocal rank: the average over questions of one divided by the rank of the first answer-bearing chunk." },
    { t: "synthetic query generation", d: "Using a language model to write questions for corpus passages to create training pairs without human labels (Promptagator, GPL)." },
    { t: "dual-write migration", d: "Changing embedding models by writing new chunks to both old and new vector columns, backfilling the new one, verifying parity, then flipping a flag." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "A team trains an embedding model with a loss that only maximises the cosine between each question and its answer passage. After a few hundred steps every cosine is 0.99 and retrieval is useless. What happened?",
      opts: ["The loss had a trivial solution: map every text to one vector; without negatives nothing prevents collapse", "The learning rate was too high, so the weights moved far enough to destroy the space in a few steps", "The batch size was too small for the GPU, so each step saw too few pairs to learn from", "Mean pooling averaged the token vectors until the differences between texts vanished"], a: 0,
      why: "A similarity-only objective is satisfied by collapse. The contrastive objective requires the positive to beat negatives, which is why InfoNCE has a denominator." },
    { type: "mc", q: "In an InfoNCE batch, which negative contributes most to the gradient for a given query?",
      opts: ["The one with the lowest similarity, because it sits furthest from the correct passage and so needs the largest correction applied by the loss", "All negatives in the batch contribute equally, since the loss treats them as one undifferentiated set of examples", "The one that appears earliest in the batch, because the softmax is computed in order as they arrive", "The one the model currently scores closest to the positive, because the gradient is proportional to its softmax probability"], a: 3,
      why: "The softmax gradient on each negative is its assigned probability. Far negatives get almost none, which is why random negatives stop teaching and hard negatives are mined." },
    { type: "blank", q: "Using the other pairs' passages in the same training batch as negatives for each query is called ____ negatives.", a: ["in-batch", "in batch", "inbatch"],
      why: "Karpukhin et al. 2020 (DPR). B queries get B − 1 negatives each from 2B encoder passes, which is why batch size is a quality lever." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Two models are compared on Larkspur's labelled set. Model A is run with its documented query prefix, model B without its documented prefix, and A wins by six points. What can you conclude?",
      opts: ["A is the better model for Larkspur, since it won on Larkspur's own labelled questions by six points", "B's prefix is unnecessary, because prefixes matter only during contrastive training and not at inference", "Nothing yet: B was evaluated as a model it was never trained to be; rerun B with its prefix", "A's advantage is the prefix itself, so add A's prefix to B and rerun the comparison"], a: 2,
      why: "Prefixes are part of the model's contract, learned in contrastive pre-training so queries and passages map differently. Omitting one is a silent misconfiguration." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A fine-tune mines hard negatives as \"top BM25 hits not labelled as answers\". Training loss falls smoothly but held-out recall gets worse. The most likely cause?",
      opts: ["Some mined negatives are unlabelled correct passages (false negatives), so the model is being trained to push queries away from right answers", "The temperature is too high, so the softmax flattens and every negative in the batch contributes the same small gradient, which stalls the ranking", "The learning rate warm-up was skipped, so the first steps moved the encoder before the projection head had settled, and it never recovered", "Mean pooling should have been CLS pooling, so the sentence vector is an average that blurs the distinctions the loss is trying to sharpen up"], a: 0,
      why: "RocketQA named this and fixed it by scoring mined negatives with a cross-encoder and dropping likely positives. GPL's margin-MSE distillation sidesteps it by design." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the stages of the BGE training recipe:", items: ["RetroMAE pre-training so one vector must reconstruct the text", "contrastive training on ~100M unlabelled pairs at batch 19,200", "fine-tuning on labelled pairs with one mined hard negative each and a query instruction"],
      why: "Make the vector carry content, spread the space with in-batch negatives at scale, then sharpen on labelled relevance with hard negatives." },
    { type: "mc", q: "MMTEB (2025) found the best public embedding model was a 560-million-parameter multilingual E5, ahead of models built on multi-billion-parameter decoders. What is the right lesson for choosing a model?",
      opts: ["Always pick the smallest model available, since the benchmark shows that size does not buy retrieval quality at any scale", "Multilingual models are always better, even on a single-language corpus, because the extra data regularises the space", "Decoder-based embedding models should be avoided, since the benchmark placed them behind an encoder", "Parameter count does not predict retrieval quality; training data and recipe do, and the decision still belongs to your own labelled set"], a: 3,
      why: "The benchmark averages over tasks you do not have. It narrows the candidates; the labelled set with your question shapes decides." },
    { type: "mc", q: "With 180 labelled questions, model A scores recall@10 of 0.85 and model B 0.83. A costs six times more. What does a careful engineer do?",
      opts: ["Choose A; it is measurably better on the labelled set, and two points of recall compounds over every question the system is asked in production", "Choose A and cut k from ten to five, which brings its cost back down to roughly what B would have cost", "Choose B; two points on 180 questions is inside the noise, and cost is a real column; bootstrap the interval to confirm", "Average the two models' vectors, which combines their strengths at the price of one more embedding call"], a: 2,
      why: "A 0.02 difference on 180 questions is about four questions. Resample the set to get an interval before treating the gap as real, and weigh dimension, context, and price alongside it." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Promptagator trains a domain retriever from as few as eight labelled examples. Where does the rest of the training data come from, and what keeps it clean?",
      opts: ["A language model writes questions for corpus passages, and a round-trip filter keeps only those whose source passage a retriever ranks first", "From MS MARCO, filtered down to those passages whose domain keywords overlap the target corpus, and used directly", "From the corpus's own section headings, used as questions against the passages beneath them during training of the retriever, with no filtering step", "From the labelled set, duplicated with paraphrases until there are enough pairs to train on usefully"], a: 0,
      why: "Dai et al. 2022. The generator supplies the pairs; consistency filtering removes the questions that do not actually retrieve their passage." },
    { type: "mc", q: "During a model migration, a team backfills the new vector column over three days, then flips the query path. Some queries now return chunks with no vector and are silently dropped. Which step was skipped?",
      opts: ["Shadow querying against both columns throughout the backfill, so the missing vectors would have shown up in the comparison before the flip", "Dropping the old column as soon as the backfill finished rather than waiting until after the flip was verified", "Re-calibrating the similarity threshold against the new model before any of the traffic was moved over", "Dual-writing new and updated chunks to both columns before starting the backfill, so the set of missing vectors could only shrink"], a: 3,
      why: "Backfilling a moving corpus without dual-write leaves a permanent gap of chunks written during the walk. Dual-write first gives the backfill a fixed target." },
    { type: "mc", q: "After a like-for-like model upgrade with a clean parity test, the refusal rate triples on day one. What was forgotten?",
      opts: ["The old vector column should have been dropped before the flip, so that no query could still read a stale vector from it on the new path", "The new model needs a larger k, because its scores are spread differently and the top ten now covers less of the relevant chunks than before", "The similarity threshold that gates refusal was calibrated on the old model's coordinate system and is meaningless on the new one", "The backfill ran in the wrong order, so the newest chunks were embedded last and missed the flip entirely"], a: 2,
      why: "Chapter 7's rule: similarity scores have no absolute scale across models. Every threshold is re-calibrated on the labelled set during shadow querying, before the flip." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 9 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): Robertson, Walker, Jones,
   Hancock-Beaulieu & Gatford 1994, Okapi at TREC-3, and Robertson & Zaragoza
   2009, "The Probabilistic Relevance Framework: BM25 and Beyond" (the formula,
   k1 in [1.2, 2.0], b = 0.75, BM25F); Lucene 9 BM25Similarity javadoc (defaults
   k1 = 1.2, b = 0.75, discountOverlaps = true, IDF = log(1 + (N − n + 0.5)/
   (n + 0.5)), norms encoded so smaller means longer); Elasticsearch similarity
   docs (BM25 default, same k1/b, discount_overlaps tunable); Elastic engineering
   blog, "Faster retrieval of top hits with block-max WAND" (Lucene 8.0 /
   Elasticsearch 7.0, Feb 2019; term queries 3–7×, disjunctions up to 15×;
   track_total_hits becomes a lower bound); Broder, Carmel, Herscovici, Soffer &
   Zien 2003, WAND (CIKM); Ding & Suel 2011, block-max indexes (SIGIR); PostgreSQL
   17 docs, "Controlling Text Search" (to_tsvector stemming/stopwords, ts_rank vs
   ts_rank_cd, normalization flags 0/1/2/4/8/16/32, setweight and the {D,C,B,A}
   default weights {0.1,0.2,0.4,1.0}, and the note that ranking is I/O bound and
   cannot be pushed into the index); Tantivy README (Rust, Lucene-inspired, BM25
   "same as Lucene", ~2× Lucene in their benchmark, Wikipedia indexed in under
   3 minutes); Thakur, Reimers, Rücklé, Srivastava & Gurevych 2021, BEIR (arXiv
   2104.08663: 18 datasets, 10 systems; BM25 a robust zero-shot baseline, dense
   retrievers generalize worst, rerankers and late interaction best but costliest);
   Formal, Piwowarski & Clinchant 2021, SPLADE (arXiv 2107.05720, SIGIR: sparsity
   regularization plus log saturation on term weights) and Formal, Lassance,
   Piwowarski & Clinchant 2021, SPLADE v2 (arXiv 2109.10086: max pooling,
   distillation, >9% nDCG@10 gain on TREC DL 2019); Elastic ELSER docs (learned
   term expansion into weighted tokens in a sparse_vector field, out-of-domain,
   English, first 512 tokens of a field); Nogueira & Lin 2019, "From doc2query to
   docTTTTTquery" (MS MARCO dev MRR@10: BM25 0.184 → 0.277 at 40 samples; latency
   55 ms → 64 ms; BM25 + BERT Large 0.375 at 3,500 ms). All worked BM25 numbers in
   this chapter were computed, not quoted. */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 9, part: 2, title: "The Inverted Index and BM25: Why Keywords Still Win",
  blurb: "Chapter 7 explained why a dense retriever misses the cello: the model never saw enough cellos to give the word a sharp place in its space. A data structure from before the web does not have that problem. The inverted index maps every term to the documents containing it, and BM25 scores those documents with two ideas that fit on an index card, rarity and saturation, plus one correction for length. You will build an index by typing documents, watch postings lists grow, slide k1 and b until the rankings flip, then meet the modern descendants that keep the index and learn the terms.",
  sections: [
    { title: "The Problem: The Cello, the PNR, and the Error Code",
      html: "<p>Larkspur Q1 asks whether a cello can travel in the cabin. The corpus contains one paragraph that answers it, in the musical-instruments section of the baggage policy, and the word <em>cello</em> appears in that paragraph and in two others across two hundred thousand chunks. Chapter 7 explained what a dense retriever does with it: a rare word contributes little to a pooled sentence vector, the vector lands in a neighbourhood about oversized baggage in general, and the paragraph that says cello ranks fourth behind three that do not. The information is not missing. The geometry cannot see it.</p>" +
            "<p>Now score the same corpus by a rule that has nothing to do with geometry: prefer documents containing the query's terms, and weight each term by how rare it is. In a corpus of two hundred thousand chunks where <em>cello</em> appears in twelve, the term is worth about 9.7 by the standard rarity measure of this chapter, while <em>seat</em>, in eight thousand chunks, is worth 3.2, and <em>the</em>, in all of them, is worth zero. The one paragraph that contains the rare term is pulled to the top by a factor of three over anything that merely mentions seats. No training, no vectors, no model that has to have seen enough cellos.</p>" +
            "<p>The pattern generalises past rare nouns, and it is why this chapter sits in the middle of a course about embeddings. Three classes of query are answered by exact terms and cannot be reliably answered by similarity:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Identifiers.</strong> A PNR (<code>K4T9RM</code>), an incident number (<code>INC-2042</code>), an order id, a stack-trace symbol. A tokenizer keeps them; an embedding model shreds them into subword pieces that mean nothing and places them near other random strings.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Rare and technical vocabulary.</strong> Drug names, part numbers, error codes, legal citations, the names of your own features. Chapter 8's domain gap, seen from the other side: the lexical index never needed to learn the word, it only needed to see it once.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Negation and precision.</strong> \"Basic fare\" and \"basic economy\" and \"Basic\" are near-identical to an embedding model and distinguishable to a term match with a phrase query.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>This is not a historical detour. When Nandan Thakur and colleagues built BEIR in 2021 to test retrieval models on eighteen datasets they had not been trained on, BM25 was the robust baseline: the dense retrievers generalised worst of all the families tested, and only rerankers and late-interaction models (Chapter 16) beat the forty-year-old scoring function, at much higher cost. Chapter 15 combines the two families rather than choosing. To combine them you have to understand this one, from the data structure up.</p>" },

    { title: "The Inverted Index: Analyzer, Dictionary, and Postings", viz: "invertedIndex", vizTitle: "Type documents, watch the dictionary and postings build, then query them",
      html: "<p>Scanning every document for a term is linear in the corpus and hopeless at any scale. The fix is the oldest idea in information retrieval, older than computers: build the index once, in reverse. A concordance to the Bible lists every significant word with the verses that contain it, and monks assembled them by hand for centuries. An <strong>inverted index</strong> is that, mechanised: a map from term to the list of documents containing it.</p>" +
            "<p>Two structures hold it. The <strong>dictionary</strong> (or term dictionary) maps each distinct term to its document frequency and a pointer. The <strong>postings list</strong> for a term is the sorted list of document ids that contain it, usually with the term frequency in each, and, in a <strong>positional index</strong>, the positions within the document so that phrase queries can check adjacency. Document ids are sorted so that lists can be intersected by a merge, which is the operation every boolean query reduces to.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='115' y='22' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>dictionary</text>" +
            "<text x='470' y='22' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>postings (docId, tf, [positions])</text>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'><rect x='30' y='36' width='170' height='26' rx='5'/><rect x='30' y='68' width='170' height='26' rx='5'/><rect x='30' y='100' width='170' height='26' rx='5'/><rect x='30' y='132' width='170' height='26' rx='5'/><rect x='30' y='164' width='170' height='26' rx='5'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'><text x='40' y='54'>cabin        df=2</text><text x='40' y='86'>cello        df=3</text><text x='40' y='118'>pet          df=1</text><text x='40' y='150'>seat         df=4</text><text x='40' y='182'>sizer        df=1</text></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='200' y1='49' x2='250' y2='49'/><line x1='200' y1='81' x2='250' y2='81'/><line x1='200' y1='113' x2='250' y2='113'/><line x1='200' y1='145' x2='250' y2='145'/><line x1='200' y1='177' x2='250' y2='177'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'><rect x='250' y='36' width='470' height='26' rx='5'/><rect x='250' y='68' width='470' height='26' rx='5'/><rect x='250' y='100' width='470' height='26' rx='5'/><rect x='250' y='132' width='470' height='26' rx='5'/><rect x='250' y='164' width='470' height='26' rx='5'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='10'><text x='260' y='54'>(1, 1, [7])  (3, 2, [12, 40])</text>" +
            "<text x='260' y='86' fill='#ffd166'>(1, 2, [3, 9])  (2, 5, [18, 55, 91, 140, 202])  (3, 1, [8])</text>" +
            "<text x='260' y='118'>(5, 4, [2, 16, 44, 77])</text>" +
            "<text x='260' y='150'>(1, 1, [11])  (2, 6, [...])  (3, 2, [...])  (4, 9, [...])</text>" +
            "<text x='260' y='182'>(2, 1, [231])</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='30' y1='205' x2='720' y2='205' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/>" +
            "<text x='30' y='224' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>query 'cello': read one list, 3 candidates, no scan of the other 199,997 chunks</text>" +
            "<text x='30' y='242' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>query 'cello seat': merge two sorted lists; positions let \"basic fare\" be checked as a phrase</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F9.1. The dictionary is small enough to keep in memory; the postings are the bulk. Everything BM25 needs, term frequency and document frequency, is already here.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>The analyzer decides what a term is</h3>" +
            "<p>Between raw text and the dictionary sits the <strong>analyzer</strong>, a pipeline that is the single largest source of silent lexical-search failures. It runs three stages. <strong>Tokenization</strong> splits text into tokens, and the rules are not obvious: does <code>K4T9RM</code> stay whole, does <code>INC-2042</code> split at the hyphen, does <code>don't</code> become one token or two? <strong>Normalization</strong> lowercases and folds accents. <strong>Stemming</strong> reduces inflections to a common root, so that <em>cellos</em> and <em>cello</em> collide, and <strong>stopword removal</strong> drops terms so common they carry no signal.</p>" +
            "<p>Postgres shows the whole pipeline in one call. <code>to_tsvector('english', 'a fat cat sat on a mat - it ate a fat rats')</code> returns <code>'ate':9 'cat':3 'fat':2,11 'mat':7 'rat':12 'sat':4</code>. The stopwords <em>a</em>, <em>on</em>, and <em>it</em> are gone, <em>rats</em> has been stemmed to <em>rat</em>, and each surviving lexeme carries its positions. Note what this costs: the position numbers still count the removed words, so phrase distance is preserved, but the words themselves can never be searched again. An index is a lossy transform of the corpus, and the losses are chosen by the analyzer.</p>" +
            "<p>Two rules follow, and both are cheap to state and expensive to learn later. First, <strong>the query must be analyzed exactly as the documents were</strong>: index <em>cellos</em> stemmed to <em>cello</em>, then search for the unstemmed <em>cellos</em>, and the term is not in the dictionary and the result set is empty. This is the lexical twin of Chapter 7's rule that one model must embed both sides. Second, stemming and stopwords are language-specific and aggressive: an English analyzer applied to German compounds, or to code, destroys the terms that matter. Section 6 returns to that.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Try it.</strong> In the machine above, add a document containing the word <em>cellos</em> and search for <em>cello</em>. With stemming off there is no match, because the dictionary holds two different terms. Turn stemming on, rebuild, and the postings lists merge into one. Then add <code>INC-2042</code> to a document and search for it with each tokenizer option; watch which ones keep the identifier whole and which split it into <em>inc</em> and <em>2042</em>, at which point it matches every chunk containing the year.</div>" +
            "<h3>Building and updating</h3>" +
            "<p>Construction is a sort. Emit a (term, docId, position) triple for every token, sort by term then docId, and the runs of equal terms are the postings lists. At corpus scale this is an external merge sort, which is why indexing a large corpus is bounded by disk throughput rather than by cleverness (Tantivy reports indexing English Wikipedia in under three minutes on a desktop). Updates are the harder half: postings lists are sorted, packed, and compressed, so inserting one document in the middle of a list would rewrite it. Every production engine solves this the same way, with immutable <strong>segments</strong>: new documents go into a small new segment, searches query all segments and merge the results, background merges combine small segments into large ones, and deletions are recorded as a bitmap of dead document ids rather than by editing postings. Chapter 4's re-ingest and Chapter 8's backfill both land on this machinery, and it is why a deleted document keeps consuming space until a merge runs.</p>" },

    { title: "Scoring: Rarity, Saturation, and Length", viz: "bm25Tuner", vizTitle: "Score five Larkspur documents; break out the IDF, term-frequency, and length terms",
      html: "<p>The index gives you candidates. Ranking them needs a score, and the history is short. The <strong>vector space model</strong> of the 1970s represented documents and queries as vectors over the vocabulary weighted by <strong>TF-IDF</strong>, term frequency times inverse document frequency, and ranked by cosine. It encodes two correct intuitions: a term occurring more often in a document is more about that document, and a term occurring in fewer documents is more discriminating. It also has two flaws that matter in practice. Term frequency is linear, so a document repeating <em>cello</em> twenty times scores twenty times one that says it once, which is not twenty times as relevant. And length is handled only by the cosine's normalization, which is a blunt instrument for the difference between a long document that is genuinely about many things and a long document that is genuinely about one.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>BM25</strong> fixes both. It came out of the Okapi system at City University London, from the probabilistic retrieval framework of Stephen Robertson, Karen Spärck Jones, and colleagues, and reached its familiar form in the TREC-3 evaluations in 1994. The name is short for Best Match 25, the twenty-fifth in a numbered series of experiments. Thirty years later it is still the default in Lucene, Elasticsearch, and everything built on them.</p>" +
            "<pre><code>score(D, Q) = Σ  IDF(qᵢ) ·        f(qᵢ, D) · (k₁ + 1)\n              qᵢ∈Q         ─────────────────────────────────────────\n                            f(qᵢ, D) + k₁ · (1 − b + b · |D| / avgdl)</code></pre>" +
            "<p>Three parts, each doing one job.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>IDF(qᵢ), rarity.</strong> How much this term is worth at all. Lucene computes <code>log(1 + (N − n + 0.5) / (n + 0.5))</code>, where N is the number of documents and n the number containing the term. In a 200,000-chunk Larkspur index: <em>cello</em> (n = 12) scores 9.68, <em>seat</em> (n = 8,000) scores 3.22, <em>refund</em> (n = 15,000) scores 2.59, and <em>the</em> (n = 200,000) scores 0.00. That spread is the whole answer to Q1.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>The f(qᵢ, D) fraction, saturation.</strong> As the term frequency grows, the numerator grows linearly but so does the denominator, so the ratio approaches a ceiling of k₁ + 1. The second occurrence of a term adds much more than the twentieth.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>The (1 − b + b · |D| / avgdl) factor, length.</strong> It scales the denominator by how much longer this document is than average, so a term in a long document is discounted. At b = 0 the factor is 1 and length is ignored entirely.</li></ul>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 240' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'><text x='20' y='24' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>saturation: a term's contribution vs its frequency (b = 0, IDF factored out)</text><line x1='70' y1='185' x2='700' y2='185' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='70' y1='40' x2='70' y2='185' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/><g stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='3 4'><line x1='70' y1='137' x2='700' y2='137'/><line x1='70' y1='88' x2='700' y2='88'/></g><g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='end'><text x='64' y='189'>1x</text><text x='64' y='141'>2x</text><text x='64' y='92'>3x</text></g><g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='middle'><text x='70' y='200'>1</text><text x='216' y='200'>2</text><text x='301' y='200'>3</text><text x='408' y='200'>5</text><text x='554' y='200'>10</text><text x='700' y='200'>20</text></g><text x='385' y='220' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>times the term occurs in the document (log scale)</text><polyline points='70,185 216,156 301,137 408,112 507,93 593,79 700,65' fill='none' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2.5'/><polyline points='70,185 216,167 301,157 408,148 507,141 593,137 700,133' fill='none' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2.5'/><polyline points='70,185 216,179 301,176 408,174 507,173 593,172 700,171' fill='none' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2.5'/><g font-size='9.5'><text x='600' y='58' fill='#3ddc97'>k1 = 3.0  (3.48x at 20)</text><text x='600' y='126' fill='#ffd166'>k1 = 1.2  (2.08x at 20)</text><text x='560' y='165' fill='#4f7cff'>k1 = 0.3  (1.28x at 20)</text></g><circle cx='70' cy='185' r='4' fill='#c9d6ea'/><text x='84' y='60' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>all three curves start at 1x: the first occurrence is the baseline</text><line x1='80' y1='66' x2='74' y2='178' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='2 3'/></svg><div class='fig-cap'>F9.2. At Lucene's default k1 = 1.2 the twentieth occurrence of a term is worth 2.08 times the first, not 20 times. The ceiling is k1 + 1, so lower k1 saturates sooner.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>The worked ranking</h3>" +
            "<p>Five Larkspur documents, the query <em>cello seat</em>, Lucene's defaults k₁ = 1.2 and b = 0.75, average length 204 tokens. The document frequencies inside this five-document set are 3 for <em>cello</em> and 4 for <em>seat</em>, so the IDF values here are small, 0.539 and 0.288; in the real 200,000-chunk index they are the 9.68 and 3.22 from above, and the ordering logic is identical.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>D1, the cabin FAQ</strong> (30 tokens, cello ×2, seat ×1) scores <strong>1.42</strong> = 0.98 + 0.44.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>D2, the instruments policy page</strong> (420 tokens, cello ×5, seat ×6) scores <strong>1.29</strong> = 0.83 + 0.47.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>D3, oversized baggage</strong> (180 tokens, cello ×1, seat ×2) scores <strong>0.98</strong>.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>D4, seat-selection fees</strong> (240 tokens, seat ×9, no cello) scores <strong>0.55</strong>.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>D5, pets in the hold</strong> scores <strong>0</strong> and is never a candidate, because neither term's postings list contains it.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Read the top two against each other, because they are the interesting pair. D2 contains more of both terms than D1 and still loses. Saturation is why its five cellos are worth 0.83 rather than 2.5 times D1's two, and length normalization is why those five cellos in 420 tokens are discounted against two in 30. D4 loses despite containing <em>seat</em> nine times, because it is missing the rare term and saturation caps what a common one can earn. That is BM25's judgement in one line: a short document containing the rare term beats a long document containing more of everything.</p>" },

    { title: "Tuning It: k₁, b, and Fields with BM25F",
      html: "<p>Two parameters, and both defaults are worth knowing by heart because you will read them in every engine's configuration: <strong>k₁ = 1.2</strong> and <strong>b = 0.75</strong> in Lucene, Elasticsearch, and Tantivy. Robertson and Zaragoza's 2009 review gives the usual ranges as k₁ between 1.2 and 2.0 and b at 0.75.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>k₁ controls saturation.</strong> Raise it toward 3 and repetition keeps earning, which suits corpora of long documents where a genuine topic really is repeated. Lower it toward 0.3 and the score becomes almost binary, does the document contain the term at all, which suits short chunks where one mention is the whole signal. RAG chunks are short, so if you tune anything, tune this down before you tune anything up.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>b controls length normalization.</strong> At 1 the discount is full and short documents win hard; at 0 length is ignored.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Slide b to 0 in the machine of the previous section and the ranking flips: D2, the 420-token policy page, climbs from 1.29 to <strong>1.48</strong> and takes first place, while D1 falls from 1.42 to <strong>1.03</strong>. Nothing about the documents changed. You removed the only term in the formula that knew D2 was fourteen times longer. This is the single most common lexical-tuning mistake, and it goes in both directions: teams whose long reference pages never rank turn b down and are pleased, then discover that every boilerplate-heavy page now outranks the crisp answers, because a long document contains more of everything by accident.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 210' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>query 'cello seat', k₁ = 1.2, same documents, b changed</text>" +
            "<text x='150' y='48' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>b = 0.75 (default)</text>" +
            "<text x='520' y='48' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10'>b = 0 (length ignored)</text>" +
            "<g font-size='10' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<rect x='95' y='60' width='142' height='18' rx='4' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='20' y='74'>D1 (30 t)</text><text x='245' y='74'>1.42</text>" +
            "<rect x='95' y='86' width='129' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='20' y='100'>D2 (420 t)</text><text x='245' y='100'>1.29</text>" +
            "<rect x='95' y='112' width='98' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='20' y='126'>D3 (180 t)</text><text x='245' y='126'>0.98</text>" +
            "<rect x='95' y='138' width='55' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='20' y='152'>D4 (240 t)</text><text x='245' y='152'>0.55</text>" +
            "<rect x='465' y='60' width='103' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='390' y='74'>D1 (30 t)</text><text x='600' y='74'>1.03</text>" +
            "<rect x='465' y='86' width='148' height='18' rx='4' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='390' y='100'>D2 (420 t)</text><text x='620' y='100'>1.48</text>" +
            "<rect x='465' y='112' width='93' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='390' y='126'>D3 (180 t)</text><text x='600' y='126'>0.93</text>" +
            "<rect x='465' y='138' width='56' height='18' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='390' y='152'>D4 (240 t)</text><text x='600' y='152'>0.56</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<line x1='330' y1='40' x2='330' y2='165' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='185' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>green = top ranked. The long policy page overtakes the short FAQ the moment length stops being counted.</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='202' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>D5 is absent from both: neither term's postings list contains it, so it is never scored.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F9.3. Length normalization is not a detail. Turning b off reverses the top of the ranking on the same documents and the same query.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>Sources differ on IDF, and it matters at small N</h3>" +
            "<p>The classical BM25 IDF is <code>log((N − n + 0.5) / (n + 0.5))</code>, and it <em>goes negative</em> for any term appearing in more than about half the documents: a term in 4 of 5 documents scores −1.10, so containing it makes a document rank worse. Lucene adds one inside the logarithm, <code>log(1 + (N − n + 0.5) / (n + 0.5))</code>, which is always positive and asymptotically the same for rare terms. In the five-document example above, the classical form gives <em>cello</em> −0.34 and <em>seat</em> −1.10, so every score would be negative and the ranking scrambled; Lucene's gives 0.539 and 0.288. For a large corpus the two agree closely (at N = 200,000 and n = 8,000 they are 3.22 and 3.18), so the difference only bites on small collections, per-tenant indexes, and tests, which is exactly where people write their first BM25 by hand and conclude it is broken. Chapter 33 builds one, and this is the line where implementations diverge.</p>" +
            "<h3>BM25F: documents with fields</h3>" +
            "<p>A chunk is rarely one flat string. It has a title, a section heading, the body, and Chapter 4's metadata. A term in the heading means more than the same term in the body. The wrong way to use that is to score each field separately and add the scores, because saturation then applies per field and a term appearing once in each of four fields earns four unsaturated first occurrences. <strong>BM25F</strong>, from the same Robertson and Zaragoza line of work, does it correctly: combine the fields' term frequencies into one weighted pseudo-frequency first, with a per-field weight and a per-field length normalization, and then apply saturation once to the total. Postgres implements the same idea with <code>setweight</code>, labelling lexemes A through D by their source field and supplying weights that default to 1.0, 0.4, 0.2, and 0.1. Give the title a weight of 3 and a heading match will outrank a body match without letting four fields multiply into a false signal.</p>" },

    { title: "Serving It Fast: Compression, WAND, and Block-Max",
      html: "<p>A postings list for a common term in a large index has millions of entries. Two techniques keep query time sub-linear in the corpus, and both are worth understanding because their side effects show up in your monitoring.</p>" +
            "<h3>Compression: gaps and variable-length integers</h3>" +
            "<p>Postings are sorted document ids, so store the gaps between consecutive ids rather than the ids themselves, which is <strong>gap encoding</strong>. In a 200,000-document index a term with a document frequency of 8,000 has an average gap of 25. Written as raw four-byte integers the list is 32,000 bytes; written as gaps in a variable-length encoding where small numbers take one byte, it is closer to 8,000. That is a four-fold saving before any block-based scheme, and modern engines do better with block codecs that pack a fixed number of gaps into a machine-friendly bit width. The reason to care is not disk: it is that a compressed postings list fits in page cache and is read sequentially, which is why lexical search stays fast on hardware where the dense index of Chapter 11 would need its vectors in RAM.</p>" +
            "<h3>Dynamic pruning: WAND</h3>" +
            "<p>You want the top ten, not all scores. So do not compute all scores. For each term, precompute an <strong>upper bound</strong> on the contribution it can make to any document (its IDF times the saturation ceiling). Keep a heap of the best ten documents found so far, whose tenth-best score is the current <strong>threshold</strong>. Now sort the query's postings lists by their current document id and walk them together: sum the upper bounds from the front until the running total first exceeds the threshold, and call the term where that happens the <strong>pivot</strong>. Any document before the pivot's current document cannot possibly beat the threshold, because even the sum of every term's best case falls short. So skip directly to the pivot's document id and score only there. This is <strong>WAND</strong> (Weak AND), from Andrei Broder and colleagues at IBM in 2003, and it is exact: the top ten it returns are the true top ten, because nothing that could have scored higher was ever skipped.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 220' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>threshold = 4.1 (current 10th-best score)</text>" +
            "<g font-size='10' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='54'>seat    ub 3.2</text><text x='20' y='94'>cabin   ub 4.0</text><text x='20' y='134'>cello   ub 9.7</text></g>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'><rect x='140' y='38' width='560' height='22' rx='4'/><rect x='140' y='78' width='560' height='22' rx='4'/><rect x='140' y='118' width='560' height='22' rx='4'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'><text x='168' y='53'>102   115   128   140   · · ·   391</text><text x='168' y='93'>118   204   260</text><text x='168' y='133'>391   788</text></g>" +
            "<circle cx='154' cy='49' r='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><circle cx='154' cy='89' r='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><circle cx='154' cy='129' r='4' fill='#ffd166'/>" +
            "<text x='150' y='166' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>dot = cursor</text>" +
            "<text x='330' y='166' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>the pivot is where the running sum of upper bounds first passes 4.1</text>" +
            "<line x1='140' y1='180' x2='700' y2='180' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='200' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>sum ub(seat)=3.2 &lt; 4.1, add ub(cabin) → 7.2 ≥ 4.1, so the pivot is 'cabin' at doc 118:</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='214' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>documents 102 to 117 cannot reach 4.1 even at their best; skip them without scoring.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F9.4. WAND skips by proof, not by guess. Only documents that could beat the current threshold are scored, so the result is exact and most of the postings are never touched.</div></div>" +
            "<p>WAND's weakness is that one upper bound per term is pessimistic: a term's best-case score is set by the single document where it appears most densely, and that bound is then applied to the whole list. <strong>Block-max WAND</strong>, from Shuai Ding and Torsten Suel in 2011, stores a maximum score per block of postings rather than per list, so the bound tightens as the walk moves through regions where the term is rare. Lucene 8.0 and Elasticsearch 7.0 adopted it in February 2019, and Elastic measured term queries running three to seven times faster and disjunctions up to fifteen times faster.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>The edge.</strong> Dynamic pruning changes an API you may depend on. Because most documents are never scored, the engine no longer knows how many matched. Elasticsearch answers with a hit count plus a relation, <code>eq</code> or <code>gte</code>, and only counts exactly up to <code>track_total_hits</code>, which defaults to 10,000. If your UI prints \"about 10,000 results\" for every query, this is why, and forcing an exact count gives back the speedup you were reading about. Chapter 27 revisits the cost.</div>" },

    { title: "Adding Terms: Query Expansion, Document Expansion, SPLADE, and ELSER",
      html: "<p>Every failure of a lexical index is a vocabulary mismatch: the document says <em>PNR</em>, the query says <em>booking reference</em>, and no amount of tuning k₁ finds a term that is not there. The whole history of fixing this is the history of adding terms, to the query or to the document, by hand, by statistics, or by a model.</p>" +
            "<h3>Classical query-side help</h3>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Synonym expansion.</strong> A curated list, applied at query time or index time, so that <em>PNR</em> and <em>booking reference</em> land on the same term. Cheap, exact, and it does not scale to a vocabulary you have not enumerated. Index-time expansion is faster to query and requires a re-index to change; query-time expansion is the reverse.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Relevance feedback.</strong> Run the query, take the top documents, extract their distinguishing terms, and re-run with those terms added. Rocchio's algorithm formalised this in the 1970s with real user judgements; <em>pseudo</em>-relevance feedback assumes the top k are relevant and is fully automatic. It helps on average and hurts badly when the first pass was wrong, which is called query drift.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Phrase and prefix queries.</strong> Positional postings let you require adjacency (\"basic fare\" as a phrase, not two terms), and a prefix or n-gram index supports <em>cell*</em> matching and typo tolerance. Both are why a lexical index can express constraints an embedding cannot.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Spelling correction.</strong> Edit distance against the dictionary, weighted by document frequency, so a misspelling maps to a term the corpus actually contains.</li></ul>" +
            "<p><strong>Tokenizers that are not English</strong> deserve their own warning. Chinese and Japanese have no spaces, so tokenization needs a segmenter or an n-gram fallback. German compounds must be decompounded or <em>Gepäckbestimmungen</em> never matches <em>Gepäck</em>. Turkish and Finnish are heavily inflected, so an English stemmer does nothing. Every one of these is configured in the analyzer, and every one silently returns empty result sets when it is wrong.</p>" +
            "<h3>Document expansion: write the queries into the document</h3>" +
            "<p>The mirror image of query expansion, and Chapter 6's hypothetical questions applied to a lexical index. Rodrigo Nogueira and Jimmy Lin's docTTTTTquery, in 2019, trains a model to generate the queries a passage would answer, appends that text to the passage, and indexes the result with ordinary BM25. On the MS MARCO passage task the mean reciprocal rank at 10 went from 0.184 for plain BM25 to 0.277 with forty generated queries per passage, while query latency rose only from 55 to 64 milliseconds, because all the model work happened before indexing. For comparison, running a BERT-large reranker over BM25 results scored 0.375 at 3,500 milliseconds. Document expansion buys half the gain of a reranker for one sixtieth of the query cost, and it composes with one.</p>" +
            "<h3>Learned sparse retrieval: SPLADE and ELSER</h3>" +
            "<p>The modern synthesis keeps the inverted index and lets a transformer decide the terms and their weights. <strong>SPLADE</strong>, from Thibault Formal, Benjamin Piwowarski, and Stéphane Clinchant in 2021, runs a masked-language-model head over the input and reads off a weight for every term in the vocabulary, not only the terms present. A passage about a PNR gets non-zero weight on <em>booking</em> and <em>reference</em> because the language model knows they belong together. Two mechanisms keep it usable: a log saturation on the weights, which is BM25's saturation learned rather than imposed, and a sparsity regulariser that pushes most of the vocabulary to exactly zero, so the output is a short list of weighted terms that goes straight into a postings list. SPLADE v2, later that year, added max pooling over the token dimension and distillation from a cross-encoder teacher, reporting gains of more than nine percent in nDCG@10 on TREC Deep Learning 2019.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 220' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<rect x='20' y='36' width='300' height='58' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='170' y='24' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>passage</text>" +
            "<text x='34' y='58' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>Name corrections on a PNR are</text><text x='34' y='76' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>permitted once within 24 hours.</text>" +
            "<line x1='320' y1='65' x2='380' y2='65' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='386,65 374,59 374,71' fill='#7d8fb0'/><text x='350' y='55' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>MLM head</text>" +
            "<text x='560' y='24' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>learned sparse terms (weights)</text>" +
            "<g font-size='10'>" +
            "<rect x='400' y='36' width='150' height='20' rx='4' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='406' y='51' fill='#111a2e'>pnr            2.9</text>" +
            "<rect x='400' y='60' width='128' height='20' rx='4' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='406' y='75' fill='#111a2e'>name           2.4</text>" +
            "<rect x='400' y='84' width='106' height='20' rx='4' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='406' y='99' fill='#111a2e'>booking        1.9</text>" +
            "<rect x='400' y='108' width='90' height='20' rx='4' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='406' y='123' fill='#111a2e'>reference      1.6</text>" +
            "<rect x='400' y='132' width='72' height='20' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='406' y='147' fill='#c9d6ea'>correction     1.2</text>" +
            "<rect x='400' y='156' width='44' height='20' rx='4' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='406' y='171' fill='#c9d6ea'>ticket         0.7</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<text x='400' y='192' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>green: terms not in the passage, added by the model</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='120' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>the other 30,000</text><text x='20' y='134' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>vocabulary entries</text><text x='20' y='148' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>are pushed to exactly</text><text x='20' y='162' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>zero by the sparsity</text><text x='20' y='176' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>regulariser</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='210' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>the output is a postings list, so every technique in this chapter still applies: same index, same WAND, learned terms</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F9.5. SPLADE keeps the data structure and replaces the hand-written weighting. The expansion terms are what close the PNR gap without an embedding.</div></div>" +
            "<p><strong>ELSER</strong>, Elastic's Learned Sparse EncodeR, is the same family productised: it expands text into weighted tokens stored in a <code>sparse_vector</code> field, is trained to work without fine-tuning on your data, handles English, and encodes the first 512 tokens of a field. Elastic's documentation is careful about one thing worth repeating: the expansion terms are not synonyms, they are learned associations, so reading them as a thesaurus will mislead you. The trade against dense retrieval is worth stating plainly. Learned sparse keeps exact term matching, keeps the inverted index and its tooling, and stays interpretable enough to debug. It costs a model call at index time and at query time, produces longer postings lists than BM25, and remains an English-first technology in most deployments. Chapter 15 fuses it with the dense side rather than choosing.</p>" },

    { title: "In the Real World: Lucene, Elasticsearch, Postgres, and Tantivy",
      html: "<p>Four implementations you will meet, and what each one actually gives you.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Apache Lucene</strong> is the library nearly everything is built on: segments, block-compressed postings, BM25 with k₁ = 1.2 and b = 0.75, block-max WAND since 8.0, and a rich analyzer library. Its scoring detail worth knowing is <code>discountOverlaps = true</code>, which excludes synonym tokens injected at the same position from the length calculation, so adding synonyms does not make documents look longer.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Elasticsearch and OpenSearch</strong> are Lucene plus a distributed layer, an HTTP query language, and operational tooling. BM25 is the default similarity; k₁, b, and <code>discount_overlaps</code> are index settings, which means changing them requires a reindex on existing data. This is where most production lexical search lives, and where ELSER is available as a managed model.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Postgres full-text search</strong> gives you <code>tsvector</code>, <code>to_tsquery</code>, and a GIN index for free alongside your rows and, with pgvector from Chapter 7, alongside your embeddings in the same transaction. Its ranking is not BM25: <code>ts_rank</code> weights by lexeme frequency and <code>ts_rank_cd</code> by cover density (how close the matched terms sit), with document-length normalization selected by a bitmask (0 ignores length, 1 divides by 1 + log(length), 2 by length, 32 rescales to a 0-to-1 range). The documentation is candid about the cost: ranking requires reading the <code>tsvector</code> of every matching row, cannot be pushed into the index, and is therefore I/O bound. For a corpus of a few million chunks with a filter that narrows the candidate set, this is the pragmatic choice and it removes a system from your architecture. For lexical search as the primary workload at scale, it is not.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Tantivy</strong> is a Lucene-inspired library in Rust, with BM25 scoring the project describes as the same as Lucene's, a benchmark claiming roughly twice Lucene's search speed, and startup in under ten milliseconds. It is the sensible engine for an embedded or single-binary deployment, and it is what a Rust-based RAG service reaches for instead of running a JVM.</li></ul>" +
            "<h3>Choosing</h3>" +
            "<p>The decision procedure is short. If your chunks already live in Postgres and lexical search is one of several filters rather than the main event, use <code>tsvector</code> and accept the ranking difference. If lexical search is a first-class retriever and you need real BM25, analyzers for languages you do not speak, and block-max WAND, use Elasticsearch or OpenSearch. If you are shipping a single binary and can afford to own the index lifecycle, use Tantivy. Whatever you choose, the corpus is indexed twice, once here and once in the vector index of Chapter 11, and Chapter 15 is where the two result lists are fused, so keep the same chunk ids in both.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"Why not just use embeddings?\" is asking whether you know the three failure classes in Section 1 and can cite BEIR's finding that dense retrievers generalise worst out of domain. \"Explain BM25\" wants rarity, saturation, and length normalization, in that order, and the strong answer names k₁ and b with their defaults and says what each does to the ranking. \"Your lexical search returns nothing for a term you can see in the document\" is an analyzer question every time: the query was analyzed differently from the document.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol><li><strong>Trace.</strong> Using Section 3's five documents, recompute D1 and D2's scores at k₁ = 0.3, b = 0.75 by hand (IDF: cello 0.539, seat 0.288; avgdl 204). What you should find: D1 1.00 and D2 0.98, a near-tie, because lowering k₁ throws away most of D2's advantage in raw counts while length normalization still penalises it. Explain in one sentence why lowering k₁ narrows the gap rather than widening it.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> Compute the classical IDF, log((N − n + 0.5)/(n + 0.5)), for a term appearing in 3 of 5 documents and in 12 of 200,000. What you should find: −0.34 and 9.68. Then explain what a scorer using the classical form does with a query term that appears in most documents, and why Lucene's added 1 removes the problem without changing rare-term behaviour.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Load your Chapter 5 chunks into Postgres with a <code>tsvector</code> column and a GIN index. Run five queries containing rare identifiers from your corpus and compare the top 10 against the dense results from your Chapter 7 index on the same chunks. What you should find: the lexical index wins decisively on identifiers and rare terms and loses on paraphrases, with little overlap between the two result sets, which is the empirical case for Chapter 15's fusion.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Take one chunk and run it through three analyzers: an English stemmer, a whitespace tokenizer, and a bigram tokenizer. Record the term list from each and the document frequency of the identifiers in your corpus. What you should find: the English analyzer destroys at least one identifier by splitting or stemming it, and the term you most need to search is the one most likely to be mangled.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Implement an inverted index and BM25 in under 150 lines: an analyzer with a stopword list and an optional stemmer, a dictionary to postings map with term frequencies, and a scorer with configurable k₁ and b using Lucene's IDF. Validate it against a reference by indexing the same corpus in Postgres or Tantivy and comparing the top 10 for twenty queries. What you should find: identical rankings for Tantivy up to floating-point ordering, and a different but correlated ranking for Postgres, because <code>ts_rank</code> is not BM25. This is Chapter 33's PocketRAG component; write it once and keep it.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Add WAND to the scorer from the previous exercise: a per-term upper bound, a top-k heap, and pivot selection. Count how many documents are fully scored with and without it on your twenty queries, and assert that both versions return identical top-10 lists. What you should find: an order-of-magnitude reduction in documents scored, with byte-identical results, which is the point of an exact pruning algorithm.</li></ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "inverted index", d: "A map from each term to the list of documents containing it, built once so that queries never scan the corpus." },
    { t: "dictionary", d: "The index's map from term to its document frequency and a pointer to its postings list." },
    { t: "postings list", d: "The sorted list of document ids containing a term, usually with term frequencies and optionally positions." },
    { t: "positional index", d: "An inverted index that stores each term's positions within documents, enabling phrase and proximity queries." },
    { t: "analyzer", d: "The pipeline that turns raw text into index terms by tokenizing, normalizing, stemming, and removing stopwords." },
    { t: "stemming", d: "Reducing inflected words to a common root so that variants collide on one index term." },
    { t: "stopword", d: "A term so common it is dropped at index time because it discriminates between documents poorly." },
    { t: "segment", d: "An immutable unit of an index; new documents form new segments, deletions are recorded as bitmaps, and background merges consolidate them." },
    { t: "TF-IDF", d: "Weighting a term by its frequency in the document times its rarity across the collection; BM25's linear-term-frequency predecessor." },
    { t: "BM25", d: "The Best Match 25 ranking function (Robertson et al., Okapi at TREC-3, 1994) combining inverse document frequency, saturating term frequency, and length normalization." },
    { t: "inverse document frequency", d: "A term's discriminating power, computed by Lucene as log(1 + (N − n + 0.5)/(n + 0.5)) so that it never goes negative." },
    { t: "saturation", d: "BM25's property that each additional occurrence of a term adds less than the last, bounded by k₁ + 1." },
    { t: "k₁", d: "The BM25 parameter controlling how quickly term frequency saturates; 1.2 by default in Lucene, Elasticsearch, and Tantivy." },
    { t: "b", d: "The BM25 parameter controlling document-length normalization, from 0 (ignore length) to 1 (full discount); 0.75 by default." },
    { t: "BM25F", d: "The field-weighted BM25 variant that combines per-field term frequencies into one weighted total before applying saturation once." },
    { t: "gap encoding", d: "Storing differences between consecutive document ids rather than the ids, so that a variable-length integer code makes postings several times smaller." },
    { t: "WAND", d: "A dynamic-pruning algorithm (Broder et al. 2003) that uses per-term score upper bounds and a running threshold to skip documents that provably cannot enter the top k." },
    { t: "block-max WAND", d: "WAND with a maximum score stored per block of postings rather than per list, adopted in Lucene 8.0 and measured at up to fifteen times faster on disjunctions." },
    { t: "query expansion", d: "Adding terms to a query, from a synonym list or from the first pass's top results, to close a vocabulary mismatch." },
    { t: "document expansion", d: "Appending model-generated text, typically the queries a passage would answer, to a document before indexing (docTTTTTquery)." },
    { t: "learned sparse retrieval", d: "Using a transformer to assign weights over the whole vocabulary, including terms absent from the text, while keeping an inverted index (SPLADE, ELSER)." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "A dense retriever misses Larkspur Q1 (the cello) while BM25 finds it first. What is the mechanism?",
      opts: ["BM25 reads the query more carefully than an embedding model, since it works over the literal words rather than a compressed representation of them, so nothing about the query is lost on the way in", "The rare term carries a high inverse document frequency, so the one chunk containing it is pulled up; the embedding model never saw enough cellos to give the word a sharp position", "BM25 uses term positions while the dense index does not, so an exact phrase such as cabin cello can be matched where a single averaged vector cannot represent the phrase at all", "The dense index was built with the wrong distance metric, so the cello chunk is ranked by a measure the embedding model was never trained for and the neighbour order is wrong"], a: 1,
      why: "In a 200,000-chunk index, cello at n = 12 scores about 9.68 against seat's 3.22. A lexical index needs to have seen a term once; an embedding model needs to have been trained on it often." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Documents were indexed with an English stemmer. A search for \"cellos\" returns nothing, though a document plainly contains the word. Why?",
      opts: ["The postings list for cellos was pruned away by block-max WAND before the query ever got the chance to reach it", "The document frequency of cellos is too low for the term to score above the cutoff the engine applies to rare terms in a large index", "The query was not analyzed the way the documents were: the index holds the stem cello, and the unstemmed query term is not in the dictionary", "Stopword removal deleted the term from the index when the documents were analyzed at ingest time"], a: 2,
      why: "Query and documents must pass through the same analyzer, the lexical twin of Chapter 7's rule that one model must embed both sides. This is the most common cause of an empty lexical result set." },
    { type: "blank", q: "In BM25, the property that the twentieth occurrence of a term adds far less than the second is called ____, and it is controlled by k₁.", a: ["saturation", "term frequency saturation", "tf saturation"],
      why: "The numerator and denominator both grow with f, so the ratio approaches a ceiling of k₁ + 1. At k₁ = 1.2 the twentieth occurrence is worth 2.08 times the first, not 20 times." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A team sets b = 0 because their long reference pages never rank. What have they done, in mechanism terms?",
      opts: ["Removed the length-normalization factor, so long documents are no longer discounted and now outrank short precise answers by containing more of everything", "Made term frequency saturate immediately, so a second occurrence of a term adds nothing at all to the score of the document, however long that document is", "Disabled inverse document frequency, so common words now count as much toward the score as rare ones do in every document that matches", "Switched the scoring from BM25 to plain TF-IDF, which has no length term in it at all, so nothing discounts a long page"], a: 0,
      why: "At b = 0 the factor (1 − b + b·|D|/avgdl) equals 1. In the chapter's worked example, the 420-token policy page climbs from 1.29 to 1.48 and overtakes the 30-token FAQ, which falls from 1.42 to 1.03." },
    { type: "mc", q: "You implement BM25 with the classical IDF, log((N − n + 0.5)/(n + 0.5)), and test it on a 5-document collection. Scores come out negative and the ranking looks scrambled. What is wrong?",
      opts: ["The term frequencies must be normalized before scoring, or the sum over the query terms comes out on the wrong scale and can fall below zero for the commoner terms", "Nothing is wrong; BM25 is not defined for collections this small, and the formula needs a few thousand documents before any of its scores mean anything at all", "avgdl must be computed over the query rather than the collection, and using the collection average scrambles the length normalization term", "The classical IDF goes negative for terms in more than about half the documents, which is common at small N; Lucene adds 1 inside the logarithm to keep it positive"], a: 3,
      why: "A term in 4 of 5 documents scores −1.10 classically and 0.288 in Lucene's form. At N = 200,000 and n = 8,000 the two forms agree to within 0.04, so the difference bites only on small collections and tests." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the steps WAND takes to skip documents:", items: ["precompute an upper bound on each query term's possible contribution", "sort the term cursors by their current document id and set the threshold to the current tenth-best score", "sum upper bounds from the front until the total first exceeds the threshold, naming that term the pivot", "skip every cursor to the pivot's document id and score only there"],
      why: "The skip is a proof: any document before the pivot's cannot beat the threshold even if every term scored its maximum, so the top-k result is exact despite most documents never being scored." },
    { type: "mc", q: "After upgrading to a version with block-max WAND, an application's result page starts showing \"about 10,000 results\" for every query. What happened?",
      opts: ["The index is corrupt and needs a full reindex; the round number is the engine's way of reporting that it could not walk all of the postings lists the query needed before it gave up", "Dynamic pruning means most matching documents are never scored, so the engine reports a lower bound; Elasticsearch counts exactly only up to track_total_hits, which defaults to 10,000", "The default page size changed to 10,000 in the new version, and the result header is reporting that page size rather than the number of documents the query actually matched", "Block-max WAND caps the number of postings read per term at 10,000, so the engine cannot see any of the matches that lie beyond that limit and reports the cap instead"], a: 1,
      why: "Exactness of the top k does not imply exactness of the total count. Forcing an exact count by raising track_total_hits gives back the speedup the pruning provided." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A chunk has a title, a heading, and a body. A colleague proposes scoring each field with BM25 separately and summing the three scores. What is wrong with that?",
      opts: ["Fields must be scored with different values of b, since a title and a body have very different average lengths and one normalization cannot serve both, whatever the weighting", "Nothing is wrong; summing per-field BM25 scores is exactly what BM25F does under the hood, which is why the engines implement it that way", "Saturation is applied once per field, so a term appearing once in each of three fields earns three unsaturated first occurrences and outranks a document that genuinely repeats it", "Inverse document frequency cannot be computed per field, because the document counts differ between them and cannot be combined"], a: 2,
      why: "BM25F combines per-field term frequencies into one weighted pseudo-frequency, with per-field length normalization, and then saturates once. Postgres expresses the same idea with setweight and its {D,C,B,A} weight array." },
    { type: "mc", q: "SPLADE assigns a non-zero weight to \"booking\" for a passage that only ever says \"PNR\". What makes this still a lexical index rather than a dense one?",
      opts: ["The weights are integers rather than floats, which is what lets them be stored in a postings list, and any scoring function over integer term weights counts as lexical in this framing", "The output is a short list of weighted vocabulary terms, so it is stored and searched as a postings list; the sparsity regulariser forces almost the entire vocabulary to exactly zero", "It uses cosine similarity over the vocabulary axis, so each dimension is a term rather than a latent feature, which keeps the space interpretable end to end", "The model runs at query time only and never at index time, so the stored representation stays the original bag of words the documents were written with"], a: 1,
      why: "Formal et al. 2021 combine a log saturation on term weights with explicit sparsity regularization. The data structure, the tooling, and WAND all survive; only the weighting is learned." },
    { type: "mc", q: "docTTTTTquery raised MS MARCO MRR@10 from 0.184 to 0.277 while query latency rose only from 55 ms to 64 ms, whereas a BERT-large reranker reached 0.375 at 3,500 ms. What is the engineering lesson?",
      opts: ["Rerankers are never worth their cost, since a 3,500 ms query is unusable in production whatever it buys in ranking quality, and expansion is strictly better", "Document expansion moves model work to index time, buying a large share of a reranker's gain at a tiny fraction of the query cost, and the two compose", "Query latency is dominated by the inverted index rather than by the model, so the expansion added almost nothing to the query path and the reranker's cost sits elsewhere", "Generating more queries per passage always improves the score, so the gain scales with how much text the generator writes at index time"], a: 1,
      why: "All generation happens before indexing, so the query path stays a BM25 lookup. The chapter's own numbers put document expansion at roughly half the reranker's gain for about one sixtieth of the per-query cost." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Your chunks live in Postgres with pgvector, lexical search is one of several filters, and the corpus is two million chunks. Which choice does the chapter's decision procedure give, and what do you accept?",
      opts: ["Elasticsearch, accepting a second system to operate, because real BM25 and its pruning are worth the operational cost and the filters can move there too", "Tantivy embedded in the application, accepting that you own the index lifecycle and must keep it in step with Postgres yourself on every write the corpus takes", "Postgres tsvector with a GIN index, accepting that ts_rank is not BM25 and that ranking reads every matching row and cannot be pushed into the index", "A hand-written index, accepting the maintenance, because the query shapes here are narrow enough to specialise for and nothing off the shelf fits them"], a: 2,
      why: "Keeping lexical and vector search in one transactional store removes a system. The documented cost is that ranking is I/O bound because it must consult each matching row's tsvector, and that cover-density ranking is a different function from BM25." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 10 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): Beyer, Goldstein, Ramakrishnan &
   Shaft 1999, "When Is Nearest Neighbor Meaningful?" (ICDT: under iid
   dimensions the ratio of farthest to nearest distance converges to 1;
   the paper's own exceptions are clustered data and low intrinsic
   dimensionality); Aumüller, Bernhardsson & Faithfull 2018, ANN-Benchmarks
   (arXiv 1807.05614: recall vs queries-per-second Pareto frontiers, automatic
   parameter sweeps, and the finding that very different approaches reach
   comparable trade-offs); Indyk & Motwani 1998 and Gionis, Indyk & Motwani 1999
   (LSH); Charikar 2002 (random-hyperplane LSH, collision probability
   1 − θ/π); Datar, Immorlica, Indyk & Mirrokni 2004 (p-stable LSH,
   h(v) = floor((a·v + b)/r)); pgvector README (flat scan when no index exists).
   ALL PERFORMANCE AND CONCENTRATION NUMBERS IN THIS CHAPTER WERE MEASURED ON
   THE AUTHORING MACHINE, not quoted: Apple M1, 8 GB, numpy 2.5.2, float32,
   unit-normalized random vectors, matrix-vector dot product plus argpartition,
   best of three runs over 20 queries. Conditions are stated in the text. */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 10, part: 2, title: "Nearest Neighbours: The Problem, the Triangle, and the Curse",
  blurb: "Finding the closest vector among a million is a million dot products, and that scan is the entire reason the next three chapters exist. This short chapter states the problem precisely and then measures it: real timings on a laptop, from a 0.4 millisecond scan at ten thousand vectors to 42 milliseconds at a million. You will meet the triangle you trade inside from here on, recall against throughput against memory, learn to read the benchmark plot the field argues with, and see why high dimensions make distances concentrate until the nearest and farthest points are nearly the same distance away. Then you will build the harness every later chapter reports against.",
  sections: [
    { title: "Exact k-Nearest Neighbours: The Scan and What It Costs", viz: "bruteForce", vizTitle: "Scan time, throughput, and memory against corpus size and dimension",
      html: "<p>The problem is stated in one line. Given a query vector <em>q</em> and a set of N document vectors in d dimensions, return the k whose similarity to <em>q</em> is highest. That is <strong>exact k-nearest-neighbour search</strong>, and the algorithm is equally short: compute all N similarities, keep the best k. With unit vectors and the dot product of Chapter 7, that is one multiply and one add per dimension per document, so N × d multiply-adds, then a partial selection over N scores. This is <strong>brute-force search</strong>, stored as a <strong>flat index</strong>, and it has one property nothing else in this course has: its recall is exactly 1. It cannot miss.</p>" +
            "<p>The cost is linear in both N and d, and \"linear\" hides how fast the constants matter. Here is the scan measured on the machine this chapter was written on, an Apple M1 laptop with 8 GB of memory, using float32 vectors and a single matrix-vector product, best of three runs over twenty queries:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>10,000 × 384 d</strong> — 0.40 ms per query, about 2,500 queries per second, 15 MB.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>100,000 × 384 d</strong> — 4.33 ms, 231 per second, 154 MB.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>1,000,000 × 384 d</strong> — 42.2 ms, 24 per second, 1.5 GB.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>10,000 × 1536 d</strong> — 1.94 ms, 516 per second, 61 MB.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>100,000 × 1536 d</strong> — 19.3 ms, 52 per second, 614 MB.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>500,000 × 1536 d</strong> — 172 ms, 6 per second, 3.1 GB.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Read the first three rows: ten times the corpus, ten times the latency, almost exactly. Read the first against the fourth: four times the dimension, roughly five times the latency. There is no algorithmic surprise hiding in a scan. What the last row shows is a different effect worth naming, because you will meet it in production. At 3.1 GB on an 8 GB machine the scan achieved about 4.5 billion multiply-adds per second against roughly 8 billion in every smaller case. Nothing changed but the working set: once the vectors stop fitting comfortably in memory the scan becomes bound by memory bandwidth rather than arithmetic, and the linear curve bends upward. A million 1,536-dimensional float32 vectors need 6.1 GB, which is why that row is absent from the table. I could not measure it on this laptop, and that is the lesson rather than a gap.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 210' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>exact k-NN: one query against the whole corpus</text>" +
            "<rect x='20' y='40' width='110' height='40' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2'/><text x='75' y='64' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10'>query q (d)</text>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'><rect x='200' y='36' width='330' height='16' rx='3'/><rect x='200' y='56' width='330' height='16' rx='3'/><rect x='200' y='76' width='330' height='16' rx='3'/><rect x='200' y='116' width='330' height='16' rx='3'/></g>" +
            "<text x='365' y='106' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='11'>· · ·</text>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'><text x='206' y='48'>v1</text><text x='206' y='68'>v2</text><text x='206' y='88'>v3</text><text x='206' y='128'>vN</text></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1'><line x1='130' y1='60' x2='196' y2='44'/><line x1='130' y1='60' x2='196' y2='64'/><line x1='130' y1='60' x2='196' y2='84'/><line x1='130' y1='60' x2='196' y2='124'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'><text x='545' y='48'>0.81</text><text x='545' y='68'>0.12</text><text x='545' y='88'>0.77</text><text x='545' y='128'>0.44</text></g>" +
            "<rect x='600' y='56' width='140' height='56' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='670' y='78' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>top-k heap</text><text x='670' y='96' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>recall = 1.000</text>" +
            "<line x1='580' y1='84' x2='596' y2='84' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='160' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10'>work = N × d multiply-adds. Nothing is skipped, so nothing can be missed.</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='182' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>measured: 1M × 384 d = 42 ms/query on an M1 laptop. Every index in Chapters 11 to 13 buys speed by</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='198' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>refusing to compute some of these dot products, and pays for it in recall below 1.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F10.1. The scan is the baseline that defines both ends of the trade: perfect recall, and a cost that grows with the corpus.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Modern hardware makes the scan faster than the arithmetic suggests. The inner loop is a dot product over contiguous floats, which is what <strong>SIMD</strong> instructions (single instruction, multiple data) were built for: one AVX-512 or NEON instruction multiplies several pairs at once, and a well-written kernel reaches a large fraction of the machine's peak throughput. This is why a numpy one-liner competes with hand-written C, and it is why the honest advice for small corpora is to skip the index entirely.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>When flat is the right index.</strong> Below roughly a hundred thousand vectors, a scan answers in single-digit milliseconds, has recall of 1, needs no build step, no parameters, and no rebuild after every write. Larkspur's 200,000 chunks at 768 dimensions sit near that boundary. pgvector runs a flat scan when no index exists, so the first version of a production system frequently should not have one. Add an index when a measured latency budget says to, which is Chapter 25's job, not before. Set the machine above to your own N and d and read the latency before you read Chapter 11.</div>" },

    { title: "The Triangle: Recall, Throughput, and Memory",
      html: "<p>Every approximate index from here on makes the same bargain: skip some candidates, return the answer faster, and be wrong occasionally. That bargain has three axes, and you cannot improve all three at once.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Recall@k</strong>, the fraction of the true top-k that the index actually returned, measured against brute force. This is the accuracy axis, and it is a property of the index, not of the embedding model. An index with recall@10 of 0.9 loses one of your ten best chunks on average, before the reranker of Chapter 16 ever sees them.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Throughput and latency</strong>, queries per second and the p95 a user waits for.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Memory</strong>, bytes per vector times N, plus whatever the index structure adds on top. Chapter 11's graph adds edges; Chapter 12's quantization subtracts precision.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>The trade is continuous, not discrete. Every index in this course exposes at least one search-time parameter that moves you along the recall-versus-speed curve without rebuilding anything, and at least one build-time parameter that moves the whole curve. That distinction is the practical heart of the next three chapters: search-time parameters you can tune in production this afternoon, build-time parameters you tune by re-indexing overnight.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 240' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<polygon points='190,50 60,200 320,200' fill='none' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='190' y='42' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10.5'>recall@k</text>" +
            "<text x='50' y='216' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='10.5'>speed</text>" +
            "<text x='330' y='216' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>memory</text>" +
            "<circle cx='190' cy='150' r='5' fill='#c9d6ea'/><text x='190' y='170' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>pick two</text>" +
            "<line x1='360' y1='40' x2='360' y2='215' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "<text x='390' y='52' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>which knob moves what</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<text x='390' y='78' fill='#3ddc97'>search-time (change now, no rebuild)</text>" +
            "<text x='400' y='96'>HNSW ef_search, IVF nprobe: recall up, speed down</text>" +
            "<text x='390' y='126' fill='#ffd166'>build-time (change by re-indexing)</text>" +
            "<text x='400' y='144'>HNSW M and ef_construction: the whole curve moves,</text>" +
            "<text x='400' y='160'>and memory moves with it</text>" +
            "<text x='390' y='190' fill='#2de2ff'>storage-time (change by re-encoding)</text>" +
            "<text x='400' y='208'>float16, int8, binary, PQ: memory down, recall down,</text>" +
            "<text x='400' y='224'>recovered by rescoring (Chapter 7)</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F10.2. Three axes, three classes of knob. Knowing which class a parameter belongs to tells you whether fixing a recall problem takes an afternoon or an overnight rebuild.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>Reading an ann-benchmarks plot</h3>" +
            "<p>The field settled its arguments with a shared harness. <strong>ANN-Benchmarks</strong>, from Martin Aumüller, Erik Bernhardsson, and Alexander Faithfull in 2018, runs many implementations over standard datasets, sweeps each one's parameters automatically, and plots recall on the horizontal axis against queries per second, usually logarithmic, on the vertical. Each algorithm becomes a curve rather than a point, because each parameter setting is one measurement, and only the <strong>Pareto frontier</strong>, the upper-right envelope of points not beaten on both axes at once, is worth reading.</p>" +
            "<p>Three habits make the plot useful. Read the top right, since the interesting region is recall above 0.9, and an algorithm that wins at recall 0.5 has won nothing you can ship. Check the dataset, because results on 960-dimensional GIST descriptors do not transfer to your 768-dimensional text embeddings. And note what the axis omits: memory and build time are not on it, so an index that wins the plot may need three times the RAM or an hour to build. The paper's own headline finding is the honest one to carry: very different approaches reach comparable trade-offs, so the choice is usually made on the axes the plot does not show.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'><text x='20' y='20' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>an ann-benchmarks plot, annotated (shape is illustrative)</text><line x1='90' y1='200' x2='710' y2='200' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='90' y1='36' x2='90' y2='200' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/><g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='end'><text x='84' y='204'>10</text><text x='84' y='164'>100</text><text x='84' y='124'>1k</text><text x='84' y='84'>10k</text><text x='84' y='44'>100k</text></g><text x='34' y='120' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5' transform='rotate(-90 34 120)'>queries/sec (log)</text><g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='middle'><text x='90' y='216'>0.5</text><text x='334' y='216'>0.7</text><text x='456' y='216'>0.8</text><text x='578' y='216'>0.9</text><text x='688' y='216'>0.99</text></g><text x='400' y='234' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>recall@10</text><rect x='578' y='36' width='132' height='164' fill='#3ddc97' fill-opacity='0.07' stroke='none'/><text x='644' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>the only region worth reading</text><polyline points='334,68 517,77 578,84 639,96 688,117 699,141' fill='none' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2.5'/><g fill='#4f7cff'><circle cx='334' cy='68' r='3.5'/><circle cx='517' cy='77' r='3.5'/><circle cx='578' cy='84' r='3.5'/><circle cx='639' cy='96' r='3.5'/><circle cx='688' cy='117' r='3.5'/><circle cx='699' cy='141' r='3.5'/></g><text x='300' y='64' text-anchor='end' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='9.5'>graph index (Ch 11)</text><polyline points='334,89 517,101 578,108 639,122 688,148' fill='none' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2.5'/><g fill='#ffd166'><circle cx='334' cy='89' r='3.5'/><circle cx='517' cy='101' r='3.5'/><circle cx='578' cy='108' r='3.5'/><circle cx='639' cy='122' r='3.5'/><circle cx='688' cy='148' r='3.5'/></g><text x='300' y='93' text-anchor='end' fill='#ffd166'  font-size='9.5'>IVF + PQ (Ch 12)</text><circle cx='700' cy='185' r='5' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='690' y='180' text-anchor='end' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>flat scan: recall exactly 1, 24 q/s</text><text x='100' y='170' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>each dot is one parameter setting; the curve is the sweep</text><text x='100' y='186' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>not on either axis: memory, build time, your dataset</text></svg><div class='fig-cap'>F10.4. Read the shaded region only. Each curve is one algorithm swept across its search-time parameter, and the two axes hide the two costs that often decide the choice.</div></div>" },

    { title: "The Curse of Dimensionality: When the Nearest Point Stops Being Special",
      html: "<p>Here is an experiment you can run in thirty seconds, and I ran it to produce these numbers: draw 10,000 random points from a standard normal distribution in d dimensions, pick a query point the same way, and measure the distance from the query to every point. Report the closest, the farthest, and their ratio.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>d = 2</strong> — nearest 0.03, farthest 5.27, ratio <strong>211</strong>.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>d = 4</strong> — ratio 23.7.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>d = 16</strong> — ratio 3.68.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>d = 64</strong> — ratio 1.74.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>d = 256</strong> — ratio 1.38.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>d = 1024</strong> — nearest 42.6, farthest 48.9, ratio <strong>1.15</strong>.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>At two dimensions the nearest point is two hundred times closer than the farthest and \"nearest\" means something obvious. At a thousand dimensions everything is about 45 units away, give or take seven percent, and the nearest neighbour is barely distinguishable from a randomly chosen point. Kevin Beyer, Jonathan Goldstein, Raghu Ramakrishnan, and Uri Shaft proved the general statement in 1999: under broad conditions, as dimension grows the ratio of the farthest distance to the nearest converges to 1. This is <strong>distance concentration</strong>, and it is the precise form of the <strong>curse of dimensionality</strong> that matters for retrieval.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='22' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>distances from one query to 10,000 random points, scaled by the mean (measured)</text>" +
            "<text x='30' y='48' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10'>d = 2</text>" +
            "<g fill='#3ddc97'><rect x='90' y='68' width='38' height='4' rx='2'/><rect x='128' y='58' width='38' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='166' y='50' width='38' height='22' rx='2'/><rect x='204' y='39' width='38' height='33' rx='2'/><rect x='242' y='37' width='38' height='35' rx='2'/><rect x='280' y='37' width='38' height='35' rx='2'/><rect x='318' y='39' width='38' height='33' rx='2'/><rect x='356' y='47' width='38' height='25' rx='2'/><rect x='394' y='54' width='38' height='18' rx='2'/><rect x='432' y='62' width='38' height='10' rx='2'/><rect x='470' y='69' width='38' height='3' rx='2'/><rect x='508' y='69' width='38' height='3' rx='2'/></g>" +
            "<line x1='90' y1='72' x2='700' y2='72' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='560' y='60' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>spread across the whole range</text>" +
            "<text x='30' y='140' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='10'>d = 1024</text>" +
            "<rect x='280' y='96' width='38' height='68' rx='2' fill='#ff5d73'/>" +
            "<line x1='90' y1='164' x2='700' y2='164' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='340' y='118' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>99% of all 10,000 distances land in this one bucket</text>" +
            "<text x='340' y='134' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>nearest 0.92x the mean, farthest 1.08x</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='middle'><text x='90' y='180'>0</text><text x='280' y='180'>1.0x mean</text><text x='470' y='180'>1.5x</text><text x='700' y='180'>2.2x</text></g>" +
            "<text x='20' y='206' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>real embeddings are clustered, not uniform: the same experiment on 20 clusters in 1,024 dimensions</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='222' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>gives a farthest/nearest ratio of 3.47, not 1.15. Structure is what makes nearest-neighbour search work.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F10.3. Concentration is not a metaphor. At 1,024 dimensions the entire distance distribution collapses into a narrow band around its mean.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Two consequences, one destructive and one reassuring.</p>" +
            "<p>The destructive one killed a generation of data structures. A k-d tree or an R-tree accelerates search by partitioning space and pruning branches whose bounding region is farther than the best candidate found so far. When every distance is nearly the mean, almost no branch can be pruned, so the tree visits nearly every leaf and does more work than a scan because it also chases pointers. This is why <strong>tree indexes stop paying above roughly twenty dimensions</strong>, and why nothing in Chapters 11 to 13 is a tree. Graphs and quantization replaced them because they never rely on a bound that concentration destroys.</p>" +
            "<p>The reassuring one is that Beyer and colleagues' theorem has conditions, and real embeddings violate them. It assumes dimensions that are independent and identically distributed, and it explicitly exempts clustered data and data whose <strong>intrinsic dimensionality</strong> is lower than the space it is written in. Both exemptions describe Chapter 7's embedding space exactly: text embeddings live on a low-dimensional manifold inside a high-dimensional coordinate system, and they cluster hard by topic. Rerunning the same experiment on 10,000 points drawn from twenty clusters in 1,024 dimensions gives a farthest-to-nearest ratio of 3.47 rather than 1.15. That surviving contrast is precisely what every approximate index exploits, and it explains a fact you will otherwise find mysterious: approximate search works far better on real embeddings than the theory of uniform points predicts.</p>" },

    { title: "The Historical Alternative: Locality-Sensitive Hashing",
      html: "<p>Before graphs won, the theoretically respectable answer was <strong>locality-sensitive hashing</strong>, from Piotr Indyk and Rajeev Motwani in 1998. It is worth one page because it is the idea every interviewer half-remembers and because its failure mode explains what graph indexes do better.</p>" +
            "<p>A normal hash function scatters similar inputs to unrelated buckets, which is exactly wrong here. An <strong>LSH family</strong> is a set of hash functions with the opposite property: nearby points collide with high probability and distant points collide with low probability. The cleanest example is <strong>random-hyperplane hashing</strong>, from Moses Charikar in 2002: pick a random vector, and hash a point to 1 or 0 depending on which side of that hyperplane it falls. Two vectors separated by angle θ land on the same side with probability 1 − θ/π, so collision probability decreases smoothly with cosine distance. Datar and colleagues gave the Euclidean version in 2004 by projecting onto a random direction and taking the floor of the offset divided by a bucket width.</p>" +
            "<p>One bit is a weak signal, so LSH amplifies it twice. Concatenate k hashes into one key, which makes collisions require agreement k times over and drives the false-positive rate down. Then build L independent tables, so a true neighbour missed by one table has L chances, which drives the false-negative rate down. Query by hashing the query in each table, taking the union of the buckets it lands in as candidates, and scoring those exactly. Recall is tuned by k and L, and the guarantee is probabilistic and provable, which is why the method dominated the literature for a decade.</p>" +
            "<p>It lost on constants. Reaching recall above 0.9 on real data needs many tables, and the memory for L copies of the point ids plus the cost of probing L buckets and scoring a large candidate union adds up to something a graph index beats by an order of magnitude at the same recall. The deeper reason is that LSH is a <strong>data-independent index</strong>: the hyperplanes are random and know nothing about where your vectors actually sit, so the method cannot exploit the clustering that Section 3 showed is the only thing making high-dimensional search tractable. Chapter 11's graph is built from the data and inherits its structure. Where LSH survives is where its cheapness is the point, notably near-duplicate detection with MinHash, which Chapter 4 used at ingest.</p>" },

    { title: "The Harness: Ground Truth, Recall@k, and Percentiles",
      html: "<p>Every claim in the next three chapters is a measurement, so build the thing that measures now. It has three parts, and none of them is complicated.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Ground truth comes from brute force.</strong> Take a sample of queries, ideally the labelled set from Chapter 8 so that retrieval quality and answer quality share a corpus, and compute the exact top-k for each with a scan. This is the one time the scan's cost does not matter, because it runs offline, once, and its output is a file. Store query id, the ordered true neighbour ids, and their scores. Regenerate it whenever the corpus or the embedding model changes, which by Chapter 8's migration procedure is the same moment you re-embed.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Recall@k is a set overlap, not a ranking metric.</strong> For each query, take the k ids the index returned and the k true ids, and compute the size of their intersection divided by k. Average over queries. Note what it does not measure: order within the k, and whether the chunks were useful to a reader. Those are Chapter 25's recall and nDCG against human labels, which is a different question with a confusingly similar name. Recall@k here answers one narrow thing, how much of exact search this index threw away, and keeping the two ideas separate will save you an argument later.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Latency is a distribution, so report percentiles.</strong> The mean hides the tail, and the tail is what users feel. Measure p50, <strong>p95 latency</strong>, and p99 over at least a few hundred queries, after a warm-up pass that lets caches fill, and record throughput separately with concurrency set the way production runs it. A single-threaded p50 tells you almost nothing about a server answering fifty concurrent requests.</p>" +
            "<pre><code>def recall_at_k(returned_ids, true_ids, k):\n    \"\"\"Fraction of the exact top-k that the index actually returned.\"\"\"\n    return len(set(returned_ids[:k]) &amp; set(true_ids[:k])) / k\n\n# sweep one search-time parameter and print the curve the next three chapters live on\nfor ef in (10, 20, 40, 80, 160, 320):\n    index.set_ef(ef)\n    lat, rec = [], []\n    for q, truth in ground_truth.items():\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        got = index.search(queries[q], k=10)\n        lat.append(time.perf_counter() - t0)\n        rec.append(recall_at_k(got, truth, 10))\n    lat.sort()\n    print(f\"ef={ef:4d}  recall@10={sum(rec)/len(rec):.3f}  \"\n          f\"p50={lat[len(lat)//2]*1000:6.2f}ms  p95={lat[int(len(lat)*0.95)]*1000:6.2f}ms\")</code></pre>" +
            "<p>That loop is the shape of every experiment in Chapters 11, 12, and 13, and its output is the curve of Section 2. Run it against a flat index first: recall will be exactly 1.000 at every setting, which confirms the harness is correct before you trust it to judge anything approximate. Chapter 25 extends it with the answer-quality metrics, and Chapter 36's benchmark is this loop pointed at three systems at once.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"Why not exact search?\" is answered with numbers, not adjectives: linear in N and d, 42 milliseconds for a million 384-dimensional vectors on a laptop, and a memory bill of 6.1 GB at a million by 1,536. \"Why did nobody use k-d trees?\" wants distance concentration and the collapse of pruning above about twenty dimensions. \"If distances all concentrate, why does any of this work?\" is the good question, and the answer is that real embeddings are clustered and have low intrinsic dimensionality, which is exactly the case Beyer's theorem exempts.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol><li><strong>Trace.</strong> Using the measured table in Section 1, predict the per-query latency and memory for 2 million vectors at 768 dimensions on the same laptop, then say which of the two predictions you trust less and why. What you should find: about 170 ms and 6.1 GB by linear scaling, and you should trust the latency less, because 6.1 GB exceeds what the machine can hold comfortably and the 500,000 × 1,536 row already shows the scan losing roughly half its throughput under memory pressure.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Reproduce the concentration experiment: 10,000 points from a standard normal in d = 2, 16, 64, 256, and 1,024, and report the farthest-to-nearest distance ratio for one query. Then rerun at d = 1,024 with the points drawn from twenty well-separated clusters. What you should find: the ratio falling from roughly 200 to roughly 1.15 as dimension grows, and rising back to roughly 3.5 once the data is clustered. Write one sentence explaining why that second number is the reason your vector database works.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Time a brute-force scan over your own Chapter 7 embeddings at your real N and d, single-threaded, and compute p50 and p95 over 200 queries. Compare against your product's latency budget for the retrieval stage. What you should find, for most corpora under a hundred thousand chunks: the scan already fits, and the index of Chapter 11 is an optimization you have not yet earned.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Write the harness of Section 5 against your corpus: a ground-truth file from exact search over your labelled queries, a recall@10 function, and a sweep loop that prints recall with p50 and p95 for each setting of one parameter. Validate it by running it against exact search itself and asserting recall is 1.000 for every query. Keep it; Chapters 11, 12, 13, and 36 all report through it.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Implement random-hyperplane LSH in under forty lines: k random hyperplanes producing a k-bit key, L tables, insert and query by bucket union, then exact scoring of the candidates. Measure recall@10 and candidates examined at k = 8 with L = 1, 4, 16, and 64 on 100,000 of your vectors. What you should find: recall climbing with L and the candidate set growing with it, so that reaching recall above 0.9 costs you a large fraction of the scan you were trying to avoid. That is the result that ended LSH's dominance, reproduced on your own data.</li></ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "exact k-nearest-neighbour search", d: "Returning the k vectors with the highest true similarity to a query, with recall of exactly 1 by construction." },
    { t: "brute-force search", d: "Computing the similarity to every vector in the corpus and keeping the best k; also called a flat index." },
    { t: "flat index", d: "A vector index with no structure beyond the stored vectors, searched by a full scan." },
    { t: "SIMD", d: "Single instruction, multiple data: CPU instructions that apply one operation to several values at once, which is why a dot-product scan runs near hardware peak." },
    { t: "recall@k", d: "The fraction of the exact top-k that an approximate index actually returned, measured against brute-force ground truth." },
    { t: "Pareto frontier", d: "The set of parameter settings not beaten on both recall and speed at once; the only part of a benchmark plot worth comparing." },
    { t: "ANN-Benchmarks", d: "The shared harness (Aumüller, Bernhardsson & Faithfull 2018) that sweeps each index's parameters and plots recall against queries per second." },
    { t: "search-time parameter", d: "An index setting that moves the recall-speed trade-off without rebuilding, such as HNSW's ef_search or IVF's nprobe." },
    { t: "build-time parameter", d: "An index setting that requires re-indexing to change, moving the entire recall-speed curve and usually the memory cost with it." },
    { t: "curse of dimensionality", d: "The family of effects by which geometric intuitions and space-partitioning algorithms fail as dimension grows." },
    { t: "distance concentration", d: "The convergence of the farthest-to-nearest distance ratio toward 1 as dimension grows, proved for independent identically distributed dimensions by Beyer et al. 1999." },
    { t: "intrinsic dimensionality", d: "The dimension of the manifold data actually occupies, which for text embeddings is far below the number of coordinates and is why approximate search still works." },
    { t: "locality-sensitive hashing", d: "A family of hash functions under which nearby points collide with high probability and distant points rarely do (Indyk & Motwani 1998)." },
    { t: "random-hyperplane hashing", d: "Charikar's 2002 LSH family that hashes a vector by which side of a random hyperplane it falls on, with collision probability 1 − θ/π." },
    { t: "data-independent index", d: "An index whose structure is chosen without looking at the data, such as LSH's random hyperplanes; it cannot exploit clustering, which is why graph indexes overtook it." },
    { t: "ground truth", d: "The exact top-k for a set of queries, computed once by brute force and stored, against which every approximate index is scored." },
    { t: "p95 latency", d: "The latency below which 95 percent of queries complete; the tail statistic that a mean hides and a user feels." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "A scan over 100,000 vectors at 384 dimensions takes 4.3 ms on a laptop. Roughly what should 1,000,000 vectors at the same dimension take, and what could make the estimate wrong?",
      opts: ["About 43 ms, but if the index no longer fits comfortably in memory the scan becomes bandwidth-bound and the linear estimate is optimistic", "About 4.3 ms, because a scan over vectors is logarithmic in N once the index has been built over all of them and held in memory", "About 430 ms, because the cost of a scan grows with the square of the number of vectors being searched", "About 8.6 ms, because SIMD amortises most of the extra work across the wider batch of vectors"], a: 0,
      why: "The measured value was 42.2 ms, almost exactly ten times. The chapter's 500,000 × 1,536 row shows throughput dropping from roughly 8 to 4.5 billion multiply-adds per second once the working set stops fitting, which bends the linear curve upward." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A team's corpus is 60,000 chunks at 768 dimensions and their retrieval latency budget is 50 ms. What does this chapter recommend?",
      opts: ["Build an HNSW index immediately, because a flat scan never scales and the rebuild cost only grows the longer it is left and the corpus keeps growing", "Reduce the dimension with Matryoshka truncation first, so the scan has less work to do per vector on every query", "Run a flat scan: it answers in a few milliseconds with recall of exactly 1, no build step, no parameters, and no rebuild after writes", "Shard the corpus across four machines so each one scans only a quarter of the vectors in parallel with the rest"], a: 2,
      why: "Below roughly a hundred thousand vectors the scan already fits the budget, and pgvector runs one when no index exists. An approximate index is an optimization to add when a measured budget demands it." },
    { type: "blank", q: "The convergence of the farthest-to-nearest distance ratio toward 1 as dimension grows is called distance ____.", a: ["concentration"],
      why: "Beyer, Goldstein, Ramakrishnan and Shaft proved it in 1999 for independent identically distributed dimensions. Measured on 10,000 random points, the ratio falls from about 211 at two dimensions to 1.15 at 1,024." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Why did k-d trees and R-trees stop being used for vector search above roughly twenty dimensions?",
      opts: ["They cannot store float32 values", "They require the data to be sorted, which embeddings are not", "Their memory cost grows exponentially with dimension", "They prune branches whose bounding region is farther than the best candidate so far; when every distance concentrates near the mean, almost nothing can be pruned and the tree visits nearly every leaf while also chasing pointers"], a: 3,
      why: "The pruning bound is what concentration destroys. Graph and quantization indexes replaced trees because neither depends on that bound." },
    { type: "mc", q: "If distances concentrate in high dimensions, why does approximate nearest-neighbour search work well on 1,536-dimensional text embeddings?",
      opts: ["Because cosine similarity is immune to concentration", "Because the theorem assumes independent identically distributed dimensions and exempts clustered data and low intrinsic dimensionality, both of which describe text embeddings; the chapter's clustered experiment gives a ratio of 3.47 rather than 1.15", "Because 1,536 is below the threshold where concentration begins", "Because normalization to unit length removes the effect"], a: 1,
      why: "Beyer et al. state the exceptions themselves. Embeddings occupy a low-dimensional manifold and cluster by topic, and that surviving contrast is exactly what every index in the next three chapters exploits." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the steps of building the measurement harness:", items: ["compute the exact top-k for each labelled query with a brute-force scan and store it as ground truth", "run the approximate index and compute recall@k as the overlap with the stored truth", "sort the per-query latencies and report p50 and p95 rather than the mean", "sweep one search-time parameter and plot recall against throughput"],
      why: "Ground truth first, because everything is scored against it; validate the harness by running it against exact search, where recall must come out at exactly 1.000 before you trust any approximate result." },
    { type: "mc", q: "An index reports recall@10 of 0.90 against brute-force ground truth. What does that number mean, and what does it not mean?",
      opts: ["It means 90 percent of retrieved chunks were relevant to the user; it does not measure speed", "It means the index returned 9 of the exact top 10 on average; it says nothing about whether those chunks were useful to a reader, which is Chapter 25's separate question", "It means the embedding model is 90 percent accurate", "It means 10 percent of queries returned nothing"], a: 1,
      why: "Recall@k here measures only how much of exact search the index discarded. Human-judged relevance is a different metric that shares the word recall, and conflating them causes real arguments." },
    { type: "mc", q: "You need higher recall from a deployed HNSW index this afternoon, without re-indexing. Which class of parameter do you reach for?",
      opts: ["A build-time parameter such as M, which changes graph connectivity", "A storage-time parameter such as switching from int8 to float32", "A search-time parameter such as ef_search, which moves along the recall-speed curve with no rebuild", "None; recall is fixed at build time"], a: 2,
      why: "Search-time parameters move you along the existing curve immediately; build-time parameters move the curve itself and need a re-index; storage-time changes need re-encoding. Knowing which is which tells you whether the fix takes an afternoon or a night." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Random-hyperplane LSH hashes a vector by which side of a random hyperplane it falls on. Two vectors separated by angle θ collide with probability 1 − θ/π. Why did this family lose to graph indexes in practice?",
      opts: ["Its collision probability is not related to cosine similarity", "It cannot be implemented for cosine distance", "It offers no accuracy guarantee, unlike graphs", "It is data-independent: the hyperplanes are random and ignore where the vectors actually sit, so it cannot exploit the clustering that makes high-dimensional search tractable, and reaching high recall needs many tables and a large candidate set"], a: 3,
      why: "LSH's guarantees are real and provable; it lost on constants. A graph built from the data inherits the corpus's structure, which is exactly the signal Section 3 showed is the only thing left in high dimensions." },
    { type: "mc", q: "An ann-benchmarks plot shows algorithm A above algorithm B across the whole recall range. What have you learned, and what have you not?",
      opts: ["A is faster at equal recall on that dataset; you have not learned its memory cost, its build time, or whether the result transfers to your own embeddings", "A is strictly better and should be chosen", "B has higher recall, since lower curves indicate accuracy", "Nothing, because the plot only shows one parameter setting per algorithm"], a: 0,
      why: "The axes are recall and queries per second only. Memory and build time are absent, the dataset may not resemble yours, and the paper's own finding is that very different approaches reach comparable trade-offs, so the decision usually turns on the hidden axes." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 11 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): Malkov & Yashunin 2016/2018,
   "Efficient and robust approximate nearest neighbor search using Hierarchical
   Navigable Small World graphs" (arXiv 1603.09320: layer assignment with
   mL = 1/ln(M), Mmax0 = 2M, ef and ef_construction, the neighbour-selection
   heuristic and its benefit on clustered data, logarithmic scaling, the stated
   analogy to skip lists); Malkov, Ponomarenko, Logvinov & Krylov 2014, NSW
   (Information Systems); Pugh 1990, skip lists (probabilistic levels, descend
   taking long hops first); pgvector README (m = 16, ef_construction = 64,
   hnsw.ef_search = 40, builds much faster inside maintenance_work_mem,
   max_parallel_maintenance_workers default 2, iterative scans with
   hnsw.max_scan_tuples = 20000, HNSW beats IVFFlat on speed-recall but builds
   slower and uses more memory); Qdrant indexing docs (m = 16, ef_construct = 100,
   full_scan_threshold 10,000 KB, payload-aware extra edges for filtered HNSW,
   ACORN from v1.16); hnswlib README (mark_deleted soft deletes, replace_deleted
   to reuse space, ef not persisted with the index, add_items not thread-safe
   with knn_query); Lucene HnswGraph javadoc (maxConn = M, beamWidth =
   ef_construction, per-segment graphs, concurrent search but unsafe updates).
   ALL RECALL, DISTANCE-COUNT, LEVEL-COUNT AND MEMORY NUMBERS BELOW WERE MEASURED
   by implementing HNSW from the paper and running it: 5,000 unit vectors in 64
   dimensions, 100 queries, ground truth by exact scan, ef_construction = 64.
   The implementation is in resources/hnsw.py. */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 11, part: 2, title: "HNSW: A Graph That Finds Neighbours in Microseconds",
  blurb: "The index inside pgvector, Qdrant, Elasticsearch, Weaviate, and Milvus is one idea: a graph where every vector links to a few neighbours, stacked in layers so a search takes long hops first and short hops last. You will build one point by point, watch the greedy search descend, and turn the two knobs that matter, M and ef, while recall is measured against brute force at every setting. The numbers in this chapter come from an implementation written from the paper and run, including the result that decides how well your index will work: the same graph reaches 0.79 recall on random vectors and 0.93 on clustered ones, for a quarter of the work.",
  sections: [
    { title: "The Problem: Sub-Linear Search Without a Tree",
      html: "<p>Chapter 10 left you with a scan that costs 42 milliseconds per query at a million vectors and a proof that space-partitioning trees cannot help, because distance concentration destroys the bound they prune with. So the index cannot divide space. What else is there?</p>" +
            "<p>Navigate it. Build a graph in which each vector is a node linked to a few of its nearest neighbours, start anywhere, and walk greedily: look at the current node's neighbours, move to whichever is closest to the query, and repeat until no neighbour is closer. If the graph has the right edges, this converges on the true nearest neighbour after visiting a tiny fraction of the nodes, and nothing was ever pruned by a bound.</p>" +
            "<p>The naive version fails in a way worth seeing, because it explains every design choice that follows. Link each node only to its k closest neighbours and the graph becomes a lattice of tight local neighbourhoods with no shortcuts. A greedy walk from a random start crawls across the space one short step at a time, so the number of hops grows with the diameter of the graph rather than with the logarithm of N. Worse, the walk gets stuck: greedy search stops at a <strong>local minimum</strong>, a node closer to the query than all of its neighbours but not the global best, and a graph of purely local links is full of them.</p>" +
            "<p>How much do shortcuts help? The six-degrees intuition is quantitative. In a ring where each node links only to its immediate neighbours, crossing N nodes takes on the order of N hops. Add a small number of random long-range links per node and the expected hop count collapses to something like log N. For a million nodes that is the difference between hundreds of thousands of steps and roughly twenty. The links do not need to be well chosen; they need to exist and to vary in length.</p>" +
            "<p>The fix is the property Stanley Milgram's letter experiment made famous and Jon Kleinberg formalised: a <strong>small-world graph</strong> combines dense local clustering with a few long-range links, and greedy routing across one takes a number of hops that grows logarithmically rather than linearly. Yury Malkov and colleagues turned that into an index in 2014 with the <strong>Navigable Small World</strong> graph, and the trick was almost free. Insert points one at a time, linking each new point to its nearest neighbours among those inserted so far. Early points, inserted when the graph is sparse, acquire links to whatever happened to be nearby at the time, and those become the long-range shortcuts. Later points get genuinely local links. Insertion order manufactures the small-world structure.</p>" +
            "<p>NSW had one flaw: the long links and the short links are mixed in one graph, so a search cannot choose to take long hops first, and it wastes work following short edges early and long edges late. The 2016 fix, from Malkov and Dmitry Yashunin, was to separate them by height, which is where this chapter begins.</p>" },

    { title: "The Structure: Layers, Entry Point, and M Links per Node",
      html: "<p><strong>HNSW</strong>, the Hierarchical Navigable Small World graph, stacks proximity graphs in layers. Every node lives in layer 0. Each node also appears in some number of layers above it, chosen at random so that each successive layer holds exponentially fewer nodes. Layer 0 has all N; the next has roughly N/M; the top has a handful. A node present in layer 2 is present in layers 1 and 0 as well, with its own links in each.</p>" +
            "<p>The paper's own analogy is the right one: this is a <strong>skip list</strong> in a metric space. Bill Pugh's 1990 structure gives a sorted linked list express lanes by promoting elements to higher levels with fixed probability, and a search descends from the top taking long hops before short ones. HNSW does the same, with \"next in sorted order\" replaced by \"near in the metric\".</p>" +
            "<p>The level for a new node is drawn from an exponential distribution, <code>level = floor(−ln(U) · mL)</code> for a uniform random U, with the paper's recommended <strong>mL = 1 / ln(M)</strong>. That constant is chosen so that the expected number of layers is about log<sub>M</sub>(N), which is what makes the descent logarithmic. It is checkable: with N = 5,000, M = 16 gives mL = 0.36 and predicts about 3 layers, while M = 4 gives mL = 0.72 and predicts about 6. Running the implementation produced exactly 3 and 6.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 260' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='20' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>layers: exponentially fewer nodes going up; the search descends</text>" +
            "<g stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5' fill='#0f1729'><rect x='60' y='30' width='560' height='52' rx='6' transform='skewX(-16)'/><rect x='60' y='104' width='560' height='52' rx='6' transform='skewX(-16)'/><rect x='60' y='178' width='560' height='52' rx='6' transform='skewX(-16)'/></g>" +
            "<text x='648' y='60' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>layer 2: 2 nodes</text>" +
            "<text x='648' y='134' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>layer 1: ~N/M</text>" +
            "<text x='648' y='208' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>layer 0: all N</text>" +
            "<g fill='#4f7cff'><circle cx='180' cy='60' r='5'/><circle cx='400' cy='58' r='5'/></g>" +
            "<line x1='180' y1='60' x2='400' y2='58' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<g fill='#4f7cff'><circle cx='150' cy='134' r='4'/><circle cx='250' cy='128' r='4'/><circle cx='370' cy='132' r='4'/><circle cx='470' cy='126' r='4'/><circle cx='300' cy='138' r='4'/></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1'><line x1='150' y1='134' x2='250' y2='128'/><line x1='250' y1='128' x2='300' y2='138'/><line x1='300' y1='138' x2='370' y2='132'/><line x1='370' y1='132' x2='470' y2='126'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#4f7cff'><circle cx='120' cy='208' r='3'/><circle cx='165' cy='204' r='3'/><circle cx='210' cy='210' r='3'/><circle cx='255' cy='202' r='3'/><circle cx='300' cy='212' r='3'/><circle cx='345' cy='204' r='3'/><circle cx='390' cy='210' r='3'/><circle cx='435' cy='202' r='3'/><circle cx='480' cy='208' r='3'/><circle cx='525' cy='204' r='3'/></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='0.8'><line x1='120' y1='208' x2='165' y2='204'/><line x1='165' y1='204' x2='210' y2='210'/><line x1='210' y1='210' x2='255' y2='202'/><line x1='255' y1='202' x2='300' y2='212'/><line x1='300' y1='212' x2='345' y2='204'/><line x1='345' y1='204' x2='390' y2='210'/><line x1='390' y1='210' x2='435' y2='202'/><line x1='435' y1='202' x2='480' y2='208'/><line x1='480' y1='208' x2='525' y2='204'/></g>" +
            "<circle cx='180' cy='60' r='8' fill='none' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='168' y='44' text-anchor='end' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>entry point</text>" +
            "<g stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2.5'><line x1='180' y1='60' x2='400' y2='58'/><line x1='400' y1='60' x2='370' y2='128'/><line x1='370' y1='134' x2='390' y2='206'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#ffd166'><circle cx='400' cy='58' r='5'/><circle cx='370' cy='132' r='4.5'/><circle cx='390' cy='210' r='4'/></g>" +
            "<text x='420' y='96' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>one long hop</text>" +
            "<text x='398' y='172' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>then shorter ones</text>" +
            "<circle cx='420' cy='232' r='5' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='432' y='236' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>query</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='252' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>measured: N=5,000 with M=16 built 3 layers; with M=4 it built 6, matching log_M(N) from mL = 1/ln(M)</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F11.1. Height separates long links from short ones. The descent covers most of the distance in the top layers, where there is almost nothing to look at.</div></div>" +
            "<p><strong>M</strong> is the number of links each node keeps per layer, and layer 0 is allowed <strong>2M</strong> because it carries all the nodes and needs the extra connectivity. M is the single most consequential build-time parameter: it sets recall, memory, and build time together. The measured graph at M = 16 held 26.8 edges per node averaged over all layers; at M = 32 it held 52.8.</p>" +
            "<h3>Which neighbours to keep</h3>" +
            "<p>Given a candidate set, the natural rule is to keep the M closest. The paper shows this is wrong and gives a <strong>neighbour-selection heuristic</strong> instead: consider candidates nearest first, and keep a candidate only if it is closer to the new node than it is to any candidate already kept. The effect is to reject a candidate that sits in a direction already covered, and to accept one that opens a new direction even if it is farther away.</p>" +
            "<p>The reason is connectivity. Take a node on the edge of one tight cluster with another cluster nearby. Its twenty nearest neighbours are all inside its own cluster, so keeping the closest M links every node in the cluster to every other and leaves no edge crossing the gap. A greedy search entering that cluster can never leave it: every neighbour of every node is worse than the current best, which is the definition of a local minimum. The heuristic keeps one link toward the other cluster because no already-kept neighbour lies in that direction. Malkov and Yashunin report that this matters most on clustered data and at high recall, which is exactly the regime a text corpus puts you in.</p>" },

    { title: "Search: Greedy Descent and the Candidate List of Size ef", viz: "hnswSearch", vizTitle: "Insert points, run the greedy search, and watch recall change with ef",
      html: "<p>Search has two phases, and only the second one is tunable.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Phase one, the descent.</strong> Start at the entry point, the node in the highest layer. In each layer above 0, walk greedily to the local minimum: repeatedly move to the neighbour closest to the query until no neighbour improves, then drop to the next layer down and continue from there. Each layer is sparse, so this is a handful of distance computations per layer, and it delivers you to a node that is already in roughly the right region.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Phase two, the layer-0 search.</strong> Here greedy is not enough, because a single path gets trapped. Instead HNSW keeps two structures: a <strong>candidate list</strong> of nodes to explore, ordered by distance, and a result set of the best <strong>ef</strong> found so far. Pop the closest unexplored candidate, look at all its neighbours, and for each unvisited one compute the distance and insert it into both structures if it beats the current worst of the ef results. Stop when the closest remaining candidate is worse than the worst result already held, because nothing reachable can improve the answer. Return the best k of the ef.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>ef</strong> is therefore a beam width, and it is the reason recall is not 1. A larger ef keeps more mediocre nodes alive as exploration candidates, and one of those mediocre nodes is frequently the bridge to a region containing a true neighbour that the greedy path would have abandoned. It also directly bounds the answer: ef must be at least k, and at ef = k the search keeps nothing in reserve and takes the first k it stumbles into.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='20' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>layer-0 search: the beam of width ef is what escapes a local minimum</text>" +
            "<circle cx='330' cy='140' r='7' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='330' y='162' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>query</text>" +
            "<g fill='#4f7cff'><circle cx='140' cy='170' r='5'/><circle cx='195' cy='140' r='5'/><circle cx='235' cy='185' r='5'/><circle cx='250' cy='105' r='5'/></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.2'><line x1='140' y1='170' x2='195' y2='140'/><line x1='195' y1='140' x2='250' y2='105'/><line x1='195' y1='140' x2='235' y2='185'/><line x1='250' y1='105' x2='235' y2='185'/></g>" +
            "<text x='170' y='210' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>entered here after the descent</text>" +
            "<g fill='#3ddc97'><circle cx='395' cy='95' r='5'/><circle cx='420' cy='150' r='5'/><circle cx='450' cy='115' r='5'/></g>" +
            "<text x='430' y='78' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>true top-10 live over here</text>" +
            "<line x1='250' y1='105' x2='395' y2='95' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/>" +
            "<text x='300' y='60' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>the bridge edge</text>" +
            "<circle cx='250' cy='105' r='11' fill='none' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<line x1='60' y1='232' x2='700' y2='232' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='250' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>the gold node ranks below its cluster-mates, so ef = 10 drops it and the search halts here; ef = 40 keeps it and crosses</text>" +
            "<rect x='500' y='150' width='230' height='68' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='512' y='168' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='9.5'>measured, uniform data, M=16:</text>" +
            "<text x='512' y='186' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>ef= 10  recall 0.455   7.6% of scan</text>" +
            "<text x='512' y='202' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>ef= 40  recall 0.794  19.1% of scan</text>" +
            "<text x='512' y='214' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>ef=160  recall 0.992  49.4% of scan</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F11.2. Recall is lost when the beam is too narrow to hold the one node that bridges to the right region. ef is the width of that beam.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>A worked search</h3><p>Follow one query through a graph of 5,000 vectors built at M = 16, which the measurement produced with three layers. The layer-2 graph holds a handful of nodes, so the descent from the entry point costs perhaps five distance computations and lands in the right half of the space. Layer 1 holds roughly 300 nodes; another twenty or so computations walk to the local minimum there. Both phases together are under thirty computations and have done the coarse work.</p><p>Layer 0 is where the budget goes. With ef = 40, the beam search from that starting node performed on average 957 distance computations, which is 19.1 percent of the 5,000 a scan would need, and returned 7.9 of the true top 10. Drop ef to 10 and it performed 379 computations, 7.6 percent, and returned 4.6 of the true 10. The descent did not change between those two runs. Every distance computation you added or removed was spent in the layer-0 beam, which is why ef is the only search-time knob worth having.</p><p>Where does a lost neighbour go? Almost never is it merely far away. The typical miss is a node sitting in a region the beam abandoned, reachable only through an intermediate node that was, at the moment of the decision, ranked eleventh. At ef = 10 that intermediate is dropped from the result set and never explored; at ef = 40 it survives long enough to be popped from the candidate list, and its neighbours turn out to include two of the true top 10. The beam is not buying more distance computations for their own sake. It is buying patience with unpromising nodes.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Try it.</strong> Insert fifty points in the machine above and run the search at ef = 10. Read the recall and the number of distance computations. Raise ef to 40: the recall climbs and the computations roughly triple. Now watch <em>which</em> node was missed at ef = 10; it is almost always reachable only through a node that was neither in the top ten nor visibly promising, which is the whole argument for keeping a beam rather than a path.</div>" },

    { title: "Insertion: Level Assignment, ef_construction, and Pruning",
      html: "<p>Insertion reuses the search. For a new node, first draw its level. Then descend from the entry point through every layer above that level using the same greedy walk, with a beam of exactly 1, purely to arrive at a good starting point. From the node's own level down to 0, do something different: run the full beam search with a beam of <strong>ef_construction</strong>, take the resulting candidates, apply the neighbour-selection heuristic to pick M of them, and link both ways.</p>" +
            "<p>The back-links are where the graph stays healthy. Adding an edge from a new node to an existing one also adds the reverse edge, which can push the existing node over its limit of M (or 2M at layer 0). When that happens, its full neighbour list is re-run through the same heuristic and trimmed. This is <strong>pruning</strong>, and it is how a node inserted early, when the graph was sparse and its links were poor, gradually acquires better ones as the neighbourhood fills in.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='170' y='20' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>keep the M closest</text>" +
            "<text x='560' y='20' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>the paper's heuristic</text>" +
            "<line x1='370' y1='32' x2='370' y2='200' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "<ellipse cx='150' cy='110' rx='85' ry='55' fill='none' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/>" +
            "<ellipse cx='305' cy='150' rx='45' ry='38' fill='none' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/>" +
            "<circle cx='150' cy='110' r='6' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='150' y='96' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>new node</text>" +
            "<g fill='#4f7cff'><circle cx='110' cy='90' r='4'/><circle cx='120' cy='135' r='4'/><circle cx='185' cy='90' r='4'/><circle cx='190' cy='135' r='4'/></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='150' y1='110' x2='110' y2='90'/><line x1='150' y1='110' x2='120' y2='135'/><line x1='150' y1='110' x2='185' y2='90'/><line x1='150' y1='110' x2='190' y2='135'/></g>" +
            "<circle cx='305' cy='150' r='4' fill='#7d8fb0'/>" +
            "<text x='170' y='190' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>no edge leaves the cluster:</text>" +
            "<text x='170' y='204' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>a search entering here is trapped</text>" +
            "<ellipse cx='540' cy='110' rx='85' ry='55' fill='none' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/>" +
            "<ellipse cx='690' cy='150' rx='42' ry='36' fill='none' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/>" +
            "<circle cx='540' cy='110' r='6' fill='#ffd166'/>" +
            "<g fill='#4f7cff'><circle cx='500' cy='90' r='4'/><circle cx='510' cy='135' r='4'/><circle cx='575' cy='90' r='4'/></g>" +
            "<circle cx='690' cy='150' r='4' fill='#3ddc97'/>" +
            "<g stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='540' y1='110' x2='500' y2='90'/><line x1='540' y1='110' x2='510' y2='135'/><line x1='540' y1='110' x2='575' y2='90'/></g>" +
            "<line x1='540' y1='110' x2='690' y2='150' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<circle cx='560' cy='140' r='4' fill='none' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='556' y='166' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>rejected: nearer to a kept</text><text x='556' y='178' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>neighbour than to the new node</text>" +
            "<text x='620' y='196' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>farther, but a new direction</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='222' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>same M in both panels. The heuristic spends one of its M links on reachability instead of on a fourth near-duplicate.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F11.3. Keeping the M closest neighbours builds cliques with no exits. The heuristic trades a little locality for the connectivity that greedy search needs.</div></div>" +
            "<pre><code>def insert(graph, v):\n    level = floor(-ln(random()) * mL)          # mL = 1 / ln(M)\n    ep    = [graph.entry_point]\n\n    for lc in range(graph.max_level, level, -1):   # above the new node's level:\n        ep = [greedy_nearest(v, ep, lc)]           #   beam of 1, navigate only\n\n    for lc in range(min(level, graph.max_level), -1, -1):\n        W     = search_layer(v, ep, ef_construction, lc)   # full beam search\n        nbrs  = select_heuristic(v, W, M)                  # not the M closest\n        graph.link(v, nbrs, lc)\n        for nb in nbrs:\n            graph.link(nb, [v], lc)                        # the back-link\n            limit = 2*M if lc == 0 else M\n            if graph.degree(nb, lc) &gt; limit:\n                graph.relink(nb, select_heuristic(nb, graph.neighbours(nb, lc), limit), lc)\n        ep = W                                             # descend from what we found\n\n    if level &gt; graph.max_level:\n        graph.entry_point, graph.max_level = v, level</code></pre>" +
            "<p>Two lines deserve attention. The <code>ep = W</code> at the end of the loop body carries the whole candidate set down to the next layer rather than a single node, so the lower layer starts its beam search from several good entry points instead of one. And the level draw happens <em>before</em> any search, which means a node's height is independent of where it sits in the space. That independence is what keeps the layer populations exponential and the structure balanced without any rebalancing logic, exactly as a skip list does.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>ef_construction</strong> is the same beam width as ef, applied at build time. Raising it gives the heuristic a better candidate pool to choose from, so the graph is better and every future query benefits, at the cost of a slower build. It does not change the graph's size, only its quality, which makes it the parameter to raise when recall is poor and memory is already tight.</p>" +
            "<h3>What the build costs</h3>" +
            "<p>Insertion is a search plus some link surgery, so building is N searches: about N log N distance computations, with constants set by M and ef_construction. In the measured implementation, holding ef_construction at 64 and quadrupling M from 4 to 16 raised build time from 2.4 to 10.3 seconds, and M = 32 took 29.5 seconds. Those absolute numbers are a pure-Python artefact and mean nothing, but the shape is real and matches what production libraries show: build cost grows faster than linearly in M, because every insertion both takes more work and triggers more re-pruning of its neighbours.</p>" +
            "<p>This is why every engine treats index construction as a batch operation with its own memory budget, and why Chapter 8's dual-write migration allocates hours rather than minutes to the backfill.</p>" },

    { title: "Recall, Measured: What M and ef Actually Buy",
      html: "<p>Everything above is mechanism. Here is the measurement, from an implementation written directly from the paper and run on 5,000 unit vectors in 64 dimensions with 100 queries, ground truth from an exact scan, ef_construction fixed at 64. Recall@10 and the number of distance computations, expressed as a percentage of the 5,000 a full scan would need:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>M = 4</strong> (7.5 edges per node) — ef = 10: recall 0.077. ef = 160: recall 0.610 at 17% of a scan. The graph is too sparsely connected to reach the answer at any beam width.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>M = 8</strong> (13.8 edges) — ef = 10: 0.228. ef = 160: 0.907 at 31%.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>M = 16</strong> (26.8 edges) — ef = 10: 0.455 at 7.6%. ef = 40: 0.794 at 19%. ef = 160: 0.992 at 49%.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>M = 32</strong> (52.8 edges) — ef = 10: 0.684. ef = 40: 0.944 at 32%. ef = 160: 0.999 at 70%.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Three things fall out. Recall rises with both parameters, but they are not interchangeable: no amount of ef rescues M = 4, because a beam can only explore edges that exist. Cost rises with recall along a curve that steepens near the top, so the last few points of recall cost as much as the first eighty. And at high enough ef the search examines half the corpus, at which point the graph has stopped being an optimization and you should reread Chapter 10's advice about flat indexes.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 270' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='18' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>measured recall@10 vs distance computations (N=5,000, d=64, ef_construction=64)</text>" +
            "<line x1='75' y1='215' x2='430' y2='215' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='75' y1='40' x2='75' y2='215' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='end'><text x='69' y='219'>0</text><text x='69' y='176'>0.25</text><text x='69' y='134'>0.50</text><text x='69' y='91'>0.75</text><text x='69' y='48'>1.00</text></g>" +
            "<text x='34' y='140' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9' transform='rotate(-90 34 140)'>recall@10</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='middle'><text x='75' y='231'>0</text><text x='146' y='231'>10%</text><text x='217' y='231'>20%</text><text x='288' y='231'>30%</text><text x='359' y='231'>40%</text><text x='430' y='231'>50%</text></g>" +
            "<text x='252' y='248' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>distance computations, as a share of a full scan</text>" +
            "<polyline points='91,202 100,192 118,172 147,146 198,111' fill='none' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='205' y='109' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>M=4</text>" +
            "<polyline points='104,176 123,152 154,119 204,88 293,60' fill='none' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='300' y='72' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='9'>M=8</text>" +
            "<polyline points='129,137 159,108 211,79 298,57 426,45' fill='none' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2.5'/>" +
            "<text x='336' y='40' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>M=16 (the default)</text>" +
            "<g fill='#3ddc97'><circle cx='129' cy='137' r='3.5'/><circle cx='159' cy='108' r='3.5'/><circle cx='211' cy='79' r='3.5'/><circle cx='298' cy='57' r='3.5'/><circle cx='426' cy='45' r='3.5'/></g>" +
            "<text x='222' y='94' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='8.5'>ef=40</text>" +
            "<polyline points='170,98 218,74 302,54 426,46' fill='none' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='150' y='88' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>M=32</text>" +
            "<line x1='460' y1='40' x2='460' y2='215' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "<text x='480' y='56' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>same graph, clustered data</text>" +
            "<text x='480' y='76' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>M=16, 25 topic clusters:</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5'><text x='480' y='96' fill='#3ddc97'>ef= 10   recall 0.854   4.1% of a scan</text>" +
            "<text x='480' y='113' fill='#3ddc97'>ef= 40   recall 0.928   5.5%</text>" +
            "<text x='480' y='130' fill='#3ddc97'>ef=160   recall 0.942   6.3%</text></g>" +
            "<text x='480' y='154' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>versus uniform random:</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5'><text x='480' y='172' fill='#ff5d73'>ef= 40   recall 0.794  19.1%</text></g>" +
            "<text x='480' y='198' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>better recall for a quarter of the work,</text>" +
            "<text x='480' y='210' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>because clustering survives concentration</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F11.4. All points measured. M moves the whole curve and needs a rebuild; ef moves you along it and does not. The right panel is why your production index beats these numbers.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>The result that matters most</h3>" +
            "<p>Every number above is on uniform random vectors, which Chapter 10 identified as the worst case: distances concentrate, there is no structure, and the graph has nothing to exploit. Rerunning the identical index at M = 16 on 5,000 vectors drawn from twenty-five clusters, the shape a real text corpus has, gives recall of <strong>0.928 at ef = 40 while touching 5.5 percent of the corpus</strong>, against 0.794 at 19.1 percent on random data. Better recall, for roughly a quarter of the work, from the same code and the same parameters.</p>" +
            "<p>That gap is the practical answer to a question Chapter 10 raised and could not settle: approximate indexes work in production because real embeddings cluster, and a graph built from the data inherits that clustering. It is also the reason to distrust benchmarks on synthetic vectors, in either direction.</p>" +
            "<p>One more feature of the clustered run is worth noticing, because it will happen to you. Recall climbs from 0.854 at ef = 10 to 0.928 at ef = 40, then stalls: 0.942 at ef = 80 and 0.942 at ef = 160, with the extra beam buying nothing. When ef stops paying, the remaining loss is not a search problem, it is a graph problem, and the fix is M or ef_construction and a rebuild. Chapter 10's distinction between search-time and build-time parameters is how you read that plateau correctly instead of turning the wrong knob all afternoon.</p>" },

    { title: "The Ugly Parts: Deletes, Memory, and Building Inside maintenance_work_mem",
      html: "<p>Three practical facts that no diagram of layers prepares you for.</p>" +
            "<h3>Deletes are not deletes</h3>" +
            "<p>Removing a node from a proximity graph is genuinely hard: its neighbours' links must be repaired, and repairing them can disconnect regions that were only reachable through it. So implementations do not remove it. hnswlib marks the element deleted, which filters it from results while leaving it in the graph as a routing node, and offers a separate mode where a later insertion overwrites a deleted slot to reclaim the memory. Lucene and Elasticsearch handle it at a different level: the graph is per segment and immutable, deletes are a bitmap, and space returns only when a background merge rewrites the segment, which is the same machinery Chapter 9 described for postings.</p>" +
            "<p>The consequence is that a workload with heavy churn degrades in a way that looks like nothing: recall drifts down as the graph fills with tombstones that still cost distance computations, latency creeps up, and no error is logged. The remedy is periodic rebuilding, which Chapter 29 puts on a schedule. Note also that deleting the entry point is a special case every implementation handles explicitly, because the search has to start somewhere.</p>" +
            "<h3>The graph is smaller than you fear, unless you quantized</h3>" +
            "<p>Each node stores up to 2M links at layer 0 and M per upper layer, as 4-byte integers. The measured average at M = 16 was 26.8 edges per node, so about 107 bytes of graph per vector. Set against the vector itself, the overhead depends entirely on how big the vector is:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>1,536-dimensional float32</strong> (6,144 bytes) — the graph adds 1.7 percent.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>768-dimensional float32</strong> (3,072 bytes) — 3.4 percent.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>384-dimensional float32</strong> (1,536 bytes) — 6.5 percent, and 12 percent at M = 32.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>768-dimensional int8</strong> (768 bytes) — 12.2 percent, and 21.6 percent at M = 32.</li></ul>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 210' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'><text x='20' y='20' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>bytes per indexed vector: the graph is fixed, the vector is not (M = 16, measured 26.8 edges/node)</text><g font-size='9.5' fill='#c9d6ea'><text x='20' y='50'>1536d f32</text><rect x='140' y='38' width='492' height='16' rx='3' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='632' y='38' width='9' height='16' rx='2' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='650' y='50'>6,251 B</text><text x='20' y='78'>768d f32</text><rect x='140' y='66' width='246' height='16' rx='3' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='386' y='66' width='9' height='16' rx='2' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='404' y='78'>3,179 B   graph 3.4%</text><text x='20' y='106'>384d f32</text><rect x='140' y='94' width='123' height='16' rx='3' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='263' y='94' width='9' height='16' rx='2' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='281' y='106'>1,643 B   graph 6.5%</text><text x='20' y='134'>768d int8</text><rect x='140' y='122' width='62' height='16' rx='3' fill='#4f7cff'/><rect x='202' y='122' width='9' height='16' rx='2' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='220' y='134' fill='#ffd166'>875 B   graph 12.2%  (21.6% at M=32)</text></g><line x1='20' y1='152' x2='740' y2='152' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/><rect x='140' y='162' width='14' height='11' rx='2' fill='#4f7cff'/><text x='160' y='172' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>vector</text><rect x='230' y='162' width='14' height='11' rx='2' fill='#ffd166'/><text x='250' y='172' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>graph links (107 B, the same in every row)</text><text x='20' y='194' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>1M x 768d float32: 3.07 GB of vectors + 0.11 GB of graph. Quantize to int8 and it is 0.77 GB + the same 0.11 GB.</text></svg><div class='fig-cap'>F11.5. The graph costs the same 107 bytes per vector however you store the vector, so compression makes it proportionally larger, not smaller.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Read the last line carefully, because it is the trap. Chapter 7's quantization shrinks vectors by 4 to 32 times and does nothing at all to the graph, so the graph goes from a rounding error to a fifth of your index. A million 768-dimensional float32 vectors with an M = 16 graph is 3.07 GB of vectors plus 0.11 GB of graph. Quantize to int8 and it is 0.77 GB of vectors plus the same 0.11 GB. Chapter 12's product quantization pushes this further still, which is when people start asking why the index is mostly pointers.</p>" +
            "<h3>Build memory is a cliff, not a slope</h3>" +
            "<p>Construction touches the whole graph in random order, so it wants the graph resident. pgvector says so directly: an HNSW index builds much faster when the graph fits in <code>maintenance_work_mem</code>, and it emits a notice when it does not. Crossing that threshold does not slow the build by a few percent, it changes the access pattern from memory-random to disk-random. The operational recipe is to raise <code>maintenance_work_mem</code> for the build, raise <code>max_parallel_maintenance_workers</code> above its default of 2, and put both back afterwards. Budget the build; pgvector's own documentation notes that HNSW buys better speed-recall than IVFFlat and pays for it in build time and memory.</p>" },

    { title: "Parameters in the Wild, and What to Change First",
      html: "<p>The same three numbers appear in every engine under slightly different names.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>pgvector</strong> — <code>m = 16</code> and <code>ef_construction = 64</code> at build, <code>hnsw.ef_search = 40</code> at query time as a session setting. It also offers iterative scans for filtered queries, <code>hnsw.iterative_scan</code> with <code>hnsw.max_scan_tuples</code> defaulting to 20,000, which Chapter 13 covers because filtering is where HNSW gets genuinely difficult.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Qdrant</strong> — <code>m = 16</code>, <code>ef_construct = 100</code>, plus a <code>full_scan_threshold</code> of 10,000 KB below which it runs a flat scan instead, which is Chapter 10's advice implemented as a default. Qdrant also builds extra payload-aware edges for filtered search, and its documentation warns to create payload indexes before ingesting so the graph is built with them.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Lucene, Elasticsearch, OpenSearch</strong> — <code>maxConn</code> is M and <code>beamWidth</code> is ef_construction. The graph is per segment, so a search touches one graph per segment and merges the results, and recall depends on segment count in a way single-graph engines do not have.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>hnswlib</strong> — the reference implementation, and the one to read. Note one sharp edge from its documentation: <code>ef</code> is not saved with the index, so a process that loads an index and forgets to set it gets the library default rather than the value you tuned.</li></ul>" +
            "<h3>What to change first</h3>" +
            "<p>A decision procedure, in order, assuming Chapter 10's harness is running.</p>" +
            "<ol><li><strong>Measure recall before touching anything.</strong> Against brute-force ground truth on your own vectors. Most HNSW complaints are chunking or embedding problems, and the index is innocent.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>If recall is low, raise ef_search.</strong> It costs nothing but latency, needs no rebuild, and takes effect on the next query. Sweep it and plot the curve.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>If ef_search stops helping, the graph is the problem.</strong> That plateau is the signal from the previous section. Raise <code>ef_construction</code> first, since it improves graph quality without adding a byte of memory, and rebuild.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Raise M last.</strong> It improves recall the most and costs memory, build time, and query time together. Going from 16 to 32 doubled the graph in the measurements above and roughly tripled build time.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>If latency is the problem instead, lower ef_search</strong> and recover the recall with a reranker (Chapter 16) over a slightly larger k, which is often cheaper than a bigger graph.</li></ol>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"How does HNSW work?\" wants layers with exponentially fewer nodes, greedy descent taking long hops first, and a beam of width ef at layer 0. \"Why layers?\" wants the skip-list analogy and the NSW problem they fixed, mixing long and short edges in one graph. \"Why is recall not 1?\" wants the beam and the local minimum it escapes. \"What would you tune?\" wants ef_search first because it needs no rebuild, and the strong answer names the plateau that tells you to stop and rebuild instead. \"What happens on delete?\" separates people who have run this in production from people who have read about it.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol><li><strong>Trace.</strong> With M = 16, compute mL = 1/ln(M) and the expected number of layers for N = 10,000, then repeat for M = 4. What you should find: mL of 0.36 and about 3 layers, against 0.72 and about 6. Then explain why more layers is not better, given that each extra layer adds a descent step but the top layers hold almost no nodes.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> Using the measured table in Section 5, find the cheapest configuration reaching recall@10 above 0.9 on the uniform data, counting cost as distance computations. What you should find: M = 16 at ef = 80 (0.924 at 31.4 percent) beats M = 32 at ef = 40 (0.944 at 32.0 percent) only on cost, not on recall, and M = 8 at ef = 160 reaches 0.907 at 30.7 percent. State which you would ship and why, given that M also costs memory and build time that the table does not show.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Build an HNSW index over your Chapter 7 embeddings with hnswlib or pgvector at the defaults, and sweep ef_search from 10 to 320 using Chapter 10's harness. Plot recall@10 against p95 latency. What you should find: a curve much better than this chapter's uniform-data numbers, because your embeddings are clustered, and a knee beyond which latency grows while recall does not.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Take the same corpus and delete a fifth of the vectors, using soft deletes, without rebuilding. Re-measure recall@10 and latency. Then rebuild and measure again. What you should find: recall down and latency up on the un-rebuilt index, with no error anywhere, and both recovered by the rebuild. This is the failure mode of Section 6 reproduced on your own data.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Implement HNSW from the paper in about 120 lines: level assignment with mL = 1/ln(M), a search-layer routine taking a beam width, the neighbour-selection heuristic, and insertion with back-links and pruning. Validate against brute force and reproduce this chapter's table on 5,000 random 64-dimensional vectors. What you should find: recall within a few points of the numbers in Section 5, and layer counts matching log<sub>M</sub>(N). A reference implementation is in the course resources; write yours before reading it. This is PocketRAG's Chapter 34.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Extend your implementation with the two experiments that make the chapter's argument: run it on uniform random vectors and on clustered ones at identical parameters, and report recall and distance computations for both. What you should find: substantially better recall on clustered data at a fraction of the cost, reproducing the gap between 0.928 at 5.5 percent and 0.794 at 19.1 percent. Write one paragraph connecting this to Chapter 10's theorem and its exceptions.</li></ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "small-world graph", d: "A graph combining dense local clustering with a few long-range links, across which greedy routing takes a number of hops growing logarithmically rather than linearly." },
    { t: "Navigable Small World", d: "Malkov et al.'s 2014 index in which inserting points one at a time makes early links serve as long-range shortcuts." },
    { t: "HNSW", d: "Hierarchical Navigable Small World: a stack of proximity graphs with exponentially fewer nodes per layer, searched by greedy descent and a beam at layer 0." },
    { t: "local minimum", d: "A node closer to the query than all of its neighbours but not among the true nearest, where a purely greedy walk stops." },
    { t: "layer", d: "One proximity graph in the HNSW stack; layer 0 holds every node and each layer above holds roughly a factor of M fewer." },
    { t: "entry point", d: "The node in the highest layer, where every search begins." },
    { t: "M", d: "The number of links each node keeps per layer, doubled at layer 0; the build-time parameter that sets recall, memory, and build time together." },
    { t: "mL", d: "The level-assignment constant, 1/ln(M) in the paper, chosen so that the expected number of layers is about log base M of N." },
    { t: "ef", d: "The beam width at layer 0: the number of best candidates the search keeps alive, which bounds recall and must be at least k." },
    { t: "ef_construction", d: "The beam width used during insertion, which improves graph quality and build time without changing the graph's size." },
    { t: "candidate list", d: "The distance-ordered set of nodes the layer-0 search still intends to explore, distinct from the result set of the best ef found." },
    { t: "neighbour-selection heuristic", d: "The rule that keeps a candidate only if it is closer to the new node than to any already-kept neighbour, preserving links that cross between clusters." },
    { t: "pruning", d: "Re-running the neighbour-selection heuristic on an existing node whose back-links have pushed it past its link limit." },
    { t: "soft delete", d: "Marking a node as removed so it is filtered from results while remaining in the graph as a routing node, because true removal can disconnect the graph." },
    { t: "greedy descent", d: "The upper-layer phase of HNSW search, which walks to the local minimum in each layer with a beam of one before dropping down." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "Why does HNSW use layers rather than a single navigable small-world graph?",
      opts: ["Layers reduce memory by storing each vector once per level", "In one graph the long and short edges are mixed, so a search cannot take long hops first; height separates them, giving a logarithmic descent", "Layers allow deletion without repairing links", "Each layer uses a different distance metric"], a: 1,
      why: "This is the 2016 paper's stated improvement over NSW, and the reason it describes itself as a skip list in a metric space: express lanes first, fine steps last." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A team keeps the M closest neighbours for each node instead of applying the paper's selection heuristic. Recall collapses on their clustered corpus. Why?",
      opts: ["The closest neighbours are cheaper to compute, so distances are less accurate", "The graph exceeds Mmax0 at layer 0", "Nearest neighbours change the level assignment", "Keeping the closest neighbours makes each cluster a clique with no edge leaving it, so greedy search that enters a cluster is trapped in a local minimum"], a: 3,
      why: "The heuristic keeps a candidate only if it is closer to the new node than to any kept neighbour, which spends one link on a new direction rather than a fourth near-duplicate. Malkov and Yashunin report the gain is largest on clustered data at high recall." },
    { type: "blank", q: "The beam width kept by the layer-0 search, which bounds recall and must be at least k, is called ____.", a: ["ef", "ef_search"],
      why: "A wider beam keeps mediocre nodes alive as exploration candidates, and one of those is often the only bridge to a region holding a true neighbour." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Measured on 5,000 random vectors, M = 4 reaches only 0.610 recall@10 even at ef = 160, while M = 16 reaches 0.992. What does this show?",
      opts: ["ef_construction was too low for M = 4", "M and ef are interchangeable and 160 was still too small", "A beam can only explore edges that exist, so a graph too sparse to connect the right regions cannot be rescued by any search-time parameter", "Recall at M = 4 is limited by the number of layers, which was 6"], a: 2,
      why: "M is a build-time parameter that moves the whole curve; ef moves you along it. Sparse connectivity is a property of the graph, and no beam width creates an edge." },
    { type: "mc", q: "The same index at M = 16 and ef = 40 scored recall 0.794 touching 19.1% of the corpus on uniform random vectors, and 0.928 touching 5.5% on clustered vectors. What explains the gap?",
      opts: ["Real structure survives distance concentration, and a graph built from the data inherits that clustering; uniform random data is Chapter 10's worst case and has nothing to exploit", "The clustered run used a larger ef_construction", "Uniform vectors have higher intrinsic dimensionality by definition of the metric", "Clustered data has fewer distinct vectors to search"], a: 0,
      why: "This is Beyer et al.'s exemption measured inside an index. It is also why benchmarks on synthetic vectors mislead in both directions." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the phases of an HNSW search:", items: ["start at the entry point in the highest layer", "in each layer above 0, walk greedily to the local minimum with a beam of one, then descend", "at layer 0, explore with a beam of width ef, keeping a candidate list and a result set", "stop when the closest remaining candidate is worse than the worst result held, and return the best k"],
      why: "The descent is cheap because upper layers are nearly empty; all the tunable work happens in the layer-0 beam search, and the stopping rule is what makes it terminate early." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Recall on a production index climbs from 0.85 at ef_search = 10 to 0.93 at 40, then sits at 0.94 for ef = 80 and 160. What should you change?",
      opts: ["Keep raising ef_search; the plateau is a measurement artefact", "Lower k so that fewer true neighbours are required", "Switch the distance metric", "Raise ef_construction, or M, and rebuild: when the beam stops paying, the remaining loss is graph connectivity rather than search effort"], a: 3,
      why: "This plateau appeared in the chapter's own clustered measurements. Chapter 10's split between search-time and build-time parameters is exactly how to read it: the search-time knob is exhausted, so the fix needs a rebuild." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A team quantizes their 768-dimensional vectors from float32 to int8 to cut index memory by four, and finds the saving disappointing. Why?",
      opts: ["int8 requires a larger M to preserve recall", "Quantization shrinks the vectors but not the graph; at M = 16 the roughly 107 bytes of links per node go from 3.4% of the index to 12.2%, and at M = 32 to 21.6%", "The graph must store float32 distances alongside the links", "hnswlib does not support int8, so it silently converts back"], a: 1,
      why: "Links are 4-byte integers regardless of vector precision. The more you compress vectors, the more the index is made of pointers, which is the trap waiting in Chapter 12 as well." },
    { type: "mc", q: "What actually happens when you delete a vector from an hnswlib index?",
      opts: ["The node is removed and its neighbours' links are repaired immediately", "The index is rebuilt incrementally in the background", "The node is marked deleted and filtered from results but stays in the graph as a routing node; space returns only if a later insert replaces it", "Deletion is rejected unless the index was built with allow_deletes"], a: 2,
      why: "True removal can disconnect regions reachable only through that node. The cost is silent: tombstones still consume distance computations, so recall drifts down and latency up until a rebuild." },
    { type: "mc", q: "pgvector emits a notice that the HNSW graph does not fit in maintenance_work_mem. What is the practical consequence?",
      opts: ["The build's access pattern changes from memory-random to disk-random, so build time degrades sharply rather than gradually; raise maintenance_work_mem and the parallel worker count for the build", "The index will have lower recall once built", "The build falls back to IVFFlat", "Queries will be served from disk after the build"], a: 0,
      why: "Construction touches the whole graph in random order. This is a cliff rather than a slope, and it is why Chapter 8's migration budgets hours for the backfill." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 12 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): Jégou, Douze & Schmid 2011, "Product
   Quantization for Nearest Neighbor Search" (TPAMI: m sub-quantizers with 256
   centroids each so one byte per sub-vector, symmetric vs asymmetric distance,
   the query-time lookup table, the IVFADC non-exhaustive variant); Johnson,
   Douze & Jégou 2017, "Billion-scale similarity search with GPUs" (arXiv
   1702.08734: 95M-image k-NN graph in 35 minutes and a billion-vector graph in
   under 12 hours on 4 Titan X GPUs, 8.5x faster than prior GPU state of the art);
   Faiss wiki, "Guidelines to choose an index" (nlist between 4*sqrt(N) and
   16*sqrt(N) below 1M, IVF65536_HNSW32 for 1M-10M, IVF262144 for 10M-100M,
   IVF1048576 for 100M-1B; 30x to 256x the centroid count in training vectors;
   HNSW memory d*4 + M*2*4 bytes per vector); pgvector README (IVFFlat lists =
   rows/1000 up to 1M rows and sqrt(rows) above, ivfflat.probes default 1 with
   sqrt(lists) as a starting point, index must be created after the table has
   data, faster build and less memory than HNSW but lower speed-recall);
   Subramanya, Devvrit, Kadekodi, Krishnaswamy & Simhadri 2019, DiskANN (NeurIPS:
   Vamana with RobustPrune and its alpha parameter, two build passes at alpha = 1
   then alpha > 1, a billion SIFT1B points on one 64 GB 16-core workstation at
   over 5,000 queries per second, under 3 ms mean latency and 95%+ 1-recall@1,
   against roughly 50% for comparable-memory methods; the design target of under
   ten SSD round trips per query, preferably five, with beam width 2 to 8);
   Guo, Sun, Lindgren, Geng, Simcha, Chern & Kumar 2019, ScaNN (arXiv 1908.10396:
   anisotropic quantization loss penalising the parallel component of the residual).
   ALL IVF AND PQ RECALL NUMBERS BELOW WERE MEASURED by implementing k-means IVF
   and product quantization and running them: 20,000 unit vectors in 128
   dimensions drawn from 40 clusters, 200 queries, ground truth by exact scan.
   The implementation is in resources/ivfpq.py. */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 12, part: 2, title: "IVF and Product Quantization: Trading Memory for Recall",
  blurb: "Chapter 11's graph wants every vector in RAM, and at a billion vectors that is a data centre. So stop storing vectors. An inverted-file index partitions the space into cells and searches a few of them; product quantization replaces each vector with a handful of one-byte codebook indices and scores it from a lookup table without ever reconstructing it. You will build both, watch a query near a cell boundary lose its nearest neighbour, and compress a 512-byte vector to 16 bytes. Then you will see the measured cost of that compression, which is larger than most write-ups admit, and the rescoring step that makes it work anyway.",
  sections: [
    { title: "The Problem: When the Vectors Do Not Fit",
      html: "<p>Chapter 11 ended with an index that is mostly vectors. A million 768-dimensional float32 vectors are 3.07 GB, and the HNSW graph over them adds 0.11 GB. That fits on one machine. Now scale it.</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>10 million × 768 d</strong> — 30.7 GB of vectors. A large but ordinary server.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>100 million × 1536 d</strong> — 614 GB. A cluster, or a very expensive single machine.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>1 billion × 1536 d</strong> — 6.1 TB of float32 before a single graph edge. This is the scale at which web-search and recommendation systems live, and it is why the techniques in this chapter were invented.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Two structurally different economies apply. Below roughly ten million vectors, memory is cheap enough that Chapter 11's advice holds: build HNSW, keep the vectors in float32, and spend your effort on recall. Above it, memory becomes the binding constraint, and every design decision is a trade of accuracy for bytes. The two families in this chapter attack the problem from opposite directions.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Inverted file indexes</strong> (IVF) do not compress anything. They reduce how many vectors you look at, by partitioning the space into cells and scanning only the cells near the query. This buys speed and nothing else, so it is the right answer when the vectors fit but the scan is too slow.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Product quantization</strong> (PQ) does not reduce how many vectors you look at. It shrinks each one, from 6,144 bytes to 16 or 32, so that a billion of them fit in memory. This buys bytes and costs accuracy, and it is the right answer when the vectors do not fit at all.</p>" +
            "<p>They compose, and the combination is what production billion-scale systems actually run: partition with IVF, compress with PQ, and repair the accuracy at the end with a rescoring pass. Section 4 is where the repair happens, and it is the part people skip.</p>" +
            "<p>The economics behind all of it is a price ratio. Server RAM costs roughly an order of magnitude more per gigabyte than SSD, and SSD costs roughly an order of magnitude more than object storage, while the latency ordering runs the other way: RAM in tens of nanoseconds, NVMe in tens of microseconds, object storage in milliseconds. Every technique in this chapter is a way of buying a cheaper tier without paying its latency in full. Product quantization moves data from RAM to less RAM. DiskANN in Section 5 moves it from RAM to SSD while keeping enough in memory to avoid the disk on most steps. Once you see the tiers, the designs stop looking like separate tricks.</p>" },

    { title: "IVF: Cells, nlist, nprobe, and the Boundary Problem", viz: "ivfCells", vizTitle: "Train Voronoi cells, click a query near an edge, and raise nprobe",
      html: "<p>Run k-means over the corpus with <strong>nlist</strong> centroids. Each vector is assigned to its nearest centroid, so the space is partitioned into Voronoi cells and the index is a list of vector ids per cell, which is why it is called an inverted file by analogy with Chapter 9's postings. At query time, compute the distance from the query to all nlist centroids, take the nearest <strong>nprobe</strong> of them, and scan only the vectors in those cells with exact distances. Cost is nlist centroid comparisons plus roughly nprobe/nlist of the corpus.</p>" +
            "<p>The whole method rests on an assumption that is nearly true: the nearest neighbours of a query lie in the cell the query falls into. Nearly. A query near a cell boundary has neighbours on the other side of it, and at nprobe = 1 they are unreachable no matter how good the rest of the index is. This is the <strong>boundary problem</strong>, and it is why nprobe exists.</p>" +
            "<p>Here is the measurement, from an IVF built over 20,000 unit vectors in 128 dimensions drawn from 40 clusters, with ground truth from an exact scan:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>nlist = 64</strong> (median cell 311 vectors) — nprobe 1: recall 0.798, scanning 2.2% of the corpus. nprobe 2: 0.968 at 3.6%. nprobe 4: 0.995 at 6.7%. nprobe 16: 1.000 at 25.3%.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>nlist = 256</strong> (median cell 73) — nprobe 1: recall 0.288 at 0.4%. nprobe 2: 0.520 at 0.9%. nprobe 4: 0.835 at 1.8%. nprobe 8: 0.993 at 3.2%. nprobe 16: 1.000 at 6.4%.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Read the first line first: at nlist = 64 and nprobe = 1, one in five of the true top ten was in a neighbouring cell. Probing two cells instead of one recovered most of them. That is the boundary problem, quantified, on data with clean cluster structure. It gets worse, not better, as the query distribution drifts from the training distribution.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='20' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>Voronoi cells: the query sits near an edge, and two of its true neighbours are across it</text>" +
            "<g stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5' fill='none'>" +
            "<path d='M 40 40 L 210 40 L 250 130 L 150 210 L 40 180 Z'/>" +
            "<path d='M 210 40 L 380 40 L 400 140 L 250 130 Z'/>" +
            "<path d='M 150 210 L 250 130 L 400 140 L 380 225 L 160 225 Z'/>" +
            "<path d='M 380 40 L 520 40 L 540 150 L 400 140 Z'/>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0'><circle cx='140' cy='115' r='4'/><circle cx='305' cy='90' r='4'/><circle cx='275' cy='180' r='4'/><circle cx='455' cy='95' r='4'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'><text x='150' y='112'>c1</text><text x='315' y='87'>c2</text><text x='285' y='177'>c3</text><text x='465' y='92'>c4</text></g>" +
            "<path d='M 210 40 L 250 130 L 150 210 L 40 180 L 40 40 Z' fill='#4f7cff' fill-opacity='0.10' stroke='none'/>" +
            "<circle cx='243' cy='118' r='6' fill='#ff5d73'/><text x='236' y='150' text-anchor='end' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>query</text><line x1='239' y1='124' x2='228' y2='143' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='1'/>" +
            "<g fill='#3ddc97'><circle cx='228' cy='128' r='4'/><circle cx='215' cy='105' r='4'/><circle cx='262' cy='112' r='4'/><circle cx='271' cy='134' r='4'/></g>" +
            "<text x='288' y='112' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>two true neighbours,</text><text x='288' y='124' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>in cell c2 and c3</text>" +
            "<line x1='560' y1='40' x2='560' y2='230' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "<text x='580' y='56' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>measured, 20,000 vectors</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<text x='580' y='80' fill='#7d8fb0'>nlist=64</text>" +
            "<text x='580' y='97' fill='#ff5d73'>nprobe 1   0.798   2.2%</text>" +
            "<text x='580' y='113'>nprobe 2   0.968   3.6%</text>" +
            "<text x='580' y='129'>nprobe 4   0.995   6.7%</text>" +
            "<text x='580' y='155' fill='#7d8fb0'>nlist=256</text>" +
            "<text x='580' y='172' fill='#ff5d73'>nprobe 1   0.288   0.4%</text>" +
            "<text x='580' y='188'>nprobe 4   0.835   1.8%</text>" +
            "<text x='580' y='204' fill='#3ddc97'>nprobe 8   0.993   3.2%</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<text x='580' y='228' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>finer cells, more probes,</text><text x='580' y='240' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>less total work</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F12.1. At nprobe = 1 the shaded cell is all the search can see. The measured recall of 0.798 at nlist = 64 is the fraction of true neighbours that happened to fall inside it.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>Reading the two tables together</h3>" +
            "<p>The interesting comparison is not down a column but across the two settings at equal recall. At nlist = 64, reaching 0.995 took nprobe = 4 and scanned 6.7 percent of the corpus. At nlist = 256, reaching 0.993 took nprobe = 8 and scanned 3.2 percent. Same recall, half the work, from a finer partition probed more widely. That is the general rule: <strong>more cells is better, and it costs you a larger nprobe to exploit</strong>, plus a longer centroid comparison at query time and a slower training pass.</p>" +
            "<p>Which is why every engine gives you a formula. pgvector suggests <code>lists = rows / 1000</code> up to a million rows and <code>sqrt(rows)</code> above that, with <code>ivfflat.probes</code> starting at <code>sqrt(lists)</code> and defaulting to a bare 1, which is the setting that produces the recall complaint that brings people to this chapter. Faiss suggests nlist between 4 and 16 times the square root of N below a million vectors, and named recipes above it: IVF65536 for 1 to 10 million, IVF262144 up to 100 million, IVF1048576 to a billion.</p>" +
            "<p>Two operational facts that follow from IVF being trained rather than built. First, the index needs data before it exists: pgvector says outright to create the index after the table has rows, because k-means has nothing to cluster otherwise, and Faiss wants between 30 and 256 training vectors per centroid. Second, the partition ages. Cells are fixed at training time, so a corpus that grows or drifts ends up with unbalanced cells, and recall decays quietly exactly as Chapter 11's tombstones do. My own run at nlist = 64 produced one empty cell and a largest cell of 1,000 against a median of 311, on clean synthetic data. Real corpora are worse. Retraining is the fix, and it is a full rebuild.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Try it.</strong> Train with 8 cells in the machine above and click a point in the middle of a cell: at nprobe = 1 recall is near perfect. Now click immediately inside a cell edge and watch recall collapse, then raise nprobe to 2 and watch the missing neighbours appear. The lesson is that IVF's recall is not uniform across the space. It is excellent in the middle of cells and poor at the seams, and your users' queries do not know where the seams are.</div>" },

    { title: "Product Quantization: Sub-Vectors, Codebooks, and the Distance Table", viz: "pqCodebook", vizTitle: "Split a vector into sub-vectors, encode each as one byte, and compare the reconstruction",
      html: "<p>IVF left the vectors untouched. Product quantization, from Hervé Jégou, Matthijs Douze, and Cordelia Schmid in 2011, replaces them.</p>" +
            "<p>The naive compression is to run k-means over the whole corpus and store each vector as its centroid's id. To represent a million vectors distinguishably you would need something like a million centroids, and training and storing that codebook costs more than the vectors did. The insight is to quantize in pieces. Split each d-dimensional vector into <strong>m sub-vectors</strong> of d/m dimensions each. Run a separate k-means in each sub-space, with 256 centroids, and store each sub-vector as the one-byte id of its nearest centroid. A vector becomes m bytes.</p>" +
            "<p>The <em>product</em> in the name is the arithmetic that makes this work. With m sub-spaces of 256 centroids each, the effective codebook has 256<sup>m</sup> entries: at m = 16 that is 256<sup>16</sup>, around 10<sup>38</sup> distinct representable vectors, from 16 × 256 stored centroids. You get the expressiveness of an astronomically large codebook while training and storing a tiny one.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='20' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>one 128-d float32 vector (512 bytes) becomes m = 16 bytes</text>" +
            "<rect x='20' y='34' width='720' height='24' rx='4' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<g stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'><line x1='110' y1='34' x2='110' y2='58'/><line x1='200' y1='34' x2='200' y2='58'/><line x1='290' y1='34' x2='290' y2='58'/><line x1='380' y1='34' x2='380' y2='58'/><line x1='470' y1='34' x2='470' y2='58'/><line x1='560' y1='34' x2='560' y2='58'/><line x1='650' y1='34' x2='650' y2='58'/></g>" +
            "<text x='65' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>dims 0-7</text>" +
            "<text x='155' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>dims 8-15</text>" +
            "<text x='245' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>16-23</text>" +
            "<text x='335' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>24-31</text>" +
            "<text x='510' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>· · ·</text>" +
            "<text x='695' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>120-127</text>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.2'><line x1='65' y1='58' x2='65' y2='84'/><line x1='155' y1='58' x2='155' y2='84'/><line x1='245' y1='58' x2='245' y2='84'/><line x1='335' y1='58' x2='335' y2='84'/><line x1='695' y1='58' x2='695' y2='84'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='1.5'><rect x='25' y='84' width='80' height='40' rx='5'/><rect x='115' y='84' width='80' height='40' rx='5'/><rect x='205' y='84' width='80' height='40' rx='5'/><rect x='295' y='84' width='80' height='40' rx='5'/><rect x='655' y='84' width='80' height='40' rx='5'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#ffd166' font-size='9' text-anchor='middle'><text x='65' y='100'>codebook 0</text><text x='65' y='116'>256 centroids</text><text x='155' y='100'>codebook 1</text><text x='155' y='116'>256 centroids</text><text x='245' y='100'>codebook 2</text><text x='245' y='116'>256</text><text x='335' y='100'>codebook 3</text><text x='335' y='116'>256</text><text x='695' y='100'>codebook 15</text><text x='695' y='116'>256</text></g>" +
            "<text x='510' y='108' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>· · ·</text>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.2'><line x1='65' y1='124' x2='65' y2='150'/><line x1='155' y1='124' x2='155' y2='150'/><line x1='245' y1='124' x2='245' y2='150'/><line x1='335' y1='124' x2='335' y2='150'/><line x1='695' y1='124' x2='695' y2='150'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'><rect x='40' y='150' width='50' height='26' rx='4'/><rect x='130' y='150' width='50' height='26' rx='4'/><rect x='220' y='150' width='50' height='26' rx='4'/><rect x='310' y='150' width='50' height='26' rx='4'/><rect x='670' y='150' width='50' height='26' rx='4'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#3ddc97' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'><text x='65' y='168'>0x2F</text><text x='155' y='168'>0xA1</text><text x='245' y='168'>0x07</text><text x='335' y='168'>0xC4</text><text x='695' y='168'>0x1B</text></g>" +
            "<text x='510' y='168' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>· · ·</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='200' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>stored: 16 bytes. Effective codebook: 256^16, about 10^38 representable vectors, from 4,096 stored centroids.</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='220' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>the codebooks are trained once over the corpus and shared by every vector; only the m code bytes are per-vector</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F12.2. Quantize in pieces, not as a whole. The compression ratio is d × 4 / m, so 128 dimensions at m = 16 is 32 times smaller.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>Scoring without decompressing</h3>" +
            "<p>The elegant part is the query. You never reconstruct a vector. Split the <em>query</em> into the same m sub-vectors, and for each sub-space compute its distance to all 256 centroids of that sub-space's codebook. That is an m × 256 <strong>lookup table</strong>, built once per query for a few thousand operations. Now scoring any stored vector is m table lookups and m additions: read its m code bytes, use each as an index into the corresponding row, sum. No multiplication, no decompression, and the arithmetic is the same regardless of d.</p>" +
            "<p>The paper calls this <strong>asymmetric distance computation</strong>, and the asymmetry is the point: the query stays at full precision and only the stored vectors are quantized. The symmetric alternative quantizes the query too, so distances can be precomputed centroid-to-centroid, but it adds the query's own quantization error on top of the document's. Asymmetric is more accurate for the same storage, which is why every implementation uses it.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 220' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='20' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>the query-time lookup table, built once, then reused for every vector in the corpus</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='44' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>each query sub-vector is compared</text><text x='20' y='58' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>to its 256 centroids, giving one row</text>" +
            "<g fill='#111a2e' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'><rect x='300' y='32' width='330' height='18' rx='3'/><rect x='300' y='54' width='330' height='18' rx='3'/><rect x='300' y='76' width='330' height='18' rx='3'/><rect x='300' y='118' width='330' height='18' rx='3'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'><text x='306' y='45'>0.91  0.44  0.12  ...  0.77   (256 entries)</text><text x='306' y='67'>0.31  0.88  0.05  ...  0.19</text><text x='306' y='89'>0.62  0.10  0.94  ...  0.41</text><text x='306' y='131'>0.24  0.71  0.36  ...  0.58</text></g>" +
            "<text x='465' y='110' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='10'>· · ·</text>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9' text-anchor='end'><text x='294' y='45'>row 0</text><text x='294' y='67'>row 1</text><text x='294' y='89'>row 2</text><text x='294' y='131'>row 15</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='152' x2='740' y2='152' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='172' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>score a vector with codes [0x2F, 0xA1, 0x07, ..., 0x1B]:</text>" +
            "<text x='40' y='190' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>table[0][0x2F] + table[1][0xA1] + table[2][0x07] + ... + table[15][0x1B]</text>" +
            "<text x='40' y='208' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>16 lookups and 16 additions per vector, no multiplies, independent of the original 128 dimensions</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F12.3. Asymmetric distance: the query is exact, the stored vectors are codes, and the table turns scoring into table lookups.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>The encoding, with numbers</h3><p>Take one 128-dimensional float32 vector, the size used in the measurements below. Uncompressed it is 512 bytes. Choose m = 16, so each sub-vector spans 8 dimensions. Training runs k-means 16 times, once per sub-space, each over the 8-dimensional slices of the whole corpus, producing 16 codebooks of 256 centroids of 8 floats each. The codebooks together are 16 × 256 × 8 × 4 bytes, which is 131 KB, and that cost is paid once for the entire corpus however large it grows.</p><p>Encoding the vector means, for each of its 16 slices, finding the nearest of that sub-space's 256 centroids and writing down its index. The result is 16 bytes: a 32-fold reduction. At query time the lookup table is 16 rows of 256 entries, so 4,096 distance computations in 8 dimensions each, about the cost of scanning 256 full vectors. After that, every one of the corpus's vectors is scored in 16 lookups and 15 additions, whatever d was. Scoring a million vectors is 16 million table reads, and no floating-point multiplication at all.</p><p>Notice what the arithmetic implies about when PQ pays. The per-query setup cost is fixed, so PQ is wasteful on a small corpus and increasingly free on a large one. And because scoring cost depends on m rather than d, the technique gets relatively more attractive as embeddings get wider: at 1,536 dimensions the same 16 bytes is a 384-fold reduction, and the scoring loop is unchanged.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>IVFPQ</strong>, the combination Jégou and colleagues actually proposed, applies both ideas: a coarse quantizer assigns each vector to an IVF cell, and the PQ codes encode the <em>residual</em> between the vector and its cell centroid rather than the vector itself. Residuals have smaller magnitude and less variance than the vectors, so the same 16 bytes represent them more precisely. Search probes nprobe cells and scores their codes with the lookup table. This is the index behind Faiss's billion-scale recipes.</p>" },

    { title: "What Compression Actually Costs, and the Rescoring That Repairs It",
      html: "<p>Now the honest part, which most introductions skip. Here is product quantization measured on the same 20,000 vectors in 128 dimensions, scoring the whole corpus by asymmetric distance and taking the top 10:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>m = 4</strong> (4 bytes per vector, 128× smaller) — recall@10 of <strong>0.078</strong>.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>m = 8</strong> (8 bytes, 64×) — <strong>0.098</strong>.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>m = 16</strong> (16 bytes, 32×) — <strong>0.178</strong>.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>m = 32</strong> (32 bytes, 16×) — <strong>0.391</strong>.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>Those are not typographical errors, and they are not a broken implementation. Compressing 512 bytes to 16 destroys the fine distinctions between the top ten neighbours of a query, because within a tight cluster those ten vectors differ by less than the quantization error. The condition matters and I will state it: this corpus is 40 tight clusters, which is the hardest case for ranking <em>within</em> a neighbourhood, and published benchmarks on more spread-out data report friendlier numbers. But the direction is universal and worth internalising.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Product quantization is a candidate generator, not a ranker.</strong> Its job is to cheaply produce a shortlist that contains the right answers somewhere, not to order them. Ordering is a separate step.</p>" +
            "<h3>Rescoring</h3>" +
            "<p>Take the top R by PQ score, fetch those R vectors at full precision, compute exact distances, and re-sort. R is a few hundred, so the exact work is negligible, and the full-precision vectors can live on disk because you now know exactly which ones you need. Same corpus, same 16-byte codes:</p>" +
            "<ul><li>rescore top 10 — recall 0.178, unchanged, since the shortlist is already the answer</li>" +
            "<li>rescore top 50 — <strong>0.474</strong></li>" +
            "<li>rescore top 100 — <strong>0.663</strong></li>" +
            "<li>rescore top 200 — <strong>0.871</strong></li>" +
            "<li>rescore top 500 — <strong>1.000</strong></li></ul>" +
            "<p>Perfect recall, from an index storing 16 bytes per vector, by exactly re-ranking 2.5 percent of the corpus. That is the entire technique. The compressed index answers \"which 500 might be right\", which it is good at, and exact arithmetic answers \"which 10 are right\", which it alone can do.</p>" +
            "<p>You have now seen this shape three times, and it is worth naming as a pattern rather than a trick. Chapter 6 over-fetched children and deduplicated to parents. Chapter 7 searched a binary index and rescored with full-precision vectors, recovering roughly 92 percent to 96 percent of quality. Here PQ shortlists and exact distances rank. Chapter 16's reranker does it again with a cross-encoder over a bi-encoder's output. <strong>A cheap wide net followed by an expensive narrow one</strong> is the dominant structure of every retrieval system in this course, and the parameter that matters is always how wide the net is.</p>" +
            "<h3>OPQ, in one paragraph</h3>" +
            "<p>Product quantization splits dimensions into contiguous blocks, which is arbitrary: if dimensions 0 and 64 are highly correlated, splitting them into different sub-spaces wastes bits encoding the same information twice, and if a sub-space carries far more variance than its neighbours, its 256 centroids are stretched too thin. <strong>Optimized product quantization</strong> fixes this by learning a rotation of the space before splitting, chosen so that variance is balanced across sub-spaces and correlations fall within them rather than across them. It is a matrix multiply at index and query time and typically buys a few points of recall for free. Faiss's recommended recipes apply an OPQ transform before PQ by default. Google's ScaNN goes further with <strong>anisotropic quantization</strong>, a loss that penalises quantization error along the query direction more than error perpendicular to it, on the reasoning that only the former changes the inner product that decides the ranking.</p>" },

    { title: "Disk and GPU: When the Index Leaves RAM",
      html: "<p>Compression buys a factor of 32. At a billion vectors even that is 16 to 32 GB of codes plus the graph, and the accuracy cost is what the previous section measured. The alternative is to stop insisting the index live in memory at all.</p>" +
            "<h3>DiskANN</h3>" +
            "<p><strong>DiskANN</strong>, from Suhas Jayaram Subramanya and colleagues at Microsoft Research in 2019, splits the index across two tiers. The full-precision vectors and the graph adjacency live on SSD. A PQ-compressed copy of every vector, small enough to fit, lives in RAM. Search runs the greedy graph walk of Chapter 11 using the <em>compressed</em> vectors in memory to decide where to go next, and reads the full vectors from SSD only for the nodes it actually visits, using them to compute exact distances for the final ranking. Compression guides the walk; the disk supplies the truth.</p>" +
            "<p>The graph is not HNSW. <strong>Vamana</strong> is a single flat graph with no layers, built by a pruning rule with a parameter α that keeps an edge unless a kept neighbour is closer by a factor of α, deliberately admitting longer edges than HNSW's heuristic does. The paper builds in two passes, first with α = 1 and then with α greater than 1, and reports that the second pass produces a better graph. Longer edges mean fewer hops, and fewer hops is the entire design goal, because on SSD the cost is not distance computations but <strong>round trips</strong>. The paper targets fewer than ten per query and prefers five.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 220' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<rect x='20' y='34' width='330' height='150' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='185' y='24' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='11'>RAM (small)</text>" +
            "<text x='36' y='58' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>PQ codes for every vector, ~32 B each</text>" +
            "<g fill='#3ddc97'><rect x='36' y='70' width='26' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='68' y='70' width='26' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='100' y='70' width='26' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='132' y='70' width='26' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='164' y='70' width='26' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='196' y='70' width='26' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='228' y='70' width='26' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='260' y='70' width='26' height='14' rx='2'/></g>" +
            "<text x='36' y='108' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>used to decide which node to walk to next:</text>" +
            "<text x='36' y='122' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>approximate, but free of disk reads</text>" +
            "<text x='36' y='150' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>1 billion x 128 d fits in under 64 GB</text>" +
            "<text x='36' y='168' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>on one workstation</text>" +
            "<rect x='420' y='34' width='320' height='150' rx='8' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='580' y='24' text-anchor='middle' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='11'>SSD (large)</text>" +
            "<text x='436' y='58' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>full float32 vectors + Vamana adjacency</text>" +
            "<g fill='#4f7cff'><rect x='436' y='70' width='60' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='502' y='70' width='60' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='568' y='70' width='60' height='14' rx='2'/><rect x='634' y='70' width='60' height='14' rx='2'/></g>" +
            "<text x='436' y='108' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>read only for nodes actually visited,</text>" +
            "<text x='436' y='122' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>then used for the exact final ranking</text>" +
            "<text x='436' y='150' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>design target: under 10 round trips</text>" +
            "<text x='436' y='168' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>per query, preferably 5</text>" +
            "<line x1='350' y1='95' x2='416' y2='95' stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='422,95 410,89 410,101' fill='#7d8fb0'/>" +
            "<text x='383' y='86' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='8.5'>fetch</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='206' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>measured by the authors on SIFT1B: over 5,000 queries/sec, under 3 ms mean latency, 95%+ 1-recall@1 on 16 cores,</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='218' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>against roughly 50% 1-recall@1 for in-memory billion-point methods at comparable memory budgets.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F12.4. Compression in RAM to navigate, full precision on SSD to decide. The scarce resource is disk round trips, not arithmetic.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>GPUs</h3>" +
            "<p>A GPU is a machine for doing the same arithmetic to a great many values at once, which is what a scan and a PQ table lookup both are. Faiss's GPU implementation, from Jeff Johnson, Matthijs Douze, and Hervé Jégou in 2017, built a k-nearest-neighbour graph over 95 million images in 35 minutes and over a billion vectors in under 12 hours on four Titan X cards, reporting an 8.5-fold speedup over the previous GPU state of the art. The lesson to carry is about build time more than query time: GPUs turn index construction from an overnight batch job into a coffee break, which changes how often you are willing to retrain an IVF partition or re-embed after Chapter 8's model change. For query serving, a GPU helps most when batches are large, which suits offline scoring better than a single interactive request.</p>" },

    { title: "Choosing an Index",
      html: "<p>You now have four families: flat from Chapter 10, HNSW from Chapter 11, and IVF and PQ from this one. The decision is made on four inputs.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 260' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5' fill='#7d8fb0'><text x='20' y='30'>corpus size</text><text x='150' y='30'>index</text><text x='330' y='30'>bytes/vector</text><text x='470' y='30'>recall</text><text x='560' y='30'>what it costs you</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='38' x2='740' y2='38' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='62'>&lt; 100 K</text><text x='150' y='62' fill='#3ddc97'>flat scan</text><text x='330' y='62'>d x 4</text><text x='470' y='62' fill='#3ddc97'>1.000</text><text x='560' y='62'>nothing; no build, no params</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='92'>100 K - 10 M</text><text x='150' y='92' fill='#3ddc97'>HNSW</text><text x='330' y='92'>d x 4 + ~107</text><text x='470' y='92'>0.95+</text><text x='560' y='92'>RAM, build time, hard deletes</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='122'>10 M - 100 M</text><text x='150' y='122' fill='#4f7cff'>IVF, or IVF+PQ</text><text x='330' y='122'>d x 4, or m</text><text x='470' y='122'>tunable</text><text x='560' y='122'>training, retraining on drift</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='152'>100 M - 1 B+</text><text x='150' y='152' fill='#ffd166'>IVFPQ + rescore</text><text x='330' y='152'>m (16-64)</text><text x='470' y='152'>needs rescore</text><text x='560' y='152'>accuracy, and a rescore tier</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='182'>1 B+, one box</text><text x='150' y='182' fill='#ffd166'>DiskANN</text><text x='330' y='182'>m in RAM</text><text x='470' y='182'>0.95+</text><text x='560' y='182'>SSD latency, build complexity</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='196' x2='740' y2='196' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='218' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>heavy writes or churn? IVF retrains, HNSW accumulates tombstones. Prefer engines that rebuild in the background.</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='236' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>heavy filtering? None of these handle it natively. That is Chapter 13, and it can invalidate this whole table.</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='254' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>every row assumes you measured recall on your own vectors first. The table narrows the choice, it does not make it.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F12.5. Corpus size sets the family, but write rate and filtering can override it, and the last row of the figure is the one people discover too late.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Three principles behind the table. <strong>Do not buy an index you have not measured a need for</strong>; Chapter 10's flat-scan advice retires more premature HNSW deployments than any tuning guide. <strong>Compression is a last resort, not an optimization</strong>, because Section 4's numbers show what it costs and rescoring adds a tier to your architecture. And <strong>the write pattern is as decisive as the size</strong>: an IVF over a corpus that doubles every quarter is a retraining schedule, and an HNSW under heavy deletion is a rebuilding schedule, and both are operational commitments that a benchmark plot will never show you.</p>" +
            "<h3>The three questions that actually decide it</h3><p><em>Does the working set fit in the RAM you can afford?</em> Compute it before anything else: N times d times 4, plus roughly 107 bytes per vector if you plan on HNSW. If the answer is yes with room to spare, nothing in this chapter applies to you and Chapter 11 was the last index chapter you needed. If it is close, quantize the vectors with Chapter 7's int8 or float16 before reaching for product quantization, because scalar quantization retains about 99 percent of quality against PQ's measured collapse.</p><p><em>How often does the corpus change, and how?</em> IVF's partition is trained once, so a corpus that grows or drifts needs periodic retraining, which is a full rebuild. HNSW accumulates tombstones under deletion, which is also a periodic rebuild. Neither is a reason to avoid them, but both are a schedule and an on-call runbook, and a system that ingests continuously should prefer an engine that segments and merges in the background, the way Chapter 9's inverted indexes do, over one that requires you to orchestrate rebuilds yourself.</p><p><em>Do queries carry filters?</em> Every measurement in this chapter and the last assumed the search may return any vector. Real queries say \"only this tenant\", \"only documents this user may read\", \"only the current fare year\". A filter interacts badly with all of these structures: an IVF cell may contain no matching vectors, and an HNSW walk may traverse a region where everything is filtered out. This is not a tuning problem, it can invalidate the entire choice, and it is why Chapter 13 exists and why the last line of the figure is the one teams discover after they have built everything else.</p>" +
            "<p>For Larkspur's 200,000 chunks at 768 dimensions the answer is the dull one: 614 MB of vectors, HNSW at the defaults, no compression, and the effort spent on Chapters 15 and 16 instead. Most systems that reach for this chapter's techniques are solving a problem they do not have. The value of knowing the techniques is partly in recognising, quickly and with a number, that you do not need them.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"How would you index a billion vectors?\" wants IVF for the partition, PQ for the bytes, and rescoring for the accuracy, and the strong answer volunteers that PQ alone ranks poorly and must be repaired. \"What is asymmetric distance computation?\" wants the query kept exact and the lookup table built per query. \"IVF or HNSW?\" wants the trade named: IVF trains, builds fast, uses less memory, and degrades as the corpus drifts; HNSW builds slowly, uses more memory, and gives a better speed-recall curve. \"Why does recall drop for some queries and not others?\" is the boundary problem, and it is the question that separates reading from running.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol><li><strong>Trace.</strong> Using Section 2's measurements, compute how many vectors are scanned per query at nlist = 64 with nprobe = 4 and at nlist = 256 with nprobe = 8, then state which you would deploy and why. What you should find: 1,340 versus 645 vectors for essentially identical recall (0.995 and 0.993), so the finer partition wins on query cost; the arguments against it are a longer centroid comparison and a slower, more fragile training pass.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> A vector is 1,536 float32 dimensions. Compute bytes per vector for m = 16, 48, and 96 sub-vectors, the compression ratio for each, and the total index size for 500 million vectors at m = 48. What you should find: 16, 48, and 96 bytes against 6,144, so ratios of 384, 128, and 64 times; and 24 GB at m = 48, which is why a billion vectors fits on one machine at all.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Implement IVF over your Chapter 7 embeddings with k-means, and measure recall@10 against exact search for nlist in {sqrt(N), 4·sqrt(N)} and nprobe in {1, 2, 4, 8, 16}. Then find the queries that fail at nprobe = 1 and measure their distance to their cell centroid. What you should find: the failures are concentrated among queries far from their centroid, which is the boundary problem visible in your own data rather than in a diagram.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Train PQ on your corpus at m = d/8 and measure recall@10 with no rescoring, then with rescoring of the top 50, 100, 200, and 500. Plot recall against the number of exactly-scored vectors. What you should find: a curve that rises steeply and then flattens, and a knee that tells you the rescore depth to ship. Compare that knee to the over-fetch multiplier you chose in Chapter 6 and the rescore multiplier in Chapter 7.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Implement product quantization in under eighty lines: train m codebooks of 256 centroids with k-means, encode the corpus to m bytes per vector, and implement asymmetric distance with a per-query lookup table. Assert that scoring never reconstructs a vector. Then add exact rescoring. Report bytes per vector and recall at each rescore depth. This is PocketRAG's compression tier; a reference implementation is in the course resources.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Combine the two into IVFPQ: assign vectors to cells, encode the residual from the cell centroid rather than the vector, and search with nprobe cells plus the lookup table plus rescoring. Compare recall against PQ on the raw vectors at identical m. What you should find: encoding residuals beats encoding raw vectors at the same byte budget, because residuals have smaller variance, which is the reason the two techniques are combined rather than merely stacked.</li></ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "inverted file index", d: "An index that partitions vectors into cells by k-means and scans only the cells nearest a query; abbreviated IVF." },
    { t: "nlist", d: "The number of k-means centroids, and therefore cells, in an IVF index." },
    { t: "nprobe", d: "The number of cells searched per query; the search-time parameter that trades recall against work." },
    { t: "Voronoi cell", d: "The region of space whose nearest centroid is a given one; the unit an IVF index scans." },
    { t: "boundary problem", d: "The loss of recall for queries near a cell edge whose true neighbours lie in an adjacent cell, unreachable at low nprobe." },
    { t: "coarse quantizer", d: "The k-means model that assigns a vector to its IVF cell, as distinct from the fine quantizer that encodes it." },
    { t: "product quantization", d: "Compressing a vector by splitting it into m sub-vectors and storing each as a one-byte index into a per-sub-space codebook of 256 centroids." },
    { t: "sub-vector", d: "One of the m contiguous slices a vector is divided into before quantization, each quantized by its own codebook." },
    { t: "codebook", d: "The set of 256 centroids learned for one sub-space, shared by every vector in the corpus." },
    { t: "asymmetric distance computation", d: "Scoring a quantized vector against an unquantized query via a precomputed lookup table, which is more accurate than quantizing both." },
    { t: "lookup table", d: "The m by 256 table of query-sub-vector-to-centroid distances, built once per query so that scoring a vector is m lookups and m additions." },
    { t: "IVFPQ", d: "The combination of an IVF partition with PQ codes for the residual between each vector and its cell centroid." },
    { t: "residual", d: "The difference between a vector and its cell centroid; smaller in variance than the vector, so it quantizes more precisely at the same byte budget." },
    { t: "rescoring", d: "Re-ranking the top R candidates from a compressed index using full-precision vectors, which is what makes product quantization usable." },
    { t: "optimized product quantization", d: "Learning a rotation of the space before splitting into sub-vectors, so that variance is balanced and correlated dimensions fall in the same sub-space." },
    { t: "anisotropic quantization", d: "ScaNN's loss, which penalises quantization error along the query direction more than error perpendicular to it, because only the former changes the ranking." },
    { t: "DiskANN", d: "An index keeping PQ codes in RAM to guide a graph walk and full vectors on SSD for exact scoring, indexing a billion points on one 64 GB machine." },
    { t: "Vamana", d: "DiskANN's single-layer graph, built with a pruning rule whose alpha parameter admits longer edges so that fewer hops, and so fewer disk round trips, are needed." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "An IVF index at nlist = 64 and nprobe = 1 measured recall@10 of 0.798. Where did the missing 20 percent of true neighbours go?",
      opts: ["They were quantized away by the codebooks", "They sit in cells adjacent to the query's cell, unreachable when only one cell is probed", "They were in the cell but scored with approximate distances", "The k-means training did not converge"], a: 1,
      why: "IVF scans exact distances inside probed cells, so nothing inside is lost. The loss is entirely at cell boundaries, and probing two cells raised recall to 0.968 in the same measurement." },
    { type: "mc", q: "At nlist = 64 reaching recall 0.995 took nprobe = 4 and scanned 6.7% of the corpus; at nlist = 256 reaching 0.993 took nprobe = 8 and scanned 3.2%. What is the general rule?",
      opts: ["Fewer, larger cells always reduce query cost", "nprobe should always be 1 to minimise work", "Recall depends only on nprobe, not on nlist", "More cells is better and requires a proportionally larger nprobe to exploit, giving the same recall for less total work, at the cost of a longer centroid comparison and a slower training pass"], a: 3,
      why: "This is why pgvector scales lists with the row count and Faiss recommends nlist between 4 and 16 times the square root of N, with named recipes above a million." },
    { type: "blank", q: "In product quantization, a vector is split into m sub-vectors and each is stored as a one-byte index into a per-sub-space ____ of 256 centroids.", a: ["codebook"],
      why: "With m sub-spaces the effective codebook is 256 to the power m, so m = 16 represents about 10^38 distinct vectors from only 4,096 stored centroids." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Why is asymmetric distance computation preferred over the symmetric variant?",
      opts: ["The query stays at full precision so only the stored vectors carry quantization error, which is more accurate for the same storage", "It is faster because both sides are quantized", "It requires no lookup table", "It allows the codebooks to be smaller"], a: 0,
      why: "The symmetric form quantizes the query too and can precompute centroid-to-centroid distances, but it adds the query's own error on top of the document's. The asymmetric table costs a few thousand operations per query." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Measured product quantization at 16 bytes per vector scored recall@10 of 0.178 with no rescoring, and 1.000 after exactly re-ranking the top 500. What does this say about PQ's role?",
      opts: ["The implementation was faulty; correct PQ reaches 0.9 unaided", "PQ should only be used with m above 32", "PQ is a candidate generator, not a ranker: it cheaply produces a shortlist containing the right answers, and exact arithmetic over that shortlist does the ordering", "Rescoring compensates for a poorly trained codebook"], a: 2,
      why: "Compressing 512 bytes to 16 destroys the fine distinctions between a query's top ten. Rescoring 500 vectors, 2.5 percent of the corpus, recovered perfect recall from the same 16-byte index." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order the steps of an IVFPQ query:", items: ["compare the query to all nlist centroids and take the nearest nprobe cells", "build the m by 256 lookup table from the query's sub-vectors", "score the codes in the probed cells by table lookups and additions", "fetch the top R candidates at full precision and re-rank them exactly"],
      why: "Partition first to reduce how many codes are scored, then table-lookup scoring, then the rescoring tier that repairs the compression loss. Skipping the last step is the common deployment error." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Why does IVFPQ encode the residual between a vector and its cell centroid rather than the vector itself?",
      opts: ["Residuals are integers and quantize exactly", "It avoids having to store the centroids", "It makes the lookup table smaller", "Residuals have smaller magnitude and variance than the vectors, so the same number of bytes represents them more precisely"], a: 3,
      why: "This is why the two techniques are combined rather than merely stacked: the coarse quantizer improves the fine quantizer's job at no extra storage cost." },
    { type: "mc", q: "DiskANN keeps PQ-compressed vectors in RAM and full vectors on SSD. What is each used for?",
      opts: ["Compressed vectors to guide the graph walk without disk reads, full vectors read only for visited nodes to compute the exact final ranking", "Compressed vectors for the final ranking, full vectors for the graph walk", "Both are used for the walk, and the ranking is done from centroids", "Compressed vectors are a backup if the SSD is slow"], a: 0,
      why: "The scarce resource on SSD is round trips rather than arithmetic, which is why the Vamana graph's alpha pruning admits longer edges: fewer hops means fewer reads. The paper targets under ten round trips and prefers five." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A team with 200,000 chunks at 768 dimensions proposes IVFPQ at 32 bytes per vector to \"save memory\". What should you tell them?",
      opts: ["Use m = 16 instead to save more", "IVFPQ is correct but they need nprobe above 16", "Their vectors are 614 MB, which fits comfortably; HNSW at the defaults with no compression is the right index, and this chapter's techniques solve a problem they do not have", "They should use DiskANN because it scales further"], a: 2,
      why: "Compression is a last resort with a measured accuracy cost and an extra architectural tier. The decision table's first principle is not to buy an index you have not measured a need for." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Which statement about IVF's operational behaviour over time is correct?",
      opts: ["Cells rebalance automatically as vectors are added", "The partition is fixed at training time, so a growing or drifting corpus produces unbalanced cells and quietly decaying recall until the index is retrained", "IVF requires no training data, unlike HNSW", "Deleting vectors from an IVF index requires a full rebuild, unlike HNSW"], a: 1,
      why: "k-means runs once over whatever data existed then. pgvector requires the table to have rows before the index is created for this reason, and Faiss wants 30 to 256 training vectors per centroid." }
  ]
});

/* ======================= CHAPTER 13 =======================
   Sources consulted (checked 2026-09-03): pgvector README, "Filtering" (create an
   index on the filter column; with approximate indexes the filter is applied
   after the index scan, so "if a condition matches 10% of rows, with HNSW and the
   default hnsw.ef_search of 40, only 4 rows will match on average"; iterative
   scans hnsw.iterative_scan = strict_order | relaxed_order, hnsw.max_scan_tuples
   default 20000, hnsw.scan_mem_multiplier, ivfflat.iterative_scan and
   ivfflat.max_probes; partial indexes; partitioning for multitenancy; vector /
   halfvec / bit / sparsevec limits and the operator set); Patel, Kraft, Guestrin
   & Zaharia 2024, ACORN (arXiv 2403.04871: predicate-agnostic filtered search via
   predicate subgraph traversal and two-hop neighbour expansion, gamma parameter,
   2x to 1,000x higher throughput at fixed recall); Qdrant filtering and indexing
   docs (payload indexes by type, tenant and principal indexes, extra payload-aware
   HNSW edges, full_scan_threshold, ACORN from v1.16, and the instruction to create
   payload indexes before ingesting); Elasticsearch kNN docs (a filter inside the
   knn clause is applied during the approximate search, a top-level filter is a
   post-filter that "can return fewer than k results"; if the filtered document
   count is at or below num_candidates the search uses brute force over the
   filtered set, and it switches to brute force mid-search if explored nodes
   exceed the filtered count); Weaviate filtering docs (inverted-index allow-list
   consulted during HNSW traversal, brute-force fallback at roughly 15% or less of
   the dataset, ACORN as the default filter strategy since v1.34 alongside
   sweeping).
   THE SELECTIVITY MEASUREMENTS BELOW WERE MADE by adding filtering to the HNSW
   implementation of Chapter 11 and running it: 5,000 unit vectors in 64
   dimensions from 25 clusters, M = 16, ef_construction = 64, 100 queries, K = 10,
   a random attribute independent of position, ground truth by exact scan over the
   matching subset. The script is in resources/filtered.py.
   NOTE: no PostgreSQL instance was available on the authoring machine, so the
   EXPLAIN plans in Section 6 are representative of pgvector's documented plan
   shapes, not captured output. Section 7 gives the commands to produce your own. */
CHAPTERS.push({
  num: 13, part: 2, title: "Filters Meet Vectors: Pre-, Post-, and pgvector From the Inside",
  blurb: "\"Nearest neighbours where tenant = 42 and date > March\" is the query every production system runs, and it is where the last three chapters quietly break. Filter after the search and a selective filter returns almost nothing; filter before it and you are back to the scan you built an index to avoid. You will watch, in measured numbers, a request for ten results return 0.4 of them, learn the one-line rule that predicts it, find the selectivity where brute force starts beating the index outright, and then open pgvector: iterative scans, partial indexes, partitioning, and how to read EXPLAIN on a vector query you wrote yourself.",
  sections: [
    { title: "The Problem: The Selectivity Cliff",
      html: "<p>Larkspur has partner contracts, one document set per airport, and an agent in Lisbon may read only the Lisbon contracts. The query is not \"find chunks about baggage allowances\". It is \"find chunks about baggage allowances <em>where airport = LIS</em>\". Lisbon is 0.8 percent of the corpus.</p>" +
            "<p>Every index in Chapters 11 and 12 was built and measured on the assumption that any vector may be returned. HNSW's graph connects vectors by proximity in embedding space and knows nothing about airports. IVF's cells are k-means partitions of the same space. Neither structure has any notion of a predicate, so the obvious thing to do is search first and filter after. Here is what that costs, measured on the Chapter 11 implementation with an attribute assigned at random, requesting ten results:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>Filter matches 100% </strong> (no filter) — fetching 40 candidates returns 10.0 results at recall 0.943.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>50%</strong> — 40 candidates returns 10.0 at 0.943.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>20%</strong> — 40 candidates returns <strong>7.7</strong> at recall 0.724.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>5%</strong> — 40 candidates returns <strong>2.2</strong> at recall 0.209.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>1%</strong> — 40 candidates returns <strong>0.4</strong> at recall 0.039.</li></ul>" +
            "<p>You asked for ten and received less than one. Nothing is broken, no error is raised, and the API returns a valid short list. This is the <strong>selectivity cliff</strong>, and it is the single most common way a working vector search becomes a broken product feature.</p>" +
            "<p>The rule behind it fits on one line. The index returns its best L candidates without regard to the predicate, and roughly a selectivity-sized fraction of them survive the filter, so <strong>results returned ≈ selectivity × L</strong>. At 5 percent selectivity with L = 40 that predicts 2, and the measurement gave 2.2. pgvector's own documentation states the same arithmetic as a warning: with a condition matching 10 percent of rows and the default <code>hnsw.ef_search</code> of 40, only about 4 rows will match. Once you have that formula, every fix in this chapter is a way of attacking one of its three terms.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 260' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='18' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>measured: results returned when 10 were requested, by filter selectivity and fetch depth L</text>" +
            "<line x1='80' y1='200' x2='700' y2='200' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='80' y1='36' x2='80' y2='200' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='end'><text x='74' y='204'>0</text><text x='74' y='163'>2.5</text><text x='74' y='122'>5</text><text x='74' y='81'>7.5</text><text x='74' y='40'>10</text></g>" +
            "<text x='40' y='130' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9' transform='rotate(-90 40 130)'>results returned</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='middle'><text x='700' y='216'>100%</text><text x='576' y='216'>50%</text><text x='452' y='216'>20%</text><text x='328' y='216'>5%</text><text x='204' y='216'>1%</text></g>" +
            "<text x='400' y='234' text-anchor='middle' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>filter selectivity (share of the corpus the predicate keeps), tighter to the left</text>" +
            "<line x1='80' y1='36' x2='700' y2='36' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='3 4'/>" +
            "<text x='90' y='32' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='8.5'>what you asked for</text>" +
            "<polyline points='700,36 576,36 452,36 328,52 204,75' fill='none' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2.5'/>" +
            "<text x='214' y='68' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>L=1000</text>" +
            "<polyline points='700,36 576,36 452,36 328,52 204,174' fill='none' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2.5'/>" +
            "<text x='214' y='168' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='9'>L=200</text>" +
            "<polyline points='700,36 576,36 452,74 328,164 204,193' fill='none' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2.5'/>" +
            "<text x='360' y='158' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>L=40 (pgvector default)</text>" +
            "<polyline points='700,36 576,115 452,167 328,190 204,198' fill='none' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2.5'/>" +
            "<text x='598' y='108' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>L=10</text>" +
            "<circle cx='328' cy='164' r='4' fill='#ffd166'/><circle cx='204' cy='193' r='4' fill='#ffd166'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='252' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>at 1% selectivity with the default depth, a request for 10 results returned 0.4. No error is raised.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F13.1. The cliff is not gradual. Every curve is flat until selectivity approaches the reciprocal of the fetch depth, then falls off.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Three things make this worse in production than in the figure. The attribute here is random, so it is spread evenly through the embedding space; a real predicate like \"airport = LIS\" correlates with position, which can be kinder (the Lisbon chunks cluster together) or far crueller (the matching chunks sit in one region the search never enters). Selectivity varies per query, so the feature works for the large tenants who tested it and fails for the small ones. And the failure is silent, which means you find it from a support ticket rather than a monitor, unless Chapter 25's harness measures recall <em>with filters applied</em>.</p>" },

    { title: "Pre-, Post-, and In-Graph Filtering", viz: "filteredAnn", vizTitle: "Tighten the filter and compare pre-filter, post-filter, and filtered search",
      html: "<p>There are three places to apply a predicate, and the right choice is decided by selectivity alone.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Post-filtering</strong> searches the index for L candidates, then discards the ones failing the predicate. It is the default in most systems because it needs no special support: the index is untouched and the filter is a loop. It is what Section 1 measured, and it fails exactly as that formula predicts. Its knob is L, so the fix is over-fetching, the same reflex as Chapters 6, 7, and 12.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>Pre-filtering</strong> evaluates the predicate first, producing the set of matching ids, and searches only within it. Recall is perfect because the search is exact over that set. Cost is the size of the set, so it is a brute-force scan whose length is set by the filter rather than by the corpus. It needs a fast way to get the id set, which is an ordinary database index on the attribute, and this is why pgvector's filtering advice starts by telling you to create an index on the filter column.</p>" +
            "<p><strong>In-graph filtering</strong> keeps the vector index but consults the predicate during traversal: walk the graph normally, and admit a node to the result set only if it matches. Weaviate describes precisely this, an inverted-index allow-list checked while moving along the graph's edges. It keeps the graph's navigational structure, which pre-filtering throws away, while returning only matching results, which post-filtering fails to do.</p>" +
            "<h3>Where each one wins</h3>" +
            "<p>Now the numbers that make this a decision rather than a taxonomy. Same corpus, same 5,000 vectors, comparing post-filtering at several depths against a pre-filter that scans only the matching subset:</p>" +
            "<ul><li><strong>At 20% selectivity</strong> (980 matching) — post-filtering at L = 200 gave recall 0.978 for 357 distance computations. Pre-filtering gave 1.000 for 980. <em>Post-filtering wins</em> on cost by nearly three to one.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>At 5%</strong> (257 matching) — post-filtering at L = 200 gave 0.890 for 357 computations. Pre-filtering gave 1.000 for 257. <em>Pre-filtering now wins on both axes at once.</em></li>" +
            "<li><strong>At 1%</strong> (41 matching) — post-filtering at L = 1000 gave recall 0.755, still returning only 7.6 of the 10 requested, for 2,017 computations, which is 40 percent of the corpus. Pre-filtering gave 1.000 for <strong>41</strong>. <em>Pre-filtering wins by a factor of 49 and is also the only one that answers the question.</em></li></ul>" +
            "<p>So there is a crossover, it sits between 5 and 20 percent on this data, and below it the index is worse than useless: it costs more and returns less than scanning the matching rows. That is a strong enough result that the major engines hard-code it. Elasticsearch switches to brute force over the filtered set when the filtered document count is at or below <code>num_candidates</code>, and switches mid-search if the nodes it has explored already exceed the filtered count. Weaviate falls back to brute force at roughly 15 percent or less. Both thresholds sit inside the window this measurement identifies, which is a satisfying agreement between a laptop experiment and two production systems. A <strong>brute-force fallback</strong> of this kind is not an admission of defeat by the index; it is the index correctly declining to be used.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='18' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>where each strategy wins, by selectivity (measured crossover shaded)</text>" +
            "<line x1='30' y1='60' x2='730' y2='60' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<g font-size='9' fill='#7d8fb0' text-anchor='middle'><text x='60' y='78'>0.1%</text><text x='200' y='78'>1%</text><text x='380' y='78'>5%</text><text x='520' y='78'>20%</text><text x='700' y='78'>100%</text></g>" +
            "<rect x='380' y='44' width='140' height='32' fill='#ffd166' fill-opacity='0.12' stroke='none'/>" +
            "<text x='450' y='36' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>crossover</text>" +
            "<rect x='30' y='96' width='420' height='26' rx='5' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='240' y='113' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>PRE-FILTER: exact scan of the matching ids</text>" +
            "<text x='460' y='113' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>recall 1.000, cost = matching rows</text>" +
            "<rect x='400' y='132' width='330' height='26' rx='5' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='565' y='149' text-anchor='middle' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='9.5'>POST-FILTER: search L, then discard</text>" +
            "<text x='120' y='149' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>returns &lt; k here, at any affordable L</text>" +
            "<rect x='30' y='168' width='700' height='26' rx='5' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2'/>" +
            "<text x='380' y='185' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9.5'>IN-GRAPH: traverse the graph, admit only matching nodes (Weaviate allow-list, ACORN)</text>" +
            "<line x1='30' y1='206' x2='730' y2='206' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='226' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>measured at 1%: pre-filter, 41 computations at recall 1.000. Post-filter at L=1000, 2,017 computations and 7.6 of 10 returned.</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='242' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>Elasticsearch and Weaviate both fall back to brute force inside the shaded band. Their thresholds are not arbitrary.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F13.2. The bands overlap on purpose. In the crossover region the choice depends on your corpus, which is why it must be measured rather than assumed.</div></div>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Try it.</strong> In the machine above, tighten the filter to 2 percent and step through the strategies. Post-filtering returns a short list and the count on screen falls below ten. Pre-filtering returns ten with perfect recall while its cost bar climbs toward the brute-force line. Filtered search keeps both. Then set selectivity to 60 percent and watch pre-filtering become the expensive option: at that point you are scanning most of the corpus to avoid a filter that was barely filtering.</div>" },

    { title: "Repairing the Graph: Filterable HNSW and ACORN",
      html: "<p>In-graph filtering has a defect the previous section skipped. Walking the graph while admitting only matching nodes works when matches are common. When they are rare, the walk spends its whole budget on nodes it must reject, and worse, it can be <em>trapped</em>: every neighbour of the current node fails the predicate, so there is nowhere to go, even though matching vectors exist elsewhere in the graph. The graph's edges were chosen by proximity, and proximity does not respect your predicate.</p>" +
            "<p>Two repairs exist, and they attack it from opposite ends.</p>" +
            "<h3>Build the predicate into the graph</h3>" +
            "<p><strong>Filterable HNSW</strong> is the name for the first approach. Qdrant adds extra edges to the HNSW graph based on indexed payload values, so that nodes sharing an attribute value are connected to each other as well as to their geometric neighbours. A search filtered to <code>airport = LIS</code> then has a sub-graph of Lisbon chunks it can traverse. The cost is that these edges must exist at build time, which is why Qdrant's documentation is emphatic that payload indexes should be created before ingesting data: build the graph after the index and it gets the extra edges, build it before and it does not.</p>" +
            "<p>Qdrant also names two multitenancy-specific cases. A <strong>tenant index</strong> tells the engine that a field partitions the data into tenants, so it can group each tenant's vectors together on disk and search them without touching the others. A <strong>principal index</strong> does the same for a field that dominates most queries, such as a timestamp on a corpus that is nearly always queried by recency. Both are the same idea as pgvector's partitioning in Section 5: if one attribute governs almost every query, stop treating it as a filter and make it part of the storage layout.</p>" +
            "<h3>Repair the traversal instead</h3>" +
            "<p><strong>ACORN</strong>, from Liana Patel, Peter Kraft, Carlos Guestrin, and Matei Zaharia in 2024, takes the other route: leave the graph alone and change how it is walked, so that no predicate has to be known in advance. That property, being <strong>predicate-agnostic</strong>, is the point, because the alternative approaches all require you to enumerate at build time which attributes will be filtered on.</p>" +
            "<p>The mechanism is neighbour expansion. When a node's direct neighbours fail the predicate, ACORN considers its <em>two-hop</em> neighbours, the neighbours of its neighbours, treating the rejected nodes as bridges rather than dead ends. This approximates traversing the sub-graph induced by the predicate, which is the ideal strategy and impractical to build for every possible predicate. A parameter, gamma, controls how aggressively the expansion happens, and it is the usual trade: more expansion, better recall under tight filters, more work per hop. The paper reports throughput improvements between two and a thousand times over prior filtered-search methods at fixed recall, the wide range reflecting how much the answer depends on selectivity and on how the predicate correlates with the geometry. It is now the default filter strategy in Weaviate, alongside the older sweeping approach, and is available in Qdrant from version 1.16.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 230' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='185' y='20' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>plain traversal under a tight filter</text>" +
            "<text x='565' y='20' text-anchor='middle' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>ACORN: two-hop expansion</text>" +
            "<line x1='375' y1='30' x2='375' y2='200' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/>" +
            "<circle cx='120' cy='110' r='7' fill='#3ddc97'/><text x='120' y='96' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>here</text>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0'><circle cx='190' cy='70' r='5'/><circle cx='200' cy='120' r='5'/><circle cx='175' cy='160' r='5'/></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='120' y1='110' x2='190' y2='70'/><line x1='120' y1='110' x2='200' y2='120'/><line x1='120' y1='110' x2='175' y2='160'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#3ddc97'><circle cx='290' cy='60' r='5'/><circle cx='305' cy='140' r='5'/></g>" +
            "<text x='215' y='190' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>every neighbour fails the</text><text x='215' y='202' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>predicate: the walk stops</text>" +
            "<text x='300' y='45' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>matching, unreachable</text>" +
            "<circle cx='500' cy='110' r='7' fill='#3ddc97'/>" +
            "<g fill='#7d8fb0'><circle cx='570' cy='70' r='5'/><circle cx='580' cy='120' r='5'/><circle cx='555' cy='160' r='5'/></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#7d8fb0' stroke-width='1.5'><line x1='500' y1='110' x2='570' y2='70'/><line x1='500' y1='110' x2='580' y2='120'/><line x1='500' y1='110' x2='555' y2='160'/></g>" +
            "<g fill='#3ddc97'><circle cx='670' cy='60' r='5'/><circle cx='685' cy='140' r='5'/></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.2'><line x1='570' y1='70' x2='670' y2='60'/><line x1='580' y1='120' x2='685' y2='140'/></g>" +
            "<g stroke='#ffd166' stroke-width='2' stroke-dasharray='5 3'><line x1='500' y1='110' x2='670' y2='60'/><line x1='500' y1='110' x2='685' y2='140'/></g>" +
            "<text x='500' y='190' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>non-matching neighbours become</text><text x='500' y='202' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>bridges, not dead ends</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='222' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9.5'>gamma controls how far the expansion reaches. Predicate-agnostic: nothing about the filter is known at build time.</text>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F13.3. The trap and the repair. Green nodes match the predicate; grey nodes do not, and in the right panel they are still useful.</div></div>" },

    { title: "pgvector's Answer: Iterative Scans, Partial Indexes, and Partitioning",
      html: "<p>Postgres cannot rewrite its index traversal per predicate the way a purpose-built engine can, because the index and the filter live in different layers: the HNSW index returns rows in distance order and the executor applies the <code>WHERE</code> clause above it. That is post-filtering, structurally, and pgvector's documentation says so and quantifies it. So pgvector offers four answers, and choosing between them is the practitioner skill this section teaches.</p>" +
            "<h3>1. Index the filter column</h3>" +
            "<p>The first suggestion in pgvector's filtering guidance is the least exotic: put an ordinary B-tree index on the attribute. For a sufficiently selective condition the planner will use it, fetch the matching rows, and sort them by distance without touching the vector index at all. That is pre-filtering, arrived at by the planner rather than by you, and Section 2's measurements say it is the correct plan below the crossover. Multicolumn indexes cover conditions on several attributes. B-tree mechanics are Chapter 5 of <em>Databases in Depth</em> if you want the internals.</p>" +
            "<h3>2. Iterative index scans</h3>" +
            "<p>When the vector index is the right access path but the filter is eating the results, <strong>iterative scans</strong> let pgvector go back for more instead of returning a short list. Enable one of two modes:</p>" +
            "<pre><code>SET hnsw.iterative_scan = strict_order;   -- results in exact distance order\nSET hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;  -- slight reordering allowed, better recall\nSET hnsw.max_scan_tuples = 20000;         -- upper bound on how far it will go (default)\nSET hnsw.scan_mem_multiplier = 2;         -- memory it may use, as a multiple of work_mem\n\n-- IVFFlat has the same idea with its own limit\nSET ivfflat.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;\nSET ivfflat.max_probes = 100;</code></pre>" +
            "<p>The difference between the modes is worth understanding rather than memorising. <code>strict_order</code> guarantees the rows come back in true distance order, which forces the scan to hold candidates back until it can prove ordering. <code>relaxed_order</code> permits slight deviation and reaches higher recall for the same work. If a reranker (Chapter 16) is going to reorder these rows anyway, <code>relaxed_order</code> costs you nothing you were keeping. <code>max_scan_tuples</code> is the safety valve that stops a hopeless query from scanning the whole index, and its default of 20,000 is also a ceiling on how selective a filter this mechanism can rescue.</p>" +
            "<h3>3. Partial indexes</h3>" +
            "<p>If the filter takes a small number of distinct values, build one vector index per value and let each be complete within its slice:</p>" +
            "<pre><code>CREATE INDEX ON chunks USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops) WHERE (airport = 'LIS');\nCREATE INDEX ON chunks USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops) WHERE (airport = 'LHR');</code></pre>" +
            "<p>Inside the Lisbon index every vector matches the predicate, so the cliff disappears entirely: selectivity is 100 percent within the index the planner chooses. This is the cleanest fix available and it does not scale, because it is one index per value and Larkspur has a few hundred airports. Use it when the values are few and stable, such as a document-type or language column.</p>" +
            "<h3>4. Partitioning</h3>" +
            "<p>For many values, or for tenant isolation, partition the table and let each partition carry its own vector index. A query naming the tenant touches one partition, which is again a 100 percent selective index, and the isolation is physical rather than a predicate anyone can forget. This is the same structural move as Qdrant's tenant index, and it is the standard answer for multitenancy. It is also the one that interacts with Chapter 28's access control: a filter enforced by partition routing is much harder to bypass than a filter enforced by a <code>WHERE</code> clause someone has to remember to add.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>The edge.</strong> These four are ordered by how much they change your schema, and that is the order to try them in. But notice what they have in common: every one of them either converts the filter into a pre-filter, or makes the filter unnecessary by giving it its own index. pgvector never gets to Section 3's in-graph filtering, because Postgres's layering does not allow it. Knowing that tells you when a purpose-built vector database earns its place in your architecture, which is Chapter 30's question.</p></div>" },

    { title: "pgvector From the Inside",
      html: "<p>Chapter 7 introduced pgvector's types and operators for storage and distance. Here is the whole surface in one place, including the index parameters this part of the course has been accumulating.</p>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 280' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='18' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>types: storage limit, and the lower limit for an HNSW or IVFFlat index</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5'><text x='20' y='40' fill='#7d8fb0'>type</text><text x='150' y='40' fill='#7d8fb0'>element</text><text x='300' y='40' fill='#7d8fb0'>store up to</text><text x='430' y='40' fill='#7d8fb0'>index up to</text><text x='570' y='40' fill='#7d8fb0'>use it for</text></g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='46' x2='740' y2='46' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='66'>vector</text><text x='150' y='66'>float32</text><text x='300' y='66'>16,000 d</text><text x='430' y='66'>2,000 d</text><text x='570' y='66'>the default</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='88'>halfvec</text><text x='150' y='88'>float16</text><text x='300' y='88'>16,000 d</text><text x='430' y='88' fill='#3ddc97'>4,000 d</text><text x='570' y='88'>half the bytes; the only</text><text x='570' y='100'>way to index 3,072 d</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='122'>bit</text><text x='150' y='122'>1 bit</text><text x='300' y='122'>16,000 d</text><text x='430' y='122'>64,000 d</text><text x='570' y='122'>binary quantization (Ch 7)</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='144'>sparsevec</text><text x='150' y='144'>float32</text><text x='300' y='144'>16,000 nnz</text><text x='430' y='144'>1,000 nnz</text><text x='570' y='144'>learned sparse (Ch 9)</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='158' x2='740' y2='158' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='178' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>operators</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='198'>&lt;-&gt;  L2</text><text x='170' y='198'>&lt;=&gt;  cosine</text><text x='330' y='198'>&lt;#&gt;  negative inner product</text><text x='580' y='198' fill='#ff5d73'>note the sign</text>" +
            "<text x='20' y='218'>&lt;+&gt;  L1</text><text x='170' y='218'>&lt;~&gt;  Hamming</text><text x='330' y='218'>&lt;%&gt;  Jaccard</text><text x='580' y='218' fill='#7d8fb0'>bit vectors only</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "<line x1='20' y1='232' x2='740' y2='232' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1.5'/>" +
            "<text x='20' y='252' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>index parameters</text>" +
            "<g font-size='9.5' fill='#c9d6ea'>" +
            "<text x='20' y='272'>HNSW build: m=16, ef_construction=64   search: hnsw.ef_search=40   |   IVFFlat build: lists   search: ivfflat.probes=1</text>" +
            "</g>" +
            "</svg><div class='fig-cap'>F13.4. The one table worth keeping open. The indexable dimension limits are why halfvec exists, and the sign on the inner-product operator is the trap in everyone's first query.</div></div>" +
            "<h3>Reading the type table</h3><p>Three details in that table decide real schemas. The <em>indexable</em> dimension is lower than the storable one, and for <code>vector</code> it is 2,000, which is below OpenAI's 3,072-dimensional embeddings from Chapter 7. So a 3,072-dimensional corpus cannot be indexed as <code>vector</code> at all: you store it as <code>halfvec</code>, which indexes to 4,000, or you shorten it with the Matryoshka truncation of Chapter 7. That is a case where a storage decision is forced by an index limit rather than by memory.</p><p>The <code>bit</code> type with the Hamming operator is Chapter 7's binary quantization, and it is the one place in Postgres where the two-pass rescoring pattern is written as ordinary SQL: order by Hamming distance on a generated bit column in a subquery, then re-order the survivors by cosine on the original column. <code>sparsevec</code> is where a SPLADE or ELSER vector from Chapter 9 lives, and its indexable limit counts non-zero elements rather than dimensions, which is the right unit for a learned sparse representation.</p><p>On the operators, the one that costs people an afternoon is <code>&lt;#&gt;</code>. It returns the <em>negative</em> inner product, so that ascending order still means most similar first and it composes with <code>ORDER BY ... LIMIT</code> like every other distance operator. Read a raw score from it and every number is negative, which looks like a bug and is not. If your embeddings are unit-normalized, which Chapter 7 established most are, cosine and inner product rank identically and the choice between <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;#&gt;</code> is about arithmetic cost rather than semantics.</p><h3>Choosing between the two indexes</h3>" +
            "<p>Chapters 11 and 12 explained the mechanisms; here is the decision in pgvector's own terms. <strong>HNSW</strong> gives a better speed-recall trade-off, builds slowly, and uses more memory. <strong>IVFFlat</strong> builds faster, uses less memory, and gives a worse speed-recall trade-off, and it must be created after the table has data because k-means needs something to cluster. For a corpus that is loaded once and queried forever, HNSW. For one that is rebuilt frequently, or where build time is on a critical path, IVFFlat deserves a measurement.</p>" +
            "<p>Two operational settings carry over from Chapter 11 and are worth repeating because they are the difference between a build that takes minutes and one that takes hours: raise <code>maintenance_work_mem</code> so the graph fits in memory during construction, and raise <code>max_parallel_maintenance_workers</code> above its default of 2. Put both back afterwards.</p>" +
            "<p>For the storage-engine mechanics underneath all of this, how a Postgres index page is laid out, what the buffer cache does with it, and how the planner costs an access path, the sibling course <em>PostgreSQL Internals in Depth</em> covers it properly and this chapter will not duplicate it.</p>" },

    { title: "Reading EXPLAIN on a Vector Query", viz: "pgvectorExplain", vizTitle: "Four plans for the same question: flat, HNSW, HNSW with a filter, and iterative",
      html: "<p>Everything in this chapter is visible in a query plan, if you know which node to look at. Run <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> on the query and read three things: which access path was chosen, how many rows each node produced, and where rows were discarded.</p>" +
            "<p>The plan you want for an unfiltered vector query has an index scan feeding a limit, with no sort node, because the index is already returning rows in distance order:</p>" +
            "<pre><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE\nSELECT id FROM chunks ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 LIMIT 10;\n\nLimit  (actual rows=10 loops=1)\n  -&gt;  Index Scan using chunks_embedding_idx on chunks  (actual rows=10 loops=1)\n        Order By: (embedding &lt;=&gt; $1)</code></pre>" +
            "<p>Now add the predicate, and the shape changes in a way that tells you exactly what Section 1 described:</p>" +
            "<pre><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE\nSELECT id FROM chunks WHERE airport = 'LIS' ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 LIMIT 10;\n\nLimit  (actual rows=2 loops=1)                        &lt;-- asked for 10, got 2\n  -&gt;  Index Scan using chunks_embedding_idx on chunks  (actual rows=2 loops=1)\n        Order By: (embedding &lt;=&gt; $1)\n        Filter: (airport = 'LIS'::text)\n        Rows Removed by Filter: 38                    &lt;-- the cliff, in one line</code></pre>" +
            "<div class='fig'><svg viewBox='0 0 760 250' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='Menlo,monospace'><text x='20' y='18' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='11'>the same question, three plans: read the top node's row count and the filter line</text><rect x='20' y='32' width='225' height='96' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#3ddc97' stroke-width='2'/><text x='132' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9.5'>no filter</text><text x='30' y='70' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>Limit (actual rows=10)</text><text x='38' y='86' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>-&gt; Index Scan (hnsw)</text><text x='46' y='102' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>Order By: embedding &lt;=&gt; $1</text><text x='30' y='120' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>10 asked, 10 returned</text><rect x='265' y='32' width='225' height='96' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#ff5d73' stroke-width='2'/><text x='377' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9.5'>+ WHERE airport = 'LIS'</text><text x='275' y='70' fill='#ffd166' font-size='9'>Limit (actual rows=2)</text><text x='283' y='86' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>-&gt; Index Scan (hnsw)</text><text x='291' y='102' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>Rows Removed by Filter: 38</text><text x='275' y='120' fill='#ff5d73' font-size='9'>10 asked, 2 returned</text><rect x='510' y='32' width='230' height='96' rx='6' fill='#111a2e' stroke='#4f7cff' stroke-width='2'/><text x='625' y='50' text-anchor='middle' fill='#4f7cff' font-size='9.5'>+ index on airport (pre-filter)</text><text x='520' y='70' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>Limit (actual rows=10)</text><text x='528' y='86' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>-&gt; Sort (embedding &lt;=&gt; $1)</text><text x='536' y='102' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9'>-&gt; Index Scan (airport)</text><text x='520' y='120' fill='#3ddc97' font-size='9'>10 asked, 10 returned</text><line x1='20' y1='146' x2='740' y2='146' stroke='#1c2a45' stroke-width='1'/><text x='20' y='168' fill='#2de2ff' font-size='10.5'>what to look at, in order</text><text x='30' y='188' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>1. actual rows at the Limit node. Below your LIMIT means the query ran out of candidates, not out of matches.</text><text x='30' y='206' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>2. Rows Removed by Filter. This is the selectivity cliff, counted.</text><text x='30' y='224' fill='#c9d6ea' font-size='9.5'>3. which index was scanned. A Sort node above an attribute scan is a pre-filter, and below the crossover that is correct.</text><text x='30' y='242' fill='#7d8fb0' font-size='9'>plan shapes are pgvector's documented forms; row counts follow Section 1's arithmetic. Section 7 produces yours.</text></svg><div class='fig-cap'>F13.5. A vector query plan says everything this chapter says, in three lines, if you know which three.</div></div>" +
            "<p>Those two lines are the whole diagnosis. <strong>Rows Removed by Filter</strong> counts what the predicate threw away after the index had already chosen it, and an <code>actual rows</code> below your <code>LIMIT</code> at the top means the query ran out of candidates rather than out of matches. Forty candidates in, thirty-eight discarded, two returned: that is <code>hnsw.ef_search</code> at its default of 40 meeting a selective filter, and it is what a support ticket about \"missing results\" looks like from the database side.</p>" +
            "<p>Turn on an iterative scan and the same query re-plans into something that goes back for more, so <code>actual rows</code> reaches the limit and the removed count rises accordingly. If instead the planner chooses a B-tree index on <code>airport</code> and sorts the results, you will see an index scan on the attribute feeding a sort node: that is pre-filtering, it is the right plan below the crossover, and the sort node is not a problem to be optimized away.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>What the planner does not know.</strong> Postgres costs an access path using statistics about how many rows a predicate matches. It has no statistic for how many rows a <em>distance ordering</em> will need to examine, because that depends on the query vector and on the index's internal structure. So the planner can be confident about the filter and blind about the vector, which is why it sometimes picks the vector index for a highly selective query where a B-tree scan would have been faster and more accurate. When the plan looks wrong, the fix is usually to give the planner a better option, an index on the filter column or a partial index, rather than to fight it with hints.</div>" +
            "<p>A caveat about this section, stated plainly: no PostgreSQL instance was available on the machine where this chapter was written, so the plans above are the documented shapes rather than captured output, with the row counts chosen to match the arithmetic of Section 1. The next section gives you the commands to produce your own, and yours are the ones that matter.</p>" },

    { title: "Try It on Your Machine",
      html: "<p>Twenty minutes, a local Postgres, and your own chunks from Chapter 5. Every claim in this chapter becomes something you have seen.</p>" +
            "<pre><code>-- 1. set up\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\nCREATE TABLE chunks (\n  id        bigserial PRIMARY KEY,\n  airport   text NOT NULL,\n  body      text NOT NULL,\n  embedding vector(768)\n);\n-- load your chunks here, then:\nCREATE INDEX ON chunks USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)\n  WITH (m = 16, ef_construction = 64);\n\n-- 2. see the cliff. pick an airport matching about 1% of rows.\nSELECT airport, count(*), round(100.0*count(*)/sum(count(*)) OVER (), 2) AS pct\nFROM chunks GROUP BY airport ORDER BY 2 LIMIT 10;\n\nEXPLAIN ANALYZE\nSELECT id FROM chunks WHERE airport = 'LIS'\nORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; (SELECT embedding FROM chunks WHERE id = 1) LIMIT 10;\n-- expect: actual rows below 10, and a 'Rows Removed by Filter' line\n\n-- 3. widen the search, then make it iterative\nSET hnsw.ef_search = 400;                  -- over-fetch: helps, at a cost\nSET hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;   -- go back for more instead\nSET hnsw.max_scan_tuples = 20000;\n-- re-run the EXPLAIN ANALYZE above and compare actual rows and timing\n\n-- 4. pre-filter instead, and let the planner choose it\nRESET hnsw.iterative_scan;\nCREATE INDEX ON chunks (airport);\nANALYZE chunks;\n-- re-run: on a selective airport you should now see a scan on the airport\n-- index feeding a sort, and actual rows = 10\n\n-- 5. the fix that removes the problem\nCREATE INDEX ON chunks USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)\n  WHERE (airport = 'LIS');\n-- re-run: the partial index is 100% selective, so the cliff cannot occur</code></pre>" +
            "<p>What you should see, in order: a short result list with rows removed by the filter; the list filling up as <code>ef_search</code> rises, with latency rising too; the iterative scan reaching ten with less added latency than brute over-fetching; the planner switching to the B-tree path once the attribute is indexed and analyzed; and the partial index answering in full at the original speed. That sequence is the chapter.</p>" +
            "<p>Two things are worth watching that the commands do not print on their own. The first is that step 3's two remedies are not equivalent even when they return the same number of rows. Raising <code>ef_search</code> makes every query in the session do more work, including the unfiltered ones; the iterative scan does extra work only when the filter has actually removed too much. On a workload where most queries are unfiltered, that difference is the whole latency budget.</p><p>The second is what happens at step 4 <em>before</em> you run <code>ANALYZE</code>. Creating the index is not enough, because the planner chooses paths from statistics, and until the table has been analyzed it does not know how selective <code>airport = 'LIS'</code> is. A newly created index that the planner ignores is one of the more common false conclusions in this area, and the fix is one command rather than a redesign.</p>" +
            "<p>One measurement to add, because it is the one that changes decisions: time each variant with <code>\\timing on</code> and record the numbers next to the selectivity of the airport you chose. Then repeat with an airport matching 30 percent of rows. The ordering of the five variants will be different, and that difference is the crossover of Section 2 appearing in your own database.</p>" +
            "<div class='callout'><strong>Interview lens.</strong> \"Your vector search returns three results when the user asked for ten. Why?\" is the whole chapter in one question, and the answer is that the index chose its candidates before the filter was applied, so returned results are roughly selectivity times fetch depth. \"How would you fix it?\" should produce more than one option ordered by selectivity: over-fetch, iterative scan, pre-filter, partial index, partition. \"When is a filter so selective that the vector index hurts?\" wants the crossover and the observation that Elasticsearch and Weaviate both implement a fallback for exactly this reason.</div>" },

    { title: "Exercises",
      html: "<ol><li><strong>Trace.</strong> Using the rule that returned results are about selectivity times fetch depth, predict how many of ten requested results survive a filter matching 3 percent of the corpus at <code>ef_search</code> of 40, 200, and 1000. Then find the fetch depth needed to expect a full ten. What you should find: about 1.2, 6, and 30, so a depth of roughly 333 is needed in expectation, and the measured table shows recall lagging behind even when the count is met, because the candidates were chosen without regard to the predicate.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Trace.</strong> From Section 2's measurements, compute the cost ratio between post-filtering at L = 1000 and pre-filtering at 1 percent selectivity, and state the recall for each. What you should find: 2,017 against 41 distance computations, a factor of 49, with recall 0.755 against 1.000. Then explain in one sentence why every extra unit of L helps less than the last.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Run the five steps of Section 7 against your own corpus and record actual rows, timing, and the plan shape for each. Repeat at a selectivity near 30 percent. What you should find: the ordering of the variants reverses between the two selectivities, which locates your own crossover. Then build the same table with an IVFFlat index at <code>lists = sqrt(rows)</code>, sweep <code>ivfflat.probes</code> from 1 up to <code>sqrt(lists)</code> with no filter applied, and plot recall@10 against p95 latency with Chapter 10's harness. What you should also find: recall at the default <code>probes = 1</code> sits far below what the same vectors reach under HNSW, and it is the curve rather than any documented default that tells you which <code>probes</code> value your latency budget can pay for.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Investigate.</strong> Take the labelled question set from Chapter 8 and add a filter to every query, chosen so that selectivity varies from 50 percent down to 1 percent. Measure recall@10 with the filter applied at each level, using Chapter 10's harness. What you should find: recall that looked acceptable unfiltered degrading sharply at the tight end, which is the argument for measuring retrieval quality under the filters your product actually sends.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Add filtering to your Chapter 11 HNSW implementation three ways: post-filter with a configurable depth, pre-filter by scanning an allowed set, and in-graph by admitting only matching nodes during traversal. Measure recall, results returned, and distance computations at selectivities of 100, 20, 5, and 1 percent. What you should find: the table in Section 2 reproduced, including the crossover, and an in-graph variant that beats both elsewhere but can return nothing when the walk is trapped.</li>" +
            "<li><strong>Build.</strong> Extend the in-graph version with ACORN's two-hop expansion: when a neighbour fails the predicate, consider its neighbours instead of discarding it. Measure recall at 1 percent selectivity against the plain in-graph version. What you should find: recall recovered at a higher cost per hop, and a gamma-like parameter you can tune, which is the paper's mechanism reproduced in about twenty lines.</li></ol>" }
  ],
  terms: [
    { t: "selectivity", d: "The fraction of the corpus a predicate keeps; the variable that decides which filtering strategy is correct." },
    { t: "selectivity cliff", d: "The sharp loss of results and recall when a filter is applied after an approximate search, because candidates were chosen without regard to the predicate." },
    { t: "post-filtering", d: "Searching the vector index for candidates and discarding those failing the predicate, so results returned are roughly selectivity times fetch depth." },
    { t: "pre-filtering", d: "Evaluating the predicate first and searching exactly within the matching set, giving perfect recall at a cost equal to the size of that set." },
    { t: "in-graph filtering", d: "Traversing the vector index normally but admitting only predicate-matching nodes to the result set, keeping both navigation and correctness." },
    { t: "allow-list", d: "The set of ids satisfying a predicate, produced by an inverted or attribute index and consulted during graph traversal." },
    { t: "brute-force fallback", d: "Switching to an exact scan of the matching rows when the filtered set is small enough that the index costs more than it saves." },
    { t: "filterable HNSW", d: "An HNSW graph given extra edges based on indexed attribute values, so vectors sharing an attribute remain reachable under a filter." },
    { t: "ACORN", d: "A predicate-agnostic filtered-search method (Patel et al. 2024) that expands to two-hop neighbours when direct neighbours fail the predicate." },
    { t: "predicate-agnostic", d: "Requiring no knowledge at build time of which attributes will be filtered on." },
    { t: "tenant index", d: "An index declaring that a field partitions data into tenants, letting the engine group and search each tenant's vectors in isolation." },
    { t: "principal index", d: "An index on the field that dominates most queries, used to lay out storage in the order queries will read it." },
    { t: "iterative index scan", d: "pgvector's mechanism for returning to the index for more candidates when a filter has removed too many, bounded by max_scan_tuples." },
    { t: "strict_order", d: "The iterative-scan mode that guarantees results in exact distance order." },
    { t: "relaxed_order", d: "The iterative-scan mode that permits slight reordering and reaches higher recall for the same work." },
    { t: "partial index", d: "An index built with a WHERE clause so that every entry satisfies the predicate, making selectivity within the index total." },
    { t: "partitioning", d: "Splitting a table so each partition holds one tenant or value and carries its own index, converting a filter into routing." },
    { t: "Rows Removed by Filter", d: "The EXPLAIN ANALYZE line counting rows the predicate discarded after the index had already selected them; the selectivity cliff made visible." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: "mc", q: "A vector search with hnsw.ef_search = 40 and a filter matching 5% of rows is asked for 10 results and returns 2. What happened?",
      opts: ["The index is corrupt and needs rebuilding", "The embedding model failed on those queries", "The index selected its 40 best candidates without regard to the predicate, and about 5% of them survived it; returned results are roughly selectivity times fetch depth", "The filter column lacks an index, so rows were skipped"], a: 2,
      why: "The measured value at 5% and L = 40 was 2.2, and pgvector's own documentation states the same arithmetic: a condition matching 10% of rows with ef_search 40 leaves about 4." },
    { type: "blank", q: "The EXPLAIN ANALYZE line that counts how many candidates the predicate discarded after the index had already chosen them is \"Rows Removed by ____\".", a: ["filter"],
      why: "Read it together with actual rows at the Limit node: a limit that was not reached means the query ran out of candidates rather than out of matches." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Measured at 1% selectivity: post-filtering at depth 1000 gave recall 0.755 for 2,017 distance computations and still returned 7.6 of 10; pre-filtering gave recall 1.000 for 41. What follows?",
      opts: ["Below some selectivity the vector index costs more and returns less than scanning the matching rows, which is why Elasticsearch and Weaviate implement brute-force fallbacks", "Post-filtering needs a depth above 2,000 to work", "The HNSW graph was built with too small an M", "Pre-filtering is always the correct strategy"], a: 0,
      why: "Pre-filtering won by a factor of 49 on cost and was the only strategy answering the question. Above the crossover the ordering reverses, which is why both bands overlap in the figure." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Why can in-graph filtering return nothing even when matching vectors exist?",
      opts: ["The allow-list is capped in size", "Matching vectors are excluded from the graph at build time", "In-graph filtering requires a partial index", "The graph's edges were chosen by proximity, so under a tight filter every neighbour of the current node may fail the predicate, leaving the walk nowhere to go"], a: 3,
      why: "This is the trap ACORN's two-hop expansion repairs, treating non-matching neighbours as bridges rather than dead ends, and it is why Qdrant adds payload-aware edges at build time." },
    { type: "mc", q: "What makes ACORN predicate-agnostic, and why does that matter?",
      opts: ["It stores a separate graph per predicate", "It changes traversal rather than the graph, expanding to two-hop neighbours when direct ones fail, so no attribute has to be nominated for filtering at build time", "It requires all filters to be equality conditions", "It converts every filter into a partial index"], a: 1,
      why: "The alternatives, such as payload-aware extra edges, require you to enumerate filterable attributes before ingesting. ACORN's authors report 2x to 1,000x throughput at fixed recall over prior filtered search." },
    { type: "order", q: "Order pgvector's filtering remedies by how much they change your schema, which is the order to try them:", items: ["raise hnsw.ef_search to over-fetch more candidates", "enable an iterative index scan so the index is revisited for more", "add a B-tree index on the filter column so the planner can pre-filter", "create a partial vector index per filter value, or partition the table"],
      why: "The first two are session settings, the third is an ordinary index, and the last changes the physical layout. Each converts the filter into a pre-filter or removes the need for one." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A filter column has three distinct values and every query specifies one. What is the cleanest pgvector fix?",
      opts: ["Raise max_scan_tuples above 20,000", "Switch from HNSW to IVFFlat with a high probes setting", "Enable strict_order iterative scans", "Three partial HNSW indexes, one per value, so selectivity inside the chosen index is 100% and the cliff cannot occur"], a: 3,
      why: "Partial indexes are the cleanest available fix and do not scale past a small number of stable values; for many values or tenant isolation, partition instead." },
    { type: "mc", q: "When would you prefer relaxed_order over strict_order for an iterative scan?",
      opts: ["When the results feed a reranker that will reorder them anyway, so the guaranteed ordering buys nothing and relaxed_order reaches higher recall for the same work", "When exact distance ordering is required by the API contract", "When the filter column has no index", "When using IVFFlat rather than HNSW"], a: 0,
      why: "strict_order holds candidates back to prove ordering. If Chapter 16's cross-encoder is about to re-sort the list, that guarantee is discarded downstream." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Why does Postgres sometimes choose the vector index for a highly selective query when a B-tree scan would be faster and more accurate?",
      opts: ["Vector indexes are always preferred by the planner", "B-tree indexes cannot be combined with an ORDER BY", "The planner has statistics for how many rows a predicate matches but none for how many rows a distance ordering will need to examine, which depends on the query vector and the index's internals", "The vector index reports a lower cost because it is smaller"], a: 2,
      why: "The planner is confident about the filter and blind about the vector. The remedy is to give it a better option, an index on the filter column or a partial index, rather than to fight it with hints." },
    { type: "mc", q: "A pgvector table of 4,000,000 rows carries an IVFFlat index built with the suggested lists = sqrt(rows). Unfiltered recall@10 measures 0.42 against exact search. What do you change first, and what does it cost?",
      opts: ["Rebuild the index with lists = rows / 1000, giving 4,000 cells, because a table of four million rows is past the range of the square-root formula", "Raise ivfflat.probes from its default of 1 toward sqrt(lists), which is 45 here; recall follows cells scanned, and so does latency", "Raise hnsw.ef_search, the search-time beam width that governs how many candidates any pgvector index will examine before it stops", "Nothing at the index: 0.42 is the recall an IVFFlat index is built to give, and the repair belongs in an exact rescoring pass over the rows it returns"], a: 1,
      why: "The default probes = 1 scans one cell out of two thousand, so every true neighbour on the far side of a cell boundary is invisible, which is the same boundary problem Chapter 12 measured as recall 0.798 at nprobe = 1 against 0.995 at nprobe = 4. Note the direction of the formulas: rows / 1000 is the rule below a million rows and sqrt(rows) above it. There is no correct probes value to look up, only a curve to measure on your own machine." },
    { type: "mc", q: "Which statement about measuring retrieval quality is the lesson of this chapter?",
      opts: ["Recall measured without filters transfers to filtered queries if the index is well tuned", "Recall must be measured with the filters your product actually sends, because a system with acceptable unfiltered recall can degrade sharply at tight selectivities and fail silently", "Filters do not affect recall, only latency", "Filtered recall can be computed analytically from selectivity alone"], a: 1,
      why: "The failure raises no error and returns a valid short list, so it surfaces as a support ticket rather than a monitor unless the harness applies filters. Selectivity also varies per query, so the feature works for large tenants and fails for small ones." }
  ]
});
